    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540986/ops-research-assistant": {
      "title": "OPS Research Assistant",
      "description": "OPS Research Assistant Apply now Job no: 540986 Work type: Temp Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Computer Science, Artificial Intelligence, Grant or Research Administration Department: 29051300 - MD-NEPHROLOGY Classification Title: OPS Research Assistant Classification Minimum Requirements: High School Diploma. Job Description: The Computational Microscopy Imaging Laboratory (CMI Lab), within the Department of Medicine, is seeking a highly organized and dependable OPS Research Assistant to support multiple ongoing federally funded research initiatives within the laboratory. The incumbent will assist faculty, postdoctoral researchers, and graduate students with computational pathology, biomedical imaging, and spatial omics research activities. Responsibilities include supporting computational image analysis, data organization and quality control, software testing, visualization of research data, scientific documentation, and general laboratory research support while working collaboratively with a multidisciplinary team of investigators, clinicians, and researchers. Essential functions: Research Data Processing and Computational Support – - Assist with preparation, organization, and processing of biomedical imaging, digital pathology, and spatial omics datasets for research projects. - Support computational image analysis, data annotation, and quality assurance activities using established software tools and laboratory workflows. - Perform routine data entry, file organization, and documentation to support ongoing research studies. Data Quality Control and Research Documentation – - Conduct quality control checks on imaging and research datasets to identify inconsistencies, missing information, or formatting issues. - Assist with maintaining standardized data organization, documentation, and research records to support reproducibility. - Organize research files and assist with maintaining laboratory documentation and project records. Software and Research Workflow Support – - Assist with testing and validation of laboratory software tools, image analysis workflows, and web-based visualization applications. - Support implementation and maintenance of established computational workflows under faculty and research staff supervision. - Assist with organizing research outputs and maintaining shared project resources. Scientific and Laboratory Support – - Assist with literature reviews, preparation of figures, presentations, progress reports, and manuscript materials. - Participate in laboratory meetings and collaborate with faculty, postdoctoral researchers, graduate students, and research staff on assigned projects. - Provide general research support for ongoing federally funded laboratory activities. Expected Salary: $18.00 Hourly Required Qualifications: High School Diploma. Preferred: B.Sc. degree in Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning, Data Science, Computer Science, Electrical Engineering, Biomedical Engineering, or a related field. Experience with image analysis, computer programming using Python and R, and machine learning frameworks such as TensorFlow and PyTorch. Familiarity with digital pathology, biomedical imaging, or spatial omics data analysis. Experience using version control systems (e.g., Git) and scientific computing tools in a Linux or Windows environment. Experience with scientific data visualization and preparation of figures for presentations or publications. Graphic design experience and the ability to create visual materials or research graphics are desirable. Demonstrated interest in artificial intelligence, computational pathology, biomedical imaging, or related research areas. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Health Assessment Required: No. Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541195/crisis-intervention-consultant-student-assistant": {
      "title": "Crisis Intervention Consultant (Student Assistant)",
      "description": "Crisis Intervention Consultant (Student Assistant) Apply now Job no: 541195 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Student Services, Counseling/Community Outreach/Social Work Department: 07010000 - HO-HOUSING-GENERAL STU AST CLERICAL & ADMIN Classification Minimum Requirements: This position is open to all students who are eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS) and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. Registered students are employed on a part-time basis, up to 20 hours per week. Enrolled as a full-time University of Florida graduate student in good standing seeking their degree in counseling or related area of study Successful completion of the core counseling curriculum and first practicum are required; or similar demonstrated academic and clinical experience Be available for 20-hours per week Job Description: The Crisis Intervention Consultant Student Assistant within Housing & Residence Life is responsible for the following: Create education materials, basic workshops, and continuing education trainings for staff related to mental health such as mediation, basic counseling skills, and stress management Provide intervention and consultation, both on-site and over the phone, for residents and Housing & Residence Life staff Serve in an on-call rotation to foster a culture of continuous care responding to student concerns and emergencies utilizing established crisis management and response protocols. Coordinate with staff and campus partners using established processes to assess needs, resolve crises, and connect residents with appropriate campus and community resources. Observe, document, and report concerns and policy violations using approved reporting systems and timelines Complete assigned administrative tasks such as: Generating reports as directed, completing required forms, and managing communications provided to residents Maintain current knowledge of Housing & Residence Life policies, procedures, emergency protocols, and documentation standards Attend all scheduled staff meetings, trainings, and individual supervisory meetings; participate in required staff development and compliance training modules. Expected Salary: $17,000 for a 12-month appointment Required Qualifications: This position is open to all students who are eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS) and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. Registered students are employed on a part-time basis, up to 20 hours per week. Enrolled as a full-time University of Florida graduate student in good standing seeking their degree in counseling or related area of study Successful completion of the core counseling curriculum and first practicum are required; or similar demonstrated academic and clinical experience Be available for 20-hours per week Preferred: Experience or coursework in crisis intervention such as skills and knowledge associated with mental status assessment and crisis theory and practice Knowledge of university and community resources Demonstrated ability to effectively communicate with a variety of audiences Demonstrated professionalism, dependability, and ability to lead or mentor others Ability to work well within a collaborative environment Special Instructions to Applicants: Must submit a résumé and cover letter with application Must submit letter of recommendation from program advisor or practicum/internship coordinator that addresses the applicant’s experience, skills, and fit for role This position may request approval for additional university employment (up to 11 hours) Application must be submitted by 11:55p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541207/agricultural-assistant-ii": {
      "title": "Agricultural Assistant II",
      "description": "Agricultural Assistant II Apply now Job no: 541207 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Marion Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Agriculture Operations, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 60095000 - AG-EQUINE SCIENCES CENTER Classification Title: Agricultural Assistant II Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and two years of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: The Department of Animal Sciences' Equine Science Center is seeking an Agricultural Assistant II. This individual will be involved in the care of horses and assist with research data collection and other duties as assigned. The employee will perform routine maintenance on facilities and equipment and keep accurate records relating to expenditures, inventories, and maintenance logs. Duties and responsibilities include but are not limited to: - Assist with record keeping on horses, personnel and equipment as needed to meet program needs. Must have basic computer skills to be able to create equipment repair and maintenance records - Perform maintenance activities for all unit grounds, buildings and fences and the maintenance of farm equipment and farm vehicles - Operate equipment used in delivering feed, mowing pastures, spreading manure and producing hay. Operate state vehicle to carry out duties around farm unit. - Perform or assist with care, feeding and management of horses in accordance with University animal care and use guidelines - Assist with research projects including animal care, data collection and records management - Perform or assist with facility construction and farm improvement - Supervise student labor as needed Normal Work Schedule: 7:00 A.M. – 4:00 P.M. (40 hrs per week) Some weekends and evenings required for animal care and production of hay. Requires work outdoors in inclement weather (heat, cold, rain, dust, pollen). This position will be considered an essential personnel position in emergency situations. Expected Salary: $19.00 per hour Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and two years of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Valid driver's license Preferred: Horse-handling experience Herd health experience Ability to manage and create content for social media Ability to keep accurate computerized records Experience operating tractors and mowers Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for veteran’s preference. If you are claiming veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541301/post-doc-associate-laboratory-of-stem-cell-and-metabolism-nutrient-sensing-pathways": {
      "title": "Post Doc Associate - Laboratory of Stem Cell and Metabolism - Nutrient Sensing Pathways",
      "description": "Post Doc Associate - Laboratory of Stem Cell and Metabolism - Nutrient Sensing Pathways Apply now Job no: 541301 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60090000 - AG-ANIMAL SCIENCES Classification Title: Postdoc Aso Classification Minimum Requirements: The appointee shall have received the doctorate in a field appropriate to the assigned duties and responsibilities. Job Description: The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . The Laboratory of Stem Cell and Metabolism at the University of Florida (UF) seeks a highly motivated and creative Postdoctoral Associate to join a multidisciplinary research team investigating the mechanisms that regulate muscle stem cell metabolism, aging, and tissue regeneration. The successful candidate will lead independent and collaborative research projects focused on nutrient-sensing pathways, cellular metabolism, mitochondrial structure and function, and mechanisms of aging in skeletal muscle stem cells using both human cells and mouse models. The position offers opportunities to integrate molecular, metabolic, genetic, and in vivo approaches to understand how metabolic and mitochondrial adaptations influence stem cell function, regenerative capacity, and age-associated decline. The Postdoctoral Associate will work closely with Dr. Feng Yue (Principal Investigator) and collaborators to develop new research directions, contribute to high-impact studies, and advance the laboratory’s broader mission of understanding metabolic adaptation, stem cell resilience, and tissue regeneration during aging. The anticipated start date is November 2026. The Postdoctoral Associate will be expected to: Set the tone for both scientific and scholarly excellence within the lab. Design, execute, and analyze experiments using molecular, cellular, genetics and physiological techniques. Drive forward complex and impactful projects, leading to high-quality and impact publications. Develop fellowship and grant applications to support independent research growth. Present findings in lab meetings, departmental seminars, and national/international conferences. Contribute to mentoring of graduate and undergraduate trainees. Engage actively with collaborators and contribute to the lab’s dynamic and collegial environment. Dr. Yue is fully committed to supporting the career development of all trainees, providing ample opportunities for one-on-one mentorship, professional growth, and preparation for both academic and industry career paths. The University of Florida is a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution, encompassing virtually all academic and professional disciplines, with an enrollment of more than 56,000 students. UF is a member of The Association of American Universities. The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences includes the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences , the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station , the Florida Cooperative Extension Service , the College of Veterinary Medicine , the Florida Sea Grant program, and encompasses 16 on-campus academic departments and schools, 12 Research and Educational Centers (REC) located throughout the state, 6 Research sites/demonstration units administered by RECs or academic departments, and Florida Cooperative Extension Service offices in all 67 counties (counties operate and maintain). The School of Natural Resources and Environment is an interdisciplinary unit housed in IFAS and managed by several colleges on campus. UF/IFAS employs nearly 4,500 people, which includes approximately 990 salaried faculty and 1,400 permanent support personnel located in Gainesville and throughout the state. IFAS, one of the nation’s largest agricultural and natural resources research and education organizations, is administered by a Vice President and four deans: the Dean of the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences, the Dean for Extension and Director of the Florida Cooperative Extension Service, the Dean for Research and Director of the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station, and the Dean for the College of Veterinary Medicine. UF/IFAS also engages in cooperative work with Florida A&M University in Tallahassee. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Experience and Education Required Qualifications: The candidate must hold a Ph.D. (or M.D./Ph.D., foreign equivalent acceptable) in a relevant biological science, such as molecular biology, cell biology, genetics, biochemistry, or a related field, and demonstrate a strong record of research productivity and peer-reviewed publications. Candidates must be supportive of the mission of the Land-Grant system. Candidates must also have a commitment to UF core values. Preferred: Expertise in cellular and molecular biology techniques, including cell culture, gene editing, immunoprecipitation (IP), Western blotting, flow cytometry, and related approaches. Experience in cellular metabolism, metabolic biology, or metabolic biochemistry. Experience with in vivo animal models and/or human primary cells. Experience with CRISPR screening, and bioinformatics/computational analysis of sequencing data. Excellent oral and written communication skills in English, with strong organizational skills, attention to detail, analytical ability, and the capacity to work both independently and collaboratively. Special Instructions to Applicants: Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Individuals wishing to apply should go online to Careers at UF and submit: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for 3 individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541302/post-doc-associate-laboratory-of-stem-cell-and-metabolism-epigenetic-mechanisms": {
      "title": "Post Doc Associate - Laboratory of Stem Cell and Metabolism - Epigenetic Mechanisms",
      "description": "Post Doc Associate - Laboratory of Stem Cell and Metabolism - Epigenetic Mechanisms Apply now Job no: 541302 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60090000 - AG-ANIMAL SCIENCES Classification Title: Postdoc Aso Classification Minimum Requirements: The appointee shall have received the doctorate in a field appropriate to the assigned duties and responsibilities. Job Description: The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . The Laboratory of Stem Cell and Metabolism at the University of Florida (UF) seeks a highly motivated and creative Postdoctoral Associate to join a multidisciplinary research team investigating the epigenetic mechanisms that regulate adipose and liver metabolism, fibrosis and metabolic disease. The successful candidate will lead independent and collaborative research projects focused on chromatin remodeling complexes, cellular metabolism, and the molecular mechanisms underlying obesity and metabolic dysfunction-associated steatohepatitis (MASH), using both human cells and mouse models. The position offers opportunities to integrate molecular, metabolic, genetic, epigenomic, and in vivo approaches to define how chromatin organization and remodeling reprogram cellular function in health and disease. The Postdoctoral Associate will work closely with Dr. Feng Yue (Principal Investigator) and collaborators to develop new research directions, contribute to high-impact studies, and advance the laboratory’s broader mission of understanding metabolic adaptation, stem cell resilience, and tissue regeneration during aging. The anticipated start date is November 2026. The Postdoctoral Associate will be expected to: Set the tone for both scientific and scholarly excellence within the lab. Design, execute, and analyze experiments using molecular, cellular, genetics and physiological techniques. Drive forward complex and impactful projects, leading to high-quality and impact publications. Develop fellowship and grant applications to support independent research growth. Present findings in lab meetings, departmental seminars, and national/international conferences. Contribute to mentoring of graduate and undergraduate trainees. Engage actively with collaborators and contribute to the lab’s dynamic and collegial environment. Dr. Yue is fully committed to supporting the career development of all trainees, providing ample opportunities for one-on-one mentorship, professional growth, and preparation for both academic and industry career paths. The University of Florida is a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution, encompassing virtually all academic and professional disciplines, with an enrollment of more than 56,000 students. UF is a member of The Association of American Universities. The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences includes the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences , the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station , the Florida Cooperative Extension Service , the College of Veterinary Medicine , the Florida Sea Grant program, and encompasses 16 on-campus academic departments and schools, 12 Research and Educational Centers (REC) located throughout the state, 6 Research sites/demonstration units administered by RECs or academic departments, and Florida Cooperative Extension Service offices in all 67 counties (counties operate and maintain). The School of Natural Resources and Environment is an interdisciplinary unit housed in IFAS and managed by several colleges on campus. UF/IFAS employs nearly 4,500 people, which includes approximately 990 salaried faculty and 1,400 permanent support personnel located in Gainesville and throughout the state. IFAS, one of the nation’s largest agricultural and natural resources research and education organizations, is administered by a Vice President and four deans: the Dean of the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences, the Dean for Extension and Director of the Florida Cooperative Extension Service, the Dean for Research and Director of the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station, and the Dean for the College of Veterinary Medicine. UF/IFAS also engages in cooperative work with Florida A&M University in Tallahassee. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Education and Experience Required Qualifications: The candidate must hold a Ph.D. (or M.D./Ph.D., foreign equivalent acceptable) in a relevant biological science, such as molecular biology, cell biology, genetics, biochemistry, or a related field, and demonstrate a strong record of research productivity and peer-reviewed publications. Candidates must be supportive of the mission of the Land-Grant system. Candidates must also have a commitment to UF core values. Preferred: Expertise in cellular and molecular biology techniques, including cell culture, gene editing, immunoprecipitation (IP), Western blotting, flow cytometry, and related approaches. Experience with epigenetic and genomic approaches, such as ChIP, ChIP-seq, CUT&RUN, ATAC-seq, and related technologies. Experience with CRISPR screening, and bioinformatics/computational analysis of sequencing data. Experience in cellular metabolism, metabolic biology, or metabolic biochemistry. Experience with in vivo animal models and/or human primary cells. Excellent oral and written communication skills in English, with strong organizational skills, attention to detail, analytical ability, and the capacity to work both independently and collaboratively. Special Instructions to Applicants: Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Individuals wishing to apply should go online to Careers at UF and submit: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for 3 individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541440/hospital-assistant-care": {
      "title": "Hospital Assistant (CARE)",
      "description": "Hospital Assistant (CARE) Apply now Job no: 541440 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 28311000 - VM-HOSPITAL Classification Title: Veterinary Assistant Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent. Job Description: The AAHA accredited University of Florida Small Animal Hospital is recruiting for a Hospital Veterinary Assistant. The veterinary assistant works with veterinarians and technicians to provide support for patient care and administrative duties in the Small Animal Hospital. This position works under immediate supervision from a manager as well as supportive oversight from veterinarians and technicians. Provides basic patient care and administrative support as directed. This individual will work one-on-one with clients, patients, and hospital staff and must be able to develop friendly, professional relationships and maintain a positive attitude through all interactions. This position is responsible for the overall cleanliness of the hospital, providing support to client service representatives in both cleaning needs as well as retrieving food orders; stocking supplies and food; and maintaining laundry services throughout the Small Animal Hospital. Individuals should genuinely care about people and animals, possess excellent communication skills, strong public relations skills, initiative to problem solve, utilize active listening skills and confidence in handling customer interactions, project a professional image, and good interpersonal skills. Must be outgoing, full of enthusiasm, and a team player. This is a full-time, 40/hr per week position. The schedule is 5 days a week, including working at least one weekend day and may require holidays to be worked as well. Expected Salary: $15.00 /hr Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This is a CARE position offering limited benefits, including health insurance and paid time off. When appropriate certifications are achieved, promotional opportunities to a level II title may be available. This position will also become eligible for transition into a TEAMS (staff) salary plan. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: Yes; Must be able to lift a minimum of 40 lbs, bend, kneel, pull, push, twist and stand for an extended period of time. Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541484/administrative-specialist-i": {
      "title": "Administrative Specialist I",
      "description": "Administrative Specialist I Apply now Job no: 541484 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Health Care Administration/Support, Human Resources Department: 29120000 - MD-PSYCHIATRY Classification Title: Administrative Specialist I Job Description: Administrative Operations & Office Support •Serve as the primary point of contact for incoming calls, visitors, and general departmental inquiries. •Maintain department calendars, schedule meetings, reserve conference rooms, and coordinate logistics for departmental activities. •Perform data entry, record maintenance, document preparation, and general administrative support functions. •Coordinate mailroom operations, including distribution of incoming and outgoing correspondence and shipments. •Travel reimbursements, and office supplies. •Assist with departmental communications and dissemination of information to faculty and staff. Human Resources Administrative Support •Provide administrative support for HR-related activities, including preparation and maintenance of job descriptions. •Assist with Graduate Assistant (GA), volunteer, courtesy appointment, and other personnel-related documentation and processes. • Generate reports and maintain personnel records and departmental tracking systems. • Support HR initiatives and special projects as assigned by the Human Resources Generalist III. • Ensure accuracy, confidentiality, and compliance with university policies and procedures regarding personnel information. Event & Meeting Coordination • Coordinate department meetings, retreats, staff recognition events, milestones, luncheons, and year-end activities. • Manage meeting logistics, including scheduling, room reservations, technology needs, catering arrangements, and event setup. • Support conference room scheduling and maintenance. • Assist in planning and executing departmental special events and professional development activities. Emergency Preparedness & Departmental Coordination • Serve as the department emergency contact coordinator and assist with maintaining departmental emergency plans and procedures. • Participate in and support building committee activities and initiatives. • Coordinate departmental communications related to emergency preparedness and operational continuity. Staff Development & Special Projects • Assist with coordinating staff professional development activities and training opportunities. • Support departmental initiatives, process improvements, and special administrative projects. • Provide additional administrative and operational support as needed to meet departmental goals and objectives. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor's degree or an equivalent combination of education and appropriate experience. Preferred Requirements: • Experience providing administrative support in a higher education, healthcare, or professional office environment. • Experience with human resources administrative processes and personnel documentation. • Experience coordinating departmental events, meetings, and staff development activities. • Knowledge of University of Florida administrative and HR systems and procedures. Expected Salary: $46,000 - 56,000 Benefits: As one of the nation’s top public research universities, the University of Florida offers a comprehensive benefits package designed to support the health and financial well-being of employees and their families. Highlights include: Competitive salary commensurate with experience Medical, dental, vision, and prescription insurance plans Flexible spending accounts and wellness programs Life and long-term disability insurance Retirement savings plans with employer contributions Generous paid leave (vacation, sick, holidays, and personal days) Eight weeks of paid parental and medical leave Eligibility for the Public Service Loan Forgiveness Program Discounts on campus and local services and activities Enjoy discounts for on-and off-campus activities and services About the Department of Psychology: Faculty in the Department of Psychiatry at the University of Florida are involved in a broad range of clinical and research initiatives through departmental centers, labs, and interdisciplinary collaborations. The department includes a large outpatient center with various specialty clinics, including one affiliated with the Center for Autism and Neurodevelopment—along with an active inpatient service, a psychiatry collaborative program for primary care providers, school-based consultation programs, and community mental health consultation services. The department offers clinical services and training in adult, child and adolescent, and addiction psychiatry. This includes operations at a freestanding psychiatric hospital, a robust consult-liaison service at UF Health Shands Hospital, and the Florida Recovery Center, which is operated by the Addiction Division. The department is also home to several research initiatives, including the Center for Addiction Research & Education and the Center for OCD and Related Disorders (COARD). UF is an active site for the NIH-funded Adolescent Brain Cognitive Development (ABCD) Study. The University of Florida is a top public university and one of the largest academic medical centers in the Southeast. UF Health is a regional leader in innovative, evidence-based mental health treatment and is committed to advancing clinical research. Researchers across the college pursue interdisciplinary work through nine major research centers and institutes. About Gainesville: Located in North Central Florida, Gainesville is home to the state’s oldest and largest university. It’s a recognized hub for education, healthcare, research, the arts, and innovation. Gainesville is frequently ranked among the top college towns and best places to live or retire. Known for its natural beauty and preserved historic charm, the city offers a wide range of parks, museums, lakes, and cultural attractions that appeal to residents and visitors alike. Things to do in Gainesville . Special Instructions to Applicants: Interested applicants should submit the following items as part of the online application: Interested applicants should submit the following materials as part of the online application: Cover Letter that outlines your interest in the position and addresses how your qualifications align with the required and preferred criteria. Curriculum Vitae List of Three References including names and contact information. Please include individuals who can speak to your qualifications, scholarly potential, and effectiveness as a colleague in an academic health sciences environment. References will be contacted by the department if the applicant is under serious consideration. This is a time-limited position. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541593/clinical-case-manager-ii": {
      "title": "Clinical Case Manager II",
      "description": "Clinical Case Manager II Apply now Job no: 541593 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Health Care Administration/Support, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29090700 - MD-PEDS-DEI Classification Title: Clinical Case Manager II Classification Minimum Requirements Master's degree in an appropriate area of specialization and two years of experience; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area of specialization and five years of appropriate experience. Job Description: This position is responsible for establishing and maintaining performance standards and other metrics for evaluation of LES case management with focus on IDEA, Part C State Performance Plan/Annual Report Indicators. This includes interpreting and putting in place standards for measuring timelines of services ,Individual Family Support Plans (IFSP)outcomes, and compliance with the statutorily required performance standards. Develop and ensure processes are implemented to evaluate and identify opportunities for improvement in the provision of high-quality services offered in accordance with IDEA. Work closely with Case Manager Supervisor to address any non-compliance relative to case management. Execute process improvements solutions to compliance barriers, using data analysis and optimization tools to support strategies for improvement plans. Assist with pre-service and ongoing training of case managers. This position performs two primary case management functions: intake and referral responsibilities and temporary Case Manager coverage during staff absences, vacancies, or periods of high caseloads. Intake and referral responsibilities include conducting initial contacts with families, explaining the Early Steps Program’s philosophy, eligibility and evaluation processes, documenting family concerns, updating child and family information, providing education regarding developmental issues, completing developmental screenings when necessary, and coordinating evaluations and other next steps. When providing temporary case management coverage, the position delivers targeted case management services, including facilitating the development, review, and revision of Individualized Family Support Plans (IFSPs) with families and multidisciplinary team members. The position ensures services are family-centered, culturally responsive, and consistent with the Early Steps natural-environments principle, while educating families about procedural safeguards, service-delivery options, transition planning, and available community resources. The position collaborates with providers and community partners to coordinate evaluations, authorizations, and service implementation and maintains accurate and timely documentation. Assigned cases are monitored for compliance with all federal and state timelines, including the 45-day timeline and six-month and annual review requirements. The position also completes required data entry and billing for targeted case management activities. Proficiency with statewide data systems, Medicaid billing procedures, and Early Steps documentation standards is essential. This position is required to participate in in-service training programs as specified by the State Early Steps Program. Completion of this requirement must be documented in the coordinator's personnel file. This position acts as a community liaison for the Early Steps Program, networking with other providers of early intervention services, including the public-school systems, private providers, and the medical system. This requires attendance at interagency. Expected Salary: $58,000-$63,000 Preferred Qualifications • Must meet State requirements for Service Coordinator • Knowledge of IDEA, Part C Early Intervention Policies • Working knowledge of child development, family dynamics, developmental disabilities, and factors which place infants/ toddlers at risk for developmental delays • Skill in working with young children and their families in collaborating with parents and other professionals. • Experience writing outcome and qualitative note taking abilities • Experience working with community support systems and resources • Experience with quality assurance • Communication and interpersonal skills which are effective, respectful, and sensitive with respect to a varied and multicultural population of children, families, and co-workers • Skills in problem-solving, mediation, conflict resolution and teamwork are essential for collaboration as a team member, as well as for working with families and community agencies • Strong computer skills. Comfortable in using Microsoft Suite: Outlook, Excel, Word and PowerPoint and databases • Ability to function under pressure in a fast-paced human service environment • Must be capable of dealing with periodic cyclical workload pressures and levels of responsibility • Planning/Organizing - Prioritizes and plans work activities; Uses time efficiently; Develops realistic action plans. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes. Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541639/academic-program-spec-iii": {
      "title": "Academic Program Spec III",
      "description": "Academic Program Spec III Apply now Job no: 541639 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services Department: 60150000 - AG-FOOD SCIENCE - HUMAN NUTR Classification Title: Academic Program Spec III Classification Minimum Requirements: Master's degree and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: Oversees planning, implementing, evaluating, and maintaining records for the DPD and DI programs, including program development, assessment, and ACEND accreditation. Collects, organizes, analyzes, and maintains data and documentation required for ACEND annual reports, self-studies, program assessments, and internal and external reviews. Supports the program directors with interpretation and implementation of ACEND Accreditation Standards and assists with the evaluation of program policies, curriculum, competencies, and student learning outcomes. Identifies and recommends opportunities for program enhancement, quality improvement, and growth. Coordinates the development and maintenance of supervised practice affiliations and experiential learning opportunities for the DI program. Coordinates student support services and program operations for the undergraduate Didactic Program in Dietetics (DPD) and graduate Dietetic Internship (DI) programs. Communicates with students regarding program requirements, policies, deadlines, and professional development opportunities. Provides advanced guidance and support to dietetics students on program processes and requirements. Coordinates DI supervised practice scheduling and serves as a liaison with preceptors and affiliated sites. Monitors student progress and completion of program requirements and supports the review and tracking of student assignments and program milestones. Supports recruitment, admissions activities, orientations, open houses, and other student engagement initiatives for both dietetics programs. Coordinates recruitment, outreach, and communication activities for the department's Didactic Program in Dietetics (DPD) and Dietetic Internship (DI) programs. Maintains and updates program websites to ensure information related to admissions, curriculum, accreditation, deadlines, policies, and student resources is accurate and current. Develops and coordinates program-related communications and social media content in collaboration with departmental communications personnel. Promotes program initiatives, recruitment events, open houses, student and alumni achievements, and other outreach activities. Serves as a point of contact for prospective students, current students, alumni, preceptors, and affiliated partners, providing information and support regarding program opportunities and requirements. Expected Salary: $60,000 - $70,000; Commensurate with experience Required Qualifications: Master's degree and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Registered Dietitian by the Commission on Dietetic Registration Preferred: Experience with an ACEND-accredited DPD, DI/SPE, CP, or other dietetics education program; experience with accreditation, student records, program assessment, recruitment, or supervised practice coordination. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume, (3) professional letters Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541641/executive-assistant-i": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant I",
      "description": "Executive Assistant I Apply now Job no: 541641 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 27010400 - HA-HSC NEWS - COMMUNICATIONS Classification Title: Executive Assistant I Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree and three years of relevant experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: ADMINISTRATIVE SUPPORT Works closely with other high-level executive assistants across the academic health center and helps to facilitate administrative tasks on behalf of UF Health Communications executive leadership. Serves as a liaison with other internal UF Health departments/units, university stakeholders and external audiences and ensures work is completed promptly and is of high quality, exercising discretion and confidentiality as necessary. Actively coordinates all calendars and schedules for assigned executive leaders while providing supplemental support to UF Health’s SVP/chief marketing & communications officer as needed. Ensures scheduled meetings have appropriate rooms reserved and/or virtual meeting links, with necessary infrastructure in place. Coordinates logistics, including collateral material needs, catering and other requirements. Facilitates development of agendas and/or materials, and prepares and distributes meeting minutes for priority meetings as needed. Collaborates with and provides back-up support for other members of the administrative team to ensure seamless support across the division. DIVISIONAL OPERATIONS Drafts and disseminates general correspondence to UF Health Communications staff regarding news, important updates, time-sensitive announcements and operational matters. Corresponds with individuals internal and external to the organization to support divisional needs. Maintains and communicates UF Health Communications daily staff outages and weekly divisional calendar schedules. Tracks staff leave, monitors time reporting and reconciles time sheets to facilitate management approvals. Updates and maintains divisional organizational charts, contact lists, leave calendars, disaster call lists and staff birthdays. Works with the media relations team to develop and distribute media on-call schedules and ensures information is consistently accurate in SPOK to facilitate timely triage of media inquiries. Media on-call changes are communicated to UF Health communications leadership. Responsible for maintaining, archiving and tracking all patient consent forms executed related to marketing and communications activities to ensure requested information can be easily retrieved and updated as needed Coordinates divisional purchases and facilitates the processing of invoices in a timely manner, including ordering supplies and reconciling UF purchasing card reports. Ensures appropriate supply inventories are maintained to support divisional operations. Maintains and updates software licenses for staff and coordinates staff access to online tools. Maintains and updates asset inventories for computer equipment and video/camera equipment/accessories, and tracks staff assignments. Coordinates travel logistics for staff, including gathering estimated travel costs for management approval, making travel arrangements, developing itineraries and assisting with travel reimbursement processing. Works with communications executive leaders and the business operations manager to facilitate the renewal of vendor/freelancer contracts. Works collaboratively with administrative team members to support onboarding of new staff, including workstation setup, securing badge/building/parking access, orientation to divisional policies and procedures, access to divisional online tools, orienting to the division’s project management system and facilitating team workflow integration. Works with administrative team members to foster an environment of collaboration to support divisional needs. Identifies opportunities to improve administrative processes and operational efficiencies. Proactively identifies potential issues, makes recommendations to support resolution and implements changes. Identifies opportunities to streamline administrative processes, improve operational efficiency and enhance consistency across divisional operations. Escalates issues and recommendations to the business operations manager as appropriate and assists with implementation of approved process improvements. PROJECT MANAGEMENT SUPPORT Responsible for coordinating transmittal of UF Health podcast scripts to support production and distribution requirements. Updates and publishes key web content on the UF Health Newsroom, including UF Health podcasts (Health in a Heartbeat and Animal Airwaves). Serves as a backup for posting news releases, stories, blogs and associated multimedia content. Assists Creative Services in fielding inquiries and fulfilling various photo gallery and corporate logo requests. Also assists with logistics related to major production shoots, including securing rentals, coordinating catering and handling other needs. Gathers, synthesizes and organizes information and assists with development of PowerPoint presentations and briefing materials for communications executive leadership. Assists UF Health Communications staff in monitoring and communicating professional organization award entry deadlines, assists with award submissions, processes entry fees and tracks divisional awards received. Plans and executes various divisional events, including retreats, new employee welcome receptions, staff farewells and other celebrations. Coordinates invitations and event logistics, working collaboratively with other administrative team members to ensure successful execution of events and divisional activities. Supports divisional events through participation in event staffing as business needs and schedules permit. Expected Salary: $29.69 - $33.52/hr Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree and three years of relevant experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience Preferred: EXPERIENCE - Bachelor’s degree and five to eight years of relevant experience or equivalent preferred SKILLS - Proficiency in the use of personal computers and multifunctional devices (required) - Proficiency with Microsoft Word, Excel and PowerPoint (required) - Proficiency in the usage of website content management systems such as WordPress (preferred) - Strong project management skills - Superior spelling, punctuation and grammatical skills - Excellent organizational skills and attention to detail ABILITIES - Ability to plan, prioritize, organize and coordinate work assignments independently in a fast-paced environment - Demonstrated ability to manage multiple projects effectively and meet deadlines - Demonstrated ability to work as part of a team toward organizational objectives - Demonstrated professionalism in interacting with others at all levels within and outside the organization. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please upload the following - CV or Resume, Cover Letter and List of Professional References. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541645/ops-assistant-aibhs-student-faculty-support-specialist": {
      "title": "OPS Assistant - AIBHS Student & Faculty Support Specialist",
      "description": "OPS Assistant - AIBHS Student & Faculty Support Specialist Apply now Job no: 541645 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Academic Advising/Support Department: 29013200 - MD-AIBHS Classification Title: OPS Assistant - AIBHS Student & Faculty Support Specialist Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree or equivalent experience. Job Description: Based in Jacksonville, this OPS position provides direct academic support and tutoring to AIBHS students, particularly those with little or no prior coding experience, and serves as in-room classroom support (a TA-type role) for AIBHS courses taught synchronously by Gainesville-based faculty. Essential Functions: Student Tutoring and Academic Support: Provide one-on-one and small-group tutoring in Python and R to students with limited or no coding background. Develop supplemental instructional materials. Hold regular tutoring and office hours. Monitor student progress and identify students needing additional intervention or referral. Classroom Support: Serve as in-room presence for synchronously taught AIBHS courses, ensuring smooth session delivery. Troubleshoot classroom technology and connectivity issues. Administer in-classroom exams and assessments under the instructor of record's direction. Coordinate exam logistics, scheduling, and space reservations. Expected Salary: $18 - $24/hour; commensurate with education and experience. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated coding proficiency in Python and R, with the ability to explain foundational programming concepts to students with no prior coding background. Preference will be given to candidates whose schedule allows them to be present at 801 W Bay St. Jacksonville, FL during AIBHS class times (TU/TH 11:30 AM - 3:00 PM, 4:00-7:30 PM, W 4:30-6:30 PM). Prior experience as a teaching assistant or tutor is preferred. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541647/regulatory-lead-internal-cro-trial-design-implementation": {
      "title": "Regulatory Lead - Internal CRO Trial Design & Implementation",
      "description": "Regulatory Lead - Internal CRO Trial Design & Implementation Apply now Job no: 541647 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29011700 - MD-OFFICE OF RESEARCH Classification Title: Clinical Research Analyst IV Classification Minimum Requirements: Master’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or a Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Regulatory Lead for Internal CRO Trial Design & Implementation provides regulatory and operational leadership for Clinical Research Hub (CRH)-supported clinical trials from concept development through study activation and implementation. The Lead functions as a regulatory subject matter expert within CRH's internal CRO model, partnering with investigators and cross-functional research teams to translate scientific concepts into feasible, compliant protocols and execution plans. This crucial role coordinates with investigators, biostatistics, data management, pharmacy, finance, contracts, compliance, and other institutional partners to support efficient, compliant study execution. Essential Functions: Trial Design and Regulatory Strategy: Lead regulatory and operational consultation during concept and protocol development for investigator-initiated and other CRH-supported clinical trials. Review study design, objectives, endpoints, eligibility criteria, intervention plans, safety monitoring, schedules of assessments, data collection, and operational feasibility. Assess regulatory pathways and submission requirements, including IRB strategy, sponsor-investigator responsibilities, and IND/IDE considerations when applicable. Identify design or compliance gaps early and develop practical mitigation plans with investigators and CRH partners. Study Start-Up and Implementation: Translate approved protocols into coordinated implementation plans and lead regulatory readiness through activation. Coordinate required approvals, essential documents, study-specific plans, delegation and training requirements, and cross-functional start-up activities with data management, pharmacy, laboratory, finance, contracts, and clinical operations. Confirm that regulatory and operational requirements are complete before study launch and support structured handoff into active study conduct. Internal CRO Regulatory Operations and Project Management: Serve as the regulatory lead for an assigned portfolio of internal CRO projects. Maintain submission and activation timelines, regulatory trackers, essential documentation, and status reporting. Coordinate initial submissions, amendments, continuing review, reportable events, and other regulatory deliverables as applicable. Manage dependencies, escalate barriers, and facilitate decisions across investigators and institutional stakeholders to keep studies moving toward planned milestones. Quality, Compliance, Training, and Inspection Readiness: Perform quality review of protocols, submissions, regulatory files, and implementation materials for consistency with applicable regulations, ICH GCP, IRB requirements, and institutional policy. Develop and maintain SOPs, templates, checklists, and training resources for the internal CRO. Provide regulatory education and consultation to investigators and research teams, support corrective action and process improvement, and promote ongoing inspection readiness. Staff Supervision and Program Improvement: Provide direct supervision to the assigned Clinical Research Analysts (CRAs), including work assignment, prioritization, coaching, quality review, performance feedback, and professional development support. Standardize internal CRO regulatory and trial implementation workflows, monitor service performance, and lead process improvements that increase consistency, efficiency, and accountability across the program. We Offer Exceptional Benefits: Low-cost State Health Plans : Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Life and Disability Insurance Generous Retirement Options to secure your future Comprehensive Paid Time Off Packages with 10+ paid holidays, paid family, sick and vacation leave Exceptional Personal and Professional Development Opportunities : Access to UF Training & Organizational Development programs, leadership development, LinkedIn Learning, and more Tuition Assistance through the UF Employee Education Program Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Eligible Employer Learn more about what we have to offer here ! About Gainesville, Florida: Home to the University of Florida, one of the nation's top public universities, Gainesville offers vibrant academic and healthcare communities alongside an affordable cost of living, no state income tax, and mild winters. Enjoy the outdoors with over 30 miles of bike trails, freshwater springs, and numerous water activities, plus easy access to beaches and world-class theme parks. Gainesville combines historic charm with modern amenities, including a great school system, a lively downtown, and a thriving local culture with farmers' markets and craft breweries. Discover professional growth and quality of life in Gainesville—where community, innovation, and work-life balance meet! For more information, check out: The Guide to Greater Gainesville and Explore Gainesville in 60 seconds Expected Salary: $90,000 - $95,000 annually; commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or a Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in clinical research, regulatory affairs, health sciences, public health, or a related field; advanced degree preferred. Four or more years of progressively responsible experience in clinical research regulatory operations, study start-up, or clinical trial management. Demonstrated experience with protocol development, trial design review, and operational feasibility assessment. Experience supporting FDA-regulated and/or investigator-initiated clinical trials, including IND/IDE and sponsor-investigator responsibilities when applicable. Strong working knowledge of ICH GCP, IRB requirements, applicable FDA regulations, and institutional human research protection processes. Experience coordinating study activation and cross-functional implementation across regulatory, data management, pharmacy, laboratory, finance, contracts, and clinical operations. Experience in a CRO, academic research organization, sponsor operations group, or comparable multi-study clinical research environment. Experience developing SOPs, regulatory templates, quality review processes, and study start-up tools. Strong project management skills with demonstrated ability to manage multiple studies, deadlines, dependencies, and stakeholders. CCRP, CCRC, RAC, PMP, or equivalent clinical research, regulatory, or project management certification preferred. Experience serving as a team lead, supervisor, or work lead and providing training, coaching, work assignment, and deliverable review for research staff or analysts. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541653/administrative-assistant-iii": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant III",
      "description": "Administrative Assistant III Apply now Job no: 541653 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 19010300 - EG-STUDENT SERVICES Classification Title: Administrative Assistant III Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and seven years of relevant experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: Assists the Associate Dean in the overall operation of the office, performing a variety of administrative duties requiring a comprehensive knowledge of the policies and procedures of the Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering and the University of Florida. Assists in coordinating the overall relations of the Associate Dean’s office with the offices and staff in Undergraduate Student Affairs and the departments in the Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering. Assists the Associate Dean in fulfillment of their responsibilities as an administrator, keeping them informed of the status and progress of on-going activities, and serves as the designated representative in follow-up matters where authority has been delegated. Acts as liaison to faculty, staff, and students, relaying directives of the Associate Dean on matters pertaining to office operations, policies, and procedures. Organizes and manages the work of the Associate Dean’s office which includes overseeing the operation and completion of daily activities; setting priorities to efficiently handle the workload and to meet deadlines. Analyzes the needs of Associate Dean’s office in terms of equipment, workflow, and materials, and implements appropriate systems. Receives, sorts, and reviews all incoming communications to the Office of the Associate Dean and determines the appropriate person to handle each item. Provides responses to telephone requests (some are of a detailed or confidential nature) not requiring the involvement of the Associate Dean. Supervises maintenance of general office files and annual disposition of archival file materials. Coordinates action items for the Associate Dean which includes being responsible for tracking and following up on all pending items. Maintains the calendar of recurring reports, projects and various deadlines required of the Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering and the Office for Undergraduate Student Affairs to help administrative staff anticipate deadlines and time commitments. Serves as manager of the reception area in Associate Dean’s office space. As the “first-face” to the office, must receive and direct guests in a courteous and professional manner to the appropriate office. Collaborates closely with other executive staff in the Dean’s office suite. Responds to inquiries from the general public and University administrators. Responsible for maintaining and securing confidential correspondence. Determines those matters that should be handled by other appropriate persons. Coordinates major Student Affairs events and meetings. Secure space for events, manage RSVPs, secure catering and decor, process event-related invoices using appropriate funding source, setup/breakdown events, provide onsite event assistance. Coordinate volunteers from 12 departments for fall, summer, and spring university wide commencements in addition to spring College recognition ceremonies. Serve as point of contact for recognition ceremonies, fielding questions from and guiding graduate candidates, parents, faculty, and staff volunteers. Oversee and direct Engineering Ambassadors in assisting with events. Update volunteer roles and disseminate info. Secure photography, music, and ASL interpreting services. Order faculty regalia and maintain Order of the Engineer inventory, ordering supplies as needed. Order awards for annual Awards Ceremony. Prepare scripts for biannual Order of the Engineer events, spring doctoral and bachelor’s recognition ceremonies, annual Awards Ceremony, and other events, as needed; prepare and coordinate with Development for program printing. Prepare agendas and certificates, as required. Meet with commencement student speakers to prepare them to give commencement speeches. Coordinates College scholarship and awards programs, including the Undergraduate Scholarship Program, Latin American-Caribbean Scholarships, Gator Engineering Attribute Awards, and ad hoc scholarships and awards. Coordinates annual preparation of application form, receipt of completed applications, preparation of applications for review, announcement of recipients, and notification of award sent to recipients. Acts as liaison for students with inquiries regarding applying for scholarships; sends reminders to students of deadlines and procedures. Independently generates new deadlines each year. Serves as the contact for all inquiries related to scholarships and awards. Reviews and clears Associate Dean’s office work schedule daily to assure continuing and prompt responses to inquiries and assignments and that his/her schedule runs efficiently. Coordinates travel (state, national, and international) for the Associate Dean, which includes inputting travel authorization requests and associated documentation, creating and submitting expense report, and ensuring correct reimbursement amount is disbursed. Preparing extensive itineraries (flights, confirmation numbers, directions, etc.), vouchers for reimbursement of travel expenses, change orders, and Foundation reimbursement requests may be required. Drafts replies by forms, letters, and email for a variety of routine, confidential, complex, and sensitive inquiries. Responds to various report requests which may be confidential in nature. When necessary, research background that is helpful for the Associate Dean in various meetings. Assists the Associate Dean with reference material for speeches and organizes presentations as necessary. Assists the Undergraduate Student Affairs staff in preparing correspondence to and certificates for students when needed. Coordinates confidential matters and special assignments as assigned by the Associate Dean, compiling necessary data to obtain the required information. Responds to inquiries from the general public and University administrators. Responsible for maintaining and securing confidential correspondence. Provides support, as reasonable, for any of the associate deans when their assistants are on leave. Cross-trains with other Dean's Office staff in the Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering to provide general front office reception and phone coverage during absences or times of heavy workload. Expected Salary: $23.00-$26.00 per hour; commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and seven years of relevant experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Must exercise confidentiality with sensitive information or data Ability to use own judgment in decision making without input from supervisor. Ability to perform assigned tasks without supervision. Ability to plan, organize and coordinate work assignments. Ability to communicate clearly and effectively both verbally and in writing. Ability to handle telephone calls in a courteous and professional manner. Ability to compose routine correspondence. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relations with others. Ability to organize files and other records. Knowledge of office procedures, grammar usage and basic math. Ability to function under highly stressful circumstances. Ability to interact cordially with co-workers to accomplish common tasks. Strong desire and experience in serving undergraduate students is preferred. Excellent communication and customer service skills required. Ability to work with frequent interruptions is required. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered for this position, you must upload a cover letter and resume with application. This position is eligible for veteran’s preference. If you are claiming veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541654/lecturer": {
      "title": "LECTURER",
      "description": "LECTURER Apply now Job no: 541654 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Allied Health Department: 26030100 - HH-APK-ADMINISTRATION Classification Title: LECTURER Classification Minimum Requirements: Preferred applicants will hold an academic doctorate degree (PhD, EdD, or DSc) in kinesiology, exercise physiology, or a closely related discipline. Applicants with a Master’s degree in kinesiology, exercise physiology, or a related field and a minimum of three (3) years of college- or university-level teaching experience will also be considered. Job Description: The Department of Applied Physiology & Kinesiology (APK) at the University of Florida (UF) is searching for a full-time, non-tenure track Lecturer to join our team. The anticipated start date is August 16, 2027, though an earlier start may be negotiable. This is a 9-month, 1.0 FTE, non-tenure track faculty position with potential summer employment opportunities. Responsibilities related to this position may include, but are not limited to, designing and teaching courses in Applied Human Physiology, Exercise Physiology, and/or Advanced Human Physiology. Primary duties for this position are instructional, but involvement in service at the department, college, and university levels is also expected. Qualifications Preferred applicants will hold an academic doctorate degree (PhD, EdD, or DSc) in kinesiology, exercise physiology, or a closely related discipline. Applicants with a Master’s degree in kinesiology, exercise physiology, or a related field and a minimum of three (3) years of college- or university-level teaching experience will also be considered. Applicants must possess good communication, collaboration, and time management skills. Applicants should be passionate about student learning and demonstrate a commitment to teaching excellence and teamwork. Additionally, applicants should have experience teaching undergraduate and graduate courses, developing new courses, and integrating experiential learning into the curriculum. The Department of Applied Physiology & Kinesiology The department is one of the premier Applied Physiology & Kinesiology programs in the world. Currently, APK employs 32 full-time faculty and enrolls approximately 1,100 students (~900 undergraduate and ~230 graduate). APK Lecturers enjoy a 9-month, renewable contract with abundant support, including access to UF’s Center for Teaching Excellence, in-house course design consultants, lecturer promotion and merit pay plans, conference travel funds, instructional grant opportunities, and team-oriented colleagues. Explore APK at the department website . The University of Florida The University of Florida is a top-ranked Public university in the United States [1] and is Florida’s oldest and largest land grant university. UF is a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities, an organization comprising the top public and private institutions in North America. With an enrollment of over 53,000 students annually, UF is home to 16 colleges and more than 200 research, service and education centers, bureaus, and institutes. The UF campus is in Gainesville, a city of approximately 141,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf of Mexico, 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, and Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, paddleboarding, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County has an excellent school system, with many public schools ranked highly in the state and nation. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville . University of Florida is a member of the State University System of Florida and an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment based on merit. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD). Expected Salary: Salary is commensurate with qualifications and experience. UF offers an outstanding benefits package. Required Qualifications: Preferred applicants will hold an academic doctorate degree (PhD, EdD, or DSc) in kinesiology, exercise physiology, or a closely related discipline. Applicants with a Master’s degree in kinesiology, exercise physiology, or a related field and a minimum of three (3) years of college- or university-level teaching experience will also be considered. Preferred: Applicants must possess good communication, collaboration, and time management skills. Applicants should be passionate about student learning and demonstrate a commitment to teaching excellence and teamwork. Additionally, applicants should have experience teaching undergraduate and graduate courses, developing new courses, and integrating experiential learning into the curriculum. Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Instructions Interested applicants must apply online at https://jobs.ufl.edu (reference job #541654) and include (a) an application letter summarizing your qualifications, career goals, and teaching interests/experience; (b) a teaching statement/philosophy; (c) evidence of teaching proficiency, including recent teaching evaluations with qualitative and quantitative feedback; (d) a curriculum vitae; and (e) names, addresses, email addresses, and telephone numbers of three references. Other evidence of teaching effectiveness such as awards or credentials may also be submitted. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from educational institutions outside the United States must be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org . The search committee will begin reviewing applications immediately. The search will remain open, with rolling review, until the position is filled. To receive full consideration for the initial review, complete applications must be received by November 23, 2026. The anticipated start date is August 16, 2027. Send inquiries and nominations to Dr. Anna Gardner, Search Committee Chair, akgardner@ufl.edu . Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541655/assistantassociatefull-professor": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor Apply now Job no: 541655 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 32020000 - PH-PHARMACEUTICS Classification Title: Assistant/Associate/Full Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Assistant: Candidates for this rank shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess equivalent qualifications based on professional experience. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Associate: Candidates for this rank shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess professional qualifications above those equivalent to the highest degree relevant to their field. They are expected to have produced creative work, professional writing, or research and successful teaching or professional experience. A candidate for this rank must have a distinguished record of achievement beyond the assistant professor level. Full: Candidates for this faculty rank shall be qualified for the rank of associate professor and, in addition, be a recognized national or international authority or an outstanding teacher in their field or specialization. A candidate for this rank must have a distinguished record of achievement beyond the associate professor level. Job Description: The Department of Pharmaceutics, College of Pharmacy ( pc.pharmacy.ufl.edu/ ), University of Florida, invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the assistant/associate/full professor level. Applicants should have a strong record of an independent research program in the general area of pharmaceutics, including, but not limited to, drug delivery, pharmacokinetics/ pharmacodynamics (pharmacometrics) or drug metabolism. The demonstration of or potential for developing an externally funded research program is essential. Faculty hires will also participate in the professional and graduate instructional efforts of the College. Areas of specialization within pharmaceutics should complement the interests of the department pc.pharmacy.ufl.edu/ and the Center for Pharmacometrics and Systems Pharmacology . The position will be located at the Gainesville or Orlando Campus of the University of Florida. Information on the Department and College is available at: pc.pharmacy.ufl.edu/ lakenona.ufhealth.org/ . Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience. Employment Benefits include: Health Insurance: UF participates in state- and university-sponsored benefits programs for individuals, families and domestic partners, and offers voluntary insurance that includes vision, dental, long-term disability and more. Retirement Options: Attractive options include Florida Retirement System Pension Plan, State University System Optional Retirement Program, Florida Retirement System Investment Plan, and Voluntary Retirement Savings Plan. Leave: UF Faculty and Staff enjoy a generous paid leave plan as well as access to a sick-leave pool, maternity/paternity leave, and leave payouts. To learn more visit: hr.ufl.edu/benefits Required Qualifications: PhD and at least 2 years of postdoctoral experience in pharmaceutics and/or PharmD with fellowship or with equivalent at least 2 years of experience. Excellent teaching and communication skills are essential. FDLE Criminal Background Check is required. Applicants will have a strong research and publication record in the field of interest. Preferred: The successful applicant will hold a PhD Degree and/or postdoctoral and/or academic experience in pharmaceutics. Extramurally-funded research program and proven track record of publication will be considered important attributes of successful candidates. Soft skills as well as clear written and verbal communication are essential to team-based research and communication between trainees, faculty, and external collaborators. An understanding of patient care will further be helpful for training and mentoring PharmD students, PhD students, and post-doctoral research associates. Special Instructions to Applicants: Candidates must apply online at Careers at UF. For full consideration, applications must be submitted by October 21, 2026 with the following required documents: CV, letter of interest, teaching philosophy, research plan and contact information for recommendation letters. Three letters of recommendation will be required for final candidates. Applications received after October 21, 2026 will be reviewed at the discretion of the search committee. The position posting will remain open and accept applications until a competitive pool of applicants is established. As a part of our review for research and research support positions, we look for a full CV which includes all professional appointments/engagements, all post-secondary education, and all publications from post-secondary education and all respective dates. Please be sure to not use acronyms in your CV. This CV should include names of entities associated with any projects. The University of Florida is an equal opportunity institution. Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541661/associate-director-academic-support-services": {
      "title": "Associate Director, Academic Support Services",
      "description": "Associate Director, Academic Support Services Apply now Job no: 541661 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support Department: 17220500 - BA-UPO INTERNATIONAL Classification Title: ASO DIR, Academic Support Svcs Classification Minimum Requirements: Master’s degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience; or a bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and six years of relevant experience. Job Description: About the Heavener School of Business: One of the nation’s top-ranked undergraduate public business schools, the Heavener School of Business offers bachelor’s degrees in Finance, General Studies, Information Systems, Management, and Marketing to more than 5,000 students. The four pillars that drive the Heavener School of Business are Academics, Career Development, International Studies, and Leadership. With this foundation, Heavener graduates are prepared to build exceptional careers and lead lives of purpose. Key Functions and Responsibilities Include but are not limited to: ADVISING • Researches, documents, and coordinates advising and administrative policies and procedures under the direction of the Director for International Programs • Provides academic and study abroad advising related to Heavener Sponsored Business (HSB) and other international programs to UF undergraduates, State University System (SUS), and out-of-state students via scheduled in-person appointments, and electronic communication platforms • Assists students in selecting appropriate programs that align with their academic and professional goals • Assists students through their study abroad application process including visa questions and processes • Collaborates with the University of Florida International Center (UFIC) to address student questions and concerns • Assists and advises students during critical or urgent situations utilizing university and partner resources • Presents at the study abroad sessions for GEB 2015 Intro to Business sections PROGRAM MANAGEMENT • Serves as a point of contact for select Heavener Sponsored Business (HSB) programs; Will be the primary contact for 2-3 programs • Collaborates with partners to ensure seamless processes for pre-approving/nominating students to study abroad programs • In collaboration with the partners, creates seamless advising process to enhance students’ experience • Collaborates with the International Center (UFIC) to ensure students complete applications • Resolves scheduling conflicts of UF exams directly with course instructors (professors) • Registers select HSB International Programs participants for UF classes • Reports registration information to UFIC and international partners • Conducts visa seminars, as needed, and works closely with partners abroad to ensure students have accurate and timely information about visa applications • Schedules and conducts pre-departure orientations for the select HSB programs three times per year • Collaborates with Assistant Director to create and published pre-departure guide for HSB programs • Represents International Programs at study abroad fairs, Business Bash, and other recruiting events • Collaborates with international partners to continuously create and/or enhance program operations and efficiency to ensure quality of student services and enhanced student experience • Manages and promotes study abroad opportunities for students ADMINISTRATION • In collaboration with the Teaching Learning and Curriculum (TLC), HSB professors, and international partners, coordinates the scheduling of exams for all HSB programs and the UF online classes • Updates textbook requirements for UF courses offered internationally • Updates the academic calendars for the HSB programs in conjunction with institutional and international partners • Assists in providing training to new employees and student assistants on processes and administration of programs PROGRAM MARKETING • Assists in setting the strategy for promoting HSB programs that align with the mission of the department and assists students in internationalizing their curriculum • Creates and manages events (e.g., leading the coordination of Study Abroad 101 info sessions) each fall and spring semesters • Coordinates representation of International Programs at student organization meetings to promote study abroad • Serves as the point of contact for coordinating with campus stakeholders and representing International Programs at study abroad fairs, Business Bash, and other recruiting events • Utilizes social media and UF information networks to inform students of study abroad opportunities • Coordinates the presentation of study abroad info sessions for GEB 2015 Intro to Business sections and the IP Staff • Represent HSB International Programs at relevant conferences • Produces timely program promotional material that aligns with the overall IP and HSB brand strategy STRATEGIC PLANNING & LEADERSHIP • Under the direction of the IP Director, seeks opportunities for international partnerships and collaborations to enhance the mission of International Programs and the Heavener School of Business • Contributes to the design and implementation of study abroad programs that permit students to engage internationally while completing degree requirements • Collaborates with UF and international partners to continually enhance programs and increase study abroad participation of HSB undergraduates • Collaborates with the director in proposing and developing new initiatives • Supports new initiatives • Collaborates with the director in evaluating the formatting and execution of programs to enhance student participation in study abroad programs, efforts to further internationalize students’ curriculum, and to streamline program delivery and efficiency • Collaborates with domestic and international partners to continuously create and/or enhance program operations • Assists the Director in preparing reports and data for publication • Coordinates and moderates in the [REWIND] re-entry workshop held for students twice a year • Produces evaluation reports and distributes them to international partners and UFIC • Assists the Director in assessing the strengths and weaknesses of the HSB sponsored programs and collaborates with the Director in executing efficiency and enhancing program structure and delivery • Assists in developing assessment tools to determine program outcomes as well as student and partner satisfaction • In collaboration with the Director, manages program evaluations for the sponsored programs • Assists in developing assessment tools to determine program outcomes as well as student and partner satisfaction • Supervises Academic Program Specialist I and the position's work related to HSB programs Work Schedule: Monday to Friday, 8:00am to 5:00pm. This position may require national and international travel Required participation in some events held in the evening or on weekends Ready to Explore the Opportunity and Apply? Employment benefits at the University of Florida include comprehensive health insurance benefits, retirement options, a generous paid leave plan , and an employee education program to support your professional development . Expected Salary: $74,000 - $78,000 Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience; or a bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and six years of relevant experience. Preferred: EDUCTION & TRAINING • Master’s or bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization EXPERIENCE • Six years of full-time experience in study abroad programming, higher ed, and/or international business • Two years of supervisory experience and/or experience leading a team • Previous experience creating and/or managing a study abroad programs • Previous experience leading students on a study abroad program KNOWLEDGE • Thorough knowledge of academic and administrative policies and procedures • Knowledge to facilitate the IP process and help students evaluate the experience Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered for this position, you must upload the following three items. 1. Cover letter 2. Resume 3. List of three professional references with contact information Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 16 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541662/clinical-coder-iii-student-health-care-center": {
      "title": "Clinical Coder III - Student Health Care Center",
      "description": "Clinical Coder III - Student Health Care Center Apply now Job no: 541662 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Health Care Administration/Support Department: 35010204 - SH-INSURANCE OFFICE Classification Title: Clinical Coder III Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and five years of experience in professional medical coding, with experience in two or three clinical specialties. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Certified Professional Coder (CPC)/ American Academy of Professional Coders (AAPC) or Certified Coding Specialist (CCS-P) required. Job Description: The Opportunity The Student Health Care Center (SHCC) at the University of Florida is seeking a Clinical Coder III. Reporting to the Coding and Reimbursement Supervisor, this position serves as a senior member of the SHCC Coding team and is responsible for optimizing billing performance through accurate, compliant, and efficient coding practices. The Clinical Coder III will educate providers on appropriate coding requirements, resolve claim rejections, and apply a thorough knowledge of anatomy, medical terminology, payer coding and billing requirements, and UF College of Medicine and UF Health policies and procedures. This position will also collaborate with the SHCC Quality Officer and Compliance Office on auditing requirements and participate in continuing education to remain current on compliant coding methods that support accurate coding and maximum reimbursement. The key responsibilities of the position include the following: CHARGE ENTRY/REVIEW Review clinic charges in EMR work queues daily, ensuring CPT and ICD-10 for accuracy and resolving all claim edits. Review chart notes to optimize E/M level, procedures and apply payer policy for bundling. Follow up on incomplete/missing charges. Meet with the Supervisor and/or Assistant Director to review individual provider performance and coding issues. CHART REVIEW Perform clinic chart reviews to provide results to the clinical team supervisors and provide feedback to the clinical team on documentation improvement. Collaborate with coders and billing staff on coding updates, reimbursement and compliance improvement strategies. Act as a point of contact for coding questions and guidance. EDUCATION AND CODING COMPLIANCE Research coding issues through all available resources, including but not limited to professional associations, federal and private payer guidelines, and coding networks. Keep abreast of annual coding updates. Provides education to providers in regards to documentation, reimbursement and compliance. Provides feedback to providers regarding billing and coding questions and works with providers to resolve these questions to ensure accurate coding. CLAIMS & REIMBURSEMENT MANAGEMENT Reviews clinic reimbursement reports and track payments/denials and resolve issues delaying reimbursement. Utilize charge entry edits and payor denials as tools to provide additional education and suggest front-end workflow strategies to ensure clean claims. Typical Schedule: 8 am to 5 pm daily (Monday through Friday) and one-hour lunch during Fall and Spring semesters. 8 am to 4:30 pm daily (Monday through Friday) with a one-half hour lunch during Summer semesters. Remote work may be available. About the Student Health Care Center (SHCC) The mission of the SHCC is to help every student achieve optimal health in pursuing personal and academic success. Our vision is to be the preferred healthcare provider for University of Florida students as a nationally recognized leader in the collegiate health specialty. The SHCC is fully accredited by the AAAHC and is located in a state-of-the-art WELL-certified building on UF’s campus. Medical services offered at the SHCC include primary care, sports medicine, urgent care, gynecology, psychiatry, allergy/immunization clinic, and travel medicine. Radiology, pharmacy, lab, and physical therapy are also on site. Learn more about SHCC here https://shcc.ufl.edu/ About the University of Florida UF is one of the nation’s top universities and a dynamic and rewarding place to work. UF is in Gainesville, one of Florida’s most livable cities. Known for its natural attractions (wetlands, forests, springs, wildlife) and cultural offerings (music, theater, visual arts, and historic districts), the area also is gaining a reputation as a thriving hub for high-tech start-ups, as well as invention and research collaborations. We are excited about what’s happening here and know you will want to be a part of it. The City of Gainesville The Guide to Greater Gainesville shares all that you need to learn about the Heart of Florida, including neighborhood and city information, schools and zoning, culture and leisure, sports and fitness, healthcare and much more. Learn more at https://guidetogreatergainesville.com/ Expected Salary: $28.74 - $31.13 per hour Exclusive Perks and Benefits Enjoy stability and support with UF’s unique range of benefits and opportunities. With a robust healthcare package, generous leave policies, retirement planning, and professional development , you can achieve a healthy work-life balance, plan for your future, and advance your career. Full-time TEAMS employees will accrue vacation leave at a rate of 6.769 hours biweekly/22 days annually and sick leave at a rate of 4 hours biweekly/13 days annually. In addition, employees are eligible for ten paid holidays each year. Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and five years of experience in professional medical coding, with experience in two or three clinical High school diploma or equivalent and five years of experience in professional medical coding, with experience in two or three clinical specialties. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Certified Professional Coder (CPC)/ American Academy of Professional Coders (AAPC) or Certified Coding Specialist (CCS-P) required. Preferred: EXPERIENCE: Experience with computer programs for word processing and various computer software applications Experience with computer-generated appointment systems and EHR KNOWLEDGE: Understanding of insurance plans, terms Ability to interpret Explanation of Benefits EPIC system knowledge and experience Knowledge of CPT, HCPCS, and ICD-10 Working knowledge of WayStar and Availity SKILLS: Organizational Verbal and written communication Math Customer service-oriented ABILITIES: Concentration Ability to work independently Ability to organize and maintain patient records Ability to plan, organize, and coordinate work assignments Ability to communicate effectively, both verbally and in writing Ability to evaluate problems and implement solutions Ability to compile and analyze data for administrative decisions Work independently using good judgment in establishing and maintaining office procedures and routines. OTHER QUALIFICATIONS: Ability to work independently Ability to organize and maintain patient records Ability to plan, organize, and coordinate work assignments Ability to communicate effectively, both verbally and in writing Ability to evaluate problems and implement solutions Ability to compile and analyze data for administrative decisions Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume, and a list of at least 3 references. Applications must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Remote work may be available. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541663/fiscal-assistant-iii": {
      "title": "Fiscal Assistant III",
      "description": "Fiscal Assistant III Apply now Job no: 541663 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 33060000 - HP-SLP LANG & HEARING SCI Classification Title: Fiscal Assistant III Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and five years of general accounts transaction experience. Appropriate college course work or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: Fiscal Duties Responsible for all purchasing and reimbursements within the PeopleSoft (PS) application for all faculty, staff, and students within the Department. This includes purchases via UF Purchasing Card (PCARD), Purchase Order (PO), and requisitions and payments for all these expenditures. Process reimbursement to individuals for appropriate out-of-pocket business-related expenses as determined by UF policy. Process myUFMarketplace invoices for approval by Department Administrator. Responsible for the preparation and processing of Supplier (vendor) invoices, payment authorizations, interdepartmental encumbrances, purchase requisitions, and change order requests for POs. Subsequent Supplier follow-up, and coordination and tracking of expenses within the PS application. Assist new Suppliers with application process and portal. Manage all leasing and contract agreements, including approvals from the UF Purchasing Office, and oversight of competitive bidding as necessary. Reconcile PCARDs issued to department associates weekly. Maintain electronic filing system for departmental expenses by fiscal year. Responsible for collection of payments from Dyslexia Reading Lab and Program; verify payments – cash, check, credit card; document payments and receipts in collections log and prepare deposit slips for PHHP Dean’s Office. Prepare check request for Scottish Rite Foundation for Dyslexia Reading Lab. Coordinate all maintenance/UF Facilities work orders and payments for the department. Serve as primary liaison for all maintenance contracts and payments for the department. Maintain maintenance agreements and contacts for repairs of all equipment and process payment. Foundation – prepare UFF-PA forms for invoices paid by Department’s Foundation accounts. Prepare and process travel encumbrances, expense reports, travel authorizations, etc. for the department faculty and students. Process Purchase Approval Requests (PARs), journal entries, and cash expense refunds. Oversee the Department’s direct billing invoices (ports, phones, mail, etc.), reconcile, and communicate any issues to the Department Administrator. Department Fiscal Training and Resource Coordinator Serve as a primary fiscal resource and point of contact for department faculty and staff, providing guidance and support regarding fiscal policies, procedures, purchasing, travel, expense documentation, and other financial processes. Provide ongoing fiscal training and mentoring for department personnel with fiscal responsibilities. Promote consistent application of fiscal procedures and share knowledge and best practices. Monitor departmental fiscal processes and identify opportunities to improve workflow, strengthen internal processes, and increase efficiency. Recommend procedural improvements to the supervisor and/or department chair, as appropriate. Develop, maintain, and update departmental fiscal reference materials, procedures, and training resources. Budget and Financial Management Provide fiscal support and input as needed to assist supervisor in development and preparation of annual departmental budgets. Track and monitor faculty internal grants, start-up funds, contingent commitments, and other designated funding to ensure accurate financial planning and appropriate spending. Prepare, review, and route personnel and payroll transactions, including Payroll Distribution Retroactive Requests (PDRRs) and Cost Transfer Forms, ensuring accuracy, appropriate documentation, and compliance with established requirements. Perform and support financial reconciliations, indirect cost (IDC) compliance reporting, revenue and expenditure projections, and other financial analyses to assist department leadership with fiscal oversight, forecasting, and decision-making. Facilities, Space, and Asset Property Manager Primary administrator of annual Space Allocation process. Responsibilities include schedule annual meetings with faculty on office and lab space, add, update and/or remove occupants in the Space Inventory and Allocation Systems (SPIN) system; enter and submit space allocation information, and verify any projects assigned to room and lab, and attend annual training. Serve as the asset/property coordinator, assist with inventory management and coordinate with department and college procurement to ensure compliance with property tracking, surplus, and applicable protocols, rules, and regulations. Office Administrative Duties Manage office supply inventory, including coordinating ordering of all routine office supplies. and placing all specialty orders, upon approval by the Department Administrator. Assist the department Administrative Assistant with faculty candidate campus visits as needed. This could include researching and booking airfare reservations, hotel rooms, car rentals, itinerary creation and any other tasks as required by the traveler. Coordinate with new faculty hires to submit moving purchase orders. Attend Department Staff and Faculty meetings as applicable. Expected Salary: $26.34 - $31.13 per hour ($55,000 - $65,000 annually); commensurate with education and experience. The University of Florida (UF) offers an exceptional benefits package, including health, dental and vision insurance; state retirement plans; a generous leave program; and tuition assistance. UF is a Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) eligible employer. For more information on benefits, please visit UF HR Benefits and Rewards at https://benefits.hr.ufl.edu/ . Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and five years of general accounts transaction experience. Appropriate college course work or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: Knowledge of University of Florida policies and procedures. Experience with PeopleSoft, Microsoft Suite, and database systems. Previous experience with general accounting transactions and ability to meet deadlines. Ability to be a self-starter, to work independently, and to work with a team. Have strong customer service skills, strong interpersonal skills, and ability to maintain interpersonal relationships. Ability to operate office equipment, ability to handle telephone calls in a courteous and effective manner. Ability to maintain a high level of confidentiality. Ability to make sound business decisions and problem solve. Ability to adhere to critical deadlines. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. This position is eligible for veteran's preference. If you are claiming veteran's preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy with your application for consideration. If an accommodation is needed to apply for this position, please call 352/392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800/955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the U.S. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: No. Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541670/accounting-specialist-revenue-cycle-accounting-specialist": {
      "title": "Accounting Specialist (Revenue Cycle Accounting Specialist)",
      "description": "Accounting Specialist (Revenue Cycle Accounting Specialist) Apply now Job no: 541670 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Business/Accounting/Finance, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 35010201 - SH-FINANCE-PATIENT ACCOUNTING Classification Title: Accounting Specialist Classification Minimum Requirements: A bachelor's degree in an appropriate area of specialization and one year of appropriate experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Opportunity The Student Health Care Center (SHCC) at the University of Florida is seeking an Accounting Specialist (working title: Revenue Cycle Accounting Specialist). Reporting to the Accounting Manager, this position is responsible for supporting insurance collections, revenue cycle operations, and financial reporting through the accurate and timely recording of insurance remittances, reconciliation of insurance and other payments, and monitoring and analysis of accounts receivable. The key responsibilities of the position include the following: Revenue & Payment Reconciliation Responsible for accurately recording daily and weekly insurance revenue and ensuring revenue is recorded in the appropriate accounting period. Reconcile insurance remittances and patient payments (bursar) to the general ledger, as well as bank deposits and payment batches, to ensure completeness and accuracy of recorded revenue. Monitor revenue activity by payer, provider, and service line, as appropriate, and investigate and resolve discrepancies as needed. Maintain accurate and organized supporting documentation for all revenue and payment reconciliations in accordance with university accounting policies and procedures. Accounts Receivable Management Analyze accounts receivable by payer, aging, provider, location, and other relevant categories to monitor outstanding balances and identify opportunities to improve collections. Identify trends in delayed or outstanding insurance payments by payer and investigate and resolve discrepancies that may impact the accuracy or timely collection of receivables. Collaborate with the billing team to address unresolved accounts, payment issues, denials, underpayments, and other barriers to timely insurance collections. Monitor collection performance and key accounts receivable metrics, prepare regular A/R reports, and communicate significant trends, variances, and collection concerns to management. Assist in establishing collection priorities for older, high-dollar, or otherwise high-risk outstanding balances and support efforts to improve overall A/R performance and cash flow. Financial Reporting & Analysis Gather, compile, and analyze financial and revenue cycle data and prepare monthly insurance collections and financial reports for SHCC leadership. Prepare and maintain reports related to insurance revenue, collections, accounts receivable, and key revenue cycle performance metrics. Analyze actual results against budget, prior periods, and established targets to identify trends, variances, and opportunities for improvement. Develop and maintain financial dashboards and key performance indicators, including payer mix, revenue trends, collection rates, A/R aging, and other relevant financial and revenue cycle metrics. Identify unusual or significant variances, investigate underlying causes, and communicate findings and recommendations to practice leadership. Provide timely financial information and analysis to support operational and strategic decision-making and assist with annual budgeting and forecasting related to insurance collections and revenue. Accounting Operations & Internal Controls Gather, compile, and analyze financial and revenue cycle data and prepare monthly insurance collections and financial reports for SHCC leadership. Prepare insurance revenue, collections, and accounts receivable reports and analyze actual results against budget, prior periods, and established targets. Develop and maintain financial dashboards and key performance indicators, including payer mix, revenue trends, collection rates, A/R aging, and other relevant financial and revenue cycle metrics. Identify unusual or significant variances, investigate underlying causes, and communicate findings and recommendations to practice leadership. Provide timely financial information and analysis to support operational and strategic decision-making and assist with annual budgeting and forecasting related to insurance collections and revenue. Typical Schedule: Typical hours will reflect those of the SHCC 8 am to 5 pm daily (Monday through Friday) and one-hour lunch during Fall and Spring semesters. 8 am to 4:30 pm daily (Monday through Friday) with a half-hour lunch during Summer. Hours and days may vary due to the needs of the SHCC. (Overtime hours may be required). About the Student Health Care Center (SHCC) The mission of the SHCC is to help every student achieve optimal health in pursuing personal and academic success. Our vision is to be the preferred healthcare provider for University of Florida students as a nationally recognized leader in the collegiate health specialty. The SHCC is fully accredited by the AAAHC and is located in a state-of-the-art WELL-certified building on UF’s campus. Medical services offered at the SHCC include primary care, sports medicine, urgent care, gynecology, psychiatry, allergy/immunization clinic, and travel medicine. Radiology, pharmacy, lab, and physical therapy are also on site. Learn more about SHCC here https://shcc.ufl.edu/ About the University of Florida UF is one of the nation’s top universities and a dynamic and rewarding place to work. UF is in Gainesville, one of Florida’s most livable cities. Known for its natural attractions (wetlands, forests, springs, wildlife) and cultural offerings (music, theater, visual arts, and historic districts), the area is also gaining a reputation as a thriving hub for high-tech start-ups, as well as invention and research collaborations. We are excited about what’s happening here and know you will want to be a part of it. The City of Gainesville The Guide to Greater Gainesville shares all that you need to learn about the Heart of Florida, including neighborhood and city information, schools and zoning, culture and leisure, sports and fitness, healthcare and much more. Learn more at https://guidetogreatergainesville.com/ Expected Salary: $65,000 to $69,000 annually Exclusive Perks and Benefits Enjoy stability and support with UF’s unique range of benefits and opportunities. With a robust healthcare package, generous leave policies, retirement planning, and professional development , you can achieve a healthy work-life balance, plan for your future, and advance your career. Full-time TEAMS employees will accrue vacation leave at a rate of 6.769 hours biweekly/22 days annually and sick leave at a rate of 4 hours biweekly/13 days annually. In addition, employees are eligible for ten paid holidays each year. Required Qualifications: A bachelor's degree in an appropriate area of specialization and one year of appropriate experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: MS Office suite, especially Excel Strong technical knowledge of accounting principles, general ledger, revenue and expenses, balance sheet and trial balance. Certification (RCMS)/Education/Experience in Revenue Cycle preferred. EHR/EMR Experience preferred, particularly Epic EHR. Ability to balance and reconcile insurance data records and deposits to the subsidiary ledger. Ability to communicate effectively, both verbally and in writing. Ability to create, compile insurance, collections, and other ad hoc reports as requested. Ability to work productively, independently, problem-solve, and prioritize tasks in order of importance; operate a computer, calculator, copier, and fax machine. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume, and a list of at least 3 references. Applications must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541671/veterinary-hospital-anesthesiology-technician-iii-care": {
      "title": "Veterinary Hospital Anesthesiology Technician I/II (CARE)",
      "description": "Veterinary Hospital Anesthesiology Technician I/II (CARE) Job no: 541671 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Campaign Department: We are seeking full time veterinary technicians to join our anesthesia team. Our techs have a passion for anesthesia and pain management. They work closely with services to provide anesthesia to patients undergoing procedures and play a key role in training veterinary students and other veterinary technicians in the nursing skills specific for anesthesia. CLICK HERE TO EXPRESS INTEREST Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
