    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541121/research-administrator-iii-office-of-research": {
      "title": "Research Administrator III - Office of Research",
      "description": "Research Administrator III - Office of Research Apply now Job no: 541121 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 29011700 - MD-OFFICE OF RESEARCH Classification Title: Research Administrator III Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree and three years of experience in grant and contract administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The College of Medicine Office of Research is hiring a Research Administrator III (RA III) to provide advanced professional oversight, strategic guidance, and comprehensive administration of sponsored projects across an as part of our Excellence in Research Management team. This RA III manages not only individual transactions but also formulates strategies and administers policies and processes to support the department’s research programs. The RA III serves as the primary contact and liaison between departmental faculty, the UF Division of Sponsored Programs (DSP), and external sponsors for all pre-award and post-award activities. This position collaborates with department and college leadership to develop and implement research administration policies, procedures, and best practices; facilitates compliance from award inception through closeout; and provides strategic advice on risk management, complex funding mechanisms, and faculty effort allocation. The RA III operates with a high degree of independence and exercises sound professional judgment on complex, nuanced matters. More About this Position Pre-Award Research Administration Serves as the primary pre-award resource for assigned faculty, independently managing the full lifecycle of proposal development from opportunity identification through submission Reviews program announcements and interprets complex sponsor guidelines, regulations, and eligibility requirements; advises PIs on compliance considerations and potential conflicts with university policy Develops accurate and comprehensive budgets for proposals including complex, multi-year, multidepartment, and multi-institutional projects; accounts for salary/fringe, tuition, subcontract costs, indirect cost rates, over-the-cap salaries, and cost-share commitments Prepares and assembles all proposal components in UFIRST and/or sponsor portals; provides technical assistance to PIs on formatting, document requirements, and system navigation Coordinates institutional approvals, subcontract documentation, and other required materials; acts as liaison with departments, colleges, and DSP to ensure timely and compliant submissions Assists PIs with post-submission activities including Just-in-Time (JIT) requests, sponsor queries, and award negotiation processes Maintains up-to-date Other Support pages for PIs in portfolio; tracks funding opportunities and advises on strategic alignment with departmental research priorities Facilitates strategically significant or complex proposals (e.g., fellowships, clinical studies, multi-PI mechanisms), providing guidance on risk management and compliance considerations Post-Award Research Administration Independently manages all post-award activities for an assigned portfolio of grants, contracts, and agreements with a high degree of autonomy Coordinates with DSP to establish projects, subprojects, and subawards in UFIRST upon award release; ensures accounts are set up accurately and that F&A returns are allocated appropriately Processes initial and subsequent payroll distributions in PeopleSoft; manages faculty effort per commitment in UFIRST with consideration for over-the-cap salaries; records all changes in department shadow systems Manages effort reporting each semester and reviews all key personnel commitments and pay distributions, coordinates adjustments with Cost Analysis, and facilitates certification corrections as needed Reviews all grant-related expenditures to ensure allowability and compliance with agency and university regulations; monitors budget projections and proactively identifies and communicates shortfalls, surpluses, or compliance risks Meets regularly with PIs to review financial summaries, expenditures, and projections; increases meeting frequency as award end dates approach Coordinates all award modifications in UFIRST including no-cost extensions, re-budgets, carryforward requests, and prior approval submissions; prepares renewals, non-competing continuations (e.g., RPPRs), and other periodic reports for sponsoring agencies Manages grant transfers for incoming faculty and oversees award closeout activities in accordance with agency, university, and federal guidelines Prepares CAS exception documents and submits for approval as applicable; ensures cost-sharing commitment plans are confirmed and documented Financial Reconciliation, Compliance, and Operational Support Reconciles project accounts and monitors encumbrances, adjusting as necessary to maintain budget accuracy and fiscal control Coordinates with departmental finance, procurement, and payroll partners on distributions, cost transfers, and appropriate allocation of expenses to sponsored accounts Supports preparation of audit documentation and internal compliance reviews; ensures all fiscal activity aligns with 2 CFR 200 (Uniform Guidance), cost accounting standards, and sponsor requirements Works collaboratively on complex non-financial compliance matters including IRB, IACUC, FCOI, and export control requirements as applicable to portfolio Develops and implements administrative procedures to streamline processes, enhance efficiency, and improve department service and support functions We Offer Exceptional Benefits: Low-cost State Health Plans : Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Life and Disability Insurance Generous Retirement Options to secure your future Comprehensive Paid Time Off Packages : (includes over 10 paid holidays, as well as paid family, sick and vacation leave) Exceptional Personal and Professional Development Opportunities : Access to UF Training & Organizational Development programs, leadership development, LinkedIn Learning, and more Tuition Assistance through the UF Employee Education Program Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Eligible Employer About the College of Medicine: The University of Florida's College of Medicine is committed to advancing health through education, research, and patient care. With a focus on innovation and excellence, the college prepares future healthcare leaders through a rigorous curriculum that combines basic sciences with hands-on clinical experience. The College of Medicine emphasizes interdisciplinary collaboration and community engagement, fostering and environment where students, faculty, and staff work together to improve healthcare outcomes. The College is home to cutting-edge research initiatives and state-of-the-art facilities, providing an exceptional training ground for aspiring medical professionals. Dedicated to enhancing the health of many populations, the College of Medicine plays a pivotal role in shaping the future of medicine. Expected Salary: $75,000 - $83,000 annually; commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree and three years of experience in grant and contract administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: Strong written and verbal communication skills; customer service focused Familiarity with PeopleSoft, UFIRST, and other UF systems Working knowledge of research administration systems and Microsoft Office Suite, especially Excel (basic formulas, sorting/filtering) Ability to understand, interpret, and communicate policies and procedures Self-starter, diplomatic, proactive, and able to manage stress and competing priorities to meet deadlines High degree of personal integrity and ethics with uncompromising standards Ability to work independently, consulting with appropriate parties as needed Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume, and contact information for three professional references. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541264/assistant-manager-operations-small-animal-hospital": {
      "title": "Assistant Manager, Operations Small Animal Hospital",
      "description": "Assistant Manager, Operations Small Animal Hospital Apply now Job no: 541264 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Executive/Director/Management, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 28311000 - VM-HOSPITAL Classification Title: Veterinary Hospital Assistant Manager Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The University of Florida Veterinary Hospitals is recruiting for an Operations Assistant Manager to support the operations within the Small Animal Hospital in Gainesville, FL. The Assistant Manager works closely with hospital leadership, clinical services, facilities personnel, and external partners to ensure a safe, efficient, and well-maintained environment that supports exceptional patient care. Primary responsibilities include oversight of Small Animal Hospital assistants, facilities, infrastructure, and grounds; coordination of maintenance and repair activities; management of facilities related projects; and support of regulatory compliance, safety, and infection control initiatives. In addition, the Assistant Manager assists with purchasing, invoice approval, budget tracking, financial reporting, and recommendations related to operational and facilities expenditures. Critical competencies for this position include analytical thinking, personnel management, excellent written and verbal communication skills, strong organizational abilities, and the capacity to effectively plan, prioritize, and manage multiple responsibilities. This position requires a post-offer health assessment and background check. Expected Salary: Salary based on education and experience Preferred: Knowledge and experience working in a clinical veterinary environment • Experience and successful track record leading and supervising a team • Operations management knowledge and experience Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes; Must be able to lift a minimum of 40 lbs, bend, kneel, pull, push, twist and stand for an extended period of time. Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541356/research-administrator-iidepartment-of-neurosurgery": {
      "title": "Research Administrator II-Department of Neurosurgery",
      "description": "Research Administrator II-Department of Neurosurgery Apply now Job no: 541356 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration Department: 29190000 - MD-NEUROLOGICAL SURGERY Classification Title: Research Administrator II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree and two years of experience in grant and contract administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: ABOUT THIS ROLE: Pre-Award Administration Provide pre-award support which includes reviewing requests for proposal (REP) and identifying the specific requirements and policies are met while ensuring the policies are within or consistent with those of UF. Work with PI to prepare an accurate budget for the corresponding proposal. Submit proposal submissions, including new research grant proposals, renewing and/or continuing grants and contracts. Provides technical assistance to PI’s in the development of proposals and submissions. Reviews subcontract certifications sent from Contracts and Grants (C&G) and obtains signatures. Performs editorial review of language, grammar, punctuation and contract terms for all proposals. Reviews contracts to determine whether the activities should be sent through the Division of Sponsored Research (DSR), Purchasing or the Health Science Center (HSC) Contracts Office and ensures that contracts are in place before services are performed. Post-Award Administration Analyzes expenses, develops cost projections and provides financial tracking and fiscal control to fulfill reporting requirements. Responsible for monitoring and maintaining activities for all projects including completing modifications, release and transfer of funds within the Ufirst system. Ensures expenditures and payments are expensed appropriately with the correct project and in accordance with agency and award stipulations and move to correct award if necessary. Reconcile reports on a monthly basis and as needed. Meet with Principal Investigators quarterly to review reconciliation reports, expenditures, and projections. Coordinate payroll distributions regarding administrative staff as per the budget and/or PI request. Keeps department master project list up to date. Produce monthly and ad-hoc reports as requested by PI. Ensure that the PI and all research staff have completed all necessary training for specified protocol. Upload protocol/IRB approval to appropriate award in UFIRST. May assist in fiscal payment processing—Pcards, MyUF Payment Solutions, MyUF Market. Forecasts salary expenditures through the end of the budget period. Responsible for effort reporting requirements on projects, including required cost sharing. Reviews subcontract certifications sent from Contract and Grants (C&G) and obtains signatures. Participates in the negotiation with grant and contracting officers to substantiate budget requests and to secure terms and conditions, which are consistent with policy. Confer with investigators and campus administrators to ensure that changes or corrections are made as needed. Space Maintain accuracy of space allocations throughout fiscal year. Expected Salary: $61,350 - $69,700 annually Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree and two years of experience in grant and contract administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess the following: Knowledge Knowledgeable in accounting principles and cost accounting standards. Knowledge of departmental, college, university, and agency policies and procedures applicable to contracts and grants. Knowledgeable of aspects of proposal processing- pre-award administration and post-award administration. Working knowledge of eRA Commons (Federal Electronic Research Administration). Skills Proficient in UFirst. Proficient in PeopleSoft. Proficient in Microsoft Word and Microsoft Excel. Proficient in Adobe Pro. Strong communication skills, both oral and written. Abilities Ability to work with people at all levels and people with different and distinct personalities and preferences. Ability to work with multiple departments. Ability to prioritize and manage priorities. Ability to multi-task as needed. Ability to work with deadlines. Ability to work under pressure at times. Ability to interpret, implement, and integrate requirements of various funding agencies. Ability to communicate effectively, both verbally and in writing. Ability to plan, organize and coordinate work assignments. Ability to work independently. Ability to utilize problem-solving techniques. Ability to maintain an appropriate and accessible filing system/database. Special Instructions to Applicants: For consideration, please complete an application and include a cover letter, resume and the names and email addresses of three to five references. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541402/emergency-critical-care-veterinary-service-technician": {
      "title": "Emergency & Critical Care Veterinary Service Technician",
      "description": "Emergency & Critical Care Veterinary Service Technician Apply now Job no: 541402 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 28311001 - VM-ICU AND PCW SERVICES Classification Title: Veterinary Technician I/II (CARE) Classification Minimum Requirements Veterinary Technician I High school diploma or equivalent and one year relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Veterinary Technician II Credentialed veterinary technician Job Description: About the Role: UF Small Animal Hospital’s ECC team is looking for Emergency and Critical Care Veterinary Service Technicians to support our board-certified criticalists. This role is focused on assisting specialists rather than managing ICU patients in the wards. You’ll be the right hand of our experts, ensuring every patient receives seamless, high-quality care. Key Responsibilities: • Attend service rounds at 7am to ensure a solid plan is made for the day • Assist ECC specialists with patient examinations and procedures, including anesthesia, wound care, and diagnostic sample collection. • Prepare and administer medications under veterinary guidance. • Perform patient discharges and client follow-ups, relay diagnostic results and care instructions. • Handle medical charges and documentation. • Teach and mentor veterinary students during clinical rotations. Why UF? We believe in inspiring action through purpose. Your work directly enables specialists to save lives, advance veterinary medicine, and mentor the next generation. At UF, technicians are valued collaborators, not just assistants. Enjoy a workplace where your contributions matter, and your growth is supported every step of the way. Our department is VECCS-accredited, and our hospital holds AAHA accreditation, reflecting our unwavering commitment to excellence in patient care. Our technicians aren’t just supporting care — they’re shaping it. You’ll be part of a teaching hospital that empowers staff to mentor students, support residents, and pursue credentials like CVT, VTS and more. Your expertise, insight, and curiosity are not only welcomed, but they are also encouraged. Outside the hospital, Gainesville and the surrounding areas offer the perfect balance of adventure and relaxation. Swim in natural springs, explore beautiful parks and lakes, enjoy a vibrant local food scene, or take short trips to Florida’s award-winning beaches and cultural attractions. Life in Florida means year-round sunshine, mild winters, and an active lifestyle. At UF, we celebrate our staff. From curated activities for National Veterinary Technician Week to staff sporting events, mentorship programs, and a supportive culture that values your professional and personal growth, you’ll find a career that challenges you, inspires you, and rewards you. Benefits & Perks: • 15 days PTO • Competitive salary, plus weekend and evening shift differential pay • Annual raise eligibility (staff performance dependent) • Mentorship, VTS support, and research opportunities • Paid CE, licensing fees, retention bonuses, and relocation assistance • Uniform allowances • Health plans available • Pet health discounts • Discounts for many restaurants, retail, theme parks and more with gatorperks Available Shifts: • 4 x 10-hour days per week • Wednesday - Saturday, 7am - 5pm • Alternating holiday shifts required - these are fairly shared amongst ECC technician staff Expected Salary: Non-credentialed Technician: $20-27/hr Credentialed Technician: $23-32/hr Required Qualifications Veterinary Technician I High school diploma or equivalent and one year relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Veterinary Technician II Credentialed veterinary technician Preferred • 2+ years of experience in ECC or emergency medicine • Credentialed veterinary technician (or currently enrolled in an AVMA-accredited veterinary technology program and/or eligible to sit for the VTNE) • Knowledge of veterinary nursing, anesthesia, diagnostics, and medical terminology Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This is a CARE position offering limited benefits, including health insurance and paid time off. When appropriate certifications are achieved, promotional opportunities to a level II title may be available. This position will also become eligible for transition into a TEAMS (staff) salary plan. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: Yes; Must be able to lift a minimum of 40 lbs, bend, kneel, pull, push, twist and stand for an extended period of time. Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541422/clinical-service-representative-inew-patient-referral-teamdepartment-of-neurosurgery": {
      "title": "Clinical Service Representative I-New Patient Referral Team-Department of Neurosurgery",
      "description": "Clinical Service Representative I-New Patient Referral Team-Department of Neurosurgery Apply now Job no: 541422 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29190000 - MD-NEUROLOGICAL SURGERY Classification Title: Clinical Service Representative I Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent. Job Description: Position is responsible for answering intake/referral phone line and routing patient’s calls to the correct team member. Schedules patient appointments in scheduling system. Assists with calling patients and/or referring physicians with appointment dates and times, or to obtain additional or corrected information. Sends out new patient letters/packets, as needed. When required, responsible for taking an accurate, legible and complete phone message. Retrieves and distributes fax information sent from patients and referring physicians. Reviews in-house referrals sent through EPIC, including verifying that requests are complete, and requests further information, as required. Checks EPIC (Electronic Patient Record System) to ensure patient is a new patient and not a return. Assists with generating medical record numbers for new patients. Assists other team members by calling insurance carriers or using on-line services to verify patient’s medical insurance including dates of service, co-pays, deductibles and provider participation. As needed, obtains from the PCP (primary care physician) an authorization number or contacts insurance carrier to obtain. Responsible for filing pending referrals in alpha sequence. Expected Salary: $15.80-$19.68 Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent. Preferred: Customer service experience Knowledgeable of phone etiquette Epic experience Knowledge of medical terminology Solid organization skills Special Instructions to Applicants: For consideration, please complete an application and include a cover letter, resume and the names and email addresses of three to five references. To claim veterans' preference, indicate the claim on the application and provide required documentation by uploading a DD-214 (or equivalent certification from the Department of Veteran Affairs) and any additional supporting documentation based on the type of claim. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541441/orthopedic-surgery-veterinary-technician-iii-care": {
      "title": "Orthopedic Surgery Veterinary Technician I/II (Care)",
      "description": "Orthopedic Surgery Veterinary Technician I/II (Care) Apply now Job no: 541441 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 28311100 - VM-SURGERY Classification Title: Orthopedic Surgery Veterinary Technician Classification Minimum Requirements: Veterinary Technician I - High school diploma or equivalent and one year relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Veterinary Technician II - Credentialed veterinary technician Job Description: Are you a skilled veterinary technician who loves both learning and sharing your knowledge and is ready to join a busy team seeing cats and dogs with a wide variety of issues requiring orthopedic intervention? We are looking for our next team members in Small Animal Surgery and currently are recruiting for an Orthopedic Surgery position! Nursing staff in our service assist with appointments, small procedures under sedation and anesthesia in both sterile and aseptic conditions, diagnostic workups including digital radiography and CT, and a host of other surgical nursing skills in addition to client communication. Members of our team work closely with boarded veterinary surgeons, residents/interns, and veterinary students in a fast paced teaching and learning environment. We offer competitive wages, benefits including health insurance and paid leave and holidays, and a team-oriented approach to each day. This is a full time position, working Monday-Friday. Expected Salary: Non-Credentialed $18.00-22.00 Credentialed $20.00-25.00 Required Qualifications Veterinary Technician I - High school diploma or equivalent and one year relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Veterinary Technician II - Credentialed veterinary technician Preferred: Credentialed Veterinary Technician Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This is a CARE position offering limited benefits, including health insurance and paid time off. When appropriate certifications are achieved, promotional opportunities to a level II title may be available. This position will also become eligible for transition into a TEAMS (staff) salary plan. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: Yes; Must be able to lift a minimum of 40 lbs, bend, kneel, pull, push, twist and stand for an extended period of time. Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541585/administrative-assistant-ii": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant II",
      "description": "Administrative Assistant II Apply now Job no: 541585 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Veteran's Preference Eligible, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29040100 - MD-ANESTHESIOLOGY-GENERAL Classification Title: Administrative Assistant II Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and four years of appropriate experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Department of Anesthesiology at the University of Florida is seeking an Administrative Assistant II to join our Faculty Support Services team. This position provides administrative and operational support to faculty within an assigned division, helping coordinate daily activities, manage competing priorities, and ensure things stay on track. We are looking for someone who is organized, proactive, and comfortable working independently in a fast-paced environment. The ideal candidate takes initiative, anticipates needs, and enjoys being part of a collaborative team where strong administrative support makes a difference. Faculty and Administrative Support Provide primary administrative support to faculty within an assigned division, serving as a reliable point of contact for day-to-day administrative needs. Maintain faculty calendars and schedules, proactively coordinating meetings, recurring commitments, and other departmental activities. Coordinate divisional meetings, including scheduling participants, preparing agendas, recording minutes, and arranging audiovisual support. Maintain faculty dashboards, prepare correspondence, and assist with routine administrative communications. Prepare, update, and maintain faculty curriculum vitae (CVs), and enter or transmit information into online systems supporting mid-cycle reviews, promotion, and tenure processes. Assist with the development and distribution of clinical call schedules by collecting availability and scheduling requests from faculty, fellows, and residents. Process continuing medical education (CME) documentation and maintain records of outstanding requirements to support faculty licensure. Collaborate with departmental communications staff to assist faculty with manuscript submissions. Support faculty in meeting required training and compliance deadlines. Provide backup logistical support for transporting faculty and fellow applicants, as needed. Fiscal and Financial Administration Coordinate faculty travel arrangements, including itineraries, travel authorizations, and reimbursements through PeopleSoft. Provide guidance to faculty regarding University of Florida travel policies, procedures, and requirements. Facilitate purchasing card transactions and reconciliations, prepare requisitions and purchase orders, and process payments for encumbered and unencumbered vouchers, travel, and Earn-to-Learn (E2E) transactions. Reconcile monthly purchasing card financial reports and maintain accurate records of expenditures. Monitor and update individual faculty Professional Development Account (PDA) balances. Assist with divisional purchasing and supply orders, including coordinating catering and refreshments for meetings. Work within assigned budget targets while ensuring transactions are processed accurately and in accordance with applicable University policies and procedures. Communication and Departmental Operations Serve as a professional and welcoming point of contact for internal and external constituents, exercising discretion and independent judgment when responding to calls, visitors, and inquiries. Answer calls to the Department of Anesthesiology switchboard, assess caller needs, and direct inquiries to the appropriate individual or department. Welcome visitors and guests and assist with directing them to appropriate locations. Receive and review faculty mail, independently respond to routine correspondence, and complete scheduling and informational questionnaires. Coordinate maintenance and repair requests for office equipment and support facilities. Identify opportunities to improve administrative processes and operational efficiency. Contribute to shared Faculty Support Services responsibilities, providing backup coverage when team members are unavailable. Handle confidential information and daily correspondence with professionalism, discretion, and appropriate attention to privacy. Independently address routine administrative challenges and resolve conflicts as appropriate. Perform other related duties as assigned to support departmental operations. Expected Salary: $20 - $23, commensurate with experience Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and four years of appropriate experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Experience with PeopleSoft. Knowledge of standard office practices, including professional telephone etiquette, business correspondence, and common administrative forms. Familiarity with medical terminology. Strong customer service orientation and commitment to providing responsive administrative support. Experience coordinating complex calendars, scheduling meetings, and preparing travel itineraries. Proficiency with Microsoft Office applications. Strong organizational skills and the ability to prioritize and coordinate multiple assignments. Demonstrated problem-solving skills and the ability to work independently. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including effective grammar, proofreading, and attention to detail. Ability to establish and maintain productive working relationships with colleagues, faculty, and external contacts. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This position is eligible for veterans' preference. If you are claiming veterans' preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See UF’s Veterans' Preference Page for more details. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541591/veterinary-clinical-education-specialist-emergency-critical-care": {
      "title": "Veterinary Clinical Education Specialist - Emergency & Critical Care",
      "description": "Veterinary Clinical Education Specialist - Emergency & Critical Care Apply now Job no: 541591 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 28311001 - VM-ICU AND PCW SERVICES Classification Title: Education/Training Specialist II Classification Minimum Requirements Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: Are you an experienced Veterinary ECC Technician ready to revolutionize the way we transform career pathways from day-one Assistants to elite Specialist Technicians, empowering our team from within? The University of Florida Emergency & Critical Care team is hiring an innovative ECC Clinical Education Specialist (ECC-CES) to help define how emergency medicine is taught across our ER, ICU, and Progressive Care Ward (PCW). What You’ll Do: • Drive Educational Innovation: Manage our ECC Trainee Technician’s, training of new ECC hires, Nursing Internships, and volunteer coordination. • Modernize Learning: Utilize cutting-edge digital tracking tools to set top-tier clinical standards. • Mentor & Lead: Provide hands-on coaching, lead technician continuing education, guide staff toward VTS certification, and support seasoned VTS technicians with ongoing career advancement. Why Join the University of Florida? As a full-time TEAMS employee at UF, you’ll enjoy a comprehensive compensation and perks package designed to support your total well-being: • Generous Time Away: Paid state holidays plus extensive paid leave accruals. • Comprehensive Health & Retirement Benefits: Excellent health insurance options, dental, vision, wellness programs and retirement plans including generous employer contributions. • In-house social worker: Orchestrates staff wellness initiatives within the hospital. • Educational Growth: Employee tuition assistance programs and continuous professional development opportunities. Ready to elevate your passion for ECC into a career-defining leadership role? Candidates selected for in-person interviews will participate in a structured assessment process designed to evaluate communication skills, clinical expertise, teaching ability, and alignment with the educational mission of the role. The interview process will consist of: • Panel interview and behavioral-based questions • A brief teaching demonstration (approximately 5-10 minutes) on either an ECC-related topic or a unrelated topic of your choice. • A communication-based practical exercise designed to assess coaching and instructional skills Candidates will receive additional details and preparation instructions prior to the interview. Expected Salary: Salary dependent on credentialing and experience, plus up to 30% differentials Preferred Credentialed Veterinary Technician (CVT/LVT/RVT) Veterinary Technician Specialist in Emergency & Critical Care (VTS-ECC) Experience developing educational programs, onboarding frameworks, competency-based learning pathways, or staff development initiatives. Experience applying adult learning principles within a clinical, academic, or healthcare environment. Experience developing and maintaining competency tracking systems, skills matrices, educational documentation, or training metrics. Experience presenting continuing education programs, facilitating workshops, or speaking at professional conferences. Demonstrated leadership experience within a veterinary teaching hospital, specialty practice, emergency hospital, or academic setting. Demonstrated commitment to continued professional growth, credentialing, specialization, and advancement of veterinary nursing. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes; Must be able to lift a minimum of 40 lbs, bend, kneel, pull, push, twist and stand for an extended period of time. Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541597/veterinary-client-services-supervisor": {
      "title": "Veterinary Client Services Supervisor",
      "description": "Veterinary Client Services Supervisor Apply now Job no: 541597 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Marion Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 28360000 - VM-WORLD EQUESTRIAN CENTER Classification Title: Vet Client Services Supervisor Classification Minimum Requirements Associates degree and two years of relevant experience; or a high school diploma or equivalent and four years of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: The University of Florida Veterinary Hospital at the World Equestrian Center provides Small Animal Primary Care and Emergency services, as well as Equine Sports Medicine, Rehabilitation, and Diagnostic Imaging services. This role offers a unique opportunity to support both equine and small animal clients while helping shape the client experience in a growing and collaborative veterinary environment. The ideal candidate is an experienced leader with a passion for customer service, team development, and operational excellence. This position requires a professional and composed individual who can effectively manage a variety of situations, provide coaching and guidance to team members, address escalated client concerns, and work collaboratively with hospital leadership to continuously improve service delivery. The successful candidate must possess excellent communication, organizational, and problem-solving skills while maintaining a positive attitude and professional demeanor. Why work for UF? We know you care about your own pets as much as we do our patients, so we offer discounts for all our employees’ pets. We encourage you to join our team and grow with us to make a difference. Benefits: The University of Florida offers competitive benefit including but not limited to; Paid Leave and Holidays Extensive Medical Benefits (including health, dental and vision) Professional Development Assistance Employee Discount This position requires a post-offer health assessment and background check. Minimum Qualifications: Associate’s degree and two years of relevant experience; or a high school diploma or equivalent and four years of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute an equivalent rate for the required experience. Expected Salary: $20 - 26 /hour Preferred • Knowledge of medical terminology, anatomy, and terminology • Knowledge of the equipment and materials used and their availability and applications. Ability to operate a computer and utilize office suite software such as excel • Experience with staff scheduling • Demonstrate consistent professional conduct and meticulous attention to detail • Excellent verbal and written communication skills as well as interpersonal skills with patients, staff, and other members of the patient care team • Critical thinking skills and a positive attitude • Excellent verbal and written communication skills as well as interpersonal skills with patients, staff, and other members of the patient care team. Ability to effectively communicate with employees of various levels and responsibilities • Able to work in a team environment and collaborate with a variety of personnel in a positive manner • Ability to effectively maintain a working environment while in stressful situations • Critical thinking skills and a positive attitude; Able to think independently and demonstrate good problem solving skills Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: Yes; Must be able to lift a minimum of 40 lbs, bend, kneel, pull, push, twist and stand for an extended period of time. Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541613/student-financial-aid-coordinator-ii-state-programs": {
      "title": "Student Financial Aid Coordinator II (State Programs)",
      "description": "Student Financial Aid Coordinator II (State Programs) Apply now Job no: 541613 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 03060000 - EM-STUDENT FINANCIAL AID ADMIN Classification Title: Student Financial Aid CRD II Classification Minimum Requirements: Master's degree in an appropriate area; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience. Job Description: The University of Florida is hiring a Student Financial Aid Coordinator II to oversee state-funded scholarships and grants such as Florida Bright Futures. This role involves managing disbursements, ensuring accurate reporting, and staying compliant with state rules. Key responsibilities include, but are not limited to: State Program Oversight Lead all processes for Florida-funded scholarships and grants, including eligibility, selection, and fiscal management. Interpret and apply state aid policies and regulations. Develop and share procedures to ensure program compliance and efficiency. Awarding & Disbursement Coordinate the awarding and timely disbursement of state financial aid. Use professional judgment in eligibility decisions. Act as the university’s main contact for the Florida Office of Student Financial Assistance. Reporting & Compliance Handle all reporting and reconciliation tasks for state aid programs. Ensure accurate data submission and monitor fund disbursement and returns to stay compliant. Additional Duties Support the Manger with additional aid programs. Participate in financial aid presentations and professional development. Perform other tasks as assigned. Expected Salary: $47,500 annually In addition, UF offers the following exceptional benefits: Health, Dental, and Vision Insurance State Retirement Plans Paid Time Off (approximately 22 days of vacation leave, 13 days of sick leave, and 11 paid holidays annually) Tuition Assistance (UF Employee Education Program) Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Eligible Employer For more information on benefits, please visit UFHR Benefits and Rewards (ufl.edu) Required Qualifications: Master's degree in an appropriate area; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience. Preferred: Student Financial Aid experience Demonstrated ability to work accurately with attention to detail Excellent organizational, interpersonal, and communication skills Microsoft Office proficiency, including Excel Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants must upload the following documents to be considered for the position: Cover Letter Resume List of References Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
