    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541636/resfellowship-prog-assistant": {
      "title": "Res/Fellowship Prog Assistant",
      "description": "Res/Fellowship Prog Assistant Apply now Job no: 541636 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29050300 - MD-RHEUMATOLOGY Classification Title: Res/Fellowship Prog Assistant Classification Minimum Requirements: Two year college degree and one year of relevant experience, or combination of education and relevant experience. Job Description: Fellowship Program Administration Work with Fellowship Director and Assistant Director to ensure documents, schedules and other key elements meet the ACGME and other institutional requirements for the program. Reconcile quarterly salary distribution reports, using New Innovations, VA- provided data and rotation schedule. Prepare for any/all accreditation site visits and program evaluations. Coordinate fellowship interviews and arrange zoom breakout rooms and itineraries for candidates and Core faculty. Prepare for and support the administrative needs of the graduating fellows and the incoming class of fellows. Conference Scheduling and Academic Support Maintain division calendar for fellowship meetings and conferences. Send out calendar invites from division team inbox as needed and send out weekly remainder emails to faculty and fellows for meetings and conferences. Maintain fellowship files and filing documents as directed by the Fellowship Director and Division Administrator. Set up all fellowship conferences, including attendance taking, order food, cleaning up space. Maintain fiscal year consult schedules for faculty and fellows. Work with the Division Administrator to create faculty consult schedule and work with the Fellowship Program Director to create the fellows’ rotation schedule. Monitor emails in the division administration team inbox and assist with faxes as needed. Account Management and Reporting Work with the Division Administrator to manage Division accounts for fellowship related expenses. Create budget tracking spreadsheets for faculty/fellows, monitor spending and ensure that expenses are tracked and all purchases are within budget. Prepare annual fellowship budget identifying new programmatic needs and expenses. Review with the Division Administrator. Coordinators travel arrangements for fellows and Fellowship Directors, including but not limited to scheduling flights, making hotel reservations, registration for conferences. Creates travel authorizations and expense reports for all fellows and Fellowship Directors. Will also complete all required template/forms required by the Department of Medicine for travel. Educational Symposium Coordination Work with the Division Administrator and Fellowship Director create then schedule quarterly Educational Symposiums, including speaker presentations. Publicize event dates with vendors, create and maintain registration documents, generate PO’s and process invoices with suppliers. Facilitate set up, break down and other logistics for on the day of events. Query, compile and analyze survey results, and use them to suggest/ implement changes in future events. Other Divisional Responsibilities Assist with coordination of Annual Interviews. Prioritize assignments and messages based on urgency or deadlines and work cooperatively with other Division, Department and University staff to ensure all assignments are completed in a timely manner and meet or exceed the standards for quality and performance. Serve as liaison to multiple internal and external entities. Work closely with Division staff to ensure that all faculty are supporting their clinical and academic needs. Provides direct back up support when absences occur in the administration office. Adheres to all office policies, procedures, and protocols established by the Division. Other duties as assigned by the Division Administrator. Expected Salary: $22.00 - $27.00/hr Required Qualifications: Two year college degree and one year of relevant experience, or combination of education and relevant experience. Preferred: Knowledge of New Innovations. Knowledge of Epic and scheduling systems. Knowledge of Workday. Special Instructions to Applicants: Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541637/ops-patient-access-and-scheduling-coordinator-bilingual-english-and-spanish": {
      "title": "OPS Patient Access and Scheduling Coordinator (Bilingual English and Spanish)",
      "description": "OPS Patient Access and Scheduling Coordinator (Bilingual English and Spanish) Apply now Job no: 541637 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 29070900 - MD-OBGYN-MATERNAL FETAL MEDICI Classification Title: OPS Patient Access and Scheduling Coordinator Classification Minimum Requirements: Some College work with two years of experience in an appropriate area; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Fluency in spoken and written English and Spanish. Reliable transportation and willingness to travel to mobile clinic sites throughout North Central Florida. Experience in patient scheduling, patient access, medical office administration, healthcare customer service, or a closely related setting. Job Description: Patient Access and Scheduling Schedule new and returning patients in Epic using approved appointment types, decision guides, and scheduling workflows. Coordinate appointments across rotating mobile clinic locations and provide accurate site, arrival, and preparation instructions. Registration and Patient Experience Serve as the first voice many patients hear and the first person they meet at clinic. Assist patients with forms, financial-assistance processes, transportation workflows, and other approved access supports. Complete registration and check-in; verify identity, demographics, contact information, preferred language, communication preferences, insurance information when applicable, and required consents. Telephone and Clinical Message Routing Answer incoming calls and determine whether each request is administrative or clinical. Complete and document clinician-directed scheduling or follow-up. Records, Administrative Support, and Quality Obtain prior prenatal, delivery, hospital, laboratory, imaging, and specialty records and identify missing information before visits. Maintain accurate and timely Epic documentation, telephone encounters, outreach attempts, and scheduling records. Mobile Unit Readiness and Administrative Inventory Share responsibility for maintaining an organized, clean, fully stocked, and patient-ready mobile clinic under the direction of the OBMOC Program Manager. Maintain front-desk, registration, office, education, and other assigned nonclinical supplies. Participate in weekly restocking and monthly inventory review; report recurring shortages or storage problems. Assist with mobile-clinic setup and breakdown and maintain safe, uncluttered work areas. Expected Salary: $15.00 hourly Required Qualifications: Some College work with two years of experience in an appropriate area; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Fluency in spoken and written English and Spanish. Reliable transportation and willingness to travel to mobile clinic sites throughout North Central Florida. Experience in patient scheduling, patient access, medical office administration, healthcare customer service, or a closely related setting. Preferred: Strong organizational skills, exceptional attention to detail, and ability to manage competing priorities. Excellent interpersonal communication and ability to build trust with patients Current Certified Nursing Assistant (CNA) certification. For an otherwise qualified candidate who is interested in adding this skill, OBMOC will support efforts to obtain CNA certification. Experience obtaining and documenting vital signs in a healthcare setting. Experience scheduling patients in Epic. Experience in obstetrics/gynecology, women’s health, maternal health, primary care, community health, or mobile healthcare. Knowledge of medical terminology and pregnancy-related appointment workflows. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541638/clin-svc-representative-i": {
      "title": "Clin Svc Representative I",
      "description": "Clin Svc Representative I Apply now Job no: 541638 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Health Care Administration/Support, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29081100 - MD-PATH - UF PATH LABS Classification Title: Clin Svc Representative I Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent. Job Description: This mid-shift position is responsible for processing, triaging, accessioning, and troubleshooting all incoming specimens received at UF Health Pathology Laboratories. Duties include: Operating centrifuge, aliquoting and processing all types of Clinical Pathology specimens including but not limited to; blood, serum, saliva, sputum, viral transport media, culture swabs, etc. Assigning each specimen a number and label materials accordingly, collecting and placing test orders, verifying billing codes, , verifying specimen stability requirements, sending specimens to outside labs, maintaining files, verifying appropriate materials received for each test, routing samples to the correct testing department and coordinating specimens to be sent to outside laboratories. Other duties include testing coordination, preparing result reports and distributing the reports. In addition, this position is responsible for maintenance of the UF Health Pathology Laboratories' files, data, and paperwork as required by regulatory agencies and to support the research, education and clinical mission of the laboratory and department. This position would be to work during the mid-shift but may vary depending on the needs of the department. Occasional holiday and weekend coverage may be assigned Clinical Pathology Specimen Triage/Receiving: Prepare Clinical Pathology laboratory specimens for designated departments/locations prior to laboratory analysis and testing. Unpacks specimens from branches or ports and routes specimens by type to various staging areas. Prepares all specimens received for testing in designated laboratory departments or locations such as staging of specimens, centrifuge, separate serum, and blood smears. Aliquots sample for departments. Prepares excess specimen samples for storage and resolves and document problem specimens. Clinical Pathology Problem Documenting and Follow-up: Any such duties needed to keep the accessioning and processing area running smoothly and performing the work in an accurate, organized and timely manner. Coordinating special testing, such as, athletic, molecular and QFT. Monitor courier drop offs and ensure runs are not missed. Monitor expected list to ensure everything is received and communicated. Monitoring emails throughout the shift to ensure information is relayed and received in a timely manner. Keep track of issues and communicate with client services and supervisors when frequent issues occur. Duties include assisting with oversight and daily processes for the Triage/Accessioning and Processing section. This position will be responsible for troubleshooting and documenting any issues that come up in the day/evening shift and report directly to their supervisor (or team lead) any and all issues that arise CP Accessioning/ Processing: Prepare Clinical Pathology laboratory specimens for designated departments/locations prior to laboratory analysis and testing. Unpacks specimens from branches or ports and routes specimens by type to various staging areas. Prepares all specimens received for testing in designated laboratory departments or locations such as staging of specimens, centrifuge, separate serum, and blood smears. Aliquots sample for departments. Prepares excess specimen samples for storage and resolves and document problem specimens. AP Triage/Accessioning: Receive and document all anatomical pathology laboratory samples. Prepare anatomic pathology laboratory samples for delivery to designated departments/locations prior to laboratory analysis and testing. Prepares excess samples for storage and resolve documentation problems. Expected Salary: Starting salary is between $17.86 - $18.77, commensurate with experience Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent. Preferred: Communication skills which will allow the prospective employee to interact with UF physicians, client physicians, and other health professionals in a professional manner. Ability to work in a potentially stressful environment with infectious and biohazardous materials. Ability to quickly learn various aspects of Lab Operations. Knowledge of Medical terminology. Typing and data entry skills. Strong attention to detail. Previous experience working in a lab or other clinical setting. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This position is eligible for veterans' preference. If you are claiming veterans' preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See UF’s Veterans' Preference Page for more details. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541640/overnight-access-services-assistant-library-assistant-3": {
      "title": "Overnight Access Services Assistant - Library Assistant 3",
      "description": "Overnight Access Services Assistant - Library Assistant 3 Apply now Job no: 541640 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Libraries/Museums, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 55050100 - LB-MARSTON SCI LIB CHAIR Classification Title: Library Assistant 3 Classification Minimum Requirements: High School diploma or equivalent and three years of library, or relevant experience; or an Associate’s degree and one year of relevant experience; or any equivalent combination of experience, training and/or education. Job Description: PLEASE NOTE: Regular work hours for Spring and Fall semesters are Sunday night through Friday morning, 11:30pm-8:30am; this schedule is changed during intersession, holidays, and other times when there are reduced hours and library closures. Note : Summer hours for this position are 2:15pm-11:15pm Monday-Thursday, and 12:15pm-9:15pm on Friday. JOB SUMMARY As a member of the Marston Science Library Access Services Unit, this position performs a variety of public service, supervisory, technical, and security roles in support of the Smathers Libraries 24-hour program and the Marston Science Library branch. Oversees access services responsibilities and the service desk and supervises student assistants during the overnight hours. Assists patrons in the use of library services, circulating tools and technology, and with computer-related problems. Directs shift change with the morning and evening staff members and with security assistants. RESPONSIBILITIES MSL Service Desk - Circulation Supervision Communicates effectively with and provides a high level of customer service to all patrons. Supervises and trains overnight OPS and student staff. Arranges coverage during student absences. Provides training and guidance on overnight projects to OPS and student employees. Provides courteous assistance with a variety of research tools. Provides tactful and timely assistance with patron inquiries by telephone, email, and verbal interaction. Possesses a working knowledge of library policies and procedures and be able to assist patrons in all circulation functions. Assists library patrons with checking items out, loan returns, recall and hold requests, and course reserve items. Supervise OPS staff during assigned desk shifts, including training, coaching, and communication of policies. Answers or refers reference questions in person or by telephone. Creates temporary guest accounts. Answers or refers Interlibrary Loan questions. Consults and advises patrons with questions about the Ares course reserve process. Answers directional, circulation, and basic reference questions, referring when necessary. Assists patrons with printers/scanners and other technology within the library as appropriate. Consults with patrons regarding technology-related questions associated with circulating technology and tools. Serves as point person for responding to patron emergency situations. Provides service with any other appropriate circulation related function or building security issue. Responsible for the service desk and building during assigned desks shifts. Communicates necessary building information/issues that arise during assigned shifts to supervisor and/or other MSL staff and reports immediate building issues to work management. Monitors exit gate alarms as patrons enter/exit the building. Provides service desk backup support when other staff members are absent. Marston Science Library Overnight Operations Leads nighttime projects and workflow at Marston Science Library. Monitors building security for patrons and staff, consulting closely with the other team members; serves as primary contact for security issues. Makes periodic trips to monitor building and security issues for patrons and staff. Gathers and organizes library materials each night and ensures that the library facilities are in good working condition. Prepares public and work areas for the morning shift. Monitors service desk supplies and forms, requests or replaces items when needed. Coordinates shift change events. Collects pertinent information concerning overnight services, usage, and project progress. Assists in recording and tracking library service issues and trends. Reads work email consistently to stay informed of new workflows and potential issues to keep level of service consistent across day and night shifts. Building Operations & Information Technology Assists with operation and maintenance of public workstations, scanners and printers. Documents and responds to problems by submitting online service requests via online ticketing system to Academic Technology or Libraries’ IT, as appropriate. Works with patrons and security to address building issues. Makes requests for repairs through ticketing systems, if needed. Enforces library and computer use policies. Updates/informs unit heads of any building service issues or events that may have occurred during evening hours and helps monitor facility operations and library equipment. Documents and responds to problems (i.e. flooding, computer issues, etc.) by submitting online services requests or by contacting campus emergency services (i.e. flooding) for issues requiring immediate attention. Research Assistance Provides reference services in person and by telephone. Assists patrons in use of electronic resources, online catalog, and basic reference materials and methods; provides appropriate referral to area and subject specialists. Answers or refers Interlibrary Loan questions. Participates in other branch and library activities Contributes to documentation of unit and departmental policies and procedures Serves on committees, as appointed Participates in staff development opportunities Other duties as assigned Expected Salary: $17.23 minimum salary; The Smathers Libraries offer a salary commensurate with experience and credentials. The University of Florida offers a competitive benefits package including health, retirement, paid time off, discount programs, professional development and worklife support. Learn more . Required Qualifications: High School diploma or equivalent and three years of library, or relevant experience; or an Associate’s degree and one year of relevant experience; or any equivalent combination of experience, training and/or education. Preferred: Excellent reliability and proven ability to manage a schedule and meet deadlines Excellent customer service skills; ability to interact with all library clientele and co-workers in a professional manner Strong oral and written communication skills Ability to supervise and direct the work of others Ability to interact and collaborate effectively and professionally with colleagues to accomplish goals Flexibility, and ability to adapt and work in a rapidly changing academic environment Initiative and ability to work independently with minimal supervision Strong time management skills, organization skills, and attention to detail Ability to interpret call number schemes, retrieve items from the shelves and re-shelve items Ability to quickly learn appropriate technology Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Process To apply, submit a cover letter detailing your interest in, and qualifications for this position your current resume or CV a list of three references including their contact information (telephone number and email address) Apply by 11:55 pm (EST) on the application close date via the Jobs at UF online application system. Applications will be reviewed as received. If you have any questions or concerns about this process, please contact Tina Marie Litchfield, George A. Smathers Libraries Human Resources Office, at tlitchfield@uflib.ufl.edu . If an accommodation due to a hearing or speech disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 University of Florida Human Resources or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD). This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541642/accountant-iii": {
      "title": "Accountant III",
      "description": "Accountant III Apply now Job no: 541642 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Business/Accounting/Finance, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 28300000 - VM-VMTH Classification Title: Accountant III Classification Minimum Requirements Bachelors degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience. Job Description: The Accountant III described herein works in the University of Florida’s College of Veterinary Medicine Veterinary Hospitals. This position reports directly to the Chief Financial Officer for the UF Veterinary Hospitals. The incumbent performs all phases of professional accounting procedures necessary for the maintenance of the general ledgers for the Veterinary Hospitals, as well as assisting the Chief Financial Officer as needed. The position requires an innovative individual with insight into the intricacies of various policies associated with the Practice Plan and the University of Florida. The incumbent is expected to reorganize and implement changes to the existing policy and propose new policies to meet challenges as they occur. The incumbent is responsible for writing monthly journal entries and the safeguarding of all transaction records and support. Also, requires meeting monthly closeout deadlines and reconciliation of the general ledger and trial balance, along with other reports needed by Hospital Board and governing body of the Practice Plan. The individual acts as a liaison between the clinical services and financial services. Coordinates the monthly reconciliation of approximately $40 million annually in transactions. This requires working closely with the Accounts Receivable office in order to facilitate the reconciliation process of the revenue and working closely with the Financial Services office in order to facilitate the reconciliation process of the expenses. Compiles information by exporting reports from the hospital information system, the financial information system, and Microsoft Access databases. Analyzes financial reports for the Hospital Board and Practice Plan governing board. Identifies, reconciles, and explains variations. Develops working papers and/or assists with special projects and compiles financial statements as needed by financial services, the Hospital Board and Practice Plan governing board. Recommends changes to existing accounting procedures and assists in development of new procedures. Works with the university and external auditors to develop the annual financial statements, MD&A, and tax return. Compiles and request/prepare special payments for residents, clinicians, and hospital revenue. - Coordinates the monthly reconciliation of approximately $50 million annually in transactions. Responsible for writing of journal entries (monthly, quarterly and yearly) and the safeguarding of all transaction records and support. Requires meeting monthly closeout deadlines and reconciliation of the general ledger, trial balance, and financial statements along with the monthly reports needed by Hospital Board and the quarterly report needed by the governing body of the Practice Plan. Responsible for creating monthly Income Statements used by the Hospital Services to monitor their individual budgets. - Reviews the end of day reports. Reconciles all payments taken daily and monitors the Practice Plan bank account. Responsible for hospital internal controls. Recommends changes to existing accounting procedures and assists in development of new procedures and policies. The individual acts as a liaison between clinical services and financial services. Maintains up to date policies and procedures on SharePoint. Trains all CSRs on their financial responsibilities. - Works with the university and external auditors to develop the annual financial statements, the MD&A, and tax return. Organizes the yearly preliminary audit and final audit. Provides auditors with reports, and answers questions as needed. Analyzes financial reports, identifies trends and cycles and explains variations. Also assists with the biannual privacy audit. - Responsible for corporation requirements associated with the Practice Plan, including, but not limited to renewing SAM, tax exemption status, corporation registration with the state, fictitious name with the state, sending any requested W-9 or vendor forms, and preparing the annual Unclaimed Property report. In addition, individual works with client services to maintain PCI compliance (risk assessments, regular updates of policies and procedures, obtaining AOC from third parties, etc.) - Clear internal accounts entered into the hospital information system and create new ones as necessary. Compiles information by exporting reports from the hospital information system, the financial information system, and Microsoft Access databases. Assists with special projects for the hospital (credit card machines, Xpress Pay, etc.). - Prepares special payments for residents, clinicians, donations, and hospital revenue. Manages outside contracts for resident salaries. Create invoices, collect payments, and updates contracts as necessary for salary and fringe changes for resident salaries. – Assists with preparation of the $50 million annual budget. Expected Salary: $75,000 - $85,000 Preferred - Knowledge of accrual methods. - Knowledge of office procedures and practices. - Knowledge of accounting principles and practices. - Proficient experience in Microsoft Access, Excel, and Word. - Ability to reconcile internal accounting records to official university documents. - Ability to write macros in Access, Excel, and Word. - Ability to use computerized accounting system. - Ability to prepare computerized spreadsheets for reporting purposes. - Ability to collect, analyze and interpret accounting data. - Ability to plan, organize and coordinate work assignments. - Ability to work independently and as part of a team. - Ability to communicate verbally and in writing. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541649/administrative-specialist-i": {
      "title": "Administrative Specialist I",
      "description": "Administrative Specialist I Apply now Job no: 541649 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 11010000 - RE-VP FOR RESEARCH Classification Title: Administrative Specialist I Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree; or an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience. Job Description: Provides primary management of the Vice President for Research’s calendar. Coordinates meetings, prioritizes scheduling requests, resolves scheduling conflicts, and adjusts the calendar as priorities change. Works closely under the guidance of the Executive Affairs Lead to understand and apply the Vice President’s scheduling priorities. Prepares and organizes meeting materials and ensures the Vice President has the information needed for scheduled meetings. Provides administrative support and event support to the Executive Affairs Lead by performing a variety of administrative duties, as needed. Event assistance may include making nametags, greeting attendees, transporting speakers and visitors, meeting caterers for food setup, audiovisual setup, event setup, and event breakdown. Requires the ability to drive state vehicle to various event locations. Provides primary front desk and phone coverage for the Office of the Vice President for Research. Greets visitors, responds to general inquiries, directs individuals to the appropriate person or office, and provides general assistance. Serves as the first point of contact for in person visitor support and provides executive escorting as needed. Coordinates key distribution and return, maintains accurate tracking records, and follows up on outstanding keys as needed. Provides backup coverage for the other assistants in the office during absences and peak periods. Assists with essential front office and administrative responsibilities to ensure continuity of operations. Provides additional support as needed to meet evolving office and organizational needs. Operates on own initiative, which includes planning, prioritizing, and maintaining flexibility to perform a variety of functions to meet deadlines. Expected to complete work assignments independently and on time. Must be highly professional in dealing with University of Florida and external constituencies. Operates a variety of standard office equipment including a computer, telephone, fax, color printer, and scanner. Expected Salary: $46,000 - $50,000 Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree; or an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience. Valid driver's license. Preferred: Experience providing calendar and administrative support to an executive or senior leader. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail. Strong written and verbal communication skills. Ability to greet and assist visitors in a professional and welcoming manner. Ability to manage multiple responsibilities and changing priorities. Proficiency with Microsoft Outlook, Word, Excel, electronic mail, and general office systems. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships. Ability to exercise good judgment and maintain confidentiality. Special Instructions to Applicants: • In order to be considered, applicants must provide a cover letter, resume, and list of professional references. • Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. • This is not a remote position and is located on the University of Florida main campus in Gainesville, FL. • A criminal background check is required. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541650/director-honors-program": {
      "title": "Director, Honors Program",
      "description": "Director, Honors Program Apply now Job no: 541650 Work type: Faculty Admin Title Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Executive/Director/Management Department: 02030000 - PV-HONORS PROGRAM Classification Title: Director, Honors Program Classification Minimum Requirements: Hold a terminal degree, Ph.D. or equivalent doctorate, and bring at least 10 years of faculty experience, including sustained achievement in teaching, scholarly or creative activity, and professional engagement within their discipline. Job Description: The Opportunity The Director provides strategic vision and leadership for the academic, co-curricular, administrative, and operational dimensions of the UF Honors Program. Working collaboratively with faculty, staff, students, university leaders, colleges, academic departments, and external partners, the Director will advance a shared vision for a nationally distinctive honors experience. The next Director will inherit a program with compelling assets: a nationally recognized advising model; innovative honors courses; signature experiential learning opportunities; a highly engaged student community; partnerships across a comprehensive research university; and a philosophy built around Opportunity, Community, and Challenge. The Director will have the opportunity to bring these strengths together around a bold, cohesive strategy that defines what honors education can mean at a large, comprehensive, research-intensive institution. This leader will be expected not simply to administer an established program, but to imagine what comes next. The UF Honors Experience The UF Honors Program is a dynamic academic community for highly motivated and intellectually curious undergraduate students. Through innovative coursework, interdisciplinary learning, leadership development, service, experiential opportunities, and close engagement with faculty and peers, Honors complements students’ academic majors while encouraging them to explore complex questions and pursue ambitious goals. At the center of that experience are three interconnected pillars. Opportunity - Honors opens doors to deeper engagement with the university and to personalized pathways for academic, professional, and personal growth. Students have access to distinctive courses, experiential learning, research and internship opportunities, faculty connections, leadership experiences, fellowship opportunities, early registration privileges, and priority housing in Honors Village. Community - Within a university of tens of thousands of students, Honors creates a smaller and highly supportive intellectual community. Students build relationships with peers, advisors, and faculty across academic disciplines and interests, developing connections that can extend well beyond their undergraduate experience. The program intentionally emphasizes collaboration rather than competition. Challenge - UF views Honors not simply as recognition for past accomplishment, but as an invitation to continued growth. Through rigorous academics, leadership, intellectual exploration, personal development, and experiences beyond the traditional classroom, students are encouraged to stretch themselves, redefine success, and discover new possibilities. The University of Florida University of Florida is Florida’s flagship university and a leading public research institution based in Gainesville. UF encompasses 16 colleges, a major academic health center, championship athletics, and a 2,000-acre campus that combines historic spaces, contemporary facilities, and preserved natural areas. UF is ranked No. 8 among U.S. public universities in the 2027 U.S. News Best Colleges rankings and is a member of the Association of American Universities. The university serves more than 62,000 students, employs approximately 32,000 people, and includes more than 6,600 faculty. UF reported record research expenditures of $1.34 billion in FY 2026. The scale and breadth of the institution creates extraordinary possibilities for honors students to connect across disciplines, engage in research and discovery, and learn from leading faculty. UF has also made significant investments in artificial intelligence education. AI has been incorporated into more than 230 courses taught by approximately 350 faculty, supported by HiPerGator, which UF describes as the most powerful university-owned supercomputer in the United States. Together, UF’s research enterprise, comprehensive academic portfolio, institutional resources, and ambitious vision create a powerful environment in which the Honors Program can continue to innovate. Compensation and Benefits The University of Florida offers a competitive salary commensurate with similar positions and includes a full benefits package. Click here to view the university’s Total Rewards Benefits plans. Our Commitment The University of Florida is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer. Hiring is contingent on eligibility to work in the U.S. The University of Florida is a public institution and is subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . Expected Salary: Commensurate based on experience and education. Required Qualifications: Hold a terminal degree, Ph.D. or equivalent doctorate, and bring at least 10 years of faculty experience, including sustained achievement in teaching, scholarly or creative activity, and professional engagement within their discipline. Preferred: The successful candidate will also bring demonstrated administrative experience in higher education and possess the following preferred knowledge, skills and abilities: A deep commitment to undergraduate education and an understanding of the academic, developmental, and experiential needs of high-achieving students. The ability to articulate and execute a compelling strategic vision for the future of honors education. A record of innovation in honors education, undergraduate student success, experiential learning, or related areas. Demonstrated administrative experience overseeing academic programs, operations, policies, resources, and budgets within a complex higher education environment, with the ability to align operational decision with programmatic priorities and institutional goals. Successful experience supervising, developing, and inspiring employees while creating a positive and high-performing workplace. The ability to build meaningful partnerships across colleges, academic units, and administrative functions and with external stakeholders, including cultivating relationships that support fundraising and resource development opportunities aligned with programmatic priorities. Knowledge of national trends, emerging practices, and opportunities in honors education. A commitment to meaningful assessment, evidence-informed decision-making, and continuous improvement. Outstanding communication, relationship-building, and consensus-building skills, with the judgement and organizational acumen to navigate a large, complex research university. A strong commitment to creating an environment in which students from varied backgrounds can thrive and achieve their full potential. Above all, the next Director should be energized by the possibility of working with outstanding students and talented colleagues to shape the future of honors education at a leading public research university. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted by December 6, 2026, at 11:55 p.m. with the following required documents: A complete CV and cover letter summarizing experience, qualifications, and interest in this role List of references for at least three (3) professional contacts that includes full name, current position, email address, and telephone number The panel may review and consider additional applications periodically after December 6, 2026, at 11:55 p.m., until a competitive applicant pool has been established. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/535998/postdoctoral-researcher": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Researcher Apply now Job no: 535998 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29240101 - MD-HOBI-GENERAL Classification Title: Postdoctoral Researcher Classification Minimum Requirements: A Ph.D. in biomedical informatics, computer science, information science, data science, (bio)-statistics, (applied) mathematics, physics, or a related STEM fields. Strong programming and data analysis skills (e.g., Python, R) Solid understanding of machine learning, deep learning, and data modeling techniques Job Description: Develop methods and tools to effectively use electronic health record (EHR) data; apply AI/ML methods to solve critical clinical and biomedical problems. Implement methods into software that meets research needs, manage and update source codes as needed. Work in an interdisciplinary team of informaticists, programmers, information quality experts, statisticians, and researchers during software development. Lead and participate in the design, implementation, and reporting of research and evaluation studies. Contributions to scientific reports, conference papers, journal articles, and grant proposals are expected, including documentation of method, presentation of data, and participation in interpretation of results. Lead and participate in collaborative projects and contribute to data processing, software development, and other collaborative efforts. Expected Salary: $61,000 - $64,000 Required Qualifications: A Ph.D. in biomedical informatics, computer science, information science, data science, (bio)-statistics, (applied) mathematics, physics, or a related STEM fields. Strong programming and data analysis skills (e.g., Python, R) Solid understanding of machine learning, deep learning, and data modeling techniques Preferred: Prior working experience with EHR data, machine learning, deep learning, imaging informatics, and large language models (LLM) is preferred. Prior working experience with popular ML packages, e.g., PyTorch, Scikit-learn, TensorFlow, Pandas, Keras, NumPy, SciPy, etc., and traditional ML models such as support vector machines (SVMs) and Gradient Boosting Machines (GBM), as well as deep learning models (GNN, CNN, GCN, etc.) including LLMs. Being able to develop informatics tools and software packages. Excellent formal and interpersonal communication skills and the ability to communicate effectively to a diverse audience, such as students, clinicians, and PIs. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time limited position. This requisition has been reposted and extended. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply . Final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an institution outside the U.S. must be evaluated by a credentialing service approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES): http://naces.org/. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 13 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/538152/assistantassociatefull-professor-medical-oncologist": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Medical Oncologist",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Medical Oncologist Apply now Job no: 538152 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty, Non-Tenure-Track Faculty, Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29050604 - MD-HEMATOLOGY-ONCOLOGY-OTHER Classification Title: Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Medical Oncologist Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants must be board-certified or board eligible in Medical Oncology and be eligible for a Florida Medical License or eligible for a Medical Faculty Certificate. Job Description: Medical Oncologist at the University of Florida. The Department of Medicine at the University of Florida is seeking applications for a full or part-time medical oncologist for a clinician-researcher track position in the Division of Hematology/Oncology at the Assistant Professor rank or higher. This role is open to ABIM board-certified or board-eligible medical oncologists with expertise in solid tumors. The position offers flexibility, allowing the candidate to shape their role within the division based on their specific expertise and interests. The successful applicant will primarily focus on clinical care but will also be expected to contribute significantly to clinical and translational research, participate in team-based research initiatives, and engage in teaching students, residents, and fellows in both clinical and inpatient settings. Additionally, the candidate will collaborate in a variety of activities, including subspecialty and multidisciplinary clinics at the UF Health main campus, cancer case and research conferences, and clinical trials. Clinical responsibilities may also include an assignment at the Malcom Randall VA Medical Center. A commitment to high-quality patient care, research, and academic excellence is essential. Faculty in the UF Division of Hematology and Oncology are members of the University of Florida Health Cancer Institute (UFHCI), the only NCI-Designated Cancer Institute based at a public university in Florida. The UFHCI blends comprehensive patient care and innovative research in a collaborative, multidisciplinary environment, with members from all 16 colleges at the University of Florida (UF). UF, ranked #7 among public universities by U.S. News & World Report and #4 by Forbes in 2025, stands as a premier research institution, driving innovation and discovery while delivering exceptional value and academic excellence. Resources available for professional development at the University of Florida Health Sciences Center include leadership, education, and research training through support from the UF Health Cancer Institute, Clinical Translational Science Institute (CTSI), formal mentorship programs, and supported opportunities for teaching and research. The UF Health Cancer Institute Clinical Research Office provides clinical research support. Foreign national candidates whose employment conditions meet federal and University requirements under an immigrant classification are eligible to apply. Tenure accruing and non-tenure accruing tracks are available. Rank and tenure status will be commensurate with qualifications About Gainesville, Florida Gainesville, Florida, ( https://www.visitgainesville.com/ ) is a vibrant university city of 140,000, home to the University of Florida. Surrounded by pristine springs, miles of trails, and one of the state’s lushest urban tree canopies, Gainesville blends year-round outdoor living with top-ranked public schools, a thriving arts and music scene, championship athletics, and an affordable cost of living. Just a short drive from the beaches on both coasts and within two hours of Jacksonville, Orlando, and Tampa, Gainesville offers big-city access with small-town charm — all enhanced by Florida’s standout benefit of no state income tax. Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Applicants must be board-certified or board eligible in Medical Oncology and be eligible for a Florida Medical License or eligible for a Medical Faculty Certificate. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . This requisition will be used to fill multiple positions. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants remain under consideration and do not need to reapply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540281/conferenceevent-manager-i-college-of-nursing": {
      "title": "Conference-Event Manager I- College of Nursing",
      "description": "Conference-Event Manager I- College of Nursing Apply now Job no: 540281 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Recreation/Event Services Department: 31010000 - NR-OFFICE OF THE DEAN Classification Title: Conference-Event Manager I-College of Nursing Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree and three years of relevant experience; or a high school diploma and seven years of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: The Events Manager for the College of Nursing is responsible for the strategic planning, execution, and evaluation of high-impact events that advance the College’s mission, visibility, and stakeholder engagement. This role manages complex event portfolios, ensures brand alignment, and collaborates with cross-functional partners to deliver exceptional experiences for internal and external audiences. High-Impact Event & Conference Execution Independently lead the end-to-end planning and execution of large-scale, high-visibility College events, including symposiums, conferences, donor events, leadership retreats, and academic ceremonies. Ensure all events align with institutional priorities, brand standards, and stakeholder expectations. Develop and maintain comprehensive project plans, timelines, and run-of-show documents to ensure seamless execution. Event Operations & Logistics Oversee complex event logistics, including venue selection, vendor sourcing, contract negotiation, catering, audiovisual services, travel, and on-site execution. Manage event registration processes, attendee communication, and hospitality services to ensure a high-quality participant experience. Serve as the primary point of contact for event-related vendors and internal stakeholders. Budget & Financial Management Develop and manage event budgets in partnership with Finance and College leadership. Track expenses, process invoices, and ensure cost-effective use of resources while maintaining high production standards. Identify sponsorship and partnership opportunities, as appropriate. Marketing, Communications & Engagement Collaborate with the College’s marketing and communications team to develop and implement event promotion strategies, including digital campaigns, invitations, and media outreach. Contribute content for event-related materials such as programs, websites, and social media. Support efforts to increase event attendance, visibility, and stakeholder engagement. Assessment & Continuous Improvement Design and implement post-event evaluation strategies, including surveys and metrics analysis. Provide comprehensive event reports with insights and recommendations for continuous improvement. Apply feedback and data to refine processes, improve attendee experience, and enhance strategic alignment. Stakeholder Collaboration Partner with faculty, leadership, alumni relations, advancement, and external organizations to plan and execute events that support academic, research, and fundraising goals. Cultivate strong relationships with internal and external stakeholders to ensure successful event outcomes Expected Salary: $70,000-80,000 Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree and three years of relevant experience; or a high school diploma and seven years of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: • Master’s Degree in a related field such as Hospitality Management, Marketing, Communications, or Public Relations • Demonstrated ability to manage large-scale, complex events with multiple stakeholders. • Strong project management, organizational, and problem-solving skills. • Excellent written and verbal communication skills. • Experience managing budgets and vendor relationships. • Experience in academic or healthcare-related event environments. • Familiarity with event management platforms and CRM systems. • Knowledge of accessibility, compliance, and risk management considerations in event planning Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume, and list of three professional references. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540507/grossing-histotechnologist": {
      "title": "Grossing Histotechnologist",
      "description": "Grossing Histotechnologist Apply now Job no: 540507 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29081100 - MD-PATH - UF PATH LABS Classification Title: Histology Technologist Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree and one year of experience as a Histology Technician; or licensed as a Histology Technologist Job Description: Performs all processes to prepare tissues for histological and transmission electron microscopy; diagnostic evaluation including accessioning, appropriate labeling of requisitions and specimen containers, printing cassettes, fixation, preparing packing list for transportation to the histology laboratory, and subsequent discarding of surgical specimens. Identify and acquire paraffin tissue blocks required for molecular testing and use these blocks to obtain an adequate amount of DNA/RNA for molecular testing. Ensure sample integrity for assays such as NGS, Fusions, and IGH arrangements. Responsible for communications with physicians regarding testing. Perform quality control, quality assurance, and proficiency testing. Participate in assay/instrument validations and Quality Initiatives. Perform processing and specialized techniques on muscle, nerve, and medical kidney specimens. Perform staining, IHC, & Immunofluorescence on these specimens. Complete, accurate, and timely gross examination and dictation of anatomic pathology specimens. Cassette printing, bottle identification, and case number designation. Expected Salary: Starting salary is between $29.50 - $35.00 per hour, commensurate with experience Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree and one year of experience as a Histology Technician; or licensed as a Histology Technologist. Preferred: Licensed as a Histology Technologist with 2+ years of hands-on research or clinical diagnostic experience in advanced molecular diagnostic and/or histological techniques, FISH and IHC – particularly using Leica instrument platforms. -Ability to use problem-solving techniques in the laboratory. -Ability to communicate with other technologists and supervisors. -Ability to collect and analyze data. -Ability to understand and apply rules, regulations, policies, and procedures for clinical testing. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This position is eligible for veterans' preference. If you are claiming veterans' preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See UF’s Veterans' Preference Page for more details. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541025/accountant-iii-temporary": {
      "title": "Accountant III- Temporary",
      "description": "Accountant III- Temporary Apply now Job no: 541025 Work type: Temp Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Business/Accounting/Finance, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 64901000 - FA-CA-ADMINISTRATION Classification Title: Accountant III Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience. Job Description: Construction Financial Reconciliation and General Ledger Management: This position will ensuring the accuracy, integrity, and completeness of financial data related to the University of Florida’s construction projects. The role performs detailed reviews and reconciliations between multiple construction financial tracking systems and the University’s general ledger (GL) to identify and resolve discrepancies. Responsibilities include analyzing project expenditures, commitments, and balances; validating financial transactions; reconciling GL activity to project records; and preparing and posting corrective or clean-up journal entries as needed. The position works closely with project managers, CFO’s construction team, and other stakeholders to ensure construction-related financial information is accurate and properly recorded. Construction Project Financial Analysis and Workday Transition Support: In support of the University’s transition to the Workday enterprise system, this position conducts comprehensive reviews of construction project financial data to ensure consistency, accuracy, and readiness for system conversion. Responsibilities include evaluating project budgets, funding sources, and current project status to verify that financial records are complete and properly aligned across systems. The role analyzes historical and current project data, identifies data integrity issues, recommends corrective actions, and assists with the validation of information for migration into Workday. Continuous Improvement: Analyze current processes and systems, research and recommend solutions to leadership, and support implementation. Review cross-functional financial practices and procedures to ensure consistency and create critical process documentation. Special Projects: Participates in special projects and performs other duties as assigned. Expected Salary: commensurate with experience Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience. Preferred: Strong analytical skills and proficiency with accounting software Ability to apply problem solving skills Excellent written and verbal communication, presentation, and business‑reporting abilities Ability to work independently and as part of a cross‑functional team. Proficient in Excel Strong interpersonal skills Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a temporary appointment. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541396/ops-fiscal-assistant-ii": {
      "title": "OPS Fiscal Assistant II",
      "description": "OPS Fiscal Assistant II Apply now Job no: 541396 Work type: Temp Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 28011200 - VM-BUSINESS OFFICE Classification Title: OPS Fiscal Assistant II Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and three years of general accounts transaction experience. Appropriate college course work or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for required experience. Job Description: This position is responsible for ensuring that vendors are paid in accordance with University and State of Florida rules and regulations, that expenses are paid with appropriate funding sources, and that supporting documentation is available and correct. This position will also ensure that purchase orders are closed after items are received and paid. Responsibilities include: Reconciles PeopleSoft general ledger transactions to in-house subsidiary systems and source documentation retained in on-base system; reconciles various UF Foundation funds benefiting the College of Veterinary Medicine by monitoring and reconciling transactions at the UF Foundation with internal database transaction records. Utilizes reporting software, such as Enterprise Analytics to generate and run various financial reports, such as payroll, source transaction, open encumbrances, and other reports as needed. Supports the Research Administration team to reconcile projects (Grants). Maintains knowledge of PeopleSoft procedures and chartfield funding sources utilized by then College of Veterinary Medicine and Veterinary Hospital departments. Scans/copies documents supporting financial transactions as needed into PeopleSoft or On-base. Cross-trains with other staff for various office-related duties. Other duties as assigned. Expected Salary: $18.00 - $20.00 / hourly Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and three years of general accounts transaction experience. Appropriate college course work or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for required experience. Preferred: • Knowledge of accounting principles and practices • Experience with PeopleSoft • Skilled in data collection and report preparation • Proficient with PC applications • Ability to work independently • Ability to plan, organize, and coordinate work assignments • Ability to communicate effectively verbally and in writing • Ability to utilize problem solving techniques • Ability to analyze and interpret accounting data • Ability to understand and apply rules, regulations, policies and procedures of accounting programs • Detail oriented Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541455/clinical-research-nurse-lead-gator-momitor": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Nurse - Lead Gator MOMitor",
      "description": "Clinical Research Nurse - Lead Gator MOMitor Apply now Job no: 541455 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Nursing Department: 29070800 - MD-OBGYN-ACADE SPEC GEN OB GYN Classification Title: Clinical Research Nurse - Lead Gator MOMitor Classification Minimum Requirements: Graduate of a nationally accredited nursing program and three years of professional nursing experience. Licensed as a registered nurse in accordance with Chapter 464, Florida Statutes Job Description: The Clinical Research Nurse will be responsible for, responding to patient alerts, assessing patient symptoms, following clinical protocols, documenting findings, plan, outcome in EMR and data collection tools. Obtain and record research data in conjunction with physicians and other professionals on the research team; enroll study subjects, interview subjects; review medical records; assist in planning nursing aspects of medical team research; complete data collection tools. Maintain and oversee nurse log databases. Establish and maintain effective working relationships with patients and their families, professional or community groups and volunteers; maintain contact with public agencies which are involved with aspects of the research. Determine eligibility of potential study participants; obtain informed consent; enroll patients in research protocol; coordinate all aspects of the patient’s care. Upload participant’s information into the Mosio text platform. Trains and direct supervision of Clinical Research Nurse positions (local and outlying counties). Creates and manages Clinical Research Nurse recruitment schedule/locations. Delegates daily Clinical Research Nurse tasks. Explain research protocol to participants; respond to patient inquiries regarding protocol; schedule patient participation; ensure compliance with research protocol; inform referring physicians of protocol requirements. Collect, record, organize, transcribe and verify accuracy of research data; Coordinate the collection of research data; create data collection tools through applications such as REDCap. Will work with administrative staff and research peers, or Principal Investigator (PI), on Institutional Review Board submissions. The Clinical Research Nurse will communicate with sponsors, auditors, monitoring agencies, and the Institutional Review Board(s) in a timely, accurate, and effective manner. Performs other duties as may be necessary to conduct study and carryout related functions of the Division. Travel to clinics for potential participate registration and to train staff as needed. Expected Salary: $75,000 - $77,000 annually, commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: Graduate of a nationally accredited nursing program and three years of professional nursing experience. Licensed as a registered nurse in accordance with Chapter 464, Florida Statutes. Preferred: Strong written and oral communication skills, Bilingual preferred. Preferred 3+ years of experience in obstetrics and/or research, specifically patient recruitment, performing research studies, follow-up visits and maintenance of study records. Special Instructions to Applicants: This is a time limited position. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Please attach curriculum vitae, cover letter, and three letters of recommendation. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541596/lecturer-in-japanese-language-and-culture": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Japanese Language and Culture*",
      "description": "Lecturer in Japanese Language and Culture* Apply now Job no: 541596 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Humanities Department: 16860000 - LS-LANGUAGE LITERATURE&CULTURE Classification Title: Lecturer Job Description: The Department of Languages, Literatures and Cultures at the University of Florida invites applications for the position of Lecturer in Japanese language and culture to begin August 16, 2024. This is a full-time, renewable, nine-month, non-tenure accruing position with the working title Instructional Assistant Professor. Duties and Responsibilities : The successful applicant will a) contribute to the development and teaching of Japanese language and culture courses (including online); b) contribute to a robust undergraduate major in Japanese language and culture by collaborating with colleagues to ensure smooth articulation across the levels of language instruction and by participating in course design and creation; c) provide extracurricular support of the program through service that includes placement testing, weekly language tables, and other activities relating to extracurricular Japanese language use. The typical teaching assignment for a lecturer is three courses per semester. The Department of Languages, Literatures, and Cultures is home to multiple language programs. It offers training in languages and cultures from all corners of the globe. From Swahili to Japanese, Russian to Arabic, we give our students the opportunity to become cross-cultural experts in an ever more internationalized world. The Department is committed to creating an environment that affirms diversity across a variety of dimensions, including ethnicity/race, gender identity and expression, and the Department especially encourages applications from women and minority groups. UF is the state’s oldest, largest, and most comprehensive land grant university with an enrollment of over 50,000 students and was ranked the Number 1 public university in the United States by Wall Street Journal in its “2024 Best Colleges in the U.S.” report. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf of Mexico, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs including magnet schools and an international baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville . Minimum Requirements: ● M.A. degree or higher in Japanese language or relevant area, including second-language acquisition, linguistics, and Japanese language pedagogy. ● Native or near-native proficiency in Japanese and English and US college/university-level teaching experience are required. ● Candidates with demonstrated expertise in language-learning technology or online course development are particularly welcome. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes Cover letter, Curriculum vitae, Statement of teaching philosophy, A list of three letter writers and the writers’ contact information. An email will be sent to your references, requesting them to upload their confidential letter to the submission packet, Sample syllabus for one course, Lesson plans or teaching material for one class session, A video or link to a video of a teaching demonstration (approx. 25 minutes), Data from teaching evaluations. Application lesson plans or teaching material and teaching demonstration video may, but need not, cover the same course material. To ensure full consideration, applications should be received by Oct 20th, 2026 but the search will remain open until the position is filled. Questions may be directed to Dr. Alassane Sow, Chair, Japanese Search Committee, at a.sow@ufl.edu . All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is an equal opportunity institution. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. If an accommodation due to disability is needed in order to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 06 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541604/technologydriven-integrative-physiology-aienabled-cell-molecular-and-genomic-biology-assistantassociate-professor": {
      "title": "Technology-Driven Integrative Physiology AI-Enabled Cell, Molecular and Genomic Biology Assistant/Associate Professor",
      "description": "Technology-Driven Integrative Physiology AI-Enabled Cell, Molecular and Genomic Biology Assistant/Associate Professor Apply now Job no: 541604 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: St. Johns Categories: Biology/Life Science Department: 57130100 - WH-ADMINISTRATION Classification Title: Technology-Driven Integrative Physiology | AI-Enabled Cell, Molecular and Genomic Biology Assistant/Associate Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree, relevant experience consistent with academic policy for faculty rank, and have the potential to support their research programs through external funding sources. Job Description: The Whitney Laboratory for Marine Bioscience is the premier marine research institute at the University of Florida. We are in an exciting phase of growth and invite applications for a tenure-track faculty appointment at the Assistant or Associate Professor level in one of two broad areas: Technology-Driven Integrative Physiology — connecting molecular, cellular, neural, biomechanical or developmental mechanisms to organismal function, behavior, and environmental response; OR AI-Enabled Cell, Molecular, or Genomic Biology — building advanced AI, machine learning, and other computational approaches to address basic biological questions. The Whitney Lab is a research-focused institute committed to technology-driven marine bioscience. Our investigators leverage a broad diversity of organisms to address fundamental questions in biology, with potential applications to issue in human health, natural resources, and the environment. We seek an exemplary, creative, and collaborative scientist who will take advantage of Whitney’s unique marine setting and contribute to our interdisciplinary research mission. This position is part of a multi-year investment in faculty hiring, infrastructure, and research innovation at the UF Whitney Lab. Our new $40 million research building (38,000 ft 2 ) opened in Spring 2026 with state-of-the-art wet and dry labs, large temperature-controlled seawater mesocosms and aquaculture tank systems, and world-leading imaging capabilities within the Whitney Laboratory Center for Biological Imaging and Discovery . The University of Florida offers competitive salaries, startup packages, and relocation support. An appointment may be made jointly with any UF department aligned with the candidate’s background (e.g., Departments of Biology , Chemistry , Soil and Water Science , College of Medicine ). The successful candidate will join a diverse UF research community with connections across the UF Scripps Institute for Biomedical Innovation and Technology , the UF Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences (IFAS) , the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences (CLAS) , the Department of Biomedical Engineering , the Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering , the Florida Museum of Natural History , the UF Genetics Institute, and the McKnight Brain Institute . UF is home to the fastest university-owned supercomputer ( HiPerGator 4 th Gen ) and the NVIDIA AI Technology Center . The successful candidate will be expected to: Develop an internationally recognized, externally funded research program Contribute to Whitney’s interdisciplinary research environment by collaborating across departments, colleges, and external partners Participate in recruiting, teaching, and mentoring undergraduate, graduate, and postdoctoral trainees Engage in service activities that support the Whitney Laboratory’s research mission. About the Whitney Laboratory The Whitney Laboratory is an off-campus cutting-edge research community located on the northeast coast of Florida, 75 miles from the UF main campus and 17 miles south of beautiful historic St. Augustine. The Whitney Lab is unique in combining high-tech research labs with direct access to field sites in both the Atlantic Ocean and the Matanzas River intercoastal waterway. Whitney research faculty direct pioneering research programs that use marine organisms and sophisticated experimental and theoretical approaches to address fundamental scientific questions. On seven acres, the UF Whitney campus features a research village consisting of six cottages that can house up to 24 visiting scientists and students, a two-story education and outreach Center for Marine Studies building with a 280-seat auditorium, atrium, K-12 classrooms, and meeting spaces. About the University of Florida The University of Florida is a public institution and member of the Association of American Universities enrolling 55,000 students, including more than 36,000 residential undergraduates. UF is among the nation’s most academically diverse universities, with 16 colleges offering 100 undergraduate degree programs, 200 graduate programs, and various professional degrees. The University of Florida ranked 7 th among top public schools in the most recent ranking by U.S. News and World Report, and in the recent Forbes 2025-2026 America’s Top Colleges, UF was ranked 5 th among public universities and 30 th overall. Expected Salary: Salary is commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Candidates must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree, relevant experience consistent with academic policy for faculty rank, and have the potential to support their research programs through external funding sources Preferred: The successful candidate will be expected to: Develop an internationally recognized, externally funded research program Contribute to Whitney’s interdisciplinary research environment by collaborating across departments, colleges, and external partners Participate in recruiting, teaching, and mentoring undergraduate, graduate, and postdoctoral trainees Engage in service activities that support the Whitney Laboratory’s research mission. Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Materials To be considered for this position, you should submit the following: • Cover letter • Curriculum vitae • Research statement (up to 3 pages single-spaced) • Contact information for three references For additional information, to submit nominations, or express interest, please contact Dr. James Liao and James Strother, search committee chairs, at wh-facultysearch@ufl.edu . The anticipated start date is Fall 2027. Application review will begin December 1, 2026. Applications will be accepted after this date until the position is filled. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES). The University of Florida is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The “government in the sunshine” laws of Florida require that all documents relating to the search process, including letters of application/nomination and reference, be available for public inspection upon request. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541607/assistant-professor-in-violin": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Violin",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Violin Apply now Job no: 541607 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Humanities Department: 13030100 - COTA-MUSIC-DIRECTOR Classification Title: AST PROF Classification Minimum Requirements Candidates for this rank must hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess equivalent qualifications based on professional experience. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Job Description: The University of Florida School of Music in the College of the Arts invites applications for a full-time, 9-month Assistant Professor of Violin. We seek an accomplished performer and pedagogue who will contribute to the continued success and growth of our BM, BA, MM, and DMA programs in violin. The successful candidate will recruit and teach a full studio of applied violin students. Other responsibilities could include teaching violin pedagogy, violin literature, or chamber music. The University of Florida College of the Arts intends to be a transformative community, responding to and generating paradigmatic shifts in the arts and beyond. As artists and scholars, we embrace the complexity of our evolving human experience and seek to empower our students and faculty to shape that experience fearlessly through critical study, creative practice, and provocation. We seek a colleague who identifies as a change-maker. We seek a colleague who will position emerging artists and researchers as catalysts for justice on local and global levels. The University of Florida is an equal opportunity institution. This position is covered by the United Faculty of Florida Contract. You can view this employment union contract here https://admin.hr.ufl.edu/compliance/employee-relations-and-ethics/union-negotiations/united-faculty-of-florida-contract/ . Responsibilities Recruit, maintain, and teach a successful full violin studio of undergraduate and graduate level students, including weekly private instruction of applied violin curriculum, studio class, and participation in area recitals/juries. (Additional responsibilities may include coaching chamber and/or teaching courses in the candidate’s area(s) of expertise.) Maintain professional activities at the regional and national level as a performing artist consistent with the expectation of tenure and promotion Service to the String area, School of Music, University, and the profession as consistent with expectation of tenure and promotion SCHOOL OF MUSIC: Organized within the College of the Arts, the School of Music plays an important role in the academic and cultural life of the University, the College’s Creative Campus initiative and the community. The School has 40 full-time and nine adjunct faculty, serving some 200 graduate and 200 undergraduate music majors. Degree programs include B.S., B.A., B.M., M.M., D.M.A., and the Ph.D. in music education, composition, ethnomusicology, and historical musicology. Program emphases include performance, music education, theory, composition, music history and literature, ethnomusicology, conducting, and sacred music. The School of Music is also home to the UF Bands program, which includes “The Pride of the Sunshine” Gator Marching Band. School of Music facilities include the School of Music Building and Steinbrenner Band Hall, among others. The University of Florida is accredited by the Southern Association of Colleges and Schools Commission on Colleges and is an accredited institutional member of the National Association of Schools of Music. For more information, visit www.arts.ufl.edu/music . THE COLLEGE OF THE ARTS: The College of the Arts is an engine of transformation that generates paradigmatic shifts in the arts and beyond, while nurturing adaptability, innovation, and excellence. We foster an environment where novel creative practice, leading research, and experiential learning thrive. Our students, faculty, and staff interrogate and respond to the ever-changing relationship between technology and the human spirit. We prepare future leaders in artistic professions and creative industries, while developing student capacity for interdisciplinary scholarship that serves and engages local and global communities. The College offers baccalaureate, masters, and doctoral degrees. Approximately 1,600 students are pursuing majors in degrees offered by the College of the Arts under the direction of 150 faculty members in its three accredited schools— the School of Art + Art History, the School of Music, and the School of Theatre + Dance, and in the Center for Arts in Medicine, the Digital Worlds Institute, and the Center for Arts, Migration, and Entrepreneurship. In addition, the college comprises the University Galleries, and the University level of the New World School of the Arts in Miami. The University of Florida: The University of Florida is a comprehensive learning institution built on a land grant foundation. We are The Gator Nation, a diverse community dedicated to excellence in education and research and shaping a better future for Florida, the nation and the world. Our mission is to enable our students to lead and influence the next generation and beyond for economic, cultural and societal benefit. UF is a graduate research institution with more than 50,000 students and membership in the prestigious Association of American Universities. Gainesville, which is consistently ranked as one of the nation’s most livable cities, is located midway between the Gulf of Mexico and the Atlantic Ocean. Together, the University and the community comprise the educational, medical and cultural center of North Central Florida. Expected Salary: $65,000 max Required Qualifications: Required Qualifications Doctoral degree in violin performance or MM degree in violin performance with equivalent professional experience Documented record of professional violin performances in the classical tradition consistent with faculty at a R-1 institution Demonstrated command of solo, chamber, and orchestral violin repertoire and clear curricular teaching approach/philosophy that adapts to the needs and interests of students Demonstrated capacity for effective engagement in studio recruitment and teaching at the collegiate level Dedication to successful collaboration, organization, and collegiality with faculty, students, and staff Evidence of skill in designing and facilitating academic work that advances curiosity, respect, open intellectual discourse, and the welcome incorporation and well-being of all in an environment of complex differences. Preferred Other desirable qualifications may include the following: Doctoral degree and collegiate teaching experience of at least 3 to 5 years (beyond graduate assistantship) Demonstrated capacity for clear, thoughtful, and engaging communication as a pedagogue, and enthusiastic commitment to creating a vibrant learning environment for students Documented record of exceptional artistic achievement as a classical musician and collaborator recognized at a national or international level Demonstrated record of successful university-level teaching experience and studio recruitment/cultivation Ability to mentor graduate students at the masters’ and doctoral levels Organizational, management and communication experience in the higher education setting Evidence of success in negotiation and collaboration toward creative and administrative goals and dedication to enhancing our existing community of outstanding scholars and musicians Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Deadline Review of applications will begin in mid-November and continue until an applicant pool is established. To ensure full consideration, all application documents must be submitted by close of business on Monday, Nov. 16, 2026. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. Application Process Applications must be submitted via the University of Florida’s online application. Applications must include the following: A cover letter that explains how you match the qualifications of this position and how your work and perspective will contribute to and enhance our transformative community A curriculum vitae A PDF with links to: A selection of performance videos demonstrating a variety of styles (30-60 minutes) A teaching demonstration video taken within the last three years (30-60 minutes) Teaching Philosophy Statement (1 pg) Names and contact information for three professional references The UF system will give you the option to upload letters of reference. We are not requiring letters of reference with initial application materials. Please ignore this option. The Search Committee may request additional materials at a later time. Inquiries may be sent to: Tiffany Lu Chair, Violin Ast Prof Search Email: lu.tiffany@ufl.edu Applications will not be removed from consideration based solely on AI software review. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES). If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call +1 (352) 392- 2477or the Florida Relay System at +1 (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541608/clin-svc-representative-i": {
      "title": "Clin Svc Representative I",
      "description": "Clin Svc Representative I Apply now Job no: 541608 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 04030100 - SL-COUNSELING & WELLNESS CTR Classification Title: Clin Svc Representative I Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent. Job Description: This position is located in the Counseling & Wellness Center. This support person must be able to deal with highly varied and detailed tasks and have a high degree of organizational skills, the ability to interact with many areas in the Division of Student Life, and to communicate well with others from within and outside of the Counseling & Wellness Center. Customer Service – answers incoming calls from clients, counselors, physicians, attorneys, pharmacies and other health care providers (internal and external). Responds to clients, counselors, physicians, other health care providers (internal and external), attorneys and pharmacies directly within scope of position. Routes calls to appropriate mental health professional, administrator or office personnel. Point of Service Operations – Responsible for client check in. Greets clients, schedules and/or confirms appointments. Determine client eligibility for services. Provides HIPAA information as necessary. Directs clients to complete paperwork or pay fees if appropriate. Assists clients with questions or concerns. Acts as witness for documentation needing signatures. Arranged Care – Schedules client appointments and consultations. Refers scheduling to CSRII, CSRIII or On-Call provider when appropriate. Obtains initial patient demographic information. Integrated Care – Inspects intake documentation for emergent concerns and refers to appropriate provider when necessary. Special Projects – employee will periodically be assigned special projects or other duties by supervisor. Expected Salary: $17/hour Preferred: Should be experienced in medical clinic appointment scheduling and medical records systems, UF ISIS student records system, Microsoft Office programs. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with others. Ability to organize and maintain accurate files. Ability to communicate effectively verbally and in writing. Ability to triage phone calls, take accurate messages and page counselors as needed. This position requires ability to understand policies, procedures, and regulations related to patient. Ability to work with health care professionals and affiliated staff. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541609/administrative-assistant-iii": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant III",
      "description": "Administrative Assistant III Apply now Job no: 541609 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Veteran's Preference Eligible, Humanities Department: 13010900 - COTA-CENTER FOR AME Classification Title: Administrative Assistant III Classification Minimum Requirements High school diploma or equivalent and seven years of relevant experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: Responsibilities Process business administration transactions required to implement the work of the Center for Arts, Migration and Entrepreneurship (CAME). Process fiscal, travel and HR transactions in support of Center initiatives and sponsored project activities, including purchasing, hiring/payment requests, and travel arrangements for center personnel. Support personnel to ensure the completion of academic certification activities and effort reporting. Assist project leaders and the Project Manager with purchasing, expense tracking, reporting and other administrative processes related to sponsored projects as needed. Liaise with the College’s centralized business office subject matter experts to ensure that all business transactions are completed in a timely manner and in compliance with UF and College of the Arts policies. Execute daily Center administrative and operational needs, attending to office supplies, mail, copier/printer, other equipment and furniture. Coordinate asset inventory, tracking and reporting; and assist with space allocation. Assist with developing and maintaining documentation on relevant policies and procedures in an organized and accessible manner. Attend Center meetings as requested to provide administrative support including creating meeting agendas, communicating timelines and reminders about Center activities and following up to ensure completion of deadlines as requested. Assist with or coordinate (depending on need and level) Center events, community initiatives and academic activities to advance center’s mission, including but not limited to conferences, symposia, panel discussions and creative performances. Serve as a point of contact for scheduling the Center’s conference room. Interface regularly with campus units, external partners and stakeholders to foster relationships, support event planning logistics and program execution. Support general communications for the Center, managing the Center email and calendar. Serve as the first point of contact for general inquiries from faculty, staff, students, the public and other stakeholders. Provide general Center information or direct requests to the appropriate area in a timely manner. Support the Center Director with email and calendar management, triaging and prioritizing messages, scheduling appointments, sending reminders and tracking follow-up correspondence. Facilitate Center correspondence and manage scheduling for the Associate Director (when applicable), the Maker-in-Residence, and Principal Investigators as related to center operations and project activities. Assist the Project Manager and the Marketing and Communications Specialist with the development and distribution of promotional content for the Center and its initiatives via the website, email newsletter, social media and other channels in alignment with brand standards for CAME and the College of the Arts. Assist with managing courtesy faculty recruitment and appointments; research funding awards; and other courtesy faculty engagement activities. THE CENTER FOR ARTS, MIGRATION AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP: The Center for Arts, Migration, and Entrepreneurship (CAME) connects networks of scholars, artists, creatives, entrepreneurs, and advocates to the engines of creative and cultural economics at the heart of migration. Defining the arts as all forms of cultural production, the center seeks to effectively create new models and power alignments that return value— entrepreneurship —to cultural producers to build more just global futures. Through community-responsive programming, research, and creative production, the center facilitates and extends the innovation, resilience, and ingenuity of diasporic and migratory communities. THE COLLEGE OF THE ARTS: The College of the Arts is an engine of transformation that generates paradigmatic shifts in the arts and beyond, while nurturing adaptability, innovation, and excellence. We foster an environment where novel creative practice, leading research, and experiential learning thrive. Our students, faculty, and staff interrogate and respond to the ever-changing relationship between technology and the human spirit. We prepare future leaders in artistic professions and creative industries, while developing student capacity for interdisciplinary scholarship that serves and engages local and global communities. The College offers baccalaureate, masters, and doctoral degrees. Approximately 1,600 students are pursuing majors in degrees offered by the College of the Arts under the direction of 150 faculty members in its three accredited schools— the School of Art + Art History, the School of Music, and the School of Theatre + Dance, and in the Center for Arts in Medicine, the Digital Worlds Institute, and the Center for Arts, Migration, and Entrepreneurship. In addition, the college comprises the University Galleries, and the University level of the New World School of the Arts in Miami. The University of Florida: The University of Florida is a comprehensive learning institution built on a land grant foundation. We are The Gator Nation, a diverse community dedicated to excellence in education and research and shaping a better future for Florida, the nation and the world. Our mission is to enable our students to lead and influence the next generation and beyond for economic, cultural and societal benefit. UF is a graduate research institution with more than 50,000 students and membership in the prestigious Association of American Universities. Gainesville, which is consistently ranked as one of the nation’s most livable cities, is located midway between the Gulf of Mexico and the Atlantic Ocean. Together, the University and the community comprise the educational, medical and cultural center of North Central Florida. In addition to salary, the University of Florida (UF) offers low cost State Health plans, a number of Dental plans to fit you and your family’s needs, and Vision. Domestic partner coverage through GatorCare is also available. Optional plans such as life, disability, legal and accident insurance are also available. UF provides a variety of leave programs including vacation, sick leave, 11 paid holidays, personal leave days, and family medical leave. TEAMS (staff) employees accrue approximately 22 vacation days annually, 13 sick days annually, and 4 personal leave days, typically used during the holiday closing period (accruals based on full-time employment). UF also provides you the flexibility to deal with life’s challenges by offering paid family leave, eight full weeks of leave over a 24-month period. Build a retirement roadmap with competitive pension plans, investment accounts and a host of voluntary add-ons, such as 457 deferred compensation and 403(b) plans. UF Employees are also eligible for the GatorPerks discount program , which can provide savings at various business and retailers! Explore UF’s plethora of benefit options here: TEAMS Benefits Expected Salary: $26.40/hour Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and seven years of relevant experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred The successful candidate will meet some of the following Bachelor’s degree and three years of appropriate experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. 3 or more years of experience providing administrative support in a fast-paced professional environment. 3 or more years of experience providing administrative support to community-based projects or activities. 1 or more years of experience providing administrative support in a publicly or privately funded project-based environment. Evidence of success in effective communications with people from a variety of backgrounds and perspectives. Evidence of success in problem solving and critical thinking skills. Evidence of high capacity for attention to detail Evidence of skill in designing and facilitating administrative work that advances curiosity, respect, open intellectual discourse, and the welcome incorporation and well-being of all in an environment of complex differences. Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Deadline: All application materials must be submitted by 11:55pm on Sunday, October 11, 2026. Application Process: Applications must be submitted via the University of Florida’s online application system. Online applications must include the following: (1) a detailed letter of application that explains how you match the particular qualifications of this position and how your work and perspective will contribute to and enhance our transformative community; (2) a resume; (3) names and contact information of three professional references. The Search Committee may request additional materials at a later time, including obtaining UF engaged and/or other personnel files from UFHR for current or previous UF employees. Inquiries may be sent to: Osubi Craig Chair, Admin Ast III Search Email: Osubi.craig@ufl.edu The search committee does not utilize AI or other software to provide any level of applicant assessment. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES). If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call +1 (352) 392- 2477or the Florida Relay System at +1 (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541610/research-administration-assistant": {
      "title": "Research Administration Assistant",
      "description": "Research Administration Assistant Apply now Job no: 541610 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 33010000 - HP-OFFICE OF THE DEAN Classification Title: Research Administration Assistant Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma and four years of relevant experience or equivalent college coursework in place of experience. Job Description: The PHHP Research Core is dedicated to fostering the scientific advancement of investigators in the college, providing helpful information, guidance, and assistance in navigating the overall administration of those projects. The Core provides college-level review of all requests for funding and proposal submissions to both internal and external agencies for the college’s eight departments and the Dean’s Office. We are looking for a meticulous, critical thinking team member with an emphasis on excellent customer support. The Research Administration Assistant position, under the guidance of the Research Administration Managers, the Accounting Specialist, and the Director of Research Administration, will assist the team with a set group of essential day-to-day operations. Those operations would include: Salary Distribution Entry For faculty to be paid from grants, their appropriate effort and time need to be recorded in the UF system. These changes are guided from discussions with the faculty and research team about the projects that they are working on and their planned projects over the semester, and is an essential part of the salary, budget, and effort systems. This position will work with the research teams and the research core to ensure that updates are made timely in the UF systems to accurately reflect the work our research teams are making. Monthly Reporting Assist the senior research administrators and the Accounting Specialist with the creation and review of monthly financial reports for the faculty. The monthly reporting processes assist faculty and RAs with the tracking and management of the research awards. Reconciliation Work with the Accounting Specialist in the PHHP Research Core to review the charges placed on research funds as the first reviewer in our reconciliation process – ensuring documentation is included on the charges, the charges routed appropriately, and resolving any errors or questions the team may have on the charges. Research Core Proposal Assistance Assist the pre-award team as part of the process in learning the research administration process. The position will work with different members of the team to create summaries which provide information about common proposals, learn how to review proposal documents for basic requirements, and assist with compiling grant proposal files during high demand moments. Research Core Fiscal Assistance As needed and requested, this position will assist the research core post-award team with routine tasks, such as assisting in making and preparing projections for faculty meetings, assisting in moving charges on to the appropriate grant funds, award start up and close out, and other transactional arrangements as necessary. Expected Salary: $21.75-$23 per hour ($45,414 - $48,024 per annum, based on education, skills and experience) Benefits The University of Florida (UF) offers an exceptional benefits package, including health, dental and vision insurance; state retirement plans; a generous leave program; and tuition assistance. UF is a Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) eligible employer. For more information on benefits, please visit UF HR Benefits and Rewards at https://benefits.hr.ufl.edu/ . Required Qualifications: High school diploma and four years of relevant experience or equivalent college coursework in place of experience. Preferred: -Prioritize workday and organized working style -Ability to multi-task, re-focus quickly after interruptions -Email inbox management, respond to customers and track ongoing communications -Ability to solve problems, work independently while concurrently works well with others -Ability to ask questions to clarify uncertainty to avoid wrong decisions Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This is a time-limited position. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for veteran's preference. If you are claiming veteran's preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy with your application for consideration. If an accommodation is needed to apply for this position, please call 352/392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800/955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the U.S. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541611/research-assistant-scientist-in-vitro-breeding": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Scientist In Vitro Breeding",
      "description": "Research Assistant Scientist In Vitro Breeding Apply now Job no: 541611 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60230000 - AG-HORTICULTURAL SCIENCES Classification Title: Res Ast Sctst Classification Minimum Requirements: Highest degree possible in field; Evidence of pertinent experience for the position and qualities for the academic unit. Job Description: The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . This is a 12-month non-tenure-accruing position that will be 100% research (Blueberry Breeding Program), available in the Horticultural Sciences Department, Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences, at the University of Florida. This assignment may change in accordance with the needs of the Blueberry Breeding Program. The faculty member will serve as the In vitro Breeding Team Leader. Duties will include working closely with the Blueberry Breeding Program’s Director to develop and integrate advanced plant tissue culture techniques and cellular manipulation strategies to advance the development of superior cultivars at the University of Florida. Additionally, the faculty member will work on elaborating research lines, managing projects, securing collaboration and funds, and actively supporting the innovation of biotechnological strategies and automation systems to optimize germplasm concervation, propagation, enhance diversity and generate new cultivars support Florida’s agriculture sector. The research outcomes will broaden the breeding capacity and will also be implemented in an operational manner. The faculty member will be responsible for reporting progress, outputs, and impacts to interested stakeholders. Background Information : The University of Florida is a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution, encompassing virtually all academic and professional disciplines, with an enrollment of more than 56,000 students. UF is a member of The Association of American Universities. The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences includes the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences , the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station , the Florida Cooperative Extension Service , the College of Veterinary Medicine , the Florida Sea Grant program, and encompasses 16 on-campus academic departments and schools, 12 Research and Educational Centers (REC) located throughout the state, 6 Research sites/demonstration units administered by RECs or academic departments, and Florida Cooperative Extension Service offices in all 67 counties (counties operate and maintain). The School of Natural Resources and Environment is an interdisciplinary unit housed in IFAS and managed by several colleges on campus. UF/IFAS employs nearly 4,500 people, which includes approximately 990 salaried faculty and 1,400 permanent support personnel located in Gainesville and throughout the state. IFAS, one of the nation’s largest agricultural and natural resources research and education organizations, is administered by a Vice President and four deans: the Dean of the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences, the Dean for Extension and Director of the Florida Cooperative Extension Service, the Dean for Research and Director of the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station, and the Dean for the College of Veterinary Medicine. UF/IFAS also engages in cooperative work with Florida A&M University in Tallahassee. The Horticultural Sciences Department is a unit within IFAS at the University of Florida and has a variety of teaching, research, and extension education programs with 34 faculty in Gainesville and 26 faculty at various Research and Education Centers throughout the state. The Horticultural Sciences Department is currently ranked top 9 in the center for world university rankings. The Blueberry Breeding Program , is part of the Horticultural Science Department and is directed and led by Dr. Patricio Munoz, an associate professor with over 20 years of expertise in plant breeding. The program aims to develop new blueberry cultivars and advance research methodologies to expedite the cultivar development process. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Experience and Education Required Qualifications: A doctorate (foreign equivalent acceptable) in plant breeding or a closely related discipline is required. Candidates must also have hands-on experience in plant breeding technologies, be experts in tissue culture and have experience related to ploidy manipulation, cellular cicle manipulation, blueberry regeneration strategies and plant clonal propagation. Also, faculty must have demonstrated skills in verbal and written communication and interpersonal relationships. Candidates must be supportive of the mission of the Land-Grant system. Candidates must also have a commitment to UF core values. Preferred: Postdoctoral experience and industry experience in plant breeding, tissue culture, working with blueberry and other specialty crops. Experience using transcriptomic information to assist in breeding strategies and tissue culture. Experience with tissue culture training and R&D leadership. Experience collaborating with industry partners and translating research into applied outcomes. Demonstrated project management and leadership experience, including building collaborations among a variety of interdisciplinary scientific teams. Grant writing and budgeting for scientific discovery. Multi-lingual to operate in an international context. Supervision skills for communications and administrative staff. Special Instructions to Applicants: Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Employment Conditions This position is available November 2026, and will be filled as soon thereafter as an acceptable applicant is available. Compensation is commensurate with the education, experience, and qualifications of the selected applicant. Nominations Nominations are welcome. Nominations need to include the complete name and address of the nominee. This information should be sent to: Please refer to Requisition # 541611 Dr. Felix Enciso Chair, Search and Screen Committee University of Florida Horticultural Sciences Department 2560 Hull Road, bldg. 0906 Gainesville, FL 32611 Electronic Mail: fencisorodriguez@ufl.edu Application Information Individuals wishing to apply should go online to Careers at UF and submit: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for three individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Unofficial transcripts Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541612/ops-clinical-service-representative": {
      "title": "OPS Clinical Service Representative",
      "description": "OPS Clinical Service Representative Apply now Job no: 541612 Work type: Temp Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 04030100 - SL-COUNSELING & WELLNESS CTR Classification Title: OPS Clinical Service Representative Classification Minimum Requirements: N/A Job Description: This position is located in the Counseling & Wellness Center. This support person must be able to deal with highly varied and detailed tasks and have a high degree of organizational skills, the ability to interact with many areas in the Division of Student Life, and to communicate well with others from within and outside of the Counseling & Wellness Center. This position is full-time, 40 hours per week, however, we are considering part-time as well. Customer Service – answers incoming calls from clients, counselors, physicians, attorneys, pharmacies and other health care providers (internal and external). Responds to clients, counselors, physicians, other health care providers (internal and external), attorneys and pharmacies directly within scope of position. Routes calls to appropriate mental health professional, administrator or office personnel. Point of Service Operations – Responsible for client check in. Greets clients, schedules and/or confirms appointments. Determine client eligibility for services. Provides HIPAA information as necessary. Directs clients to complete paperwork or pay fees if appropriate. Assists clients with questions or concerns. Acts as witness for documentation needing signatures. Arranged Care – Schedules client appointments and consultations. Refers scheduling to CSRII, CSRIII or On-Call provider when appropriate. Obtains initial patient demographic information. Integrated Care – Inspects intake documentation for emergent concerns and refers to appropriate provider when necessary. Special Projects – employee will periodically be assigned special projects or other duties by supervisor. Expected Salary: $17/hour Required Qualifications High school diploma or equivalent Preferred: Should be experienced in medical clinic appointment scheduling and medical records systems, UF ISIS student records system, Microsoft Office programs. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with others. Ability to organize and maintain accurate files. Ability to communicate effectively verbally and in writing. Ability to triage phone calls, take accurate messages and page counselors as needed. This position requires ability to understand policies, procedures, and regulations related to patient. Ability to work with health care professionals and affiliated staff. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position, set to end in June 2027. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541616/academic-program-spec-ii": {
      "title": "Academic Program Spec II",
      "description": "Academic Program Spec II Apply now Job no: 541616 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services Department: 33160000 - HP-ENVIRONMENTAL GLOBAL HLTH Classification Title: Academic Program Spec II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: Student Program Coordination Responsible for all aspects of coordinating student programs, including Certificate, MHS, and PhD level programs. Assist students with questions, forms, and registration. Coordinate student registration for department-controlled and elective courses. Work with students to ensure proper, up-to-date forms are accurate; schedule appointments with academic advisors after forms have been reviewed. Serve as liaison between curriculum committee, academic advisor, and research mentor on student records, registration, and sensitive student issues. Responsible for understanding, interpreting, updating, and communicating the student handbook; provide accurate, consistent information to students and assist faculty with handbook queries. Assist with procurement of course-related materials; complete course textbook registration. Assure syllabi are completed on a timely basis and distributed to registered students. Assist faculty in managing Canvas files and course shells. Triage inquiries to appropriate respondents. Establish and manage academic web pages for student information. Prompt students throughout each semester regarding internal and university-wide deadlines to ensure timely graduation. Maintain academic website accuracy and post academic announcements. Participate in student recruitment activities including organizing applicant folders, scheduling applicant reviews, inputting applicant information into departmental database, and providing Admissions Committee with summary data. Serve as note taker and coordinator for Curriculum, Admissions, and Program committees; perform action items from meeting minutes. Maintain and organize all records for MHS, PhD, and certificate students. Manage academic advertisement materials including brochures, digital content, and program announcements. Maintain deadline calendar and ensure faculty meet all class and student requirement deadlines. Create and advertise semester courses; obtain and schedule classrooms; maintain course matrix to ensure alignment of PhD, MPH, MHS, BPH and BHS courses taught by EGH faculty. Work closely with PhD students during their defense semester to ensure completion of all graduation steps. Establish and maintain accurate weekly view of core courses. Assist with and prompt faculty to complete UCC1 and UCC2 forms. Schedule orientations, student gatherings, and committee meetings. Assist Chair with assigning students to committees. Set up classrooms with AV equipment as needed. Meet yearly with PhD Program Director and PhD students individually to confirm students are on track. In coordination with the Department Administrator, monitor off-book program expenses and budget. MHS Program Coordination Connect with applicants to EGH programs; collect, review, and assist in admission decisions on application materials. Work with the Office of Admissions and Graduate School to admit students and complete petitions for challenging packages. Reach out to prospective and pending applicants to guide them through the application process. Oversee the EGH program inbox and respond to inquiries. Submit memos/petitions to the Office of the University Registrar (OUR) for student file updates. Contact current Capstone students monthly to ensure they are on track to graduate. Help organize and present at the annual new student orientation and annual Capstone meeting. Oversee online admission applications; compose and sign routine admissions and advisement correspondence. Assist incoming international students with visa or visa transfer requests in coordination with the Graduate School and International Center. Access and manage academic student system documents. Assist with graduate student appointment/stipend letters; submit approved credit transfer information to the Graduate School; update committee members; assist Department Administrator with Graduate Faculty roster maintenance. Manage all student files for EGH academic programs. Provide accurate, timely information regarding student enrollment, dissertation committees, TA duties, etc. Securely maintain all student information in organized folders. Guide students through departmental and university policies regarding records and file access. Work with the Chair to execute the MHS strategic enrollment plan through outreach and dissemination, including researching recruitment avenues, attending recruitment events, and collaborating on marketing materials. Conduct exit interviews. Prepare and present annual MHS program reports including enrollment metrics and marketing/recruitment evaluation. Refine Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) and policies in response to program growth. Department Support Serve as the primary face of the department, greeting, assisting, and referring visitors and students as appropriate. Route calls, take messages, triage routine requests, and make appointments as needed. Assist with website content, including articles, photos, and periodic auditing for accuracy. Provide assistance to the Administrative Specialist II when necessary to organize and conduct events such as faculty retreats, luncheons, dinners, and gatherings, including but not limited to catering orders, meeting set-up, and clean-up. Provide a welcoming, professional atmosphere to students, faculty, staff, visitors, and community members. Serve as point of contact for visiting speakers and scholars, including communications and drafting of posters, flyers, and other printed or digital materials. Coordinate transitional onboarding for new hires including interview scheduling, IRB coordination, and HR liaison activities. (Note: Faculty candidate travel logistics and itinerary coordination now primarily managed by Administrative Specialist II, Position #00036332 [5]. This position retains academic-facing onboarding coordination.) Staff Mentorship Mentor support staff as needed or requested by the Chair. Provide guidance related to workflow, communications, processes, and procedures. Direct activities, train, assign, and monitor work. Respond to inquiries and resolve problems. Other duties as assigned Expected Salary: $68,000 - $75,000 annually; commensurate with education and experience. The University of Florida (UF) offers an exceptional benefits package, including health, dental and vision insurance; state retirement plans; a generous leave program; and tuition assistance. UF is a Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) eligible employer. For more information on benefits, please visit UF HR Benefits and Rewards at https://benefits.hr.ufl.edu/ . Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Experience working with students, faculty, or the public. Experience coordinating projects in an academic or research setting. Experience working with people from other cultures. Experience working with Canvas. Ability to maintain a high level of professionalism and integrity. Excellent communication, organizational, and computer skills. Experience with Microsoft Office Suite software, website development, and Adobe software products. Ability to work independently, maintain confidentiality, and exercise good judgment. Experience working in a research or academic environment. Willingness and ability to learn new tasks, problem solve and maintain a high level of attention to detail across all deliverables. Ability to remain calm and professional under pressure while adhering to departmental hierarchy and established protocols. Ability to interact with faculty, staff, and visitors in person, via written communications, and on the telephone, with professionalism and friendliness. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. If an accommodation is needed to apply for this position, please call 352/392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800/955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the U.S. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
