    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541317/simulation-technician-i-anaclerio-learning-center-alac": {
      "title": "Simulation Technician I - Anaclerio Learning Center (ALAC)",
      "description": "Simulation Technician I - Anaclerio Learning Center (ALAC) Apply now Job no: 541317 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 29010806 - MD-ANACLERIO LEARNING CENTER Classification Title: Education/Training Specialist I Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience Job Description: The Simulation Technician I supports the Anaclerio Learning and Assessment Center (ALAC) by coordinating and assisting educational activities, including simulation and experiential learning events. This position provides professional support for training and development initiatives for the benefit of individual and organizational performance. About This Role Technical Operations Support faculty, staff, and students in the learning and assessment center and simulation center to achieve course objectives, including tours and simulation sessions for internal and external audiences. Operate simulators, audio/visual, computer, and other simulation-related equipment. Facilitate simulation case training, prop placement, moulage set up, and faculty training on simulation equipment. Assist with risk management and safety assurance, including routine checks of exam tables and simulation equipment, looking for safety hazards, and supporting risk management simulations. Serve as a back up to the ALAC Simulation Operations Specialist with recording of center activities through starting and monitoring equipment. Administrative Operations Assist with room scheduling for the Center for Experiential Learning and Simulation. Maintain proficiency in technology according to simulation industry standards of best practice through ongoing educational development. Monitor material and resource usages to promote cost efficiency and minimize waste. Assist in data collection, utilization reporting, educational research activities, and development of new courses or training initiatives as requested. Maintenance Perform maintenance of simulation equipment as needed, including preventative maintenance. Tracks inventory levels of CELS supplies and equipment, and submit requests for needed items to the Administrative Specialist. About the College of Medicine The University of Florida's College of Medicine is committed to advancing health through education, research, and patient care. With a focus on innovation and excellence, the college prepares future healthcare leaders through a rigorous curriculum that combines basic sciences with hands-on clinical experience. The College of Medicine emphasizes interdisciplinary collaboration and community engagement, fostering and environment where students, faculty, and staff work together to improve healthcare outcomes. The College is home to cutting-edge research initiatives and state-of-the-art facilities, providing an exceptional training ground for aspiring medical professionals. Dedicated to enhancing health care, the College of Medicine plays an impactful role in shaping the future of medicine. For more information about the College of Medicine and its programs, visit College of Medicine . We Offer Exceptional Benefits Low-cost State Health Plans : Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Life and Disability Insurance Generous Retirement Options to secure your future Comprehensive Paid Time Off Packages with 10+ paid holidays, paid family, sick and vacation leave Exceptional Personal and Professional Development Opportunities : Access to UF Training & Organizational Development programs, leadership development, LinkedIn Learning, and more Tuition Assistance through the UF Employee Education Program Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Eligible Employer Learn more about what we have to offer here ! Expected Salary: $23.50 - $24.75 per hour Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Basic Life Support should be obtained within 90 days of hire (based on course availability). Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: Bachelor's degree in technology or health-related field. Experience in simulation operations and training. At least one year of healthcare-based training experience, including but not limited to being part of an SP program, hospital, or EMS. Valid Basic Life Support card. Paramedic certification. Previous experience in instructing basic and/or advanced life support training. • Licensure in a health-related field highly preferred. Flexibility in schedule a plus. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume, and contact information for three professional references. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541324/hematologic-malignancy-transplant-cellular-therapy-hospitalist": {
      "title": "Hematologic Malignancy, Transplant & Cellular Therapy Hospitalist",
      "description": "Hematologic Malignancy, Transplant & Cellular Therapy Hospitalist Apply now Job no: 541324 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty, Non-Tenure-Track Faculty, Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29050603 - MD-HEMATOLOGY-ONCOLOGY-BMTU Classification Title: Hematologic Malignancy, Transplant & Cellular Therapy Hospitalist Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants must be board certified or board eligible in Hematology, Medical Oncology, or Hematology & Oncology and be eligible for a Florida Medical License or Medical Faculty Certificate. Job Description: The University of Florida Department of Medicine is seeking applications for a full or part time Hematologic Malignancy, Transplant and Cellular Therapy Hospitalist for a clinician track position in the Division of Hematology & Oncology at the Assistant Professor rank or higher. This position is designed for a board certified or board eligible hematologist. Applicants must be eligible for a Florida medical license. The successful candidate will provide inpatient hematology consultations, with a focus on the care of patients with hematologic malignancies and those undergoing transplant and cellular therapy. Responsibilities will include participation in the education of medical trainees, including medical students, residents, and Hematology & Oncology fellows, as well as engagement in quality and safety initiatives. The position can be individualized based on the candidate’s interests and career goals, with opportunities for leadership, education, quality improvement, and research. The UF Division of Hematology & Oncology faculty are closely engaged with and partner with the University of Florida Health Cancer Institute (UFHCI), the only NCI Designated Cancer Center based at a public university in Florida. UFHCI provides comprehensive cancer care within a collaborative, multidisciplinary environment and brings together expertise from across the University of Florida. The University of Florida is a leading public research university and a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities. UF is consistently recognized among the nation’s top public universities by U.S. News & World Report and has a nationally recognized research enterprise with more than $1.3 billion in annual research expenditures. Resources for professional development at the University of Florida Health Sciences Center include leadership, education, and research training through the UF Health Cancer Institute, Clinical and Translational Science Institute (CTSI), formal mentorship programs, and supported opportunities for teaching, quality improvement, and research. Foreign national candidates whose employment conditions meet federal and University requirements under an immigrant classification are eligible to apply. Tenure accruing and non-tenure accruing tracks are available. Rank and tenure status will be commensurate with qualifications. Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Applicants must be board certified or board eligible in Hematology, Medical Oncology, or Hematology & Oncology and be eligible for a Florida Medical License or Medical Faculty Certificate. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541425/assistantassociate-professor-of-artificial-intelligence-in-education": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Artificial Intelligence in Education",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Artificial Intelligence in Education Apply now Job no: 541425 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Grant or Research Administration, Artificial Intelligence Department: 18050000 - ED-STL SCHL-TEACH - LEARN Classification Title: AST/ASO Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates for Assistant Professor shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess equivalent qualifications based on professional experience. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Candidates for Associate Professor shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess professional qualifications above those equivalent to the highest degree relevant to their field. They are expected to have produced creative work, professional writing, or research and successful teaching or professional experience. A candidate for this rank must have a distinguished record of achievement beyond the assistant professor level. Job Description: The College of Education at the University of Florida seeks a 9-month assistant or associate tenure-track faculty with expertise in artificial intelligence (AI) in education. This position will be part of the Educational Technology or Research and Evaluation Methodology academic program and UF’s Institute for Advanced Learning Technologies . The faculty member will join a dynamic community of scholars and engage in collaborative work with colleagues across UF and beyond, including but not limited to: the AI 2 Center , t he UF Artificial Intelligence and Informatics Research Institute , Computer & Information Science & Engineering , Digital Worlds Institute , UF Libraries, McKnight Brain Institute , Neuroscience , Psychology/Cognitive Sciences , Florida Museum of Natural History , Harn Museum of Art and UF Online . Potential areas of expertise and scholarship include: Generative AI in teaching and learning AI and professional learning AI and educational assessment AI literacy for learning and instruction across formal and informal education contexts AI for inclusive and accessible education AI ethics and policy Other innovative areas of inquiry related to AI and other emerging learning technologies Responsibilities: Conduct scholarly activities commensurate with expectations of tenure-track faculty at an AAU research-intensive university Vigorously pursue external funding to enhance research and development Publish in respected refereed scholarly journals salient to the candidate’s line of research Present research at national and international conferences Advise, engage, and mentor graduate students in research and teaching activities Teach courses (face-to-face, blended, and/or online) Provide service to the program, school, college, university, and the profession ABOUT THE COLLEGE: Founded in 1906, the College of Education at the University of Florida has a long history of mission-driven excellence, working to advance research, prepare the next generation of educators and solve society’s most pressing challenges. The college is No. 2 in the nation among all Graduate Education Schools, according to U.S. News & World Report. In Online Graduate Education, the college has ranked No. 1 or No. 2 for the last 11 years. Among specialty rankings, both the Counseling (No. 2) and Special Education (No. 5) programs have maintained top-five designations for the past four years. In total, the college ranks top 20 in six program areas including Elementary Teacher Education (No. 12), Curriculum and Instruction (No. 17), Educational Administration (No. 19) and Higher Education (No. 19). Novel interdisciplinary research and public scholarship—in collaboration with other UF colleges, school districts, communities and education partners across the nation—are yielding powerful real-world results in teaching and learning. Last year, faculty researchers tallied more than $133.4 million in research expenditures supporting studies and projects addressing education's most challenging issues. To sustain the college's innovative edge and societal benefit, heightened emphasis on AI and emerging technologies, student experience and interdisciplinary research will effectively prepare tomorrow's leaders in all education disciplines. With both on-campus and online options, the college offers a variety of bachelor's and advanced degree programs and concentration areas within nine academic specialties. The college's educator preparation programs have been accredited by CAEP and NCATE since 1954. Follow us: @UF_COE (X), @uf_coe (Instagram), @university-of-florida-college-of-education (LinkedIn), and @UF.COE (Facebook) ABOUT THE UNIVERSITY: The University of Florida, founded in 1853, is a public land-grant, sea-grant and space-grant research university and one of the most academically diverse universities in the nation. U.S. News & World Report ranks UF No. 7 among public universities. National rankings such as No. 1 for Online Bachelor’s by U.S. News and No. 1 by the Wall Street Journal are a result of UF's commitment to provide the highest-quality education at the best value. UF is a Carnegie R1 institution, home to 16 colleges and more than 200 research, service and education centers, bureaus and institutes. The university boasts more than 6,100 faculty members, 63,000 students and 525,000 living alumni. Faculty scholars last year generated more than $1.25 billion in research awards. UF recently expanded its $70 million AI partnership with NVIDIA, adding an additional $24 million investment to make AI accessible to students and faculty across campus and the state. The Florida Legislature designated UF as a \"preeminent'' state university in 2013. ABOUT THE AREA: UF is situated in Gainesville, a vibrant college town in North Central Florida that blends big-city cultural and athletic offerings with small-town friendliness and charm, along with excellent schools. Gainesville, located about halfway between the Atlantic Ocean and Gulf of America, is recognized as one of the nation’s best college towns and is easily accessible to three major metropolitan areas (Jacksonville, Orlando and Tampa). The National Arbor Day Foundation has recognized Gainesville as a \"Tree City USA\" every year since 1982. Livability ranked Gainesville as one of the Best Places to Live in the U.S. Add the allure of Florida's famous sunshine, tropical breezes, and abundant wilderness, springs, rivers and seashores nearby, and it is little wonder that top-caliber faculty and students find our university to be the perfect balance of premier academics and a fulfilling lifestyle. Expected Salary: Salary is commensurate with qualifications and experience. Required Qualifications: Doctorate in relevant academic discipline Expertise in AI associated with education and one or more of the areas mentioned above Record of scholarship as demonstrated through publications and presentations at national conferences (commensurate with experience) Record of relevant teaching experience with diverse populations (commensurate with experience) Technical skills related to area of expertise Ability to positively contribute to the degree programs within the UF Ed Tech or the Research and Evaluation Methodology program and to the advancement of IALT Evidence of effective teaching Strong knowledge of research methods Ability to positively contribute to national visibility, reputation, and success of UF Preferred: Evidence of seeking, securing, and successfully managing externally funded projects Evidence of effectively mentoring students Experience in teaching online and face-to-face classes Experience working in an interdisciplinary higher education environment Evidence of collaboration with faculty, students, communities, and other stakeholders Active engagement with professional organizations Ability to lead interdisciplinary R&D teams Evidence of outreach and dissemination of research via creative and non-traditional channels Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Procedures: Applicant should upload all materials to the University of Florida’s employment website https://jobs.ufl.edu. If technical assistance is needed, please contact Human resources at (352) 392-2477. Complete applications include: (1) a cover letter addressing required and desired qualifications, (2) curriculum vitae or resumé, (3) contact information (including email addresses) for three references, who after further review will be asked to send a letter of recommendation through the online application system. Applicants should reference requisition 541425, position number 00043953. Inquiries about the position can be addressed to the Chair of the Search Committee, Dr. Pasha Antonenko ( p.antonenko@coe.ufl.edu ). Review of candidates will begin November 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Applications received after November 1, 2026 may be considered at the discretion of the committee and hiring authority. The selection process will be conducted in accordance with the provisions of Florida’s “Government in the Sunshine” and Public Records laws. Search Committee meetings and interviews are open to the public and all applications, resumes, and other documents related to the search will be available for public inspection. Final candidates will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credentialing Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org . Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541481/maintenance-generalist-i": {
      "title": "Maintenance Generalist I",
      "description": "Maintenance Generalist I Apply now Job no: 541481 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 63571300 - FS-OPER FACILITIES-MNT MCH Classification Title: Maintenance Generalist I Classification Minimum Requirements: A high school diploma (or equivalent) and zero (0) to two (2) years of experience in the field or in a related area. Job Description: Assists and performs semi-skill and unskilled maintenance and repairs in accordance with industry/trade practices. Provides daily maintenance support to building users through responses to calls for service, scheduled preventative maintenance inspections and similar service. Performs tasks not limited to minor plumbing, electrical, HVAC repairs, changing filters, light bulbs, light carpentry tasks to include replacing ceiling tiles, patching and painting, flooring materials such as VCT, plank, and carpet tiles, stair treads, minor decorative maintenance and other tasks not requiring a licensed skilled trades person or journeyman. Performs scheduled preventative maintenance and unscheduled or special work. Paint walls and/or equipment and make minor painting repairs. Inspect all mechanical/electrical equipment in machine rooms, penthouses, and basements for proper operation. Assists in the operating or testing machinery or equipment to diagnose machine malfunctions. Assists in emergency repairs to pumps, motors, compressors, valves, plumbing pipes and fixtures, and all mechanical components. Enter maintenance data via the work order system for work requests (WR) and time. Provide support services to other trades (e.g., properly route or phase WR) as may be required for the timely and efficient repair of buildings and/or equipment. Obtain parts from Centrals Stores for assigned WR. Clean and maintain shop, work areas, and personal equipment. With oversight, monitor inventory, research and order supplies and parts. Provide support service to other trades, as may be required for timely and efficient repair of buildings and/or equipment. Read, interpret, and work from construction blueprints, renovation, and floor plan drawings. Diagnose mechanical problems and determine how to correct them, checking blueprints, repair manuals, or parts catalogs, as necessary. Promotes safety and environmental protection by immediately reporting unsafe situations, spills, and accidents. Follow University safety plans/procedures/protocols; and participating in appropriate safety training. Assists in building refurbishing to include new ceilings, walls, doorways, masonry work, case work installation and carpentry work. Inspects electric door operators, elevators, and reports any malfunctions to work management. Proof of a State of Florida Class E operator’s license required Criminal Background Check and post offer health assessment is required. NORMAL WORK SCHEDULE: 3 PM- 1:30 AM M-Thurs Total hours per week: 40 Emergencies as needed; swing shift, evening shift, weekends, after hours shift, on-call, etc., as required Expected Salary: $16.00 to $18.50 Hourly based on experience Required Qualifications: A high school diploma (or equivalent) and zero (0) to two (2) years of experience in the field or in a related area. Preferred: Experience in multiple trades. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541515/ast-dir-facilities-operations": {
      "title": "AST DIR, Facilities Operations",
      "description": "AST DIR, Facilities Operations Apply now Job no: 541515 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Executive/Director/Management, Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades Department: 63540100 - FS-OPER EG OPR ENGR-ADMIN Classification Title: AST DIR, Facilities Operations Classification Minimum Requirements: Master's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience. Job Description: Project and Program Management Provide engineering support to Facilities Services departments regarding asset performance, system upgrades, and infrastructure reliability. Assist in planning, scheduling, and executing system testing for reliability improvements. Working directly with the Facilities Services team and other department representatives, initiate the planning process by analyzing project requirements as it relates to architectural, mechanical, electrical and plumbing items. Support small/minor projects and deferred capital renewal projects by defining scope, creating work plans, and budget. Evaluate the appropriateness of project requirements, develop scope narratives, and recommend the most appropriate and cost-effective solution for completing the work, including meeting UF Standards. Provide engineering expertise to support Operations, Utilities, and PD&C. In consultation with others, recruit, interview and select vendors with appropriate skills for the project activities. Manage a facility inspection program, in partnership with FS Operations, to identify facility deficiencies and to assign timeframes and cost estimates for deficiency corrections. Develop and maintain a five (5) year capital plan for both buildings and utilities infrastructure. Design & Construction Oversight Perform engineering and design reviews of consultant and contractor work. Provide internal design reviews and construction inspections across capital and maintenance projects to ensure technical accuracy and standards compliance. Work closely with university stakeholders, external consultants, and contractors, to provide technical input into new construction, refurbishment, engineering, and capital renewal projects. Provide engineering guidance on systems design, technical specifications, lifecycle costings, and cost-in-use to ensure effective resource utilization and sustainability. Actively support the UF’s sustainability goals by promoting energy-efficient and low-carbon engineering solutions in all projects. Manage all activities via the University’s Computer-Aided Facilities Management systems, ensuring work is accurately scheduled, executed, and tracked. Coordinate closely with PD&C Design Teams on projects to ensure quality project outcomes. Design Standards & Policy Development Review, revise, and develop infrastructure and facility standards, including architectural, Utility, M/E/P, structural, and site design guidelines. Ensure uniformity and consistency in standards across Facilities Services departments. Develop documentation and procedures to ensure compliance with university requirements, industry best practices, and regulatory guidelines. Assists with identifying and developing possible process improvements for the Capital Improvements Program. Team Leadership and Performance Management Lead a multidisciplinary team including engineers and quality assurance personnel. Manage recruitment, development, scheduling, and performance reviews. Promote a culture of high standards, accountability, and continuous improvement. Works on developing continuous education requirements, in-field training needs, and supports overall program development for training of assigned staff. Works with various university departments and industry regulators on safety plans and documentation of safe work practices Must possess a Class E Driver’s License Criminal Background Check and PK Yonge Background check required. PE License Preferred NORMAL WORK SCHEDULE: Monday – Friday 8:00AM - 5:00PM Responds to emergencies as required. Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience Required Qualifications: Master's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience. Preferred: Bachelor’s degree in engineering, preferably in mechanical, electrical or civil engineering. Professional Engineer License in the State of Florida Proficient in the use of MS Office, MS Project, and pdf mark-up software Familiarity with Revit, AutoCad, and BIM Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541520/ops-student-assistant-graphic-designer": {
      "title": "OPS Student Assistant Graphic Designer",
      "description": "OPS Student Assistant Graphic Designer Apply now Job no: 541520 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Communications/Public Relations/Marketing, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services Department: 15010000 - DCP-DEAN'S OFFICE Classification Title: OPS Student Assistant Graphic Designer Classification Minimum Requirements: Currently enrolled as a University of Florida student. Experience using Adobe InDesign and Adobe Illustrator . Basic understanding of typography, layout, color, and graphic design principles. Attention to detail and ability to meet project deadlines. Job Description: Design signage, posters, banners, displays, and other print materials. Create and edit layouts using Adobe InDesign and Illustrator. Prepare artwork and files for printing and production. Assist with event materials, publications, promotional graphics, and other design projects. Make revisions based on feedback and follow established UF and DCP brand standards. Help organize design files and project assets. Up to 20 hours per week , with scheduling flexibility around classes. Expected Salary: $15/hr Required Qualifications: Currently enrolled as a University of Florida student. Experience using Adobe InDesign and Adobe Illustrator . Basic understanding of typography, layout, color, and graphic design principles. Attention to detail and ability to meet project deadlines. Preferred: Experience with Adobe Photoshop . Experience designing for print, signage, or large-format graphics. Familiarity with preparing files for professional printing. Students from graphic design, visual communication, advertising, architecture, digital arts, and related programs are encouraged to apply. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please provide at least five examples of your design work showing experience with Adobe InDesign and/or Illustrator. Examples may be submitted as a link to an online portfolio or as a PDF. Class projects, personal projects, freelance work, and professional work are all welcome. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541521/assistantassociate-professor-position-in-aibased-quantitative-research-method-in-education": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor Position in AI-Based Quantitative Research Method in Education",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor Position in AI-Based Quantitative Research Method in Education Apply now Job no: 541521 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Artificial Intelligence Department: 18070000 - ED-SHDOSE-SCHL OF HUM DEV&ORG Classification Title: AST/ASO PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates for Assistant Professor must hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess equivalent qualifications based on professional experience. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Candidates for Associate Professor shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess professional qualifications above those equivalent to the highest degree relevant to their field. They are expected to have produced creative work, professional writing, or research and successful teaching or professional experience. A candidate for this rank must have a distinguished record of achievement beyond the assistant professor level. Job Description: The University of Florida has pioneered the effort to integrate AI education in every major and every college. The College of Education seeks a tenure-track Assistant or Associate Professor in AI-based Quantitative Research Methods in Education . This faculty member will join the Research and Evaluation Methodology program (REM; https://education.ufl.edu/research-evaluation-methods ), a productive and collegial community of scholars committed to high-quality teaching, research, and service. In addition, this faculty member will have the opportunity to collaborate with researchers in a variety of units centered around AI and technology-enhanced learning, such as the Institute for Advanced Learning Technologies ( https://ialt.education.ufl.edu ). Successful applicants for the tenure-track Assistant or Associate Professor position are expected to develop and maintain a clear, systematic line of methodology-focused research; vigorously pursue external research funding; publish in top-tier methodological journals or proceedings; present research findings at meetings of professional associations; and contribute to advising and teaching graduate students. Additionally, candidates will engage in service to the program, school, college, university, and profession in general. The nine-month, tenure-accruing faculty appointment begins August 2027. Salary is negotiable, highly competitive, and commensurate with qualifications and experience. Potential areas of expertise and scholarship may include: AI-enhanced psychometrics, Computational Psychometrics, Digital-based assessment, and related areas Causal machine learning for designing and analyzing experimental and quasi-experimental studies Reinforcement Learning (RL) in educational research Natural Language Processing (NLP)-based methods in educational assessment, measurement, and quantitative analysis Issues of validity, reliability, and heterogeneous impacts in AI-enhanced quantitative methodology Integration of machine learning and Bayesian or frequentist statistical approaches Advancements to educational quantitative research methodology through AI Responsibilities: Conduct scholarly activities commensurate with expectations of a tenure-track assistant or associate faculty member at a research-intensive university Build a collaborative and diverse community of scholars among colleagues within and outside of the college, especially within UF’s AI research community Vigorously pursue external funding opportunities with the support of the College’s Office of Educational Research Publish in top-tier refereed methodological journals or proceedings Advise, engage, and mentor graduate students on research activities and theses and dissertations. Design and teach advanced methodological courses (face-to-face, blended, and/or online) for graduate students and, potentially, undergraduate students Provide service to the university and the profession ABOUT THE COLLEGE: The College of Education at the University of Florida, founded in 1906, has consistently been ranked No. 1 and No. 2 in the nation in Online Graduate Education for the last nine years, No. 2 nationally for undergraduate education and 6th nationally among public and private education schools (U.S. News & World Report). Three of the College's academic programs occupy top-20 spots in the U.S. News rankings: Counselor Education (1st), Special Education (4th), and Elementary Education (14th). Cutting-edge interdisciplinary research and public scholarship—often conducted in partnership with other UF colleges, school districts and communities across the state and nation—are yielding powerful learning systems and models that are helping to transform education in today’s changing world, starting with our youngest learners. Faculty researchers last year garnered more than $150.3 million in external awards supporting studies and projects addressing education’s most critical issues. With both on campus and online options, the college offers 29 bachelor’s and advanced degree programs and concentration areas, within nine academic specialties. To sustain the college's innovative edge, heightened emphasis on emerging technologies, student experience and interdisciplinary research will effectively prepare tomorrow's leaders in all education disciplines. The College's educator preparation programs have been continuously nationally accredited since 1954. Follow us: @UF_COE (X), @uf_coe (Instagram), @university-of-florida-college-of-education (LinkedIn), and @UF.COE (Facebook) ABOUT THE UNIVERSITY: The University of Florida, founded in 1853, is a public land-grant, sea-grant and space-grant research university and one of the most academically diverse universities in the nation. U.S. News & World Report ranks UF 7th among public universities. National rankings such as 1st by the Wall Street Journal and 4th by Forbes in their respective lists of Public Universities are a result of UF’s commitment to provide the highest quality education at the best value. UF is home to 16 colleges and more than 200 research, service and education centers, bureaus and institutes, and boasts more than 6,600 faculty members, 60,000 students and 405,000 living alumni. Faculty scholars last year generated more than $1.26 billion in research awards. UF recently launched a $70 million AI partnership with NVIDIA, taking the first step toward ensuring equitable access to bringing AI to students and faculty from across campus and the state. The Florida Legislature designated UF as a “preeminent'' state university in 2013. ABOUT THE AREA: UF is situated in Gainesville in North Central Florida, a vibrant college town that blends big-city cultural and athletic offerings with small-town friendliness and charm, along with excellent schools. Gainesville, located about halfway between the Atlantic Ocean and Gulf of Mexico, is often recognized as one of America’s best college towns and is easily accessible to three major metropolitan areas (Jacksonville, Orlando and Tampa). The National Arbor Day Foundation has recognized Gainesville as a “Tree City USA” every year since 1982. USA Today ranked Gainesville as the number one place to move in the Sunshine State. Add the allure of Florida’s famous sunshine, tropical breezes, and abundant wilderness, springs, rivers and seashores nearby, and it is little wonder that top-caliber faculty and students find our College and University to be the perfect balance of premier academics and a fulfilling lifestyle. Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, highly competitive, and commensurate with qualifications and experience. Required Qualifications: Doctorate in relevant academic discipline. ABD candidates will be considered if the degree is conferred prior to August 15, 2027. Record of scholarship as demonstrated through publications and presentations at national conferences (commensurate with experience) Preferred: Experience in teaching face-to-face classes as well as blended or online classes Evidence of success in proposing, securing, and managing externally-funded projects Evidence of commitment to collaboration with faculty and students Evidence of commitment to and experience in mentoring students Commitment to active engagement with and/or leadership in professional organizations of educational research methodology Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Procedures: Applicants for the faculty positions should send: (1) a letter of application addressing area and record of scholarship, philosophy and/or experiences of teaching, and other relevant information to the required and desired qualifications above, (2) curriculum vitae, (3) up to three sample publications, and (4) contact information for at least three references who may be contacted and asked to send a letter of recommendation through the online application system. Applicants should reference requisition 541521 . All candidates should submit application materials electronically at https://jobs.ufl.edu . Inquiries about the position can be addressed to Search Committee Chair Wei Li at wei.li@coe.ufl.edu . Review of applications will begin on November 8, 2026 . Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the search committee and hiring authority. If you need technical assistance, please contact Interfolio Scholar Services at 1-877-997-8807. The selection process will be conducted in accordance with the provisions of Florida’s “Government in the Sunshine” and Public Records laws. Search Committee meetings and interviews are open to the public and all applications, resumes, and other documents related to the search will be available for public inspection. Final candidates will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credentialing Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org . If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541531/director-school-of-landscape-architecture-and-planning-and-chair-department-of-landscape-architecture": {
      "title": "Director, School of Landscape Architecture and Planning and Chair, Department of Landscape Architecture",
      "description": "Director, School of Landscape Architecture and Planning and Chair, Department of Landscape Architecture Apply now Job no: 541531 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Executive/Director/Management Department: 15040000 - DCP-LANDSCAPE ARCHITECTURE Classification Title: SCH DIR & PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Terminal degree in landscape architecture or some combination of professional qualification in landscape architecture (BLA, licensure, etc.) and terminal degree in a related field. Examples of relevant degrees may include Doctor of Philosophy (PhD), Doctor of Design (DDes), Master of Landscape Architecture (MLA). Distinguished record in teaching, research or creative scholarship, and service sufficient to merit tenure upon hire at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor. Administrative experience including management of budgets, personnel and curriculum at the undergraduate or graduate levels. Demonstrated record of: advancing the knowledge of landscape architecture and its evolving role in society and effectively communicating its value within government, industry, and academic sectors; commitment to undergraduate and graduate education, engagement with students and promotion of student success; commitment to faculty development and mentoring; and commitment to collaborative working across disciplines. Job Description: The University of Florida seeks a visionary leader and educator to serve as Director of the School of Landscape Architecture and Planning (SLA&P) and Chair of the Department of Landscape Architecture. Our mission is to prepare future practitioners and scholars committed to advancing the efficacy, impact, and knowledge of landscape architecture and planning, to engage in research and scholarship that enhances the understanding and practice of the professions while addressing societal challenges, and to provide service to the communities of our state, region and abroad. We invite applications from candidates who align with our mission, are passionate about collaboratively leading students and faculty, are committed to research and creative scholarship, and who operate with the highest levels of ethical standards and integrity. This twelve-month appointment is eligible for tenure-upon-hire and is available starting July 1st, 2027. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Education and Experience Required Qualifications: Terminal degree in landscape architecture or some combination of professional qualification in landscape architecture (BLA, licensure, etc.) and terminal degree in a related field. Examples of relevant degrees may include Doctor of Philosophy (PhD), Doctor of Design (DDes), Master of Landscape Architecture (MLA). Distinguished record in teaching, research or creative scholarship, and service sufficient to merit tenure upon hire at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor. Administrative experience including management of budgets, personnel and curriculum at the undergraduate or graduate levels. Demonstrated record of: advancing the knowledge of landscape architecture and its evolving role in society and effectively communicating its value within government, industry, and academic sectors; commitment to undergraduate and graduate education, engagement with students and promotion of student success; commitment to faculty development and mentoring; and commitment to collaborative working across disciplines. Preferred: Ph.D. or licensure in landscape architecture or a related field Evidence of collaborative leadership Experience with professional program accreditation at undergraduate or graduate levels Experience establishing and implementing a strategic plan with clear goals and outcomes Experience working with intercollegiate, interdisciplinary, and international partnerships Experience recruiting, mentoring, and retaining outstanding faculty and staff Experience with student recruitment and retention Experience in fundraising and cultivating donor relationships Experience working with advisory boards and maintaining relationships with alumni Experience with extramural funding for research or creative scholarship Experience leading and/or actively participating in the field’s professional and academic organizations Special Instructions to Applicants: Required Submissions: Letter of interest that details how the applicant’s experience meets the required and desired qualifications for this position; Curriculum vitae, including relevant administrative experience; Names and contact information for three (3) references. References will not be contacted until after consulting with candidates. Interested applicants must apply online at https://jobs.ufl.edu. The job number is 541531. Applications will be reviewed beginning October 25, 2026, and the position will remain open until filled. Inquiries: Inquiries should be directed to Dr. Ravi Srinivasan, Search Committee Chair (sravi @ufl.edu). The University of Florida is an equal Opportunity Employer. To apply for this position, visit jobs.ufl.edu. Job number for this vacancy is 541531 and the deadline date to apply is October 25, 2026. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. Further information is available at http://www.dcp.ufl.edu. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541537/director-of-the-me-rinker-sr-school-of-construction-management": {
      "title": "Director of the M.E. Rinker, Sr. School of Construction Management",
      "description": "Director of the M.E. Rinker, Sr. School of Construction Management Apply now Job no: 541537 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Executive/Director/Management Department: 15030000 - DCP-RINKER SCH OF CONSTR MGMT Classification Title: SCH DIR & PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: An earned doctorate in construction management, construction engineering, civil engineering, architectural engineering or closely related discipline, and a record commensurate of appointment as a tenured Associate Professor or Professor at UF; Established record of achievement in scholarship, education and professional service in the field of construction management or allied disciplines. Demonstrated success in obtaining funding for scholarly research, growing industry partnerships, and securing other forms of revenue generation; A strong record of leadership and administrative experience in an academic institution, or comparable organization. Job Description: The Director is responsible for leading a growing group of 28 faculty, an undergraduate program of 400 students, a graduate program of 90 students offering both Masters and Doctorate degrees, and three well-established research centers. The Director is expected to establish a long-term vision for the School, informed by an understanding of the evolving future of the profession and industry, as well as seek out new opportunities for outreach and impact at the state, national and international levels. The Director is responsible for making sound administrative, budgetary and personnel decisions within the School. The Director mentors and evaluates faculty and staff, as well as facilitates a collegial and productive work environment. The Director works directly with the School’s Industry Advisory Board to build and sustain ongoing relationships with alumni and the professional construction community. The Director participates in fundraising efforts, in coordination with the College of Design, Construction and Planning and the University of Florida Foundation, to grow and manage the School’s substantial endowment. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Education and Experience. Required Qualifications: • An earned doctorate in construction management, construction engineering, civil engineering, architectural engineering or closely related discipline, and a record commensurate of appointment as a tenured Associate Professor or Professor at UF; • Established record of achievement in scholarship, education and professional service in the field of construction management or allied disciplines; • Demonstrated success in obtaining funding for scholarly research, growing industry partnerships, and securing other forms of revenue generation; • A strong record of leadership and administrative experience in an academic institution, or comparable organization. Preferred: Demonstrated commitment to excellence in undergraduate and graduate education; Excellent oral and written communication skills in both academic and professional settings; Three to five years of experience in a supervisory role in the industry. Special Instructions to Applicants: All applicants should apply at https://jobs.ufl.edu using requisition number 541537. Application packages must include the following: (1) cover letter, (2) curriculum vitae, (3) a statement of leadership philosophy and administrative vision for the School, (4) a statement of research interests, (5) a statement of teaching philosophy, and (6) the names, addresses and telephone numbers of three professional references (including one reference from industry). Review of applications will begin November 20, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Questions about the position may be directed to Prof. Nancy Clark (Chair of the Search Committee): nmclark@ufl.edu or (352)294-1455. To apply for this position, visit jobs.ufl.edu. Job number for this vacancy is 541537 and the deadline date to apply is November 20, 2026. If accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. Further information is available at http://www.dcp.ufl.edu. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541538/assistant-directorassistant-instructional-professor": {
      "title": "Assistant Director/Assistant Instructional Professor",
      "description": "Assistant Director/Assistant Instructional Professor Apply now Job no: 541538 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Humanities Department: 16280000 - LS-HISTORY Classification Title: Lecturer Job Description: The Department of History in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences at the University of Florida invites applications for a full-time (1FTE), nine-month, non-tenure-accruing position in United States history and Assistant Director of the Samuel Proctor Oral History Program (SPOHP), at the rank of Lecturer, with the working title of Assistant Director/Assistant Instructional Professor. The appointment will begin on August 16, 2027. The successful candidate will play a key leadership role in advancing SPOHP’s mission of teaching, training, research, and public engagement. Working closely with faculty, staff, and students, the Assistant Director will help foster student development through experiential learning opportunities and provide leadership in oral history training and project-based scholarship. The candidate will also contribute to the Department of History’s vibrant intellectual community and commitment to innovative, student-centered teaching. Drawing on expertise in oral history, public history, and United States history, the successful candidate will teach a 2-2 course load that includes courses in oral history methodology, public history, and areas of U.S. history that complement SPOHP’s mission and support the curricular needs of the Department. Teaching responsibilities should emphasize active, hands-on learning and engage students in oral history research, community partnerships, digital and public humanities initiatives, and other forms of applied historical practice. As Assistant Director, the successful candidate will oversee SPOHP’s internship program, including intern recruitment, training, mentorship, and supervision. The Assistant Director will also develop and lead oral history workshops for students and public audiences; help coordinate public programs, outreach, and communications; and contribute to the development and implementation of grant- and contract-funded projects as opportunities arise. The position will work closely with the SPOHP Director, program staff, faculty, and campus and community partners. The Samuel Proctor Oral History Program is an award-winning, nationally respected program that has been a cornerstone of the University of Florida campus for six decades. SPOHP advances oral history research, teaching, and public engagement; provides significant experiential-learning opportunities for undergraduate and graduate students; and stewards a collection of more than 10,000 oral histories and sound recordings. The Assistant Director will join a professional staff of five, along with undergraduate and graduate assistants, and will play a significant role in the program’s next chapter. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to benefit from the support, resources, and activities of relevant programs across the University of Florida, including the Center for the Humanities and the Public Sphere, UF Libraries, Beyond120, the Gulf Scholars Program, and other interdisciplinary and experiential-learning initiatives. Applicants are encouraged to visit the program website (Samuel Proctor Oral History Program) and departmental website (Department of History) to learn more about SPOHP and the Department of History. Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience. The University of Florida, a top-ranked public research institution, offers a comprehensive and competitive benefits package to support employees’ health, financial security, and work-life balance. • Comprehensive medical, dental, vision, and prescription coverage. • Life insurance, long-term disability, accident, and illness insurance. • Retirement savings and investment options with employer contributions. • Generous paid time includes vacation, sick leave, annual holidays, personal holidays, and winter break. • 8 weeks of paid parental and medical leave. • Access to wellness programs, flexible spending accounts, and an employee assistance program. • Discounts for on-campus and off-campus activities and services. To learn more, click on the link: https://hr.ufl.edu/benefits Required Qualifications: • PhD in History or a closely related field. • Previous experience in teaching, training, or mentoring at the undergraduate, graduate, or community level. • Conducting, managing, or supporting oral history projects, including the collection, interpretation, preservation or public presentation. • Working collaboratively with students, faculty, staff, and community partners. • Organization and administrative skills, including the ability to manage projects, coordinate activities, and meet deadlines. Preferred: • Demonstrated record of effective college or university level teaching in oral history, public history, United States history, or related fields, particularly using experiential or community-engaged pedagogy. • Experience developing public humanities programming or public-facing oral history projects, including events, exhibits, digital projects, or other forms of interpretation. • Experience designing and leading oral history workshops or hands-on training for students, educators, or community participants. • Experience collaborating across academic disciplines or with libraries, cultural organizations, schools, historical societies, or community groups. • Experience supporting communications and outreach for an academic, cultural, or public humanities program. • Experience developing or implementing grant- or contract-funded projects, including proposal preparation, project coordination, or reporting. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: • Letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position • A complete curriculum vitae • Names and contact information for three professional references (the search committee will request confidential letters of recommendation only from candidates selected for its long list). Additional materials may be requested from finalists. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Review of applications will begin on November 16, 2026, and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. All inquiries may be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Jamin Wells at jamin.wells@ufl.edu . All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541539/camera-pipe-inspection-tech-i": {
      "title": "Camera Pipe Inspection Tech I",
      "description": "Camera Pipe Inspection Tech I Apply now Job no: 541539 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 63700700 - FS-UTILITIES-WASTE WATER Classification Title: Engineering Technician I Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and one year of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: Camera Inspections for Sanitary system: Weekly inspections of campus sanitary system, including pipe network and manholes. Utilize sewer camera equipment to collect data, identify problem areas, and inspect areas to assist other groups or vendors performing work. Camera Inspections for Storm system: Weekly inspections of campus stormwater system, including pipe network, manholes, grates and drainage. Utilize sewer camera equipment to collect data, identify problem areas, and inspect areas to assist other groups or vendors performing work. Troubleshooting/Camera Maintenance: Be able to take care of and work through maintaining inspection camera and its components to ensure good video quality during inspections. Be able to make the appropriate repairs and replace parts as needed. Safety: Ensure the safety of themselves and other workers by following proper safety procedures, utilize equipment correctly, and wear appropriate personal protective equipment. Daily tasks include: Follow MOT procedures to ensure Campus safety for vehicle and pedestrian traffic. Determine and follow manhole entrance procedures (4 gas meter, tripod, confined space permit, etc.). Understand Biohazards and proper precautions to take when working around them (gloves, respirators, inoculations, etc.). Understand arc flash and electrical shock hazards, takes appropriate safety measures when working around electrical panels and equipment. Training: Begin OTJ training for operating inspection camera, MOT training, Complete NASSCO Pipeline Assessment Certification Program (PACP), Lateral Assessment Certification Program (LACP), and Manhole Assessment Certification Program (MACP) or equivalent pipeline assessment training. Requires Class E Drivers’ License. Required to follow and adhere to all Facilities Services Policies and Directives. Criminal background check and physical examination required. NORMAL WORK SCHEDULE: 7:00 AM – 3:30 PM (Monday through Friday). Workdays and shift times may vary in response to customer needs for coverage and/or departmental needs. Occasionally holiday and weekend work required. Total hours per week is 40. Management retains the right and discretion to modify schedules, building assignments, shifts and/or days for operational purposes. Position is considered as Essential Personnel as defined by UF Policy. Expected Salary: $19.25 to $24.00 Hourly based on experience Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and one year of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: EDUCATION & TRAINING: mechanical, control systems, electrical KNOWLEDGE OF: wastewater, wastewater collections systems, or water distribution SKILLED IN: troubleshooting, service, SCADA systems ABILITY TO: -Ability to plan, organize, and coordinate work assignments -Ability to communicate effectively -Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with others Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541542/assistant-professor-in-voice-mezzo-soprano-or-tenor": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Voice (Mezzo Soprano or Tenor)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Voice (Mezzo Soprano or Tenor) Apply now Job no: 541542 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Humanities Department: 13030100 - COTA-MUSIC-DIRECTOR Classification Title: AST PROF Classification Minimum Requirements Candidates for this rank shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess equivalent qualifications based on professional experience. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Job Description: Assistant Professor in Voice (Mezzo Soprano or Tenor) Position: Full-time, 9-month, tenure accruing faculty position Date of Expected Hire: August 16, 2027 Salary: $61,000-$63,000 with a comprehensive and highly competitive leave and benefits package The School of Music in the College of the Arts invites applicants for a tenure-track position as an Assistant Professor in Voice. We seek an accomplished singer with national and/or international visibility who will contribute to the continued success and growth of our BM, BA, MM, and DMA programs in voice. The successful candidate will add their dynamic personality and enthusiasm for teaching to our School’s existing community of outstanding scholars and musicians. This position will be filled by a mezzo soprano or tenor. The University of Florida College of the Arts intends to be a transformative community, responding to and generating paradigmatic shifts in the arts and beyond. As artists and scholars, we embrace the complexity of our evolving human experience and seek to empower our students and faculty to shape that experience fearlessly through critical study, creative practice, and provocation. We seek a colleague who identifies as a change-maker. We seek a colleague who will position emerging artists and researchers as catalysts for justice on local and global levels. This position is covered by the United Faculty of Florida Contract. You can view this employment union contract here https://admin.hr.ufl.edu/compliance/employee-relations-and-ethics/union-negotiations/united-faculty-of-florida-contract/ . Responsibilities: Recruit and teach undergraduate and graduate students in applied studio voice. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in related voice subjects. Maintain an active national and international profile in performance, research, and/or other creative activities. Engage deeply in our learning community, finding innovative ways to connect the arts to discourses in other disciplines. Participate in shared governance through service to the School, College and University. Contribute to the development and teaching of UF Quest 1 courses, which form a part of the general education requirement in the Humanities. ( https://quest.ufl.edu/ ) Other courses and service as needed and assigned by the unit director. SCHOOL OF MUSIC: Organized within the College of the Arts, the School of Music plays an important role in the academic and cultural life of the University, the College’s Creative Campus initiative and the community. The School has 40 full-time and nine adjunct faculty, serving some 200 graduate and 200 undergraduate music majors. Degree programs include B.S., B.A., B.M., M.M., D.M.A., and the Ph.D. in music education, composition, ethnomusicology, and historical musicology. Program emphases include performance, music education, theory, composition, music history and literature, ethnomusicology, conducting, and sacred music. The School of Music is also home to the UF Bands program, which includes “The Pride of the Sunshine” Gator Marching Band. School of Music facilities include the School of Music Building and Steinbrenner Band Hall, among others. The University of Florida is accredited by the Southern Association of Colleges and Schools Commission on Colleges and is an accredited institutional member of the National Association of Schools of Music. For more information, visit www.arts.ufl.edu/music . THE COLLEGE OF THE ARTS: The College of the Arts is an engine of transformation that generates paradigmatic shifts in the arts and beyond, while nurturing adaptability, innovation, and excellence. We foster an environment where novel creative practice, leading research, and experiential learning thrive. Our students, faculty, and staff interrogate and respond to the ever-changing relationship between technology and the human spirit. We prepare future leaders in artistic professions and creative industries, while developing student capacity for interdisciplinary scholarship that serves and engages local and global communities. The College offers baccalaureate, masters, and doctoral degrees. Approximately 1,600 students are pursuing majors in degrees offered by the College of the Arts under the direction of 150 faculty members in its three accredited schools— the School of Art + Art History, the School of Music, and the School of Theatre + Dance, and in the Center for Arts in Medicine, the Digital Worlds Institute, and the Center for Arts, Migration, and Entrepreneurship. In addition, the college comprises the University Galleries, and the University level of the New World School of the Arts in Miami. The University of Florida: The University of Florida is a comprehensive learning institution built on a land grant foundation. We are The Gator Nation, a community dedicated to excellence in education and research and shaping a better future for Florida, the nation and the world. Our mission is to enable our students to lead and influence the next generation and beyond for economic, cultural and societal benefit. UF is a graduate research institution with more than 50,000 students and membership in the prestigious Association of American Universities. Gainesville, which is consistently ranked as one of the nation’s most livable cities, is located midway between the Gulf of Mexico and the Atlantic Ocean. Together, the University and the community comprise the educational, medical and cultural center of North Central Florida. In addition to salary, the University of Florida (UF) offers low cost State Health plans, a number of Dental plans to fit you and your family’s needs, and Vision. Domestic partner coverage through GatorCare is also available. Optional plans such as life, disability, legal and accident insurance are also available. UF provides a variety of leave programs including vacation, sick leave, 11 paid holidays, personal leave days, and family medical leave. Nine-month faculty employees accrue approximately 13 sick days annually, and 4 personal leave days, typically used during the holiday closing period (accruals based on full-time employment). UF also provides you the flexibility to deal with life’s challenges by offering paid family leave, eight full weeks of leave over a 24-month period. Build a retirement roadmap with competitive pension plans, investment accounts and a host of voluntary add-ons, such as 457 deferred compensation and 403(b) plans. UF Employees are also eligible for the GatorPerks discount program , can provide savings at various businesses and retailers! Explore UF’s plethora of benefit options here: FACULTY Benefits Expected Salary: $61,000-$63,000 with a comprehensive and highly competitive leave and benefits package Required Qualifications: The successful candidate must meet all of the following Terminal degree in voice (ABD will be considered) OR a commensurate record of professional and academic achievement. Mezzo Soprano or Tenor. Documented record of achievement in the field at a national or international level. Demonstrated capacity for effective engagement in recruitment. Experience contributing to common Voice Area courses, including Diction, Literature, and Pedagogy. Evidence of a commitment to innovative and cross-disciplinary curricula. Evidence of skill in designing and facilitating academic work that advances curiosity, respect, open intellectual discourse, and the welcome incorporation and well-being of all in an environment of complex differences. Preferred Qualifications: The successful candidate will meet some of the following Doctoral degree in voice. Three to five years collegiate teaching experience beyond the graduate assistantship. Demonstrated record of teaching and/or programming varied styles of repertoire. Ability to teach across various vocal styles including classical, contemporary commercial music (CCM) and musical theater. Ability to assist with opera productions. Capacity for developing and contributing to innovative curricular programs, such as UF Quest. ( https://quest.ufl.edu/ ) Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Deadline: Review of applications will begin on November 2, 2026, and continue until an applicant pool is established. To ensure full consideration, all application documents must be submitted by November 1, 2026. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. Application Process: Applications must be submitted via the University of Florida’s online application system. Online applications must include the following (1) a detailed letter of application that explains how you match the particular qualifications of this position and how your work and perspective will contribute to and enhance our transformative community; (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) links to performance and teaching samples (Samples should be submitted online and be no longer than 15 minutes in length. Please note: No CDs will be accepted.). Candidates may also submit evidence of their research as appropriate. (4) names and contact information of three professional references. The Search Committee may request additional materials at a later time, including letters of reference. Inquiries may be sent to: Name: Megan M. Sheridan, PhD Chair, Assistant Professor in Voice Search Email: mmsheridan@ufl.edu The search committee does not utilize AI or other software to provide any level of applicant assessment. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES). If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call +1 (352) 392- 2477or the Florida Relay System at +1 (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541543/assistant-professor-in-printmaking-in-the-expanded-field": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Printmaking in the Expanded Field",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Printmaking in the Expanded Field Apply now Job no: 541543 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 13020100 - COTA-ART-DIRECTOR Classification Title: AST PROF Classification Minimum Requirements Candidates for this rank must hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess equivalent qualifications based on professional experience. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Job Description: Assistant Professor in Printmaking in the Expanded Field Position: Full-time, 9-month, tenure accruing faculty position Date of Expected Hire: August 16, 2027 Salary: $74,000 annually with a comprehensive and highly competitive leave and benefits package The School of Art + Art History in the College of the Arts seeks an Assistant Professor of Studio Art with expertise in printmaking in the expanded field to join our forward-looking interdisciplinary program at a top-ranked public university. We seek an innovative artist-educator to lead the development of printmaking curricula in the expanded field. The ideal candidate has an active studio practice that engages the complex materiality, history, and theories of printmaking, as well as emerging technologies and methods, and their creative research is informed by contemporary art theories and practice. They will have a strong knowledge of historical and contemporary reproductive and fabrication media that they present as a vibrant and relevant technology. The successful candidate will demonstrate a clear commitment to interdisciplinary artistic practices with an emphasis on printmaking, including DIY strategies, ‘maker tools,’ and historical and future-focused print methods and ideas. The University of Florida College of the Arts intends to be a transformative community, responding to and generating paradigmatic shifts in the arts and beyond. As artists and scholars, we embrace the complexity of our evolving human experience and seek to empower our students and faculty to shape that experience fearlessly through critical study, creative practice, and provocation. We seek a colleague who identifies as a change-maker. We seek a colleague who will position emerging artists and researchers as catalysts for justice on local and global levels. This position is covered by the United Faculty of Florida Contract. You can view this employment union contract here https://admin.hr.ufl.edu/compliance/employee-relations-and-ethics/union-negotiations/united-faculty-of-florida-contract/ . Responsibilities: Teach a 2/2 course load at the undergraduate and graduate levels in printmaking in the expanded field in an interdisciplinary studio art curriculum. Course assignments may also include Studio Art foundations and non-majors courses, including courses in the university’s General Education curriculum. Course assignments may also include interdisciplinary graduate courses, such as a seminar for first-year MFA in Art students or a team-taught critique seminar. Mentor and advise graduate students and supervise graduate committees. Maintain an active profile of creative research, consistent with the expectation of tenure and promotion Collaborate with staff in shared supervision of the printmaking facilities. Actively participate in our learning community and in shared governance through service to the school, college, university, and profession. SCHOOL OF ART + ART HISTORY: The School of Art + Art History (SAAH) believes that art, design, and scholarship are critical to the cultural life and creative advancement of our local, national, and international communities. We empower each of our 360 undergraduate and 130 graduate students with knowledge, skills, and curiosity to engage thoughtfully and courageously with our changing world. The school offers BA, BFA, MA, MFA, and PhD programs, both residential and online, and students can study art education, art history, curatorial studies, graphic design, design & visual communications, museology (museum studies), and studio art. The 4Most Gallery and the three University Galleries provide exhibition space for international contemporary art and student work. The school has facilities in three on-campus buildings, and three off-campus sites. SAAH is accredited by the National Association of Schools of Art and Design. The School of Art + Art History nurtures a culture of critical inquiry in our scholarly and creative work. The school has 38 full-time (including one director) and 11 part-time faculty, plus 11 staff members. The faculty publish, present, curate, and exhibit widely and internationally at the highest level. They bring their scholarship and creative work into their teaching, offering innovative learning experiences to their students. The school’s programs have a focus on experiential learning and research that engages with our global and local communities. The school’s four galleries serve as a laboratory where students can participate in the lifecycle of the work of art, as artists who display their work, as curators who organize exhibitions, and as historians and critics who articulate what they see. The galleries hosted 26 exhibitions last academic year with dozens of additional exhibitions displayed in other school spaces. The school’s programs in art and design view their work as interdisciplinary and often collaborative with other disciplines across campus. The school’s innovative and growing design program aims to use the State of Florida as a laboratory to develop solutions to real-world problems. The endowed Harn Eminent Scholar in Art History Chair and partially endowed Visiting Artist Series sponsored lectures, masterclasses, and studio visits from 20 artists, scholars, and designers last year, with an additional 30+ paying virtual visits to SAAH classrooms. The annual SAAH New York and Museum Studies trips connect students to alumni and other industry professionals who are thriving in their careers. THE COLLEGE OF THE ARTS: The College of the Arts is an engine of transformation that generates paradigmatic shifts in the arts and beyond, while nurturing adaptability, innovation, and excellence. We foster an environment where novel creative practice, leading research, and experiential learning thrive. Our students, faculty, and staff interrogate and respond to the ever-changing relationship between technology and the human spirit. We prepare future leaders in artistic professions and creative industries, while developing student capacity for interdisciplinary scholarship that serves and engages local and global communities. The College offers baccalaureate, masters, and doctoral degrees. Approximately 1,600 students are pursuing majors in degrees offered by the College of the Arts under the direction of 150 faculty members in its three accredited schools— the School of Art + Art History, the School of Music, and the School of Theatre + Dance, and in the Center for Arts in Medicine, the Digital Worlds Institute, and the Center for Arts, Migration, and Entrepreneurship. In addition, the college comprises the University Galleries, and the University level of the New World School of the Arts in Miami. The University of Florida: The University of Florida is a comprehensive learning institution built on a land grant foundation. We are The Gator Nation, a diverse community dedicated to excellence in education and research and shaping a better future for Florida, the nation and the world. Our mission is to enable our students to lead and influence the next generation and beyond for economic, cultural and societal benefit. UF is a graduate research institution with more than 50,000 students and membership in the prestigious Association of American Universities. Gainesville, which is consistently ranked as one of the nation’s most livable cities, is located midway between the Gulf of Mexico and the Atlantic Ocean. Together, the University and the community comprise the educational, medical and cultural center of North Central Florida. In addition to salary, the University of Florida (UF) offers low cost State Health plans, a number of Dental plans to fit you and your family’s needs, and Vision. Domestic partner coverage through GatorCare is also available. Optional plans such as life, disability, legal and accident insurance are also available. UF provides a variety of leave programs including vacation, sick leave, 11 paid holidays, personal leave days, and family medical leave. Nine-month faculty employees accrue approximately 13 sick days annually, and 4 personal leave days, typically used during the holiday closing period (accruals based on full-time employment). UF also provides you the flexibility to deal with life’s challenges by offering paid family leave, eight full weeks of leave over a 24-month period. Build a retirement roadmap with competitive pension plans, investment accounts and a host of voluntary add-ons, such as 457 deferred compensation and 403(b) plans. UF Employees are also eligible for the GatorPerks discount program , which can provide savings at various business and retailers! Explore UF’s plethora of benefit options here: FACULTY Benefits Expected Salary: $74,000 annually with a comprehensive and highly competitive leave and benefits package Required Qualifications: The successful candidate must meet all of the following MFA or PhD in Studio Art Practice with an emphasis in printmaking Relevant teaching or instructional experience beyond graduate teaching assistantships Active studio practice and record of creative research with evidence of emerging or established recognition in the field, commensurate with experience Understanding of and an ability to respond to the legacies, histories and theories of printmaking Evidence of capacity to collaborate with faculty, students, and staff Understanding of the materials and processes of printmaking Evidence of skill in designing and facilitating academic work that advances curiosity, respect, open intellectual discourse, and the welcome incorporation and well-being of all in an environment of complex differences. Preferred The successful candidate will meet some of the following Full-time college and/or graduate-level teaching experience beyond graduate assistantship Evidence of success in teaching at the undergraduate level, including foundations courses Evidence of success in mentoring undergraduate and graduate students Broad and detailed understanding of the materials and processes of printmaking and related reproductive and fabrication media, including technologies such as digital fabrication Evidence of innovative pedagogy Capacity to advance interdisciplinary collaborations in teaching and research Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Deadline: Review of applications will begin December 4, 2026, and continue until an applicant pool is established. To ensure full consideration, all application documents must be submitted by December 4, 2026. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. Application Process: Applications must be submitted via the University of Florida’s online application system. Online applications must include the following: a detailed letter of application that explains how you match the qualifications of this position and how your work and perspective will contribute to and enhance our transformative community curriculum vitae links to an online portfolio or single PDF of 15-20 each of personal and student work statement of teaching philosophy names and contact information for three professional references, a fourth administrative or technical staff member reference is welcome but not required. The search committee will notify the applicant before contacting references. We are not requiring letters of reference with initial application materials. Please ignore that option. The Search Committee may request additional materials at a later time. Inquiries may be sent to: Julia Morrisroe Chair, Assistant Professor of Ceramics in the Expanded Field Search Email: julia01@ufl.edu The search committee does not utilize AI or other software to provide any level of applicant assessment. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES). If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call +1 (352) 392- 2477or the Florida Relay System at +1 (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541544/assistant-professor-in-ceramics-in-the-expanded-field": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Ceramics in the Expanded Field",
