    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541545/administrative-spec-ii": {
      "title": "Administrative Spec II",
      "description": "Administrative Spec II Apply now Job no: 541545 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Grant or Research Administration Department: 02080130 - PV-UF PROMISE Classification Title: Administrative Spec II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Administrative Specialist II supports the administrative and operational functions in a student support office environment. Serving as a liaison, the position ensures operational efficiency, regulatory compliance, and administrative support for the Director and department. Key Responsibilities Include: Supervision & Staff Leadership: Direct, train, and schedule undergraduate student assistants and student desk personnel; evaluate performance and manage daily front-office customer service and lobby management workflows. Federal Grant Activities: Coordinate pre- and post-award federal grant activities, monitor sponsored research funding, track grant deliverables and expenditure compliance, and review budget reconciliation for accuracy. Compliance, Safety & HR Initiatives: Oversee unit compliance with University, state, and federal privacy/security rules (including FERPA); act as the Building Emergency Coordinator; compiles information for submission the Provost Business Office for processing/approvals. Central Unit Collaboration & Fiscal Oversight: Serve as primary operational liaison to the central Unit Administrative team (Provost Business Office); compiles information in UFGO for approval and vendor purchases, and supports monthly departmental budget monitoring. Departmental Operations & Executive Support: Provide administrative support to the OAS Director, maintain operational procedure manuals, oversee data reporting (Enterprise Analytics), and manage special administrative projects. Expected Salary: $53,000 - $56,000 annually; commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Prior experience supporting federal grant pre- and post-award processes and monitoring sponsored research accounts. Demonstrated experience supervising student employees/staff or administrative support personnel in a higher education setting. Strong working knowledge of University of Florida administrative systems, including myUFL, myUF Marketplace, UFGO, UFirst, Enterprise Analytics, and Salesforce software. Familiarity with FERPA regulations, and workplace safety compliance standards. Strong proficiency in desktop productivity, collaboration, and visual design tools, specifically Canva, Google Docs and Sheets, Microsoft Excel, Word, PowerPoint, and Microsoft Teams. Professional written and oral communication skills, with demonstrated ability to articulate complex policies, prepare analytical reports, and communicate effectively with university stakeholders. Proven ability to exercise independent judgment, manage multiple priorities, and work/collaborate effectively with central administrative business units. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, applicants should include the following: 1. A detailed cover letter outlining your relevant experience in federal grant administration, personnel supervision, and compliance oversight. 2. A current resume or curriculum vitae. 3. Contact information for three professional references. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541546/stas-receptionist": {
      "title": "STAS Receptionist",
      "description": "STAS Receptionist Apply now Job no: 541546 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services Department: 19140000 - EG-COMPUTER - INFO SCI & ENG Classification Title: STAS Front Desk Receptionist Classification Minimum Requirements: High School Diploma or equivalent. Dependable, punctual, and able to maintain a consistent work schedule. Current enrollment as an undergrad student in good academic standing. Must have the availability to work Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday afternoons 12:30-4:30. Job Description: Serves as the primary front-desk contact for the CISE administrative office, managing phones, greeting visitors, and responding to general inquiries. Oversees daily reception operations, including message handling, mail and package distribution, and maintaining an organized and professional front-desk area. Provides administrative and logistical support for departmental activities by assisting with events, creating flyers, maintaining conference rooms, managing office supplies, and supporting faculty and staff with office equipment. Maintains accurate directories and mail assignments, monitors and restocks supplies, and assists with departmental asset tracking as needed $15 per hour Required Qualifications: High School Diploma or equivalent. Dependable, punctual, and able to maintain a consistent work schedule. Current enrollment as an undergrad student in good academic standing. Must have the availability to work Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday afternoons 12:30-4:30. Preferred: Strong verbal and written communication skills, with the ability to interact professionally with students, faculty, staff, and visitors. Demonstrated reliability, punctuality, and ability to follow established procedures. Basic computer proficiency, including familiarity with Microsoft Office. Proficiency with basic office technology, including phones, printers, copiers and scanners. Ability to work independently while also collaborating effectively with administrative staff. Special Instructions to Applicants: Must have availability Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday afternoons 12:30-4:30 Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 29 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541548/postdoctoral-associate": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate Apply now Job no: 541548 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Engineering, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 19340000 - EG-BIOMEDICAL ENGINEERING Classification Title: Postdoctoral Associate Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants are expected to have a Ph.D. in a field such as veterinary sciences, biomedical sciences, engineering, or neuroscience. Other degree fields will also be considered if the applicant has skill sets that complement our research team. Applicants must have experience with manuscript preparation and submission and must be comfortable working with large animals (training will be provided upon hire). Applicants must also demonstrate the ability and willingness to mentor and manage graduate students. Experience with grant writing and prior formal mentoring of students is desirable. Job Description: The Orthopaedic Biomedical Engineering (OrthoBME) Laboratory is seeking a post-doctoral fellow to help lead an observational clinical study on osteoarthritic pain in veterinary equine populations. Osteoarthritis (OA) is common in horses, and like human OA, equine OA is often idiopathic, develops over years, and shows a poor match between the severity of joint damage and the severity of symptoms. These parallels make naturally-occurring equine OA a powerful population for developing pain measures that can translate to humans. As such, our study is being conducted entirely in privately-owned equine patients with naturally occurring fetlock OA, where the overall goal of the study is to determine the best and most reliable measures of naturally-occurring OA pain in horses. Following enrollment visits conducted by the UF College of Veterinary Medicine, our assessment team travels to the equine patient (farms and ranches near Gainesville) and then performs on-site measurements of neural processing, autonomic nervous system function, activity, and gait mechanics, among other physiologic tests. We are seeking a post-doctoral fellow to join this NIH-funded project, helping our team establish reliable and valid behavioral measures of OA pain in horses. Using psychometric statistical approaches, our team will then evaluate the reliability and validity of these measures and compare them with data from ongoing human OA pain studies at the UF Pain Research and Intervention Center of Excellence. Again, testing is conducted at the horses' housing sites in the Gainesville-Ocala area, in partnership with the UF College of Veterinary Medicine. The OrthoBME lab is focused on understanding how chronic joint degeneration leads to chronic pain and disability. To achieve this goal, our team works with samples and data collected from rodent models, large animal veterinary patients, and human patients ( www.orthobme.com ). A key tenet of our laboratory environment is team-based science. We feature a highly collaborative environment and often take on ambitious projects that integrate the skills and abilities of multiple scientific fields. As such, strong communication skills, willingness to learn, and the ability to work with and mentor others are highly important for all members of our team. The post-doctoral fellow will serve as a day-to-day scientific leader for this project and will work closely with a team of graduate students. Responsibilities will include coordinating study activities and timelines, mentoring graduate students in data collection and analysis, supporting the training and calibration of study personnel, and helping maintain data quality and protocol standardization. Ideally, the post-doctoral fellow will also lead the quantitative sensory testing (QST) portion of the project, adapting human QST protocols to the horse. Prior experience with QST is not required; training will be provided. This position is also an opportunity to expand the post-doctoral fellows own research program into veterinary populations, and as such, the post-doctoral fellow will be encouraged to develop independent research directions, contribute to manuscripts and grant proposals, and build skills for a career leading a research team. The J. Crayton Pruitt Family Department of Biomedical Engineering (UF BME) is housed in a state-of-the-art building that is located in the medical school and steps from engineering. The University of Florida is one of only a few comprehensive universities, having medical, veterinary, dental, nursing, public health, and engineering disciplines all co-localized on the same, contiguous campus. UF is a highly collaborative environment that provides researchers with many opportunities for learning (e.g., top seminar programs, excellent core research facilities) and research collaborations. Gainesville is located in the northern region of Florida, within 1-1.5 hours of each coast, and about 2 hours to Orlando and Tampa. It is a small to medium-sized city with a low cost of living, excellent public and private schools, and southern hospitality. While Gainesville is widely recognized as the home of the Gators, it is quickly becoming known as a center for innovation and a place with a lifestyle that's comfortable for families, yet attractive for young professionals. Further, the UF BME partners with many local research centers and institutes including the McKnight Brain Institute, the Clinical and Translational Science Institute, the National High Magnetic Field Laboratory, and the Malcom Randall VA Medical Center. Expected Salary: $62,000-$70,000, depending on experience. Required Qualifications: Applicants are expected to have a Ph.D. in a field such as veterinary sciences, biomedical sciences, engineering, or neuroscience. Other degree fields will also be considered if the applicant has skill sets that complement our research team. Applicants must have experience with manuscript preparation and submission and must be comfortable working with large animals (training will be provided upon hire). Applicants must also demonstrate the ability and willingness to mentor and manage graduate students. Experience with grant writing and prior formal mentoring of students is desirable. Preferred: Above all, we are looking for a colleague with strong team management and mentoring skills who is excited to work with graduate students and help them succeed. We are also looking for someone with an interest in expanding their research to large animals, and, ideally, an interest in performing quantitative sensory testing, as described above. Prior experience with large animals, pain research, sensory testing, behavioral assessment, or biomechanics would be useful but is not required, as we are able to train these skills in new staff. For this position, applicants are expected to have a Ph.D. in a field such as veterinary sciences, biomedical sciences, engineering, or neuroscience. The field of study is less important than a willingness to work with large animals, to interact with patient/clients of the UF College of Veterinary Medicine, and to mentor and lead a team of graduate students. A high-degree of professionalism during the on-site visits is required. Other degree fields will also be considered if the applicant has skill sets that complement our research team. Special Instructions to Applicants: In addition to applying online to Careers at UF ( https://jobs.ufl.edu/ ), applicants send a CV, a brief statement of interest, and contact information for two references to Dr. Allen at kyle.allen@bme.ufl.edu . Review of applications will start immediately and continue until the position is filled. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541549/assistant-professor-in-the-field-of-population-health": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in the field of Population Health",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in the field of Population Health Apply now Job no: 541549 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Education/Training/Instructional Design, Social/Behavioral Science Department: 16920000 - LS-SOCIOLOGY CRIMINOLOGY&LAW Classification Title: Assistant Professor Job Description: The Department of Sociology and Criminology & Law at the University of Florida, College of Liberal Arts and Sciences invites applications for a full-time, nine-month, tenure-accruing position beginning August 16, 2027, at the rank of Assistant Professor with specialization in population health broadly defined. We seek scholars whose research examines the social, economic, political, environmental, and/or demographic factors that shape population health outcomes and health inequalities. Applicants’ research programs may focus on areas including, but not limited to, health disparities and inequities; aging and health; family and health; mental health; disability; reproductive health; health behaviors; environmental influences on health; social epidemiology; or the consequences of social stratification for health and well-being. Researchers employing all types of methodological approaches are encouraged to apply. We seek applicants whose records show commitment to rigorous scholarship with the promise of external funding, as well as excellence in teaching. The ability to contribute significantly to graduate education and the mentoring of graduate students is highly desirable. The Department of Sociology and Criminology & Law is a dynamic department with over 350 undergraduate Sociology majors and 46 Sociology graduate students. The Department has two undergraduate programs, sociology and criminology, both offered via in-person and online courses. We also offer minors in Sociology of Social Justice & Policy and Environmental Justice & Policy. Faculty conduct research in diverse areas, including criminology and criminal justice; psychology and law; families and gender; health, aging, and the life course; environment and resources; and other areas. Our commitment to methodological rigor and innovation is reflected by our investment in the department’s International Ethnography Lab and the Computational Sociology and Criminology Lab. Many faculty are affiliated with other academic programs at UF, including the School of Natural Resources and the Environment, the Center for Latin American Studies, the Center for European Studies, the Department of Gender, Sexuality and Women’s Studies, and the Health Science Center. More information about the Department is available at https://soccrim.clas.ufl.edu/ , including biographical information about our faculty, students, and staff; and a description of the various projects and research interests in which our members are involved. UF is the state’s oldest, largest, and most comprehensive land grant university with an enrollment of over 50,000 students and was ranked Number 1 public university in the United States by Wall Street Journal in its “2024 Best Colleges in the U.S.” report with exceptional research and teaching. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf of Mexico, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs including magnet schools and an international baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville . Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience and includes a full benefits package. Employment Benefits include: Health Insurance: UF participates in state- and university-sponsored benefits programs for individuals, families, and domestic partners, and offers voluntary insurance that includes vision, dental, long-term disability and more. Retirement Options: Attractive options include Florida Retirement System Pension Plan, State University System Optional Retirement Program, Florida Retirement System Investment Plan, and Voluntary Retirement Savings Plan. Leave: UF staff enjoy a generous paid leave plan as well as access to a sick-leave pool, maternity/paternity leave, and leave payouts. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Sociology or a relevant social science field by date of hire. Demonstrated expertise in population health, health disparities, social epidemiology, demography, medical sociology, aging, health policy, or related areas. Preferred: An established record of publication Evidence of rigorous scholarship and the potential to obtain external funding Evidence of excellence in teaching and the ability to mentor graduate students Ability to contribute to diverse theoretical viewpoints and open academic exchanges through scholarship, teaching, mentoring, and professional service Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online at jobs.ufl.edu. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: (1) a letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, experience, and suitability for the position. (2) a complete curriculum vitae . (3) a statement on teaching and research goals. (4) the names and email addresses of three references. An email will be sent to your references, requesting them to upload their confidential letter to the submission packet. Applications will be reviewed beginning October 26, 2026 , and the position will remain open until filled. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541550/postdoctoral-associate": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate Apply now Job no: 541550 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Grant or Research Administration, Engineering, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 19340000 - EG-BIOMEDICAL ENGINEERING Classification Title: Postdoctoral Associate Classification Minimum Requirements: The successful applicant must have a Ph.D. in immunology, immunology‑related life sciences, or a closely allied field, has hands‑on experience in autoimmune disease models or adaptive immune modulation, and is comfortable integrating materials science with biologic assays. Qualified applicants must have experience working successfully in a team setting, possess excellent written and oral communication skills, and a strong publication record. Job Description: Job description/Research Training and Responsibilities : A full-time postdoctoral position is available to study immuno-engineering and develop novel, biomaterial-based immunotherapeutics is available in the laboratory of Dr. Jamal Lewis ( https://www.ufllewislab.com/ ) . This position is for an initial one-year appointment and has the potential to be renewed for an additional year based on performance and funding availability. We seek an enthusiastic postdoctoral researcher to advance a novel platform of immune‑modulatory biomaterial particles designed to attenuate aberrant immune responses in rheumatoid arthritis. The candidate will work beside materials scientists, chemists, and immunologists on particle synthesis, in‑vitro and in‑vivo validation, and translational pathway design. For this highly collaborative position, the successful applicant will be expected to work closely with other faculty in the Biomedical Engineering department, and researchers at UF College of Medicine and the UF College of Pharmacy. The successful applicant must have a Ph.D. in immunology, immunology‑related life sciences, or a closely allied field, has hands‑on experience in autoimmune disease models or adaptive immune modulation, and is comfortable integrating materials science with biologic assays. Qualified applicants must have experience working successfully in a team setting, possess excellent written and oral communication skills, and a strong publication record. Preference will be given to applicants with demonstrated expertise using various biomedical imaging modalities, small animal procedures, and those who have a strong track record as graduate students as evidenced by their published work. Attributes of UF and Gainesville : The BME Department is housed in a state-of-the art building that is located in the medical school and steps from engineering. The University of Florida is one of only a few comprehensive universities, having medical, veterinary, dental, nursing, public health, and engineering disciplines all co-localized on the same, contiguous campus. UF is a highly collaborative environment that provides researchers with many opportunities for learning (e.g., top seminar programs, excellent core research facilities) and research collaborations. Gainesville is located in the northern region of Florida, within 1-1.5 hours of each coast, and just 1.5-2 hours to Orlando and Tampa. It is a small to medium-sized city with a low cost of living, excellent public and private schools, and southern hospitality. UF BME and the IMBL : The J. Crayton Pruitt Family Department of Biomedical Engineering is one of only a few departments in the nation to be co-localized with a top-ranked medical school, veterinary school and dental school. The department is housed in a state-of-the-art building that is co-located with the medical school and steps from engineering. The IMBL is housed in the brand new, Wertheim Engineering Building (opened in 2020), just a few steps away from the Reitz Union (the center of campus). Biomedical Engineering at UF partners with many local research centers and institutes including the McKnight Brain Institute, the Clinical and Translational Science Institute, the National High Magnetic Field Laboratory and the Malcom Randall VA Medical Center. Expected Salary: $60,000.00 Required Qualifications: The successful applicant must have a Ph.D. in immunology, immunology‑related life sciences, or a closely allied field, has hands‑on experience in autoimmune disease models or adaptive immune modulation, and is comfortable integrating materials science with biologic assays. Qualified applicants must have experience working successfully in a team setting, possess excellent written and oral communication skills, and a strong publication record. Preferred: Preference will be given to applicants with demonstrated expertise using various biomedical imaging modalities, small animal procedures, and those who have a strong track record as graduate students as evidenced by their published work. Special Instructions to Applicants: In addition to applying online to Careers at UF, Applicants should send a CV, a brief statement of interest, and contact information for three references to Dr. Jamal Lewis at jamalslewis@ufl.edu . Review of applications will start immediately and continue until the position is filled. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541552/it-analyst-ii": {
      "title": "IT Analyst II",
      "description": "IT Analyst II Apply now Job no: 541552 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Information Technology Department: 27010703 - HA-TEACHING LABS Classification Title: IT Analyst II Classification Minimum Requirements: Associate’s degree and two years of relevant experience; or a high school diploma or equivalent and four years of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: This role evaluates business requirements and delivers technical solutions to address user needs. The position provides application support, user training, project planning, coordination, and management for both internally developed and vendor-supported software systems, including Microsoft Teams and SharePoint Online. A significant portion of the role is focused on designing, developing, and maintaining custom business applications and automated workflows using Power Apps and Power Automate. Provides IT assistance and support, provides programming expertise, manages training to user community, and maintains daily operation of vendor supported and in-house developed applications with appropriate security measures to ensure a high degree of data integrity, accuracy and availability. Works closely with customers and other developers to ensure timely delivery of accurate products that demonstrate consistent attention to detail. Identifies target audiences and their needs. Creates project plan, budget, and timeline. Tracks project activities, deliverables and timelines. Creates and implement communication plan, evaluation and testing plan, roll-out plan. Maintains familiarity with information technologies associated with application development, documentation methodologies, tools, products and languages employed in support of the UF Health IT mission. Provides training, documents procedures and creates troubleshooting documentation to help provide the best user experience for select applications Expected Salary: $61,000 - $65,000 Required Qualifications: Associate’s degree and two years of relevant experience; or a high school diploma or equivalent and four years of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: • Demonstrated experience with Microsoft Power Platform solutions, including Power Apps, Power Automate, and Power BI, is highly preferred. • Ability to develop, maintain and improve high-quality software applications which demonstrate a high degree of data integrity, accuracy and availability is required. • Must be able to test and implement applications with appropriate security measures in an integrated, complex information technology environment with minimal disruption of network services and resources. • Detailed understanding of structured query language (SQL).. • Familiarity with software products used to create reports and manage enterprise data such as Power BI. • Familiarity with, or a demonstrated ability to acquire sufficient knowledge needed to effectively work with products and to perform actions in systems and environments to the extent necessary to facilitate creation, deployment and maintenance of software applications is required. • Ability to analyze, determine causative issues, resolve technical challenges and report effectively at a level commensurate with the position is required. • Must formulate solutions collaboratively and effectively in a team setting • Must be able to organize and manage complex activities and to complete assignments in a timely fashion with attention to detail and accuracy with exceptional interpersonal and communication skills (oral and written, American English). • An ability to effectively coordinate efforts among customers, IT professionals and other support staff is required. • Must proactively foster skills development among team members. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please upload the following - CV or Resume, Cover Letter and List of Professional References. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541553/ops-lab-field-technician-blueberry-breeding-and-genomics-lab": {
      "title": "OPS Lab & Field Technician (Blueberry Breeding and Genomics Lab)",
      "description": "OPS Lab & Field Technician (Blueberry Breeding and Genomics Lab) Apply now Job no: 541553 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Agricultural Sciences, Agriculture Operations Department: 60230000 - AG-HORTICULTURAL SCIENCES Classification Title: OPS-Spons Prjs Non-Clerical Classification Minimum Requirements: Ability to perform job responsibilities with satisfactory proficiency Commitment to the time requirements of this position Available from now through the upcoming blueberry season No bee or bug allergies Job Description: The UF Blueberry Breeding and Genomics Lab is currently seeking highly motivated individuals to assist the Blueberry Breeding Program with basic molecular work and field activities related to the program. These activities will take place in both the molecular lab and our field trial locations starting now until May 2027, with the possibility of extending the position upon performance. Essential Duties and Responsibilities for Field: Be available to work in 8-hour shifts Weekdays 8:00AM – 5:00PM Conduct basic molecular work, such as DNA extraction, PCR, gel electrophoresis, gene cloning and transformation Work outdoors or greenhouse in various weather conditions Assist with the collection of field samples and breeding seasonal work Load and unload field sample equipment and coolers from the truck bed Collect and document data accurately Be flexible with sudden changes to the schedule Expected Time Commitment: 20-40 hours/week Expected Salary: $15/Hour Required Qualifications: Ability to perform job responsibilities with satisfactory proficiency Commitment to the time requirements of this position Available from now through the upcoming blueberry season No bee or bug allergies Preferred: Experience in molecular lab and field work Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541555/ops-student-assistant-lab-field-technician-blueberry-breeding-and-genomics-lab": {
      "title": "OPS Student Assistant - Lab & Field Technician (Blueberry Breeding and Genomics Lab)",
      "description": "OPS Student Assistant - Lab & Field Technician (Blueberry Breeding and Genomics Lab) Apply now Job no: 541555 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services Department: 60230000 - AG-HORTICULTURAL SCIENCES Classification Title: STU AST - NON-CLERICAL & ADMIN Classification Minimum Requirements: · Ability to perform job responsibilities with satisfactory proficiency · Commitment to the time requirements of this position · Available from now through the upcoming blueberry season · No bee or bug allergies Job Description: The UF Blueberry Breeding and Genomics Lab is currently seeking highly motivated individuals to assist the Blueberry Breeding Program with basic molecular work and field activities related to the program. These activities will take place in both the molecular lab and our field trial locations starting now until May 2027, with the possibility of extending the position upon performance. Essential Duties and Responsibilities for Field: · Be available to work in 8-hour shifts Weekdays 8:00AM – 5:00PM · Conduct basic molecular work, such as DNA extraction, PCR, gel electrophoresis, gene cloning and transformation · Work outdoors or greenhouse in various weather conditions · Assist with the collection of field samples and breeding seasonal work · Load and unload field sample equipment and coolers from the truck bed · Collect and document data accurately · Be flexible with sudden changes to the schedule Expected Time Commitment: 20-31 hours/week Expected Salary: $15.00/hour Required Qualifications: · Ability to perform job responsibilities with satisfactory proficiency · Commitment to the time requirements of this position · Available from now through the upcoming blueberry season · No bee or bug allergies Preferred: · Experience in molecular lab and field work Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition will be used to fill multiple positions. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 29 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/538108/astasofull-prof-cardiovascular-medicine": {
      "title": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF - Cardiovascular Medicine",
      "description": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF - Cardiovascular Medicine Apply now Job no: 538108 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty, Non-Tenure-Track Faculty, Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29050400 - MD-CARDIOLOGY Classification Title: Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Cardiovascular Medicine Job Description: The University of Florida, Department of Medicine, and Division of Cardiovascular Medicine is seeking a tenure/non-tenure, Assistant/Associate/Full Professor with innovative leadership and good interpersonal skills to become an active and integral member of the Division of Cardiology, Section of Heart Failure. Rank and Tenure status will be commensurate with qualifications. The University of Florida has a long track record of being a national leader in advanced heart failure. With a celebrated transplant and mechanical circulatory support history, combined with unique opportunities for research and teaching, ours is a premiere academic program. We are seeking a dynamic physician to join our growing team. Applicants should have a record of distinguished accomplishments and proficiency in clinical duties, administration, and clinical investigation to include mentoring new physicians, fostering a progressive and dynamic work environment, and promoting teaching and research. Responsibilities will include heart failure services at the UF Health Shands Hospital at the University of Florida. Clinical duties will include performance and teaching in heart failure services. Coverage of inpatient heart failure services and general cardiology services as well as outpatient clinical duties and other duties as assigned. Individuals with expertise in Infiltrative Cardiomyopathies, Cardio Oncology, or Inheritable Cardiomyopathies are encouraged to apply. Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Applicants must have a medical degree and Florida Medical License. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . This requisition has been reposted. Previous applications are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes. Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541145/ufhealth-physician": {
      "title": "UFHealth Physician",
      "description": "UFHealth Physician Apply now Job no: 541145 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Marion Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29050604 - MD-HEMATOLOGY-ONCOLOGY-OTHER Classification Title: UFHealth Physician Classification Minimum Requirements: Licensed to practice medicine in accordance with Chapter 458, Florida Statutes, plus four years of appropriate experience. Completion of an accredited fellowship in Hematology and Oncology, Medical Oncology, or Hematology fellowship program may substitute for the required experience. Job Description: Provides comprehensive hematology and oncology patient care, including the evaluation, diagnosis, treatment, coordination of care, preventive care, and ongoing management of patients with hematologic and oncologic conditions in the outpatient clinic and infusion suite. Oversees the clinical care of patients receiving treatment in the outpatient infusion suite and/or clinic, including assessing treatment readiness, reviewing laboratory results, managing treatment related complications, and collaborating with nursing and pharmacy staff to facilitate the safe delivery of chemotherapy, immunotherapy, anti-neoplastic treatment, blood products, and supportive care. Works collaboratively with members of the multidisciplinary healthcare team, including pharmacists, advanced practice providers, nurses, social workers, and other support staff to coordinate comprehensive patient care. Orders, reviews and interprets diagnostic reports, including laboratory studies, pathology, imaging, and other diagnostic tests, and follows up in a timely manner. Prescribes chemotherapy, immunotherapy, targeted therapies, blood products, supportive medications, and other treatment regimens as appropriate to the patient's diagnosis and clinical condition. Develops individualized treatment plans aligned with clinical and institutional pathways and refers patients to appropriate specialists, clinical services, supportive care programs, and community resources as needed. Directs and coordinates the patient care activities of nursing, infusion, and support staff as appropriate. Follows established departmental policies, procedures, quality improvement initiatives, patient safety standards, regulatory and accreditation requirements, and infection prevention practices. Participates in tumor boards, multidisciplinary conferences, peer review, quality assurance activities, provider meetings, and other clinical and operational meetings as requested. Assists in the development, implementation, and updating of clinical protocols, treatment pathways, and standards of practice as requested. Identify, consider and enroll (or refer), when appropriate for clinical trials to support optimal cancer patient care. Engage in lifelong learning and demonstrate high-quality care delivery and decision making. Support an environment of inquiry, excellence, and collaboration. May manage the daily clinical operations of a hematology and oncology practice or patient care program. NORMAL WORK SCHEDULE Monday through Friday, 7:30 a.m. to 5:30 p.m. Extended work hours may be required based on clinic, infusion suite, and patient care needs. Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Licensed to practice medicine in accordance with Chapter 458, Florida Statutes, plus four years of appropriate experience. Completion of an accredited fellowship in Hematology and Oncology, Medical Oncology, or Hematology fellowship program may substitute for the required experience. Preferred: Ambulatory clinical experience providing care to adult hematology and oncology patients. Experience managing patients receiving chemotherapy, immunotherapy, transfusions, and other systemic or supportive therapies. Experience participating in or leading a multidisciplinary oncology care team Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541156/assistant-director-of-accreditation-housestaff": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Accreditation - Housestaff",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Accreditation - Housestaff Apply now Job no: 541156 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 29012600 - MD-HOUSESTAFF Classification Title: AST DIR, Education/Training Programs Job Description: The Assistant Director of Accreditation provides operational leadership for the accreditation and continuous quality improvement functions of the University of Florida College of Medicine Graduate Medical Education (GME) Office. Reporting to the GME Director of Administration, this position manages a major segment of the institution's education and training operations and supports the oversight of ACGME-accredited residency and fellowship programs, including ACGME Non-Standard Training (NST) programs, within a large, complex academic medical center. The Assistant Director serves as a institutional resource for accreditation standards, program review, policy implementation, and program improvement. The position translates accreditation requirements into practical guidance, coordinates institutional review and monitoring activities, supervises assigned professional and support staff, and partners with program directors, coordinators, faculty, and GME leadership to promote consistent and sustainable compliance. As part of the GME leadership team, the Assistant Director is cross-trained in the Director's core accreditation responsibilities and provides leadership continuity for assigned functions when delegated or during the Director's absence. About This Role: Accreditation Oversight and Program Consultation Direct day-to-day accreditation support and consultation for assigned GME programs and institutional functions. Interpret and apply ACGME Institutional, Common Program, and specialty-specific requirements; provide timely, consistent guidance to program directors, coordinators, faculty, and GME staff. Manage routine accreditation monitoring activities, including review of program letters, citations, Areas for Improvement, accreditation statuses, action plans, and required institutional follow-up. Monitor institutional compliance with Program Letters of Agreement (PLAs) and provide oversight of the PLA process, including review of status reports, identification and escalation of gaps or expired agreements, and verification that designated staff and programs complete required actions. Provide institutional support and operational oversight for ACGME NST programs, including interpretation of applicable requirements, ongoing compliance monitoring, and guidance to program and department leadership. Develop and maintain accreditation resources, templates, calendars, standard operating procedures, and communication tools that support consistent institutional practices. Identify emerging compliance risks, recommend corrective strategies, and escalate significant or systemic concerns to the Director, Associate DIO, and DIO as appropriate. Serve as a liaison between the GME Office, clinical departments, program leadership, and institutional stakeholders on assigned accreditation matters. Program Review, Applications, and Site Visit Readiness Coordinates complex program review and accreditation-readiness activities from planning through follow-up. Coordinate institutional preparation for ACGME institutional and program site visits, focused reviews, self-studies, and other accreditation-related reviews. Review draft new program applications, complement increases, participating site additions, program changes, and related submissions for completeness, internal consistency, and alignment with institutional expectations. Assist with the development, institutional review, and submission of new ACGME NST program applications and renewals, and coordinate preparation for NST-related site visits, reviews, and required follow-up. Provide structured consultation to programs preparing responses to citations, requests for progress reports, adverse actions, or other ACGME communications. Conduct targeted audits of accreditation records and program documentation; track findings, responsible parties, deadlines, and completion of corrective actions. Maintain readiness materials and standardized processes that allow the GME Office to respond efficiently to planned and unplanned accreditation activity. Committee Operations and Institutional Governance Lead or support GME committee processes that advance institutional oversight and regulatory compliance. Manage the operational work of assigned GMEC subcommittees, including agenda development, document review, presentation of findings, action tracking, and maintenance of an auditable record. Support the Accreditation Requirements Review Committee (ARRC) and other assigned committees; chair meetings or workgroups when delegated by the Director. Oversee the operational work of the NST Committee, including application review, agenda and meeting coordination, documentation of decisions, action tracking, and communication with programs and institutional leadership. Prepare accreditation analyses, recommendations, and decision materials for GMEC and GME leadership. Partner with the Director in developing, reviewing, and implementing institutional GME policies and procedures. Ensure assigned committee actions and approvals are completed within applicable institutional and ACGME timelines. Data Monitoring and Continuous Quality Improvement Use institutional and program-level data to support early identification of risk and continuous program improvement. Monitor ACGME survey results, milestones, evaluation data, work hour information, accreditation trends, and other indicators for patterns requiring program or institutional action. Develop dashboards, reports, and concise leadership summaries that translate accreditation data into actionable recommendations. Support the Institutional Program Review Committee (IPRC), Annual Program Evaluation oversight, Special Review processes, and other institutional quality improvement activities. Establish performance measures for assigned accreditation functions and evaluate the timeliness, consistency, and effectiveness of processes. Collaborate with GME leadership, analysts, and institutional partners to improve data quality, reporting, documentation, and governance. Education, Training, and Resource Development Design and deliver education that strengthens accreditation knowledge and operational consistency across the institution. Develop and deliver onboarding, workshops, written guidance, and continuing education for program directors, associate program directors, coordinators, faculty, and GME staff. Assess learning and resource needs and revise educational offerings based on accreditation changes, recurring findings, stakeholder feedback, and institutional priorities. Translate complex regulatory information into clear, practical tools and examples for varied audiences. Promote shared learning and dissemination of effective accreditation and program-management practices across programs. Leadership, Supervision, and Operational Continuity Provide direct leadership for assigned staff and contributes to the strategic and operational management of the GME Office. Supervise assigned managers, professionals, and support staff; establish priorities, assign work, provide coaching, evaluate performance, and support professional development. Participate as a member of the GME management team and contribute to strategic planning for accreditation, education, and program quality functions. Manage special projects and cross-functional initiatives delegated by the Director, Associate DIO, or DIO. Maintain cross-training and working knowledge of the Director's core accreditation portfolio; assume delegated authority for defined functions and provide operational leadership during planned absences or leadership transitions. Represent the institution at regional or national GME accreditation meetings as delegated Foster a collaborative, service-oriented, accountable, and inclusive work environment. We Offer Exceptional Benefits Low-cost State Health Plans : Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Life and Disability Insurance Generous Retirement Options to secure your future Comprehensive Paid Time Off Packages with 10+ paid holidays, paid family, sick and vacation leave Exceptional Personal and Professional Development Opportunities : Access to UF Training & Organizational Development programs, leadership development, LinkedIn Learning, and more Tuition Assistance through the UF Employee Education Program Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Eligible Employer Learn more about what we have to offer here ! Expected Salary: $82,000- $90,000 annually Required Qualifications: Master's degree in an appropriate area of specialization and two years of appropriate experience, or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area of specialization and four years of appropriate experience. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: Master's degree in education, health professions education, higher education administration, healthcare administration, public health, business administration, or a related field. Five or more years of progressively responsible experience in graduate medical education, academic medicine, accreditation, education administration, or a related regulatory environment. Demonstrated knowledge of ACGME Institutional, Common Program, and specialty-specific requirements and experience supporting program or institutional accreditation activities. Experience coordinating accreditation reviews, new program applications, site visits, Special Reviews, Annual Program Evaluations, or responses to accreditation findings. Supervisory or team-lead experience, including prioritizing work, coaching staff, and managing performance. Experience using a residency management system such as New Innovations and accreditation systems such as the ACGME Accreditation Data System. Experience analyzing educational or compliance data and developing reports, dashboards, presentations, or recommendations for leadership. Strong consultation, writing, presentation, project management, judgment, and relationship-building skills in a complex, decentralized organization. Ability to manage multiple high-priority deadlines, exercise discretion with sensitive information, and communicate regulatory expectations clearly and diplomatically. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume, and contact information for three professional references. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541428/biological-scientist-ii": {
      "title": "Biological Scientist II",
      "description": "Biological Scientist II Apply now Job no: 541428 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Orange Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60870000 - AG-MREC-APOPKA Classification Title: Biological Scientist II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in biology or a related field and three years of experience or Master's degree in biology or a related field with one year of relevant experience. Job Description: Plant Breeding and Germplasm Management -Plan, modify designs, and implement breeding nurseries, field trials, greenhouse experiments, and controlled-environment experiments. Assist with the development, maintenance, evaluation, and advancement of germplasm. Driving to site locations will be required. Molecular and Genomic Research -Perform molecular biology procedures, including DNA/RNA extraction, PCR, genotyping, and related laboratory techniques. Perform sequencing, QTL mapping, GWAS, genomic prediction, and other quantitative-genetic analyses. Research Program Coordination -Coordinate day-to-day research activities with faculty PI, graduate students, undergraduate researchers. Maintain laboratory, greenhouse, field, and research equipment. Train and supervise students, technicians, and other research personnel. Data Management and Analysis -Maintain, organize research records and databases. Assist with statistical analysis and visualization of breeding and experimental data. Other -Collaborate with multidisciplinary research teams and contribute to grant-funded research objecti Expected Salary: $53,000; commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in biology or a related field and three years of experience or Master's degree in biology or a related field with one year of relevant experience. A valid State of Florida Driver’s License is required. Preferred: PhD’s degree in plant breeding, genetics, horticulture, agronomy, or specialty-crop research. Experience with controlled crosses, plant propagation, seed handling, and breeding population management. Experience conducting field or greenhouse experiments. Experience with DNA extraction, PCR, genotyping, or other molecular biology techniques. Experience with plant phenotyping and image-based data collection. Experience using R, Python, or other statistical/data-analysis software. Experience working with QTL mapping, GWAS, genomic selection, genomic prediction, or other quantitative-genetic approaches. Good inter-personal skills and demonstrated ability to work in an interdisciplinary environment Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 19 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541500/postdoctoral-associate-developmental-programming-of-metabolic-disease": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate - Developmental Programming of Metabolic Disease",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate - Developmental Programming of Metabolic Disease Apply now Job no: 541500 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science Department: 29060000 - MD-MOLECULAR GENTCS - MICROBIO Classification Title: Postdoctoral Associate Classification Minimum Requirements: PhD, MD/PhD, or equivalent doctoral degree in a relevant biomedical discipline. Job Description: The Karst laboratory in the Department of Molecular Genetics and Microbiology at the University of Florida College of Medicine is seeking a highly motivated postdoctoral associate to join a newly funded NIH R01 project investigating how the early-life environment programs long-term metabolic health. Our laboratory recently discovered that maternal bile acids delivered during lactation act as developmental signals that regulate neonatal growth through the bile acid receptor TGR5. Remarkably, transient manipulation of this pathway during lactation has lasting consequences: offspring exposed to reduced maternal milk bile acids are protected from diet-induced obesity, insulin resistance, hyperinsulinemia, and fatty liver in adulthood. The successful candidate will investigate the cellular and molecular mechanisms underlying this developmental programming. Current studies focus on how bile acid signaling during early postnatal life establishes adipose tissue architecture and function, including effects on adipocyte progenitor populations and their developmental trajectories. Approaches will include mouse models, flow cytometry and cell sorting, lineage tracing, metabolic phenotyping, and single-cell genomic and epigenomic approaches. This is a new and rapidly developing research direction in the laboratory, providing substantial opportunity for the successful candidate to contribute intellectually to the project, develop independent questions, and shape its future direction. We are looking for a scientist who is curious, rigorous, collaborative, and interested in understanding how transient exposures during critical developmental windows can produce lifelong changes in physiology and disease susceptibility. Expected Salary: $58,656 - $63,900 and is commensurate with qualifications and experience. Required Qualifications: PhD, MD/PhD, or equivalent doctoral degree in a relevant biomedical discipline. Preferred: Candidates should have a strong background in biomedical or biological sciences and demonstrated experience conducting rigorous, hypothesis-driven research. Preferred applicants will have experience with molecular, cellular, or in vivo experimental approaches relevant to the project. Special Instructions to Applicants: This is a time-limited position and all applicants MMUST apply through Careers at UF at https://jobs.ufl.edu/. Search for posting #541500. In order to be considered, you must upload references, research interests, and resume/CV. A Criminal Background check is required, and this is not a remote work position. It is located on the University of Florida main campus in Gainesville, FL. We Offer Exceptional Benefits: • Low-cost State Health Plans: Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance • Life and Disability Insurance • Generous Retirement Options to secure your future • Comprehensive Paid Time Off Packages: (includes over 10 paid holidays, as well as paid family, sick, and vacation leave) • Exceptional Personal and Professional Development Opportunities: Access to UF Training & Organizational Development programs, leadership development, LinkedIn Learning, and more • Tuition Assistance through the UF Employee Education Program • Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Eligible Employer To learn more about Gainesville and the surrounding areas, please visit the Guide to Greater Gainesville, which shares information about our neighborhoods, schools, zoning, culture, leisure, and more at https://guidetogreatergainesville.com/. Gainesville is a vibrant city with a low cost of living, diverse population, excellent public schools, Division I NCAA sports, and no state income tax. The area is known for its nature parks, bike trails, sporting events, museums, mild winters, and warm summers. Being centrally located, Gainesville is easily situated for a day trip to beautiful beaches along the Atlantic Ocean and the Gulf of Mexico, as well as to theme parks like Disney World. Other nearby cities include Orlando, Jacksonville, and Tampa. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at https://www.visitgainesville.com/. To learn more about the University of Florida (UF), please visit https://www.ufl.edu/. Interested applicants should submit a cover letter describing their research interests and career goals, CV, and contact information for three references to skarst@ufl.edu. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (EDT) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541503/research-assistant-scientist-neuroscience": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Scientist, Neuroscience",
      "description": "Research Assistant Scientist, Neuroscience Apply now Job no: 541503 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Dentistry Department: 34080200 - DN-NEUROSCIENCE Classification Title: Research Assistant Scientist, Neuroscience Classification Minimum Requirements: Minimum requirements include Ph.D. (or an equivalent degree) in neurobiology, cell biology, or in any related field. Job Description: The College of Dentistry, Department of Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery is seeking applications for a full-time, non-tenure track, Research Assistant Scientist. The University of Florida College of Dentistry (UFCD) is one of the leading dental schools in the United States devoted to improving and promoting oral health throughout Florida, the nation and worldwide through excellence in teaching, research, patient care and service. The Caudle lab is looking for a Research Assistant Scientist interested in conducting research on the neurobiology of pain. The ideal candidate has experience in whole cell voltage clamp techniques, transcriptomics, single neuron transcriptomics, and in running rodent behavioral assays. Experience with immunohistochemistry is also desirable. Minimum requirements include Ph.D. (or an equivalent degree) in neurobiology, cell biology, or in any related field. The candidate is expected to demonstrate strong knowledge and skills in the neurobiology of pain, and have at least five years of experience in the field. The University of Florida, a member of the AAU, is the largest and most comprehensive public university in the state of Florida, with large undergraduate, graduate, and postgraduate educational programs. The UF Health Science Center and its six colleges (Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, Veterinary Medicine) are co-located on the Gainesville campus, with additional teaching, research, and patient care sites across Florida, and internationally. Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: Minimum requirements include Ph.D. (or an equivalent degree) in neurobiology, cell biology, or in any related field. Preferred: The ideal candidate has experience in whole cell voltage clamp techniques, transcriptomics, single neuron transcriptomics, and in running rodent behavioral assays. Experience with immunohistochemistry is also desirable. The candidate is expected to demonstrate strong knowledge and skills in the neurobiology of pain, and have at least five years of experience in the field. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to apply applicants should attach a curriculum vitae, a cover letter describing their interest in the position, and a list of three references with their application for consideration. The University of Florida is an Equal Opportunity Institution . The selection process will be conducted in accordance with the provisions of Florida’s “Government in the Sunshine” and Public Records law. Search committee meetings and interviews will be open to the public, and all applications, resumes, and other documents related to the search will be available for public inspection. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. For more information about the University of Florida College of Dentistry, visit: www.dental.ufl.edu . Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541509/assistantassociate-professor-extension-agronomy": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor – Extension Agronomy",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor – Extension Agronomy Apply now Job no: 541509 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Gadsden Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60770000 - AG-NFREC-QUINCY Classification Title: Ast/Aso Prof Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates for this rank shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Job Description: The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . This is a 12-month tenure-accruing position that will be 40% research (Florida Agricultural Experiment Station) and 60% Extension (Florida Cooperative Extension Service), available in the North Florida Research and Education Center (NFREC), Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences, at the University of Florida. Tenure will accrue in the Agronomy Department . This assignment may change in accordance with the needs of the unit. The NFREC is one of the largest and the most diverse units of UF/IFAS, consisting of research and education campuses in Quincy, Marianna, and Live Oak, Florida. NFREC has faculty members representing 8 academic departments and the School of Forest, Fisheries, and Geomatics Sciences, 5 emeritus faculty members and a dynamic population of graduate students, post-doctoral associates, and scientific/farm/administrative staff. This variety of faculty, staff and programs provides a unique setting for interdisciplinary work within the NFREC campuses, with colleagues on the main UF campus and faculty at other Centers around the state. The incumbent is expected to develop a dynamic integrated research and Extension program focusing on the primary agronomic cropping systems in North Florida including peanut, cotton, corn, and soybean. This requires integral knowledge of whole plant response to agronomic practices from planting until harvest, as well as developing good understanding of off-season management options. Expertise in variety selection, planting methods and rates, irrigation, and cropping systems are essential for the successful candidate. Activities dealing with optimizing crop management and production, digital agriculture, alternative rotational and emerging crops, winter crops, and integrated crop livestock systems are potential research and Extension avenues. Future-focused skills in the use of artificial intelligence (AI) and/or machine learning to collect and integrate agronomic and environmental data towards specific objectives including the investigation of advanced technologies for optimization of crop inputs and cropping systems are encouraged. The incumbent is expected to develop an internationally recognized program while meeting the specific needs of the region. The successful candidate will develop collaborative projects with supporting UF/IFAS research and Extension faculty located at main campus and across North Florida including agronomists, plant pathologists, entomologists, breeders, economists, and scientists in water quality, soil science, as needed. The faculty member will actively seek contract and grant funding to support his/her program. The significant appointment in Extension means that the incumbent is expected to participate in Florida’s Cooperative Extension Strategic Initiatives and become a strong leader and resource for County Agents and Regional Specialized Agents across Florida’s northern tier who work in agronomic crops, by providing regular In-Service Training, writing Extension documents (EDIS articles), publishing in newsletters, etc. The incumbent will be expected to interact with stakeholders, including producers, industry representatives, commodity groups, and other clientele to further the UF/IFAS mission. The successful candidate will participate actively in undergraduate and graduate education by chairing and serving on graduate student committees, supervising thesis, dissertation and undergraduate research, and publishing research results with students. Faculty are encouraged to participate in professional development activities related to teaching and advising and may teach courses and seminars. Because of the IFAS land-grant mission, all faculty are expected to be supportive of and engaged in all three mission areas—Research, Teaching and Extension—regardless of the assignment split specified in the position description. Background Information : The University of Florida is a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution, encompassing virtually all academic and professional disciplines, with an enrollment of more than 56,000 students. UF is a member of The Association of American Universities. The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences includes the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences , the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station , the Florida Cooperative Extension Service , the College of Veterinary Medicine , the Florida Sea Grant program, and encompasses 16 on-campus academic departments and schools, 12 Research and Educational Centers (REC) located throughout the state, 6 Research sites/demonstration units administered by RECs or academic departments, and Florida Cooperative Extension Service offices in all 67 counties (counties operate and maintain). The School of Natural Resources and Environment is an interdisciplinary unit housed in IFAS and managed by several colleges on campus. UF/IFAS employs nearly 4,500 people, which includes approximately 990 salaried faculty and 1,400 permanent support personnel located in Gainesville and throughout the state. IFAS, one of the nation’s largest agricultural and natural resources research and education organizations, is administered by a Vice President and four deans: the Dean of the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences, the Dean for Extension and Director of the Florida Cooperative Extension Service, the Dean for Research and Director of the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station, and the Dean for the College of Veterinary Medicine. UF/IFAS also engages in cooperative work with Florida A&M University in Tallahassee. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Education and Experience Required Qualifications: A doctorate (foreign equivalent acceptable) in Agronomy, Crop Science or a closely related discipline is required. Candidates should have demonstrated skills in verbal and written communication, interpersonal relationships, and procurement of extramural funding. Candidates must be supportive of the mission of the Land-Grant system. Candidates must also have a commitment to UF core values. Preferred: Postdoctoral experience is desirable. Previous experience in Extension in a land-grant university setting Demonstrated ability to develop a robust educational program for stakeholder groups including the use of traditional and multimedia tools. An established network of collaborators in other states in agronomic crop systems. Demonstrated ability to work in a multidisciplinary, collaborative team environment. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, candidates should apply and submit materials by November 27, 2026. The position will remain open until a viable applicant pool is determined. Employment Conditions This position is available February 1, 2027 and will be filled as soon thereafter as an acceptable applicant is available. Compensation is commensurate with the education, experience, and qualifications of the selected applicant. Nominations Nominations are welcome. Nominations need to include the complete name and address of the nominee. Nominee information should be sent to: Please refer to Requisition # 541509 Dr. Cheryl Mackowiak Chair, Search and Screen Committee University of Florida North Florida Research and Education Center 155 Research Rd. Quincy, FL 32351 Telephone: 850-875-7126 Electronic Mail: echo13@ufl.edu Application Information Individuals wishing to apply should go online to Careers at UF and submit: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for three individuals willing to write letters of recommendation from a professional perspective Unofficial transcripts Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. NFREC-Quincy is situated about 20-30 minutes’ drive west of Tallahassee, Florida. The area is rich in natural resources, such as state parks, lakes, rivers, and forests. Entertainment and housing options range from rural to metropolitan with many events, restaurants, and social opportunities available. The area is near to some of the Gulf Coast’s best beaches for swimming, fishing and boating. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541511/residencyfellowship-program-coordinator-iii-department-of-pediatrics": {
      "title": "Residency/Fellowship Program Coordinator I/II - Department of Pediatrics",
      "description": "Residency/Fellowship Program Coordinator I/II - Department of Pediatrics Apply now Job no: 541511 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 29091500 - MD-PEDS-MEDICAL EDUCATION Classification Title: Res/Fellowship Program Crd I/II Classification Minimum Requirements Level I: Bachelor’s degree and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience. Level II: Bachelor’s degree and three years of experience coordinating educational programs, accredited residency and/or fellowship programs; or an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience. Job Description: The physicians who care for Florida's children tomorrow are training in UF Pediatrics today — and the coordinators in the Pediatric Medical Education Office are a significant reason that training runs well. If you're an organized, people-centered administrator who thrives in a complex academic environment, we have multiple openings in the Department of Pediatrics' central medical education hub that are worth your attention. These positions may each be filled at the Residency/Fellowship Program Coordinator I or Coordinator II level, depending on the qualifications and experience of the successful candidate. The Coordinator is responsible for the day-to-day administration of assigned fellowship programs, with a strong focus on program operations and accreditation compliance. Working closely with the Program Director, the Coordinator serves as a primary point of contact for fellows, faculty, the institutional GME office, and accrediting bodies. The Coordinator plays an integral role in fellowship recruitment and educational program administration. Responsibilities include managing the full fellowship recruitment cycle, including ERAS administration, applicant communications, interview scheduling, and NRMP Match activities. The position also coordinates educational activities such as didactic conferences, clinical rotations, faculty teaching schedules, and fellow evaluation processes. At both appointment levels, Coordinators contribute to broader PME office operations by providing administrative support and coverage across Pediatric programs. This includes supporting Program Directors, Associate Program Directors, and Chief Residents and facilitating effective communication and coordination among fellows, faculty, program leadership, and institutional stakeholders. Expected Salary: Salary is commensurate on education and experience. Required Qualifications: Level I: Bachelor’s degree and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience. Level II: Bachelor’s degree and three years of experience coordinating educational programs, accredited residency and/or fellowship programs; or an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience. Preferred Residency/Fellowship Program Coordinator I — Preferred qualifications: Experience in an academic, healthcare, or university administrative environment Familiarity with University of Florida travel and purchasing rules and regulations Strong written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to build effective working relationships across faculty, trainees, and administrative peers Demonstrated ability to proofread and manage detailed correspondence accurately Residency/Fellowship Program Coordinator II — Preferred qualifications: Experience managing residency or fellowship recruitment cycles, including ERAS and Match coordination Experience with University of Florida systems including myUFL and institutional travel and purchasing regulations Demonstrated ability to manage accreditation committee support, milestone reporting, and case log oversight Strong written and verbal communication skills and proven ability to work effectively with faculty, program directors, and trainees across multiple concurrent programs Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, please submit the following documents: A cover letter detailing relevant experience and qualifications A current resume/CV Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541516/professor-of-maize-genomics": {
      "title": "Professor of Maize Genomics",
      "description": "Professor of Maize Genomics Apply now Job no: 541516 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60230000 - AG-HORTICULTURAL SCIENCES Classification Title: Prof Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates for the faculty rank of professor shall be qualified for the rank of associate professor and, in addition, be a recognized national or international authority or an outstanding teacher in their field or specialization. A candidate for this rank must have a distinguished record of achievement beyond the associate professor level. Job Description: The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . This is a 9-month tenure-accruing position that will be 20% teaching (College of Agricultural and Life Sciences), 80% research (Florida Agricultural Experiment Station), available in the Horticultural Sciences Department, Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences, at the University of Florida. This assignment may change in accordance with the needs of the unit. The programmatic emphasis for this position centers on the intersection of evolutionary plant genomics, and artificial intelligence to identify foundational gene networks underpinning maize domestication, anthropogenic improvement and adaptation to cultivated systems and environmental change. An expectation of this position is a leadership role in large-scale pangenomic initiatives to characterize the diversity of crops like maize and its wild relatives, providing critical crop agnostic resources for crop improvement and breeding. Research areas of concentration include the following: Genetic architecture of adaptation: Investigating how maize adapts to diverse and changing environments, with a particular focus on adaptations to abiotic stresses from highlands across the Americas; Domestication and introgression: Analyzing the genomic signatures left by the domestication of teosinte and the subsequent gene flow between wild and domesticated varieties; Computational tool development: Creating and benchmarking artificial intelligence pipelines for de nova genome assembly, transposable element annotation, and genomic quality assessment. The expectation for this position includes successful acquisition of extramural funding from sources like NSF (PGRP, CNIC, etc.), FFAR, USDA-ARS, and/or industry partners sufficient to support the research program. Publications for this position are expected to appear in top 5% and Q1 journals as appropriate for the audience of the data including, Nature, Science, Nature Genetics, Genome Biology, Plant Cell and the Proceedings of the National Academy Sciences. The successful candidate will engage in scholarly activities related to instruction, including teaching undergraduate and/or graduate courses, advising and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students, participating in curriculum revision and enhancement, seeking funding for the teaching program, supervising undergraduate and graduate research and creative work, publishing teaching-related scholarship, producing learning tools, and engaging in professional development activities related to teaching and advising. Faculty are encouraged to support and participate in the CALS Honors Program, distance education, and international education. Teaching and co-teaching courses in the areas of Plant genetics and Genomics is expected via existing courses, such as Advanced Genetics (PCB 5065-4 Cr., Team Taught), Journal Colloquium in Plant Molecular and Cellular Biology (PCB 7922) -1 Cr.) and development of a graduate course in Evolutionary and Ecological Genomics and Crop diversity (2-3 credits), based on identified gaps in the current course offerings and skill set of the candidate. Tenure will accrue in the Horticultural Sciences Department. Other than research and teaching duties described above, the faculty member will engage in Extension activities appropriately related to the program area. Because of the IFAS land-grant mission, all faculty are expected to be supportive of and engaged in all three mission areas—Research, Teaching and Extension—regardless of the assignment split specified in the position description. Background Information : The University of Florida is a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution, encompassing virtually all academic and professional disciplines, with an enrollment of more than 56,000 students. UF is a member of The Association of American Universities. The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences (IFAS) includes the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences , the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station , the Florida Cooperative Extension Service , the College of Veterinary Medicine , the Florida Sea Grant program, and encompasses 16 on-campus academic departments and schools, 12 Research and Educational Centers (REC) located throughout the state, six Research sites/demonstration units administered by RECs or academic departments, and Florida Cooperative Extension Service offices in all 67 counties (counties operate and maintain). The School of Natural Resources and Environment is an interdisciplinary unit housed in IFAS and managed by several colleges on campus. UF/IFAS employs nearly 4,500 people, which includes approximately 990 salaried faculty and 1,400 permanent support personnel located in Gainesville and throughout the state. IFAS, one of the nation’s largest agricultural and natural resources research and education organizations, is administered by a Vice President and four deans: the Dean of the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences, the Dean for Extension and Director of the Florida Cooperative Extension Service, the Dean for Research and Director of the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station, and the Dean for the College of Veterinary Medicine. UF/IFAS also engages in cooperative work with Florida A&M University in Tallahassee. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Education and Experience Required Qualifications: A doctorate (foreign equivalent acceptable) in Genetics or a closely related discipline is required. Candidates should have demonstrated skills in verbal and written communication, interpersonal relationships, and procurement of extramural funding. Candidates must be supportive of the mission of the Land-Grant system. Candidates must also have a commitment to UF core values. Preferred: Postdoctoral research, experience in building successful scholarly collaborations, demonstrated success in grant funding in genomic research and skills in teaching in the emerging genomic sciences are preferred. Special Instructions to Applicants: Nominations Nominations are welcome. Nominations need to include the complete name and address of the nominee. This information should be sent to: Please refer to Requisition # 541516 Dr. Bala Rathinasabapathi Chair, Search and Screen Committee University of Florida Horticultural Sciences Department, Gainesville, FL 32611 Telephone: 352-273-4847 Facsimile: 352-392-5653 Electronic Mail: brath@ufl.edu Application Information Individuals wishing to apply should go online to Careers at UF by 11:55 PM of the closing date and submit: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for 3 individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Unofficial transcripts The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541517/human-resources-generalist-iicollege-of-nursing": {
      "title": "Human Resources Generalist II-College of Nursing",
      "description": "Human Resources Generalist II-College of Nursing Apply now Job no: 541517 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Human Resources, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 31010100 - NR-DEAN-ADMINISTRATION Classification Title: Human Resources Generalist II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Human Resources Generalist II is a key member of the College of Nursing Human Resources team, providing comprehensive, customer-focused HR support throughout the employee lifecycle for faculty, staff, postdoctoral associates, OPS employees, graduate assistants, and other academic personnel. This is an in-person position that requires regular on-site presence to support the College's workforce and HR operations. Reporting to the Associate Director of Human Resources, this position is responsible for coordinating recruitment and hiring activities, onboarding and offboarding processes, payroll and leave administration, personnel transactions, records management, and general HR operations. The Human Resources Generalist II ensures compliance with university policies, state and federal regulations, and employment laws while delivering high-quality service to employees and leadership. The Human Resources Generalist II works closely with college leadership, faculty, staff, and administrators to provide guidance on human resources processes, policies, and procedures. This position supports the College's workforce needs through the efficient administration of HR transactions, staffing activities, employee support services, and operational initiatives that foster a positive employee experience and effective HR service delivery. The ideal candidate is detail-oriented, highly organized, and able to manage multiple priorities in a fast-paced academic healthcare environment. Success in this role requires strong interpersonal and communication skills, sound judgment, a commitment to confidentiality, and the ability to build collaborative relationships while providing responsive and professional human resources support. Recruitment and Staffing • Provides comprehensive support throughout the recruitment and hiring process for faculty and staff positions. • Coordinates position postings, recruitment activities, candidate communications, interview scheduling, and hiring processes. • Ensures recruitment activities comply with university policies, equal employment opportunity guidelines, and hiring procedures. • Assists hiring managers and search committees with recruitment timelines, candidate evaluations, interview processes, and hiring documentation. • Develops and coordinates campus visit itineraries for faculty and staff candidates, including scheduling meetings, travel arrangements, accommodations, and other logistics. • Drafts offer letters, appointment documentation, and related hiring materials. • Serves as a resource to hiring managers regarding recruitment procedures, employment requirements, and staffing best practices. • Supports workforce planning, recruitment outreach activities, and retention initiatives as assigned. Onboarding and Offboarding • Coordinates pre-employment activities, including background screenings, employment verifications, credential collection, and required documentation. • Facilitates onboarding processes for new faculty and staff to ensure a smooth transition into the College and University. • Prepares and distributes onboarding materials and coordinates access to systems, equipment, workspaces, and required resources. • Conducts new employee orientations and serves as a resource regarding policies, benefits, and university procedures. • Ensures completion of required onboarding documentation, training requirements, and compliance activities. • Coordinates employee separations, retirements, transfers, and offboarding activities. • Processes separation paperwork and facilitates completion of required separation procedures. • Coordinates the return of university property, removal of system access, and completion of offboarding checklists. • Maintains onboarding and offboarding records and identifies opportunities to enhance the employee experience. HR Administration, Payroll, Leave, and Employee Transactions • Processes a variety of personnel transactions, including hires, reappointments, employment changes, salary actions, funding changes, terminations, retirements, and other employee lifecycle events. • Coordinates payroll-related activities and reviews transactions for accuracy and compliance. • Reviews and processes personnel funding distributions and payroll adjustments. • Assists employees and supervisors with time reporting, leave administration, and payroll-related questions. • Processes leave requests and leave-related transactions in accordance with university policies and applicable regulations. • Coordinates Family and Medical Leave (FMLA) and other leave programs, including documentation, tracking, and system processing. • Ensures graduate assistants, fellows, OPS employees, and other personnel actions are processed accurately and timely. • Maintains personnel records and employment documentation in accordance with university and regulatory requirements. • Provides guidance to faculty, staff, and administrators regarding HR policies, procedures, and employment-related processes. • Collaborates with the Associate Director of Human Resources and University stakeholders to ensure efficient processing of personnel transactions and compliance with applicable regulations. Employee Support, Records Management, and Operational Excellence • Provides responsive customer service and HR support to faculty, staff, and administrators regarding employment processes and HR services. • Maintains personnel files, employment records, licensure documentation, academic credentials, and other required records. • Assists with compliance reporting, audits, credential tracking, and data requests. • Develops and maintains HR procedures, reference materials, and process documentation. • Participates in special projects, process improvement initiatives, and operational activities supporting College and University objectives. • Identifies opportunities to improve efficiency, accuracy, service delivery, and the employee experience. • Supports employee engagement, recognition, orientation, and other HR initiatives as assigned. • Performs other related duties to support the operational needs of the College of Nursing Human Resources Office. Expected Salary: $60,000-$65,000 Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: • Bachelor’s degree in Human Resources, Business Administration, Public Administration, or a related field. • Two or more years of professional human resources experience, preferably in higher education, healthcare, or other complex organizational environments. • Experience supporting faculty and staff recruitment, onboarding, payroll administration, leave processing, and employee lifecycle transactions. • Knowledge of human resources principles, practices, and applicable federal and state employment laws and regulations. • Experience interpreting and applying HR policies, procedures, and employment-related guidelines. • Demonstrated ability to manage multiple priorities, meet deadlines, and maintain a high level of accuracy and attention to detail. • Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, including Excel, Word, Outlook, Teams, and SharePoint. • Experience using Salesforce or similar customer relationship management (CRM) systems to track requests, manage workflows, and provide customer service support. • Experience utilizing Smartsheet for project tracking, workflow management, reporting, dashboards, or process improvement initiatives. • Experience using Asana or similar project management platforms to coordinate work, manage deadlines, and collaborate across teams. • Demonstrated ability to communicate effectively with diverse stakeholders, including faculty, staff, administrators, and external partners. • Strong customer service skills with the ability to handle sensitive and confidential information with discretion and professionalism. • Experience developing and maintaining process documentation, reference guides, and standard operating procedures. • Ability to analyze information, identify process improvements, and recommend solutions to enhance operational efficiency. • Experience working in a collaborative, team-oriented environment while maintaining the ability to work independently and exercise sound judgment. • Professional certification in Human Resources (SHRM-CP, SHRM-SCP, PHR, or equivalent) preferred. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541519/the-agency-administrative-services-manager": {
      "title": "The Agency Administrative Services Manager",
      "description": "The Agency Administrative Services Manager Apply now Job no: 541519 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Executive/Director/Management, Human Resources, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 23140100 - CJC-THE AGENCY-ADMIN Classification Title: MGR, Administrative Services Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: University of Florida College of Journalism and Communications — The Agency Bring Your Organizational Leadership to The Agency If you're a strategic organizer, collaborative problem-solver, and people-focused professional who enjoys turning complex operations into seamless experiences, we invite you to apply for The Agency Administrative Services Manager position at the University of Florida College of Journalism and Communications. The University of Florida College of Journalism and Communications is seeking The Agency Administrative Services Manager to provide operational and administrative leadership for The Agency, a dynamic environment where students gain hands-on experience working on real-world communications projects. The Agency Administrative Services Manager will oversee the administrative functions that support The Agency's day-to-day success. This position will manage office operations and physical space, coordinate student staff operations and human resources activities, oversee financial and personnel processes, support events and external communications, and serve as a central point of contact for students, clients, university partners, and other stakeholders. The ideal candidate is a highly organized and collaborative professional who can manage competing priorities, solve problems, coordinate resources, and keep complex operations running smoothly. This role is well suited for someone who enjoys working with people, supporting students, managing details, and contributing to a creative, goal-oriented team. What You'll Do Lead student staff operations by recruiting, hiring, onboarding, scheduling, supervising, and managing performance; develop staffing plans and allocate student resources based on project needs, skills, and capacity. Coordinate HR and payroll functions for student employees, including timekeeping, appointments, scheduling, budgets, and personnel matters, in partnership with the College HR team. Manage financial and administrative operations, including account reconciliation, invoices, reimbursements, travel expenses, purchasing, requisitions, encumbrances, and office supply needs. Support students and serve as a central point of contact for The Agency by coordinating immersion course registration, connecting students with academic resources, managing calendars and inquiries, and liaising with College departments, university partners, clients, and external stakeholders. Oversee Agency records, workspace, meetings, and events by maintaining organized files and physical space, coordinating stakeholder meetings and events, and supporting efficient day-to-day operations and workflows. Why Join The Agency? This is an opportunity to play a critical role in an environment where education, creativity, communications, and real-world experience come together. You will work closely with students, faculty, staff, clients, and university partners while helping create the operational foundation that allows creative teams to succeed. The Agency Administrative Services Manager will have the opportunity to make a meaningful impact by developing student talent, strengthening operational processes, coordinating resources, and supporting innovative communications work within one of the nation's leading journalism and communications programs. The Agency at UF, Marketing and Communications Agency Work Schedule Monday–Friday | 9:00 a.m.–5:00 p.m. Eastern Time Schedule flexibility may be available based on business needs and with approval from the Assistant Director of Operations. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Education and Experience Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and three years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Strong project coordination, organizational, and time-management skills, with exceptional attention to detail and the ability to manage multiple priorities. Ability to thrive in a fast-paced, goal-oriented, and collaborative environment while effectively managing competing priorities and stakeholder needs. Experience supervising staff and supporting students, with the ability to coordinate resources, schedules, and cross-functional teams. Strong problem-solving, critical-thinking, decision-making, and communication skills, both written and verbal. Proficiency with Microsoft Outlook, Word, and Excel, with familiarity using project management tools such as Monday.com, Airtable, Asana, or Trello. Experience in resource planning, scheduling, or coordinating teams in a professional or academic setting; experience in communications, advertising, marketing, or a creative agency environment is a plus. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please submit your applications online via CAREER AT UF Applications must include: A cover letter detailing previous journalistic or public media experience. A current résumé. Name, email addresses, and phone numbers of three references. Any other supporting documentation that you deem relevant to the position. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-4621 or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http//www.naces.org/. The application packet will be submitted as one PDF file through the UF Jobs website at https://jobs.ufl.edu/. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening, which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The University of Florida is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University is committed to non-discrimination with respect to race, creed, color, religion, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender identify and expression, marital status, national origin, political opinions or affiliations, genetic information and veteran status in also aspects of employment including recruitment, hiring, promotion, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits and training. Florida’s Government-in-the-Sunshine Laws require that all documents relating to the search process, including letters of application, nomination and reference, be available for public inspection. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541523/director-of-advancement-george-a-smathers-libraries": {
      "title": "Director of Advancement, George A. Smathers Libraries",
      "description": "Director of Advancement, George A. Smathers Libraries Apply now Job no: 541523 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Libraries/Museums, Advancement, Executive/Director/Management, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 55010600 - LB-LIB DEVELOPMENT-OB TRAVEL Classification Title: Director of Advancement Classification Minimum Requirements: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and three years of appropriate experience in Development or a related field; or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and five years of experience in Development or a related field. One year of supervisory experience. Job Description: JOB SUMMARY Few areas of a university have the ability to impact students, faculty, researchers, and communities as broadly as their libraries. The George A. Smathers Libraries are seeking a Director of Advancement to lead the vision and strategy for a comprehensive philanthropy and external relations program. In partnership with the Interim Dean of University Libraries and UF Advancement leadership, the Director will drive major gift fundraising, cultivate meaningful donor relationships, oversee stewardship and volunteer engagement, and build a strong philanthropic pipeline in support of the Libraries’ strategic priorities. This is a unique opportunity for an accomplished advancement professional to shape a growing fundraising program and inspire philanthropic investment in the resources, collections, and initiatives that fuel discovery and learning across the university and beyond. RESPONSIBILITIES Strategic Development Work closely with the Dean of Libraries and the Senior Assistant Vice President of Development to define, prioritize and develop plans to meet funding needs. Build a portfolio of fundraising prospects at the major gift levels ($100,000+) and strategically manage relationships through face-to-face visits to bring gift solicitations to closure. Within 18-24 months of the start date, develop a portfolio that will consistently raise $1.5 - $2 million per year or more in support of the George A. Smathers Libraries, averaging 120-160 personal visits annually and 10-15 gift solicitations per year of $25,000 or more, with emphasis on gifts of $100,000 or more. Build and manage a portfolio of major gift donors as well as manage a qualification pipeline. Serve as the primary fundraising strategist for Libraries, identifying, qualifying, and stewarding donor prospects up to the major gift level. Strategically manage relationships through face-to-face visits with Special Collections donors, prospects, and friends. Actively identify and research alumni and friends in targeted areas; work with the SAVP to develop and implement strategy for securing gifts in these areas, including advancement plans to inform constituents in identified markets about the funding needs and gift opportunities. Actively build donor pipeline through in-person visits, volunteer engagement, and other strategies. Work closely with the Dean, SAVP and the Office of Estate & Gift Planning in identifying and qualifying prospects who have the potential of making a planned gift to Libraries. Actively qualify and manage major gift prospects in accordance with their interests, capacity, and propensity, consulting with the SAVP on philanthropic engagement strategy and key campus partnerships for collaboration. Work with University Initiatives to increase parent and family giving to Libraries. In collaboration with the Dean of University Libraries, develop solicitation strategies on case-by-case basis, being cognizant of development office and Library needs and procedures. Oversee all stewardship efforts for the Libraries. Coordinate stewardship efforts with colleges and support units as well as other colleagues within UF Advancement and University of Florida, as appropriate. Acknowledge and recognize gifts in ways that are meaningful to donors and appropriate to the vision, mission, and values of the Smathers Libraries and UF Advancement, ensuring the advancement team is providing timely recognition, appropriate and meaningful recognition. L eadership & Management Recruit, hire, retain and develop the Smathers Libraries Advancement team including a Development Coordinator and Assistant Director of Development. Provide leadership, counsel, and accountability on matters relating to fundraising, including establishing goals in alignment with library objectives, developing metrics and strategic planning. Provide ongoing leadership and direction to team members in building the Libraries’ donor pipeline through the qualification and solicitation of leadership annual giving donors to the Libraries. Lead the Libraries participation in university wide fundraising campaigns and initiatives. External Relations & Volunteer Management Implement a series of strategic events designed to promote Special Collections and other library priorities, to engage a larger audience and build a major gift pipeline. Oversee the design and execution of fundraising plans that would include special events, VIP and donor lunches and dinners, campus visits with the dean, catered events, Library tours with the dean, team retreats, and meetings and initiatives that promote the engagement of donors and friends. Develop support for the program by effectively involving stakeholders, such as volunteers, staff, and members of the Library Leadership Board, in efforts supporting the fundraising program. Understand and articulate the unique contributions of Smathers Libraries to education, research, and service on local, national, and international levels. Oversee and manage activities and public support programs and events for the Smathers Libraries. Coordinate development and alumni activities with Libraries’ public relations staff; assist with Libraries public relations and promotional activities. Attend and strategically engage and cultivate donors and prospects at events, providing effective follow-up. Provide leadership and direction to the Development Coordinator in the management of a successful Friends of the Libraries program to generate gifts between $1,000 and $25,000 that will generate unrestricted support for the library, steward and cultivate those closest to the library, and build a pipeline of new potential donors. Lead the advancement team in executing fundraising initiatives for the Smathers Friends of the Libraries and other donor groups, effectively building a pipeline of prospects to ensure future success of the fundraising programs. Engage with the UFA Annual Giving Office to ensure successful Friends of the Libraries prospect and pipeline management. Provide leadership and support strategic planning efforts as they relate to Smathers Friends of the Libraries. Administrative Responsible for effective and timely prospect tracking, record keeping, reporting and administration aligned with the metrics and reporting from UF Advancement. Plan and monitor the budget, using resources for best results in fundraising and planning activities involving volunteers, prospects, faculty and administration. Ensure best practices in office systems, practices, files and record keeping; ensure effective use of strategic tools and resources such as contact reports, prospect management, research, analysis, and the donor database. Assist in evaluating and analyzing programs using established attributes and metrics to produce accurate reports, to guide effective decision making. Supervise the Libraries’ Development Coordinator and, once hired, the Assistant Director of Development. Library Leadership Board Cultivate the Leadership Board, engaging a small group of individuals who can support at a high level or make significant connections on behalf of the library. With the Dean of University Libraries, plan, manage, and successfully execute all Library Leadership Board meetings, events, and activities; responsible for preparation of meeting materials and logistics. Engage with individual prospects, including Library Leadership Board members. Coordinate stewardship reports and activities for individual prospects including Library Leadership Board members with appropriate members of the administration. Develop support for Smathers Libraries by effectively involving stakeholders, including Library Leadership Board Members, and staff efforts supporting the fundraising program. Create a structured process for the identification, recruitment, evaluation, recognition, and replacement of volunteers to strengthen fundraising effectiveness. Collaborate with the Dean and the SAVP regarding overall gift strategies for Libraries Leadership Board members as appropriate. Expected Salary: $98,000 - $115,000 annually, commensurate with education and experience. Exceptional Benefits We Offer: Affordable State Health Plans: Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Comprehensive Coverage: Life and Disability Insurance Secure Retirement Options: Generous plans to safeguard your future Paid Time Off: Including 11 paid holidays, as well as family, sick, and vacation leave Professional and Personal Growth: Opportunities through UF Training & Organization Development, leadership development, LinkedIn learning, and more Tuition Assistance: Benefit from the UF Employee Education Program PSLF Eligibility: We are a Public Service Loan Forgiveness Eligible Employer The University of Florida offers a competitive benefits package. Click here to learn more. Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and three years of appropriate experience in Development or a related field; or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and five years of experience in Development or a related field. One year of supervisory experience. Preferred: At least 4-6 years of major gift fundraising experience with documented success in cultivating and soliciting major gifts of $100,000 and above from individual prospects with campaign experience. At least 4-6 years of external affairs experience with success in cultivating relationships with individuals and corporate prospects. Prior experience in a college or university setting, enthusiasm for fundraising in a comprehensive research university, and the desire to work collegially within a goal-oriented fundraising organization. Proven experience leading a high performing team, including prior management of development officers. Track record of recruiting, developing, and retaining a high caliber staff, building a team, and creatively organizing and managing them to maximize the support of external constituencies. Proven track record of developing and implementing innovative fundraising strategies designed to maximize performance of a development team. This includes knowledge of fundraising principles, methods, and standards; a demonstrated ability to construct, articulate, implement, and monitor an annual strategic development plan; and an excellent track record of entrepreneurship, innovation, and creative problem solving. Expertise in developing solicitation strategies. The ability to work strategically, develop and implement constituency programs and activities, along with a talent for motivating volunteers. Business acumen and the ability to work collaboratively as a team player. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, strong interpersonal abilities, and the capacity to engage effectively and collaboratively with a wide range of individuals are essential. Support the University’s commitment to creating a respectful and collaborative work environment that values integrity, professionalism, and mutual respect in all interactions; demonstrated success collaborating with stakeholders; strong project management skills; experience in a data-driven environment; excellent organizational abilities; and the capacity to lead, influence, and inspire others are essential. Proficient in the following areas: Windows operating systems, Microsoft Word, Excel, and Access, as well as internet browsers and email platforms. Familiarity with donor databases, contact management systems, and tracking systems is also highly preferred. The ability and willingness to travel. A valid driver license and good driving record are essential.* *Business expenses are reimbursed through a standard accounting system. The ability to pay for expenses using your own personal credit card or funds and have them reimbursed is essential. Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Process In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume and three references. Apply by 11:55 pm (EST) on the application close date via the Jobs at UF online application system. Applications will be reviewed as received. If you have any questions or concerns about this process, please contact Tina Marie Litchfield, George A. Smathers Libraries Human Resources Office, at tlitchfield@uflib.ufl.edu . Work sponsorship is not available for this position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 20 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541524/assistant-director-of-bar-support": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Bar Support",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Bar Support Apply now Job no: 541524 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Education/Training/Instructional Design, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 24010000 - LW-DEANS OFFICE Classification Title: Program Manager Classification Minimum Requirements: A Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and five years of appropriate experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Program Manager of Bar Support helps provide strategic support for the Levin College of Law's academic success and bar readiness program, in collaboration with the Associate Dean for Academic Success and the Dean of Students, assisting in the assessment, enhancement, and ongoing development of program components to support students and graduates from matriculation through bar admission. The Program Manager works closely with faculty, administrators, staff, alumni, and external partners to identify and support students at risk of bar exam underperformance; helps design and deliver workshops, programs, and resources that promote student achievement, professional development, bar readiness, and licensure success; and supports the program's alignment with evolving accreditation standards and licensure requirements, including the NextGen bar exam. The Program Manager teaches academic success and/or bar preparation coursework, provides individualized advising and coaching to students and graduates, and contributes substantially to the structure and delivery of the Law School's bar readiness program. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS Student Advising, Coaching & Bar Exam Preparation: The Program Manager provides individualized academic advising, coaching, and bar exam preparation support to law students and graduates, including those identified as at risk of underperformance. Working with faculty, administrators, staff, alumni, and external partners, the Program Manager helps identify students at risk of bar exam underperformance and connects them with appropriate resources and support. Teaching, Curriculum & Program Delivery The Program Manager teaches one or more academic success and/or bar preparation courses each year and designs and delivers workshops, seminars, and resources on study strategies, exam-taking skills, substantive bar subjects, and professional development. The Program Manager contributes substantially to the structure and delivery of the Law School's bar readiness program. Program Strategy, Data Analysis & Compliance The Program Manager assists in the strategic planning, implementation, and continuous improvement of the Law School's academic success and bar readiness program. This includes analyzing student performance data (e.g., grades, bar results, practice exam scores) to identify at-risk students and evaluate program effectiveness; monitoring developments in bar admission requirements, including the transition to the NextGen bar exam; helping ensure program alignment with accreditation standards; and maintaining accurate records and preparing reports on program outcomes. Collaboration, External Relations & Other Duties The Program Manager collaborates with faculty, administrators, and staff to integrate academic success principles into the broader curriculum, and builds and maintains relationships with alumni, bar preparation vendors, and other external partners. The College of Law is growing and changing daily, and all College of Law community members, including the Program Manager, are expected to perform other duties as assigned in furtherance of the Law School's academic success mission. Expected Salary: $90,000 minimum Required Qualifications: A bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and five years of appropriate experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience Admission to a State Bar Preferred: J.D. from an ABA-accredited law school Florida Bar preferred, or willingness to obtain Demonstrated experience in academic support, bar preparation, legal education, or a closely related field. Strong understanding of bar exam content, format, and study methodologies Excellent teaching, coaching, and communication skills. Ability to work effectively and empathetically with a diverse student population Strong organizational skills and ability to manage multiple priorities Experience teaching in a law school academic success or bar preparation program Familiarity with the NextGen bar exam and current trends in bar licensure Experience analyzing student performance and outcomes data Experience developing curricula or programmatic content Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541525/visiting-assistant-professor-in-communication-design": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor in Communication Design",
      "description": "Visiting Assistant Professor in Communication Design Apply now Job no: 541525 Work type: Visiting Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Grant or Research Administration Department: 13020100 - COTA-ART-DIRECTOR Classification Title: VIS AST PROF Classification Minimum Requirements Candidates for this rank shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess equivalent qualifications based on professional experience. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Job Description: The School of Art + Art History in the College of the Arts at the University of Florida invites applications for a three-semester (Spring 2027, Fall 2027, Spring 2028) appointment as Visiting Assistant Professor of Communication Design. This position will contribute to a vibrant program with a forward-looking curriculum at a top public research university focused on cultivating research and the student experience. This position will teach undergraduate courses in the BFA and minor in Graphic Design, as well as our signature campus-wide UF Quest course on how design works as a tool for shaping, understanding, and communicating who we are in everyday life. Design studio courses focus on contemporary design practice and typically include design research, applying design principles to co-create solutions that meet users' needs, and interaction design. The Quest course is taught as a hybrid course, with students attending in-person sessions with a graduate student once per week to supplement asynchronous online content. As the lead instructor, this position’s role will be to manage the course, organize and deliver the asynchronous content, supervise and mentor graduate assistants, and design lesson plans for the in-person sessions. The University of Florida College of the Arts intends to be a transformative community, responding to and generating paradigmatic shifts in the arts and beyond. As artists, designers, and scholars, we embrace the complexity of our evolving human experience and seek to empower our students and faculty to shape that experience fearlessly through critical study, creative practice, and provocation. We seek a colleague who identifies as a changemaker. We seek a colleague who will position students as catalysts for justice on local and global levels. This position is covered by the United Faculty of Florida Contract. You can view this employment union contract here https://admin.hr.ufl.edu/compliance/employee-relations-and-ethics/union-negotiations/united-faculty-of-florida-contract/ . Responsibilities: Teach a 3-2 or 2-3 load in the Fall and Spring semesters We anticipate that 1–2 courses each year will be sections of UF Quest 1: Design + Identity in Everyday Life. We anticipate that the other courses will be upper-level courses in the BFA in the Graphic Design major or minor. Actively participate in our learning community, including finding innovative ways to connect design to critical discourse in other disciplines. Participate in shared governance through service to the design program, School, College, and University. SCHOOL OF ART + ART HISTORY: Organized within the College of the Arts, the School of Art + Art History nurtures a culture of critical inquiry in our scholarly and creative work. Our educational mission is to empower each student with knowledge, skills, and insight to engage thoughtfully with our changing world. The SA+AH believes that art, design, and scholarship are critical to our local, university, regional, national, and international communities. We pursue positive transformation and impact through socially engaged, local and global education, research, and creative works. Our community asks challenging questions, takes risks, and strives for excellence through an interdisciplinary, inclusive, and often collaborative practice. School of Art + Art History faculty publish, curate, and exhibit widely and internationally. They bring their scholarship and production into their teaching, offering innovative, engaged learning experiences to their students. We foster a diverse, equitable, and inclusive community through mutual respect and acceptance, assuming the best in others to enable a culture where everyone can flourish. Degree programs include the BA, BFA, MA, MFA, and PhD. Areas of study include art education, art history, design, museum studies, and studio art. The school has 400 undergraduate students enrolled in our majors and 190 graduate students in our residential programs and online art education MA. Also a part of the School of Art + Art History are the 4Most Gallery and the University Galleries—University Gallery, the Gary R. Libby Gallery, and Grinter Gallery — which provide exhibition space for contemporary art, including student work. The University of Florida is accredited by the Southern Association of Colleges and Schools Commission on Colleges and the National Association of Schools of Art and Design. The art education program is accredited by NCATE. For more information, visit www.arts.ufl.edu/art . THE COLLEGE OF THE ARTS: The College of the Arts is an engine of transformation that generates paradigmatic shifts in the arts and beyond, while nurturing adaptability, innovation, and excellence. We foster an environment where novel creative practice, leading research, and experiential learning thrive. Our students, faculty, and staff interrogate and respond to the ever-changing relationship between technology and the human spirit. We prepare future leaders in artistic professions and creative industries, while developing student capacity for interdisciplinary scholarship that serves and engages local and global communities. The College offers baccalaureate, masters, and doctoral degrees. Approximately 1,600 students are pursuing majors in degrees offered by the College of the Arts under the direction of 150 faculty members in its three accredited schools— the School of Art + Art History, the School of Music, and the School of Theatre + Dance, and in the Center for Arts in Medicine, the Digital Worlds Institute, and the Center for Arts, Migration, and Entrepreneurship. In addition, the college comprises the University Galleries, and the University level of the New World School of the Arts in Miami. The University of Florida: The University of Florida is a comprehensive learning institution built on a land grant foundation. We are The Gator Nation, a diverse community dedicated to excellence in education and research and shaping a better future for Florida, the nation and the world. Our mission is to enable our students to lead and influence the next generation and beyond for economic, cultural and societal benefit. UF is a graduate research institution with more than 50,000 students and membership in the prestigious Association of American Universities. Gainesville, which is consistently ranked as one of the nation’s most livable cities, is located midway between the Gulf of Mexico and the Atlantic Ocean. Together, the University and the community comprise the educational, medical and cultural center of North Central Florida. In addition to salary, the University of Florida (UF) offers low cost State Health plans, a number of Dental plans to fit you and your family’s needs, and Vision. Domestic partner coverage through GatorCare is also available. Optional plans such as life, disability, legal and accident insurance are also available. UF provides a variety of leave programs including vacation, sick leave, 11 paid holidays, personal leave days, and family medical leave. Nine-month faculty employees accrue approximately 13 sick days annually, and 4 personal leave days, typically used during the holiday closing period (accruals based on full-time employment). UF also gives you flexibility to handle life’s challenges with paid family leave: eight full weeks over a 24-month period. Build a retirement roadmap with competitive pension plans, investment accounts and a host of voluntary add-ons, such as 457 deferred compensation and 403(b) plans. UF Employees are also eligible for the GatorPerks discount program , which can provide savings at various business and retailers! Explore UF’s plethora of benefit options here: FACULTY Benefits Expected Salary: $70,000 annually with a comprehensive and highly competitive leave and benefits package Required Qualifications: The successful candidate must meet all of the following: Terminal degree (e.g., MFA, MDes, PhD) in Communication Design or closely related field. Experience as instructor of record in higher education beyond graduate student assistantship. Knowledge of contemporary design issues, conceptual approaches to design, design thinking, and design research. Evidence of skill in designing and facilitating academic work that advances curiosity, respect, open intellectual discourse, and the welcome incorporation and well-being of all in an environment of complex differences. Preferred The successful candidate will meet some of the following: Evidence of success as an undergraduate instructor, preferably in a BFA or other bachelor’s program that prepares students for professional practice Experience teaching and/or professionally creating interaction design Experience with community-based projects that connect academic research with public interest, for example, co-design and/or participatory design projects. Evidence of a commitment to innovative and cross-disciplinary curricula. An active engagement with design research, creative work, or professional practice Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Deadline: Review of applications will begin October 13, 2026, and continue until an applicant pool is established. To ensure full consideration, submit all application documents by October 9, 2026. Applications received after this date may be considered at the committee's and/or hiring authority's discretion. Application Process: Applications must be submitted via the University of Florida’s online application system. Online applications must include the following: (1) a detailed cover letter that explains how you match the particular qualifications of this position and how your work and perspective will contribute to and enhance our transformative community; (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) a PDF portfolio (one file, 5 MB maximum) or a link to a website, which includes 10-15 examples of your creative research, scholarship, professional praxis, or related production, and a research statement; (4) a PDF teaching portfolio (one file, 5 MB maximum) or a link to a website, which includes a teaching philosophy and materials from 1–2 design courses a course syllabus, semester timeline, and 5–10 examples of student outcomes with assignment description(s); and (5) names and contact information of four professional references, among which one must be a staff member with whom you have worked in the past. The UF system will give you the option to upload letters of reference. We are not requiring letters of reference with initial application materials. Please ignore that option. The Search Committee may request additional materials at a later time. Inquiries may be sent to: Professor Maria Rogal Chair, Visiting Assistant Professor in Communication Design Email: mrogal@ufl.edu Please keep in mind that search committee members, not AI or other software, review application materials. The selected candidate must provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES). Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541527/student-assistant-software-developer": {
      "title": "Student Assistant - Software Developer",
      "description": "Student Assistant - Software Developer Apply now Job no: 541527 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Computer Science, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 29680240 - MD-CTSI-SRVC CNTR-CTS-IT Classification Title: STU-AST - CLERICAL & ADMIN Classification Minimum Requirements: This position is open to all UF students who are eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student STAS and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. Registered students are employed on a part-time basis, up to 20 hours per week. Job Description: The Student Assistant assists with data entry and the development of data-driven applications, systems, and infrastructure to support biomedical research within the Department of CTS-IT at the University of Florida. Candidates should be self-starters who are willing to perform data entry and data management tasks using Python and/or R. Experience with Linux/UNIX is a plus. Candidates must understand and follow security requirements and best practices when working with human research data. They should be comfortable following established designs and specifications to build applications and systems, while also knowing when to ask questions or seek guidance from their team lead when they encounter a blocker. Experience working as part of a team is desired. Additional experience with source control, software testing, unit testing, time tracking, and task management is a plus. Strong written and verbal communication skills are preferred. Technical experience with one or more programming languages is beneficial, with specific experience in Python, R, Bash, Git or GitHub considered a plus. This position is ideal for students who are interested in gaining practical software development and research data experience while working alongside a technical team. Expected Salary: $18.00 per hour Required Qualifications: This position is open to all UF students who are eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student STAS and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. Registered students are employed on a part-time basis, up to 20 hours per week. Preferred Qualifications: Students are encouraged to apply early in their graduate studies and should have an interest in working in the position for at least two semesters or longer, if their academic schedule allows. Special Instructions to Applicants: Interested students should submit a resume and cover letter. This is a part-time position of up to 20 hours per week during business hours (8:00 AM–5:00 PM). Students can work four-hour shifts in the following blocks: Morning: 8:00 AM–12:00 PM Afternoon: 1:00 PM–5:00 PM Both: 8:00 AM–12:00 PM and 1:00 PM–5:00 PM Scheduling will be coordinated based on the student's availability and the needs of the team. Please contact Mel Moreno at CTS-IT, melimore86@ufl.edu, for any questions about this position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541528/provost-and-executive-vice-president-for-academic-affairs": {
      "title": "Provost and Executive Vice President for Academic Affairs",
      "description": "Provost and Executive Vice President for Academic Affairs Apply now Job no: 541528 Work type: Other Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Executive/Director/Management Department: 02010100 - PV-ADMINISTRATION Classification Title: Executive VP & Provost Classification Minimum Requirements: Must have a terminal degree and a strong academic record at the full-professor level, combined with substantial senior academic administrative experience. Job Description: Key Responsibilities This scholar-leader will combine distinguished academic credentials with exceptional institutional leadership in support of the following key responsibilities: Serve as the University’s chief academic officer and principal advisor to the President on academic matters. Develop, articulate, and execute a compelling academic vision aligned with UF’s mission, strategic priorities, and aspirations for national and international leadership. Work collaboratively with deans, faculty, academic leaders, students, and staff to establish university-wide academic priorities and translate them into measurable outcomes. Strengthen UF’s position as a premier public research university and enhance its academic reputation, impact, and competitiveness. Identify emerging opportunities for academic innovation, interdisciplinary collaboration, and institutional distinction. Recruit, retain, develop, and recognize a world-class faculty committed to excellence in teaching, research, and service. Champion excellence in teaching, research, scholarship, creative activity, clinical practice, and public service. Strengthen faculty development, mentoring, leadership development, and career advancement. Ensure that promotion, tenure, evaluation, compensation, and recognition systems support academic excellence and institutional priorities. Foster a culture characterized by intellectual vitality, academic freedom, professional respect, transparency, collegiality, and shared responsibility. Work with the Board of Trustees, Florida Board of Governors, and other governing bodies on academic matters and regulatory requirements. Critical Leadership Capabilities The next Provost should demonstrate exceptional capability in the following areas: Visionary Academic Leadership: The ability to articulate a bold, coherent academic vision and translate that vision into priorities, investments, structures, and measurable results. Institutional Scale and Complexity: The capacity to lead across a large, comprehensive research university with wide-ranging academic disciplines, professional schools, health sciences, research enterprises, and external constituencies. Faculty Credibility and Trust: The intellectual standing and authenticity to earn the respect of faculty while engaging constructively with differing perspectives and maintaining strong standards of academic excellence. Strategic Resource Allocation: The ability to make difficult choices about people, programs, facilities, and financial resources while protecting academic quality and investing strategically in areas of opportunity. Talent Leadership: A demonstrated ability to identify, recruit, develop, retain, and empower exceptional faculty, deans, and academic leaders. Collaboration Across Boundaries: The ability to break down institutional silos and create productive partnerships among colleges, disciplines, research centers, health sciences, students, industry, government, and communities. Change Leadership: Organizational skills to lead significant changes while maintaining institutional stability, engaging stakeholders, communicating clearly, and preserving trust. Student-Centered Leadership: A genuine commitment to ensuring that academic excellence translates into exceptional educational experiences and outcomes for students at every level. Research and Innovation Leadership: The ability to identify emerging opportunities, make strategic investments, and position UF for greater research impact, innovation, entrepreneurship, and national leadership. Public and Land Grant University Stewardship: An appreciation for the distinctive responsibilities of a public land-grant university and its obligations to students, the state of Florida, and society, including a commitment to connecting the University’s academic and research strengths with the needs of communities across the state. Governance and Political Acumen: The judgment to navigate effectively among faculty governance, university administration, trustees, state-level governance, government partners, and external stakeholders while maintaining the integrity of the academic mission. Communication and Relationship Building: The ability to communicate a compelling vision, listen deeply, explain difficult decisions, build coalitions, and represent the University with credibility and confidence. Data-Informed and Technology-Enabled Leadership: The ability to use evidence, analytics, emerging technologies, and artificial intelligence thoughtfully to improve academic quality, institutional effectiveness, research productivity, and student success. Student Success and Educational Excellence: Advance an outstanding undergraduate, graduate, and professional educational experience. Leadership Priorities The successful candidate will be expected to work with the President and University community to: Define and execute a compelling next-generation academic strategy for UF. Further elevate UF’s standing among leading public research universities. Recruit and retain exceptional faculty and academic leaders. Strengthen research competitiveness and interdisciplinary collaboration. Advance educational innovation and student success. Identify strategic opportunities presented by artificial intelligence and other transformative technologies. Strengthen academic program quality, relevance, and sustainability. Build greater alignment among university-wide priorities, college strategies, and resource allocation. Foster a culture of academic excellence, innovation, intellectual openness, shared governance, and institutional trust. Advance UF's land-grant mission by strengthening the University's engagement and impact across the state of Florida, while extending its national and global reach. Total Rewards As one of the nation’s top public research universities, The University of Florida is dedicated to elevating benefits offerings that support the wellbeing of faculty and staff and to be responsive to a range of individual and family needs. The salary for this role will be commensurate with experience. About the University of Florida UF, the state’s flagship institution, is a top-ranked public research university located in Gainesville. With 16 colleges, a major academic health center, and championship-winning athletics, UF is a leader in education, research, and innovation. Its 2,000-acre campus integrates historic charm with state-of-the-art facilities and preserved natural spaces, fostering interdisciplinary collaboration. Ranked 7th among U.S. public universities in the 2026 U.S. News Best Colleges rankings, UF continues to drive advancements across multiple fields. As Florida’s land-grant university, UF has a distinctive responsibility to translate its strengths in education, research and innovation into meaningful impact for the people and communities of Florida. Through its statewide reach and partnerships, UF connects academic expertise and discovery with the needs and opportunities of a rapidly growing and changing state. With over 62,000 students, 33,000 employees, and a $10 billion annual budget, UF is among the largest and most well-funded universities in the nation. A member of the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU), UF boasts 6,600+ faculty, including National Academy members and award-winning scholars. In 2026, UF research expenditure surpassed $1.34 billion, demonstrating a strong commitment to discovery and technological advancement. UF’s entering Class of 2030 is the largest and most academically qualified in the university’s history, with 7,725 students selected from 96,000 applicants. The university leads in artificial intelligence education, incorporating AI into 200+ courses taught by 300 AI-focused faculty, supported by HiPerGator, the most powerful university-owned supercomputer in the U.S. UF’s strength lies in its comprehensive academic offerings, robust state support, and forward-thinking vision. With continuous growth and transformation, UF is dedicated to its mission “to be a premier university that the state, nation, and world look to for leadership.” For more information, visit www.ufl.edu . Expected Salary: Commensurate based on experience and education. Required Qualifications: Must have a terminal degree and a strong academic record at the full-professor level, combined with substantial senior academic administrative experience. Preferred: Experience overseeing academic programs, faculty and staff, budgets, strategic planning, and university-wide initiatives Demonstrate strong knowledge of shared governance, accreditation, faculty development, student success, and the ability to work effectively with presidents, faculty, deans, trustees, and other stakeholders. Significant experience in a research-intensive environment and a demonstrated record of institutional leadership is expected. Special Instructions to Applicants: The Search Committee will begin reviewing applications immediately and will continue to accept applications and nominations until the position is filled. For full consideration, applications must be submitted by November 1, 2026, at 11:55 p.m. Applications should include a current curriculum vitae and a letter of interest describing relevant experience and interest in the position. Submission of materials via email is strongly encouraged. Nomination letters should include the name and contact information of the nominee. Application materials should be submitted to: Vickie Antolini, Senior Client Partner Anna Flagg, Senior Associate Korn Ferry Organization Consulting 2005 Market Street, Suite 910 Philadelphia, PA 19103 Email: UF-Provost@kornferry.com Refer to code “UF-Provost” in subject line The University of Florida is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541529/ast-prof": {
      "title": "AST PROF",
      "description": "AST PROF Apply now Job no: 541529 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Business/Accounting/Finance, Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 17020100 - BA-MANAGEMENT BUS OFFICE Classification Title: Assistant Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. degree or equivalent in Management Job Description: The University of Florida, in Gainesville, Florida is seeking an Assistant Professor for the Department of Management. Duties and responsibilities include teaching, research, and service. Teaching duties include teaching graduate-level courses, mentoring PhD students, providing effective instruction in strategic management, and participating in departmental service. Research duties center on the Future of Work, with expectations to conduct high-quality studies that advance understanding of anticipated changes in the workforce, work tasks, work environments, management practices, organizational adaptation, and strategic leadership. Position also requires impactful research published in top-tier journals while contributing meaningfully to the department and broader academic community. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. degree or equivalent in Management Special Instructions to Applicants: For more information about the position, including instructions on how to apply, please visit us on-line at UF Careers website, and reference job opening ID: 541529. UF is a member of the State University System of Florida and an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment based on merit. Application should include the name and contact information of three professional references. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 29 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541530/uf-health-physician-hematology-medical-oncology-medicine": {
      "title": "UF Health Physician - Hematology & Medical Oncology  -  Medicine",
      "description": "UF Health Physician - Hematology & Medical Oncology - Medicine Apply now Job no: 541530 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: St. Johns Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 30050000 - JX-MEDICINE AT JAX Classification Title: UF Health Physician - Hematology/Oncology - Medicine Classification Minimum Requirements: Licensed to practice medicine in accordance with Chapter 458, Florida Statutes plus four years of appropriate experience. Completion of a residency program may substitute for the required experience. Job Description: UF College of Medicine - Jacksonville is seeking to hire a Hematology & Medical Oncology Physician to join UF Health North and UF Health St. Johns, Florida. This is an outstanding opportunity to be a part of a patient-centered, collegial group committed to delivering high-quality clinical care. The selected candidate(s) will enjoy a market-based, competitive salary and comprehensive benefits package. UF Health – The Power of Together! At the University of Florida College of Medicine - Jacksonville and UF Health, together, we strive to create unstoppable momentum toward the goal of improving individual and community health through discovery, clinical and translational science and technology, exceptional education and patient-centered, innovative, high-quality health care. At the heart of our mission is to provide high-quality clinical care and to promise every patient their best experience possible. UF College of Medicine - Jacksonville is seeking to hire a Hematology & Medical Oncology Physician to join UF Health North and UF Health St. Johns, Florida. This is an outstanding opportunity to be a part of a patient-centered, collegial group committed to delivering high-quality clinical care. The selected candidate(s) will enjoy a market-based, competitive salary and comprehensive benefits package. About UF Health: UF Health represents the commitment of more than 30,000 faculty and staff dedicated to delivering outstanding patient care, advancing medical research, and educating the next generation of health care professionals. With a network that includes 12 hospitals and numerous outpatient locations across Gainesville, Jacksonville, and Central Florida, UF Health is a leader in high-quality, patient-centered care. About UF Health Durbin: UF Health Durbin is a 42.5 acre health and wellness campus that will advance health care services as part of UF’s regional expansion plan for Jacksonville and St. Johns County. Located in northeast St. Johns County, UF Health Durbin Park will include a full-service 99-bed hospital with a focus on acute, intensive, emergency care, orthopaedics, neurosurgery, general surgery, cardiovascular medicine, and women’s services as well as a multidisciplinary medical office building. Future potential site development include the ability to expand hospital beds as well as an ambulatory surgical center, outpatient imagining and additional medial office building. We Offer Exceptional Benefits: Explore your benefit options at https://hr.ufl.edu/benefits/eligibility/staff-teams/ Low-cost State Health Plans: Medical, Dental, and Vision InsuranceLife and Disability Insurance Florida Retirement System Retirement Plans Paid Time Off including for vacations, holidays, paid family leave, and sick leave Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Eligible Employer Sovereign immunity malpractice status No state taxes Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: Licensed to practice medicine in accordance with Chapter 458, Florida Statutes plus four years of appropriate experience. Completion of a residency program may substitute for the required experience. Federal DEA Certification Completion of an accredited Internal Medicine residency Completion of an accredited Hematology/Oncology fellowship Preferred: Specialty License or Certification; Board certified or Board Eligible in Specified Area of Medical Specialty; Certification/Licensure in the state of Florida and Federal DEA Certification Experience participating in multidisciplinary oncology programs Clinical research or quality improvement experience Interest in teaching medical students and residents Experience utilizing EPIC electronic health record Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, please submit an application at Careers at UF with the following documents attached: Resume/Curriculum Vitae The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at https://naces.org/members/ The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. This requisition will be used to fill multiple positions. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541532/executive-vice-president-for-health-affairs": {
      "title": "Executive Vice President for Health Affairs",
      "description": "Executive Vice President for Health Affairs Apply now Job no: 541532 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Executive/Director/Management Department: 27000000 - OFFICE OF HEALTH AFFAIRS Classification Title: Executive Vice President for Health Affairs Classification Minimum Requirements: Substantial and progressive leadership experience within the academic health sciences as dean, institute director or other broadly scaled leadership role, with responsibility for strategy development and implementation, educational and research program development, and financial management. Personal success in establishing and expanding an institutional research program in the health sciences, including strategic development and implementation in collaboration with scholars and practitioners outside the candidate’s own field; success developing and leading multidisciplinary educational programs in collaboration with other university or institutional leaders. Job Description: KEY RESPONSIBILITIES Strategic Vision Develops, articulates, resources and implements a strategic vision for the University of Florida’s health colleges, centers and institutes that supports the university’s and UF Health’s strategic plans. Acts in all areas as a forward-thinking and transformational leader. Research and Academic Excellence Holds overall responsibility and authority for research and education across the health sciences (UF health educational programs include clinical education programs and undergraduate, graduate and professional education). Fosters and supports excellence in basic, clinical and translational research programs in the health colleges and their contributions across the university. Operations Leverages the educational and research strengths of UF health sciences in alignment with the clinical mission of UF Health. Ensures the university’s health colleges and related programs are operated in a financially sound manner, and that capital and other investments are used efficiently. Develops a structured process to optimize decision-making and outcomes. With the UF Health President/System CEO and the UF President, develops and administers financial models supporting the clinical, educational and research missions of the health sciences. Governance While reporting to the President, leads collaboration with the health sciences’ college deans and other senior leaders. Provides leadership and direction for UF’s academic programs in the health sciences. Culture Develops and fosters an environment of respect, competence, transparency, innovation and principle-driven management. Ensures that interdisciplinary collaborations enhancing educational and research programs in the health sciences are encouraged and supported. Supports and champions collaboration across all of UF. Advocacy Cultivates strong, credible and trusting relationships with alumni, donors, government leaders and related external stakeholders in support of the educational, research and clinical missions of UF and UF Health. Advocates for the initiatives of constituent units in the health sciences. Acts as an effective representative and spokesperson for the health sciences at the university. Plans and executes fundraising initiatives pertaining to the health sciences in support of college and university activities. Fundraising and Development The EVPHA will play a leadership role in fundraising for the academic health center (UF Health overall), leading efforts in the university’s capital campaign to support the organization’s growth and strategic initiatives. DESIRED OUTCOMES Growth Oversee successful expansion and successful transformation of the educational and basic, translational and clinical research enterprises of the health sciences at the university in alignment with UF and UF Health goals. Ensure growth is coordinated with inter-university initiatives and contributes to revenue-enhancing strategies, e.g., through new educational program offerings, strategic recruitment of outstanding research groups and integration of research opportunities into clinical care and population health. Research Improve quality and quantity of basic, translational, clinical and population-based health sciences research and expand research activity to increase the overall impact of UF’s research enterprise. Foster collaborative, programmatic research programs that encompass health science and non-health science colleges, institutes and centers. Education Continue development and implementation of an innovative and interprofessional educational strategy. Support the health colleges’ education and training initiatives. Enable greater achievement in workforce development through support of initiatives across the health sciences at the University of Florida. UF Health Alignment Promote the growth, reputation and impact of the health colleges and the clinical enterprise by enhancing education and research, including the development of innovative educational strategies and development of novel prevention and therapeutic programs. UNIVERSITY OF FLORIDA The University of Florida (UF) is the state’s flagship institution, a top-ranked public research university located in Gainesville. With 16 colleges, a major academic health system and championship-winning athletics, UF is a leader in education, research and innovation. Its 2,000-acre campus integrates historic charm with state-of-the-art facilities and preserved natural spaces. Ranked 7th among U.S. public universities in the 2026 U.S. News & World Report’s “Best Colleges” rankings, UF continues to drive advances across multiple fields. With over 62,000 students, 33,000 employees and a $10 billion annual budget, UF is among the largest and most well-funded universities in the nation. A member of the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU), UF boasts over 6,600 faculty, including National Academy members and award-winning scholars. In 2025, UF research expenditures surpassed $1.3 billion, demonstrating a strong commitment to discovery and technological advancement. UF’s entering Class of 2030 is the largest and most academically qualified in the university’s history, with 7,725 students selected from 96,000 applicants. The university leads in artificial intelligence education, incorporating AI into 200+ courses taught by 300 AI-focused faculty, supported by HiPerGator, the most powerful university-owned supercomputer in the U.S. UF’s strength lies in its comprehensive academic offerings, robust state support and forward-thinking vision. With continuous growth and transformation, UF is dedicated to its mission “to be a premier university that the state, nation and world look to for leadership.” Visit www.ufl.edu for more information. UF HEALTH UF Health, the world-class academic health system of the University of Florida, combines scientific innovation at campuses around Florida with outstanding compassionate care at a network of hospitals and clinical practices throughout the state. The $6.5 billion system optimizes collective expertise to improve patient care, education, discovery and the health of our communities. With main campuses in Gainesville and Jacksonville as well as satellite sites in Central Florida, St. Johns County, Ocala and several other locations, UF Health provides quality health care to patients across Florida. The country’s only academic health center with six health-related colleges (Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine) and a partner teaching hospital located on a single, contiguous campus, the system features 13 hospital facilities in total (including two teaching hospitals and five specialty hospitals), more than 100 physician medical practices and outpatient sites, and research centers and institutes, including The Herbert Wertheim UF Scripps Institute for Biomedical Innovation and Technology, located in Jupiter. UF Health is a world leader in interdisciplinary research, generating more than half of UF’s total research awards. Research activities reflect a depth of purpose by focusing on the translational nature of biomedical research, following fundamental research from clinical research to patient care. The backbone of UF Health is a talented and dedicated workforce of more than 32,000 faculty and staff who provide lifesaving care and research breakthroughs for more than four million patients who come to UF Health each year from around the U.S. and more than 40 countries. UF Health is committed to improving lives and health through a variety of community-based programs and activities as part of the state’s “safety-net’’ hospital system, often caring for people who have little or no medical coverage. Expected Salary: Commensurate based on experience and education. Required Qualifications: Substantial and progressive leadership experience within the academic health sciences as dean, institute director or other broadly scaled leadership role, with responsibility for strategy development and implementation, educational and research program development, and financial management. Personal success in establishing and expanding an institutional research program in the health sciences, including strategic development and implementation in collaboration with scholars and practitioners outside the candidate’s own field; success developing and leading multidisciplinary educational programs in collaboration with other university or institutional leaders. Preferred: IDEAL EXPERIENCE Executive Leadership in Complex Academic Environments Substantial and progressive leadership experience within the academic health sciences as dean, institute director or other broadly scaled leadership role, with responsibility for strategy development and implementation, educational and research program development, and financial management Research and Education Leadership Personal success in establishing and expanding an institutional research program in the health sciences, including strategic development and implementation in collaboration with scholars and practitioners outside the candidate’s own field; success developing and leading multidisciplinary educational programs in collaboration with other university or institutional leaders. Operational Acumen Demonstrated managerial abilities and enthusiasm for the administrative and financial responsibilities of the position, with understanding of the financial pressures facing academic health centers and health care delivery organizations and the principles of mission-based budgeting and funds flow. External Leadership and Engagement Demonstrated success representing a complex academic health enterprise and building strong relationships with external stakeholders, including donors, alumni, community partners, government leaders and other key constituencies. Experience leading philanthropic engagement and fundraising efforts while effectively advancing institutional priorities at the state and federal levels. Personal Scholarship Strong preference for a physician-scientist or clinician-scientist with a strong record of scholarship, including a record of substantial, extramural peer-reviewed funding and national/international recognition; academic credentials sufficient to engender respect within the University of Florida and UF Health and support appointment as a tenured full professor. CRITICAL LEADERSHIP CAPABILITIES Acting Strategically The EVPHA will partner with constituent deans and UF/UF Health senior leaders to develop, refine and execute a strategy that optimizes UF’s scholarship in the health sciences and contributions to the clinical mission by: Clearly articulating the future direction and ideal culture of the organization while leading by example and challenging established practices, as necessary. Identifying and prioritizing critical factors in making strategic decisions affecting the university’s health colleges and programs. Discovering, creating and expanding synergies between colleges in the health sciences. Pursuing revenue-enhancing opportunities through new or expanded education/research offerings. Addressing opportunities in the larger research landscape to enhance UF’s position as a leading AAU institution. Identifying and pursuing opportunities to advance the health sciences through philanthropy, government partnerships and other external sources of support. Collaborating and Influencing The EVPHA will develop relationships with all stakeholders to effectively guide the health sciences’ contributions to UF and UF Health by: Demonstrating appreciation for all the health sciences and supporting inter-college collaboration while championing collective activity across all UF entities. Connecting productively within the health sciences enterprise, with the broader university, and across UF Health to develop long-term collaborations and partnerships. Developing a culture that respects, values and promotes the dignity of UF’s students, staff, faculty and stakeholders. Building and maintaining productive relationships with donors, alumni, government leaders, policymakers and other external stakeholders, working in partnership with university leadership, advancement and government relations to advance UF and UF Health priorities. Leading Transformation The EVPHA should be able to thrive in a complex, fast-paced institution that is executing change on multiple fronts while balancing complementary and competing priorities and be prepared to lead the academic mission of UF’s health sciences by: Identifying and leading changes necessary to advance education and research in the health sciences, embracing transformational initiatives (e.g., Artificial Intelligence at UF, Drug Discovery at The Herbert Wertheim UF Scripps Institute for Biomedical Innovation & Technology) to elevate the stature and productivity of the university. Adapting agreed-upon plans to the political realities, strengths and constraints of the university, health system and state, engaging with all relevant stakeholders to gain necessary buy-in through customized influence efforts. Establishing practices that encourage and reinforce innovation and impact new thinking in UF’s health colleges, institutes and centers. Special Instructions to Applicants: The University of Florida is being assisted in this recruitment by Spencer Stuart. For questions about the opportunity, to nominate an individual or to indicate personal interest in being considered as a candidate, please email the search’s confidential mailbox at UF_EVPHealthAffairs@spencerstuart.com .For full consideration, applications must be submitted by November 1, 2026, at 11:55 p.m. FOR ADDITIONAL INFORMATION, PLEASE CONTACT: Chuck Jordan, Consultant Office telephone: 404.504.4429 E-mail address: cjordan@spencerstuart.com Assisted by: Mary Elizabeth “M.E.” Welton Office telephone: 404.504.4463 E-mail address: mewelton@spencerstuart.com Kathryn Sugerman, Consultant Office telephone: 310.443.3525 E-mail address: ksugerman@spencerstuart.com Assisted by: Kimberly Harvalis Office telephone: 310.443.3510 E-mail address: kharvalis@spencerstuart.com Shannon L. Yeatman, Consultant Office telephone: 404.504.4436 E-mail address: syeatman@spencerstuart.com Assisted by: Melinda Lee Office telephone: 404.504.4437 E-mail address: mklee@spencerstuart.com Donna Russell, Senior Associate Office telephone: 617.897.3327 E-mail address: drussell@spencerstuart.com Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541534/streetlight-program-assistant-department-of-pediatrics": {
      "title": "Streetlight Program Assistant - Department of Pediatrics",
      "description": "Streetlight Program Assistant - Department of Pediatrics Apply now Job no: 541534 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29094400 - MD-PEDS-STREETLIGHT Classification Title: OPS Program Assistant Classification Minimum Requirements Classification Minimum Requirements are the baseline requirements for Staff Titles and Academic Titles found at the University of Florida. Job Description: The Streetlight Program Assistant (OPS) herein supports the Streetlight program’s mission to provide age-appropriate peer support to chronically ill adolescents as a model of pediatric palliative care. The Streetlight Program Assistant assists with administrative duties, patient support, and volunteer support. ADMINISTRATIVE SUPPORT Recruits, screens, and orients new volunteers Reviews EPIC charts and filters patient census and medical information into a Streetlight team-member appropriate \"need-to-know\" format Records daily patient referrals from medical staff and consolidates information into task-lists for team members Manages Streetlight encrypted SharePoint site featuring weekly team educational readings, announcements, and educational web links Manages REDCap database including transcription of patient demographics, diagnoses, and volunteer psychosocial chart entries Facilitates communication and collaboration with interdisciplinary team members to best meet the needs of patients enrolled in Streetlight VOLUNTEER SUPPORT Assists with new volunteer orientation and continued education in palliative care, chronic and terminal illness, HIPAA compliance and best practices to provide psychosocial care to the target population Conducts project-based volunteer meetings Organizes and archives team education materials Coordinates team member graduation ceremony and other social events PATIENT SUPPORT Introduces Streetlight program to new designated patients Provides psychosocial support to patients including inpatient room visits, outpatient appointment visits, and event planning Provides end-of-life support including creation of legacy projects Regularly coordinates activities for patients/families (milestone events, unique long-term patient events, etc.) including management of logistics, catering, and community involvement. Organizes weekly meal deliveries for Cystic Fibrosis patients through the BJ’s CF Dining Days Program PROGRAM PROMOTION AND EXPORTATION Maintains Streetlight active patient database, used for program census Expected Salary: $20 per hour Required Qualifications: Qualifications refer to evidence-based or demonstrated knowledge, skills, abilities, and experiences required for the role. For TEAMS positions the required qualifications should reflect Classification Minimum Requirements. For Faculty roles, these qualifications may reflect college-specific requirements needed for success the role. Preferred Qualifications Graphic design, video/photo editing, additional languages, social media proficiency, video game proficiency, group facilitation Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 28 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/532862/clinical-coder-iii-department-of-neurosurgery": {
      "title": "Clinical Coder III - Department of Neurosurgery",
      "description": "Clinical Coder III - Department of Neurosurgery Apply now Job no: 532862 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Veteran's Preference Eligible, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29190000 - MD-NEUROLOGICAL SURGERY Classification Title: Clinical Coder III Job Description: The Department of Neurosurgery is seeking an experienced Surgical Coder to provide specialized diagnosis and procedural coding for operative procedures and inpatient consulting services performed by our faculty in Gainesville and at Halifax Regional Medical Center. The ideal candidate will have strong knowledge of anatomy, medical and surgical terminology, coding practices, and insurance billing requirements. As part of the Lillian S. Wells Department of Neurosurgery at the University of Florida College of Medicine, this position supports a nationally recognized academic department providing comprehensive care for complex neurological conditions across adult and pediatric populations. The department combines advanced clinical care with innovative research, clinical trials, education, residency, and fellowship programs. This role offers an opportunity to contribute to a collaborative environment while developing specialized expertise in neurosurgical coding. Key Responsibilities Perform highly specialized diagnosis and procedural coding for operative procedures and inpatient consulting services provided by Department of Neurosurgery faculty in Gainesville and at Halifax Regional Medical Center. Review operative reports, progress notes, and other clinical documentation to identify billable services and accurately assign or verify ICD-10 and CPT codes. Verify physician attestation statements and other required documentation to support compliant coding and billing. Conduct comprehensive chart reviews to ensure all appropriate services are captured and meet regulatory, documentation, and coding requirements. Ensure proper documentation of teaching provider involvement for services performed with residents and fellows. Monitor operative documentation and follow up on unsigned or missing operative records to support timely charge entry. Enter and maintain accurate charges for surgical procedures and non-surgical inpatient services in Epic. Monitor, research, and resolve TES edits and other coding or billing issues within Epic. Verify patient demographics, guarantor information, insurance coverage, and claim data for accuracy and resolve discrepancies. Establish and maintain guarantor and insurance accounts in Epic, including subscriber relationships, policy information, claim addresses, authorizations, injury information, and other required billing data. Verify insurance eligibility through electronic systems and direct communication with insurance carriers. Review encounter information and identify emergency admissions, hospital-to-hospital transfers, endovascular cases, and other encounter characteristics for statistical and departmental reporting. Collaborate with the Assistant Director and departmental leadership to determine appropriate surgical charges and evaluate insurance reimbursement. Monitor and analyze denials, write-offs, reimbursement trends, and billing issues, identifying opportunities to improve revenue cycle performance and insurance collections. Assist department leadership in developing and implementing processes that improve coding accuracy, documentation, reimbursement, and overall revenue cycle performance. Research complex coding questions using authoritative resources, including professional coding organizations, federal and private payer guidelines, coding networks, and other industry resources. Provide coding and billing education and guidance to attending surgeons, fellows, residents, and other clinical staff. Keep faculty and trainees informed of changes to coding requirements, payer guidelines, documentation standards, and billing practices. Serve as a subject matter expert and resource for neurosurgical coding, developing and maintaining specialized knowledge of complex neurosurgical procedures and applicable coding requirements. Participate in monthly Coder Forum meetings to review Epic product-line performance, coding trends, and opportunities for improvement. Conduct independent research and prepare reports and analyses related to coding practices, reimbursement, compliance, and departmental performance. Participate in professional development activities, including coding meetings, seminars, classes, and continuing education, to maintain current knowledge and coding expertise. Meet regularly with the Department Chair, Administrator, Assistant Director, and/or other departmental leaders to address coding, documentation, reimbursement, and product-line issues. Serve as a liaison between the Department of Neurosurgery and internal and external partners, including University of Florida Physicians (UFP), the UF Compliance Office, and other clinical, coding, and revenue cycle stakeholders. Expected Salary: $25.40 - $28.38 per hour Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and five years of experience in professional medical coding, with experience in two or three clinical specialties. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Certified Professional Coder (CPC)/ American Academy of Professional Coders (AAPC) or Certified Coding Specialist (CCS-P) required. Preferred Qualifications: Knowledge of medical terminology, surgical coding. Knowledge of business and office practice. Knowledge of Excel, and other applicable software. Ability to analyze financial data and able to generate understandable reports. Ability to communicate effectively, verbally and in writing. Special Instructions to Applicants: For consideration, please complete an application and include a cover letter, resume and the names and email addresses of three to five references. This position is eligible for veteran’s preference. If you are claiming veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. This requisition has been reposted and extended. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540384/maintenance-generalist-ii-jupiter-fl": {
      "title": "Maintenance Generalist II - Jupiter, FL",
      "description": "Maintenance Generalist II - Jupiter, FL Apply now Job no: 540384 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Palm Beach Categories: Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 37021000 - SR-FACILITIES Classification Title: Maintenance Generalist II Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and two years of relevant experience. Appropriate vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: The full-time Maintenance Generalist II will support the demands of the Institute’s maintenance operations and perform services and repairs which include welding, plumbing, painting, carpentry and as a locksmith. The position is considered essential and must be available during emergencies, such as hurricanes. Responsibilities and Duties: General Maintenance Operations: Builds and/or installs shelves, cabinets, lab tops, doors and desk keyboard trays; hangs signs, pictures and boards; repairs door hinges, knobs and closures. Assist with event setup and general repairs. Replaces ballasts, electrical outlets, light switches and light bulbs; hangs light fixtures; repairs or replaces frayed power cords and plugs. Inspects sinks, drinking fountains, urinals, toilets, unclogs drains, install pipes, performs light repairs of sinks and garbage disposals. Replaces floor and ceiling tiles, caulks around bathroom fixtures. Works with specialized lab support equipment including hoods, incubators, freezers or other lab equipment as assigned. Moves equipment, freezers and furniture. Responds to emergency calls and equipment alarms; evaluates problem and makes repairs or requests appropriate specialist. Supply Maintenance: Estimates labor and material costs for work requests; may order supplies. Performs routine work orders and preventative maintenance tasks, as assigned. Accurately records daily hours worked and time spent on individual tasks. Works daily with the Facilities Computer Maintenance Management System (CMMS) Software (Micromain); BAS software, and the Footprints work order system. The ability to communicate effectively in English, both verbally and in writing, is an essential function of this position. May perform related tasks and work of other Facilities department personnel, as required or assigned. Interprets blueprints, drawings, layouts or other specifications to determine installation or repair requirements. Expected Salary: $25.00 per hour. Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and two years of relevant experience. Appropriate vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: Previous experience working in a research, hospital, or university setting is preferred. Previous experience with plumbing, electrical, mechanical, carpentry, and/or welding experience is preferred. Previous trade experience (1 or more) in plumbing, electrical, pipefitting, HVAC, etc. is preferred. Excellent interpersonal communication and writing skills and the ability to evaluate mechanical and electrical systems. Must have a valid driver’s license issued from the state where the position is located. Must be available on holidays, weekends, and emergency responses as the property is open 24 hours a day, 7 days a week. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Please provide names and email addresses of three to five references as part of the application process. When ready, the hiring department will contact the listed references via email requesting their reference letters to be uploaded directly to the application website. This position is eligible for veteran’s preference. If you are claiming veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See the Qualified Positions link on our applicant FAQs page for a list of eligible positions. Join our facilities team with the opportunity to receive a competitive sign-on bonus! We’re looking for dedicated professionals to help maintain and enhance our facilities. This limited-time bonus is available to new hires who join our team and meet eligibility requirements—apply today and take advantage of this exciting opportunity! This position is open until filled, and application review will begin July 3, 2026. The University of Florida is a member of the State University System of Florida and an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment based on merit. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: Yes. Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540575/student-account-specialist-i-collections": {
      "title": "Student Account Specialist I, Collections",
      "description": "Student Account Specialist I, Collections Apply now Job no: 540575 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 64801000 - FA-UNIVERSITY BURSAR-ADMIN Classification Title: Student Account Specialist I Classification Minimum Requirements: A bachelor's degree, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: This position within the University Bursar is located in the CFO Division. The primary focus of this position is to assist with collection efforts for students who have become delinquent on loans administered by the University. Counsels borrowers about potential deferment, cancellation, and forbearance options. Submits economic hardship, deferment, or forbearance requests to the Accounting Manager for approval. Coordinates submission of student loan accounts to collection agencies. Assists with collection efforts prior to sending to collection agencies. Assists with timely placement to collection agencies, monitoring collection agency activity, and reviewing monthly payment processing. Provides account documentation when requested by collection agencies. Communicates with currently enrolled students with past due accounts receivable balances to enlist the student in a payment plan or to otherwise collect on the balance due. Monitors student holds to ensure service indicators are preventing students from progressing in their education without settling past due accounts. Creates proactive student communication to increase student retention while minimizing balances past due. Respond to student inquiries regarding credit bureau, delinquent status, academic transcript requests, or other disputes. Counsels' students and borrowers with financial holds about available options. Provides supporting documentation on charges, loans, and payments to outside agencies as appropriate. Assists current university or State of Florida employees to pay past due accounts via payroll deductions or the state wage withholding program. Performs all other duties as required. Expected Salary: $19.16 - $22.00 per hour Preferred: Ability to plan, organize, and coordinate files, records, and work assignments. Ability to effectively communicate verbally and in writing. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with others. Ability to work independently and as a team. Ability to utilize problem-solving techniques Experience with MS Word and Excel Ability to determine work priorities, assign work, and ensure proper completion of work assignments. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540780/clinical-coder-iidepartment-of-neurosurgery": {
      "title": "Clinical Coder II-Department of Neurosurgery",
      "description": "Clinical Coder II-Department of Neurosurgery Apply now Job no: 540780 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veteran's Preference Eligible, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29190000 - MD-NEUROLOGICAL SURGERY Classification Title: Clinical Coder II Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and three years of professional medical coding experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Certified Professional Coder (CPC)/ American Academy of Professional Coders (AAPC) or Certified Coding Specialist (CCS-P) required. Job Description: The position is responsible for reviewing clinical documentation and entering accurate diagnoses and procedural codes for operative procedures and inpatient consulting services performed by the Department of Neurological Surgery faculty at both Gainesville and Halifax. Key Responsibilities Review operative reports, progress notes, and other clinical documentation to identify billable services and determine the appropriate ICD-10 and CPT codes. Verify physician attestation statements and ensure required documentation is complete and supports coding and billing requirements. Enter and maintain accurate charges for surgical procedures and non-surgical inpatient services in Epic. Perform comprehensive chart reviews to ensure all appropriate services are captured and coding and regulatory requirements are met. Monitor and resolve TES edits in Epic, addressing discrepancies and ensuring charges are accurately processed. Verify patient demographics, insurance coverage, and billing information and resolve discrepancies as needed. Establish and maintain guarantor and insurance information in Epic, including subscriber relationships, policy information, claim addresses, authorizations, dates of injury, and other required claim details. Verify insurance eligibility through electronic resources and direct communication with insurance providers. Review patient and encounter information to determine appropriate classifications, including emergency admissions, hospital-to-hospital transfers, and endovascular cases, for departmental reporting and statistical purposes. Maintain accurate databases containing surgical charges, diagnosis codes, CPT codes, dates of service, and attending physician information. Monitor operative documentation and follow up on unsigned or missing operative reports to support timely and accurate billing. Ensure appropriate documentation of teaching provider involvement for services involving residents and fellows. Work collaboratively with the Associate Director and departmental leadership to evaluate surgical charges, coding practices, and insurance reimbursement opportunities. Research complex or evolving coding questions using professional coding organizations, federal and private payer guidelines, coding networks, and other authoritative resources. Serve as a resource to attending surgeons, fellows, residents, and staff by providing coding education, guidance, and support. Keep faculty and trainees informed of coding, documentation, billing, and regulatory changes that may affect reimbursement. Participate in monthly Coder Forum meetings to discuss Epic product-line performance, coding trends, challenges, and opportunities for improvement. Collaborate with departmental leadership to develop and implement processes that improve coding accuracy, compliance, documentation, and reimbursement. Conduct independent research and prepare reports and analyses related to coding practices, reimbursement, and departmental performance. Participate in professional development opportunities, including coding seminars, classes, meetings, and continuing education, to maintain current knowledge and skills. Expected Salary: $24.50-$27.50 per hour Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and three years of professional medical coding experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Certified Professional Coder (CPC)/ American Academy of Professional Coders (AAPC) or Certified Coding Specialist (CCS-P) required. Preferred: Knowledge of medical terminology, surgical coding Certification as a coder, either CPC (American Academy of Professional Coders) or CCS-P (American Health Information Management Association). Knowledge of business and office practice; Knowledge of Excel, and other applicable software. Ability to analyze financial data and able to generate understandable reports. Ability to communicate effectively, verbally and in writing. Special Instructions to Applicants: For consideration, please complete an application and include a cover letter, resume and the names and email addresses of three to five references. The University of Florida is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer. To claim veterans' preference, indicate the claim on the application and provide required documentation by uploading a DD-214 (or equivalent certification from the Department of Veteran Affairs) and any additional supporting documentation based on the type of claim. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted and extended. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541416/visiting-lecturer": {
      "title": "Visiting Lecturer",
      "description": "Visiting Lecturer Apply now Job no: 541416 Work type: Visiting Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 16940100 - LS-WRITING PGM GENERAL Classification Title: Visiting Lecturer Job Description: The University Writing Program at the University of Florida invites applications for a Visiting Lecturer with the working title Assistant Instructional Professor, to begin January 1, 2027. This is a full-time, nine-month, non-tenure-accruing faculty appointment with the possibility of renewal for a maximum of three years. The University Writing Program is a thriving, independent academic unit offering a wide range of writing-intensive courses in areas such as first-year composition, themed critical thinking and writing, discipline-focused writing, and professional writing. A significant goal of the University Writing Program is to cultivate effective visual and written communication in academic and professional contexts. The teaching assignment is 3/3. The successful candidates will teach writing-intensive courses, support students in developing effective written, visual, and rhetorical communication, and contribute to the instructional mission of the University Writing Program. Applicants should have rhetorical expertise and teaching experience. Advertised Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Minimum Requirements: PhD in Composition and Rhetoric or a related content area with teaching experience in composition. Preferred Qualifications: Rhetorical expertise and teaching experience in writing, composition, or communication. Relevant professional experience in writing, communication, or a related field. Strong interest or experience in professional, community-based, or discipline-specific communication. Interest or experience in experiential learning, including student exchanges, study abroad courses, or community-based outreach. Interest or experience with artificial intelligence, digital media, visual communication, or related areas of writing instruction. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: Letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position A complete curriculum vitae Names and contact information for three professional references Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Review of applications will begin on September 28, 2026 and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 16 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541448/assistantassociate-professorfamily-medicine": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor-Family Medicine",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor-Family Medicine Apply now Job no: 541448 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30160000 - JX-COMMUNITY MEDICINE-JAX Classification Title: Assistant/Associate Professor-Family Medicine Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates must be MD/DO and have a Florida Medical License Job Description: The Department of Community Health and Family Medicine at the University of Florida College of Medicine-Jacksonville seeks a family medicine/internal medicine physician, MD/DO, to join us. The position is for Clinical Assistant/Associate Professor level on a clinical non-tenure accruing track at our outpatient care office - UF Health Family Medicine. This position is up to fulltime and will report to the medical director and chair of the department at the University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville. Base salary is based on experience and guaranteed with significant additional productivity incentives and benefits package. UF Health is the Southeast’s premier academic health center and an integral part of the University of Florida. Our mission is to promote health through outstanding and high-quality patient care, innovative and rigorous education in the health professions and biomedical sciences, and research across the spectrum of basic, translational and clinical investigation. UF Health encompasses the Gainesville-based University of Florida colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine; the UF Health Shands family of hospitals in Gainesville; UF Health Jacksonville medical center; UF Health North; an academic campus in Jacksonville that is home to the UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville and includes degree programs offered by the colleges of Nursing and Pharmacy; UF Health St. Johns in St. Augustine; UF Health Neighborhood Hospital in Ocala; and our campus in Central Florida, which includes UF Health Spanish Plaines Hospital and UF Health Leesburg Hospital. It also includes primary care and specialty practices throughout Central, North Central and northern Florida and Southeast Georgia. The UF Health network of hospitals and physician practices manages more than 3 million inpatient and outpatient visits each year, and serves patients from all 67 Florida counties, from across the nation and from dozens of countries around the world. About College of Medicine – Jacksonville (COM-J): The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three colleges at the Health Science Center Jacksonville. The college's 16 clinical science departments house more than 500 faculty members, 1,560 staff and 450 residents and fellows, as well as medical students from UF and around the country. We offer an incredible breadth of clinical training programs and proud to train many of best primary care providers and specialists throughout the region, the state and the country. Research, discovery and innovation are critical aspects of our clinical campus and we have some of the country’s leading researchers at our locations searching for and finding new treatments and clinical options. The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville faculty, administrators, residents, fellows, students and staff work as a team in pursuit of our common mission — to heal, to comfort, to educate and to discover through quality health care, elimination of health disparities, medical education, innovation and research. To learn more about our college, leadership, mission, faculty resources, and the city of Jacksonville visit https://med.jax.ufl.edu/administrative-affairs/faculty-orientation-guide/ . Expected Salary: Negotiable based on Qualifications and Experience. Preferred: Board certified or board eligible. Special Instructions to Applicants: The Search Committee will begin to review applications on November 1, 2026. Please provide letter of intent and Curriculum Vitae. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541468/recsports-student-assistant-safety-instructor": {
      "title": "RecSports Student Assistant - Safety Instructor",
      "description": "RecSports Student Assistant - Safety Instructor Apply now Job no: 541468 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Recreation/Event Services, Student Services Department: 04020100 - SL-RECSPTS ADMIN Classification Title: STU-AST-NON-CLERICAL & ADMIN Job Description: The Department of Recreational Sports is currently looking for Safety Instructors to join our energetic team and move Gators toward lifelong wellbeing! The Recreational Sports Safety Instructor position is responsible for instructing safety courses on standards set by the American Red Cross. Successful candidates will begin work at the start of Spring of 2027. For questions or additional information, please contact Adam Caldwell, caldwelladam@ufl.edu . For more information about the role and onboarding process, visit: RecSports Student Employment Webpage. RecSports Team Member Responsibilities Develop professional relationships with co-workers, participants, and guests. Uphold and exceed the expectations of the Cornerstones of RecSports Service: accountability, anticipating needs, attitude, and atmosphere. Demonstrate a desire to learn and apply knowledge as a RecSports ambassador. Take ownership of RecSports facilities and programs by exhibiting a responsible work ethic. Promote and maintain cleanliness and appearance within and surrounding RecSports facilities. Enforce RecSports policies and procedures to promote participant safety and satisfaction. Respond to all emergency situations and adhere to established protocol including pre- and post- incident procedures. All RecSports employees are expected to abide by the RecSports Student Staff Career Readiness Principles that clarifies shared expectations and standards in effort to best serve our patrons, develop each team member, and prepare student employees for future career endeavors. Job Position Responsibilities Teach all course materials to the standards set forth by the American Red Cross by following the Instructor Manual, while observing the set safety measures and guidelines. Arrive before the start of class, as scheduled, to collect supplies and prepare for class. Dress professionally and act responsibly. Provide a safe and welcoming environment, conducive to a productive learning environment. Use a variety of learning tactics to provide a learning opportunity for all participants. Be available to answer participant questions, demonstrate skills, and provide global and individual feedback. Clean and disinfect all supplies after use and return to appropriate storage location. Complete Safety Course Shift reports at the conclusion of each course, noting any issues with equipment, supplies, technology, and/or participants. Attend mandatory meetings and/or trainings. Enforce RecSports policies and procedures being courteous and helpful to individual students and classes. Maintain an acceptable level of knowledge through a personal commitment to learning new information, instructional techniques, and updating/obtaining new certifications. Participate in the facilitation of the Safety Audit program and in-service trainings, as needed. Perform all other duties as assigned by the Aquatics & Risk Management Administrative Team. RecSports Fall Kick-Off 2027: Tuesday, August 17th 8am – 9:30am Fall Kick-Off is our all-staff event to discuss department-wide updates, facilitate unit training sessions, as well as cultivate community and positive energy within our RecSports team. All RecSports Student Staff are expected to attend and will be paid for their training time. Expected Salary: $16.50 per hour Required Qualifications: Requirements/Qualifications University of Florida student Contribute 3-5 hours/month, dependent up on course schedule (evening and weekend hours) Required: Current American Red Cross CPR/AED/First Aid Certification or recognized equivalent or willing to obtain by start date Must demonstrate a basic understanding of effective instruction strategies and tools Complete and maintain UF online certifications in GET803, OOC101, PRV802, ITT102, GET811 This position is open to all students who are eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS) and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. Registered students are employed on a part-time basis, up to 20 hours per week. *Federal Work Study students are encouraged to apply! Special Instructions to Applicants: Questions: Contact Adam Caldwell at caldwelladam@ufl.edu. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541480/postdoctoral-scholar-in-natural-language-processing-and-mammalian-evolution": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Scholar in Natural Language Processing and Mammalian Evolution",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Scholar in Natural Language Processing and Mammalian Evolution Apply now Job no: 541480 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Education/Training/Instructional Design, Grant or Research Administration Department: 56021500 - NH-MAMMALS Classification Title: Postdoctoral Scholar in Natural Language Processing and Mammalian Evolution Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in natural language processing, computer science, data science, ecology, evolutionary biology, biodiversity informatics, bioinformatics, or a closely related field, completed or expected to be completed before the start date. Job Description: Centuries of field observations, dietary studies, and taxonomic accounts contain an extraordinary amount of information about what mammals eat. However, these data remain embedded in unstructured text, scattered across thousands of publications, and are reported using inconsistent terminology and formats. This project seeks to unlock these hidden data by developing a human-in-the-loop AI pipeline that uses LLMs to read scientific literature at scale, extract consumer-food relationships, and convert qualitative descriptions into standardized ordinal dietary rankings suitable for evolutionary biology research. Our central hypothesis is that the published literature contains a vast reservoir of latent dietary information that can be systematically transformed into reproducible quantitative datasets. Existing mammalian diet databases rely heavily on manual expert curation, limiting their scope and scalability. By combining AI-assisted information extraction with expert validation, this project will create high-resolution dietary datasets spanning the mammalian tree at a scale that would be impractical through manual curation alone. The Postdoctoral Researcher will play a leading role in developing the dietary ranking framework and constructing the resulting database. Working closely with an interdisciplinary team of ecologists, biodiversity scientists, and AI researchers, the successful candidate will contribute to the development and evaluation of LLM-based information extraction methods, analyze large-scale dietary datasets, and help advance computational approaches for ecological synthesis. The Postdoctoral Researcher will conduct independent and collaborative research, publish in leading scientific journals, contribute to grant proposals, mentor graduate and undergraduate students, and help shape the future direction of this growing interdisciplinary research program. The project also provides excellent opportunities to develop expertise in AI for biodiversity science while building a strong publication record and collaborative network for an academic or research career. This position will initially be awarded for one year and, contingent upon strong performance and conduct and the availability of funds, may be renewed for up to three years. The University of Florida is an Equal Opportunity institution. Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the United States. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law. Expected Salary: $60,000 Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in natural language processing, computer science, data science, ecology, evolutionary biology, biodiversity informatics, bioinformatics, or a closely related field, completed or expected to be completed before the start date. · Demonstrated research experience in at least one of the following areas: o Natural language processing, large language models (LLMs), information extraction, or machine learning; or o Ecology, evolutionary biology, biodiversity informatics, or similar, with a focus on large-scale, quantitative research. · Strong programming skills in Python and experience with scientific computing, data processing, and reproducible research workflows. · Experience designing, implementing, and evaluating computational methods or data analysis pipelines. · Excellent written and oral communication skills, with evidence of peer-reviewed publications or a strong publication trajectory. · Ability to work independently while contributing effectively to an interdisciplinary, collaborative research team. Preferred: · Experience with modern NLP and LLM techniques, including transformer models, prompting strategies, named entity recognition, relation extraction, or information extraction from scientific literature. · Experience working with biodiversity, ecological, evolutionary, or biological trait datasets. · Familiarity with annotation workflows, corpus development, or evaluation of machine learning models. · Experience with statistical analysis, multivariate methods, or comparative and phylogenetic analyses. · Experience using high-performance computing (HPC), cloud computing, or large-scale machine learning workflows. · Familiarity with open science, FAIR data principles, and research data management. · Experience mentoring undergraduate or graduate students and contributing to collaborative research projects. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. Application reviews will begin Dec 15, 2026. Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. A complete application includes: A complete curriculum vitae Letter of intent, summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position, two page maximum. Names and contact information of three professional references We expect the Postdoctoral Researcher to begin in late 2026 or spring of 2027, with some flexibility in start date. Initial review of applicants will begin on 15 October 2026. Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541489/recsports-ops-massage-therapist": {
      "title": "RecSports - OPS Massage Therapist",
      "description": "RecSports - OPS Massage Therapist Apply now Job no: 541489 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Allied Health, Recreation/Event Services, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 04020100 - SL-RECSPTS ADMIN Classification Title: OPS Time-Limited Job Description: The Department of Recreational Sports is currently looking for a Massage Therapist to join our energetic team and move Gators toward lifelong wellbeing! Massage Therapists at the Department of Recreational Sports are provided with a room, electric massage table, sheets, oil, and a laundering service. Therapists will also have access to RecSports marketing channels and materials for promotional opportunities. Therapists are provided access to all RecSports facilities, fields, and programs, including group fitness, weight and cardio rooms, Lake Wauburg, intramural sports, and more! For questions or additional information, please contact Emma Edgeworth at edgeworthemma@ufl.edu . RecSports Team Member Responsibilities Develop professional relationships with co-workers, participants, and guests. Uphold and exceed the expectations of the Cornerstones of RecSports Service: accountability, anticipating needs, attitude, and atmosphere. Demonstrate a desire to learn and apply knowledge as a RecSports ambassador. Take ownership of RecSports facilities and programs by exhibiting a responsible work ethic. Promote and maintain cleanliness and appearance within and surrounding RecSports facilities. Enforce RecSports policies and procedures to promote participant safety and satisfaction. Respond to all emergency situations and adhere to established protocol including pre- and post- incident procedures. All RecSports employees are expected to abide by the RecSports Student Staff Career Readiness Principles that clarifies shared expectations and standards in effort to best serve our patrons, develop each team member, and prepare student employees for future career endeavors. Job Position Responsibilities Provide Massage Therapy modalities to clients within the scope of the State of Florida Massage Therapy license. Maintain a clean work environment including treatment areas. Monitor Massage Therapy materials (sheets, oils, etc.), keeping treatment rooms well stocked at all times. Provide directions and information to valid users and guests regarding facilities and programs. Answer main phone lines and direct calls to appropriate personnel, take Massage Therapy reservations, and answer facility or program inquiries. Learn the responsibilities and tasks required of the supervising positions. Contribute to the promotion of the Massage Therapy Program with regards to activities, events, programs, and off-site services. All RecSports employees are expected to abide by the RecSports Student Staff Career Readiness Principles that clarifies shared expectations and standards in effort to best serve our patrons, develop each team member, and prepare student employees for future career endeavors. RecSports Fall Kick-Off 2027: Tuesday, August 17th 8am – 9:30am Fall Kick-Off is our all-staff event to discuss department-wide updates, facilitate unit training sessions, as well as cultivate community and positive energy within our RecSports team. All RecSports Student Staff are expected to attend and will be paid for their training time. Expected Salary: $34-$36 per hour Required Qualifications: Requirements/Qualifications State of Florida Massage Therapy License and proof of professional liability insurance First Aid, CPR, AED certification or ability to attain it within the first week of employment Interest in health and wellness, recreation, fitness, and campus involvement Availability to work shifts as well as by appointment, including weekend hours Availability to work three permanent shifts, at minimum, each week, totaling at least 10 hours per week Complete and maintain UF online certifications in GET803, OOC101, PRV802, ITT102, GET811 This position is open to all students who are eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS) and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. Registered students are employed on a part-time basis, up to 20 hours per week. *Federal Work Study students are encouraged to apply! Preferred: Preferred Qualifications 1 to 3 years of hands-on experience Certifications in specialized modalities Experience with dynamic cupping and hot stone Background in a clinical or spa environment Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants applying for this requisition may be hired as either Student Assistants or OPS employees. If hired, applicants who are full-time University of Florida students will be hired as Student Assistants; all other applicants will be hired as OPS employees. This position is open to all students who are eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS) and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. Registered students are employed on a part-time basis, up to 20 hours per week. This is a time-limited position. For more information, please reach out to Emma Edgeworth at edgeworthemma@ufl.edu . Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541494/recsports-student-assistant-outdoor-adventures-associate": {
      "title": "RecSports Student Assistant - Outdoor Adventures Associate",
      "description": "RecSports Student Assistant - Outdoor Adventures Associate Apply now Job no: 541494 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Recreation/Event Services, Student Services Department: 04020100 - SL-RECSPTS ADMIN Classification Title: STU-AST-NON-CLERICAL & ADMIN Job Description: The Department of Recreational Sports is currently looking for Outdoor Adventures Associates to join our energetic team and move Gators towards lifelong wellbeing! Outdoor Adventures Associates serve the department by working in a team environment to provide excellent customer service through outdoor gear rentals, merchandise sales, and ensuring a superior CORE facility for our users. Successful candidates will begin work at the beginning of Spring 2027. For questions or additional information, please contact Braja Smith at b.smith2@ufl.edu . For more information about the role and onboarding process, visit RecSports Student Employment Webpage . RecSports Team Member Responsibilities Develop professional relationships with co-workers, participants, and guests. Uphold and exceed the expectations of the Cornerstones of RecSports Service: accountability, anticipating needs, attitude, and atmosphere. Demonstrate a desire to learn and apply knowledge as a RecSports ambassador. Take ownership of RecSports facilities and programs by exhibiting a responsible work ethic. Promote and maintain cleanliness and appearance within and surrounding RecSports facilities. Enforce RecSports policies and procedures to promote participant safety and satisfaction. Respond to all emergency situations and adhere to established protocol including pre- and post- incident procedures. All RecSports employees are expected to abide by the RecSports Student Staff Career Readiness Principles that clarifies shared expectations and standards in effort to best serve our patrons, develop each team member, and prepare student employees for future career endeavors. Job Position Responsibilities Assist patrons and participants with rentals and adventure trip registrations in-person and over the phone. Perform sales and rental transactions for outdoor gear and merchandise. Maintain cleanliness and organization of the Outdoor Adventures facility and warehouse. Assist with preparation and cleaning of adventure trip gear. Make appropriate referrals to other departments at UF RecSports Clean, maintain, and repair rental gear. Manage gear rental reservations, check-ins, and check-outs. Inspect rental gear for damages. Regularly communicate with administrative staff Willingness to innovate and develop new systems as Outdoor Adventures expands its scope of offerings. RecSports Fall Kick-Off 2027: Tuesday, August 17th 8am – 9:30am Fall Kick-Off is our all-staff event to discuss department-wide updates, facilitate unit training sessions, as well as cultivate community and positive energy within our RecSports team. All RecSports Student Staff are expected to attend and will be paid for their training time. Expected Salary: $15.00 per hour Required Qualifications: Requirements/Qualifications Successful completion of departmental training once hired. Shadow Shift Requirement: New Outdoor Adventures Associates are required to complete at least one shadow shift Interest in outdoor recreation and campus involvement Outdoor recreation knowledge/experience preferred Availability to work weekend and weekday facility hours. Ability to attend monthly Outdoor Adventures Associate meetings. Commitment of approximately 9 hours/week, or a minimum of two shifts per week. Complete and maintain UF online certifications in GET803, OOC101, PRV802, ITT102, GET811. Successfully complete background check. This position is open to all students who are eligible to work on campus and will be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS) and must maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. Registered students are employed on a part-time basis, up to 20 hours per week. *Federal Work Study students are encouraged to apply! Special Instructions to Applicants: Applicants must submit a resume to be considered. For more information, contact Braja Smith at b.smith2@ufl.edu . Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541507/tenuretrack-assistant-or-associate-professor-in-media-and-technology": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant or Associate Professor in Media and Technology",
      "description": "Tenure-Track Assistant or Associate Professor in Media and Technology Apply now Job no: 541507 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Communications/Public Relations/Marketing, Education/Training/Instructional Design, Grant or Research Administration Department: 23030000 - CJC-MEDIA PROD, MGMT, TECH Classification Title: AST/ASO PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: A terminal degree (Ph.D., J.D., or equivalent) in media, communication, or a related social science or humanities discipline by August 2027. Job Description: The Department of Media Production, Management, and Technology (MPMT) at the University of Florida invites applications for a nine-month, tenure-track Assistant or Associate Professor position (who currently has tenure) starting August 2027. We seek an energetic scholar with an active research program centered on any aspect of media and technology. We welcome candidates from a broad range of methodological traditions and theoretical perspectives. While the research program is open to any domain within media and technology, the candidate must be prepared to teach RTV 3432: Ethics and Problems in Media, a core course serving undergraduate majors in both Digital Film and Video Production and Media Management and Technology. Research & Teaching Responsibilities Conduct original research that advances scholarly understanding of media and/or technology including, but not limited to: media infrastructure technology practices sports media and communication media systems platform studies entertainment media digital culture and strategy social/mobile media science and technology studies Teach undergraduate core courses, specifically RTV 3432: Ethics and Problems in Media, bridging ethical, regulatory, and societal issues across both creative production and strategic media management contexts and other courses in the department, with opportunities to develop specialized upper-level electives and graduate seminars. Pursue internal and external research funding. Publish in leading academic venues. Mentor students and contribute to departmental, college, and university service. Minimum Qualifications A terminal degree (Ph.D., J.D., or equivalent) in media, communication, or a related social science or humanities discipline by August 2027. Demonstrated experience for national and international scholarly impact. (Assistant level) Established record of national and international scholarly impact. (Associate level) Demonstrated success in research funding. Evidence of excellence in university teaching. Preferred Qualifications Ability to engage students across both creative production and media management specializations in ethical and critical decision-making. Commitment to supporting a collaborative academic culture where all faculty, staff, and students can thrive. Preference will be given to candidates whose research, professional experience, and teaching demonstrate the ability to bridge the department's two tracks: (1) Digital Film and Video Production and (2) Media Management and Technology. About the Department & College: The MPMT department serves approximately 500 undergraduate majors across two core specializations: Digital Film and Video Production and Media Management and Technology. Our faculty prepare students for a variety of careers in film and video production, social media strategy, audience analytics, UI/UX design, and digital marketing. Media Production, Management, and Technology (MPMT) - UF College of Journalism and Communications The College of Journalism and Communications enrolls over 2,500 undergraduate and 700 graduate students across four departments. Faculty benefit from premier research hubs, including the Atlas Lab for social media listening, the Media Effects and Technology Lab, the Center for Public Interest Communications, and UF's university-wide AI Initiative. Homepage - UF College of Journalism and Communications About the University & Community The University of Florida is a top-tier public research university (AAU) with over $1 billion in annual research expenditures and state-of-the-art supercomputing infrastructure. Located in Gainesville, UF offers a vibrant university community surrounded by natural springs, a rich cultural scene, and excellent quality of life within two hours of major metropolitan areas and coastal beaches. Explore Gainesville in 60 Seconds. https://youtu.be/e_7nUKAFanM?si=7Z4s36Q80iaj6Nwg The University of Florida offers a competitive salary commensurate with similar positions and includes a full benefits package. To view the university’s Total Reward benefit package, please click here: https://hr.ufl.edu/benefits Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience and education Required Qualifications: A terminal degree (Ph.D., J.D., or equivalent) in media, communication, or a related social science or humanities discipline by August 2027. Preferred: Preference will be given to candidates whose research and teaching demonstrate the ability to bridge the department's two tracks: (1) Digital Film and Video Production and (2) Media Management and Technology. Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications must be submitted online at jobs.ufl.edu and include: A cover letter detailing your research agenda and teaching philosophy A current curriculum vitae Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., student evaluations or teaching portfolio) Copies of two representative lead-authored publications or scholarly works Names, email addresses, and phone numbers for three professional references Review of applications will begin on October 23 and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries may be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Jasmine McNealy, at jmcnealy@ufl.edu. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http//www.naces.org/. The application packet will be submitted as one PDF file through the UF Jobs website at https://jobs.ufl.edu/. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening, which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The University of Florida is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Florida’s Government-in-the-Sunshine Laws require that all documents relating to the search process, including letters of application, nomination and reference, be available for public inspection. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541508/assistant-professor-of-creative-production-tenuretrack": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Creative Production (Tenure-Track)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Creative Production (Tenure-Track) Apply now Job no: 541508 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Communications/Public Relations/Marketing, Education/Training/Instructional Design, Information Technology Department: 23030000 - CJC-MEDIA PROD, MGMT, TECH Classification Title: AST PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: A terminal degree (MFA, PhD, or equivalent) in film, media production, or a related field by August 2027 A strong record of creative achievement, such as award-winning films, documentaries aired on network or streaming services, VR experiences showcased at SIGGRAPH, or new media installations. Evidence of teaching/mentoring effectiveness The candidate should demonstrate their potential in establishing a national and/or international reputation. Job Description: The Department of Media Production, Management, and Technology (MPMT) in the University of Florida's College of Journalism and Communications seeks to hire a tenure-track Assistant Professor with a demonstrated record of creative production to begin Fall 2027. We seek a dynamic scholar/practitioner whose creative output advances storytelling through innovative media formats. We value candidates who push boundaries in media innovation and foster content that addresses contemporary social issues, cultural narratives, or technological disruptions. The ideal candidate will demonstrate excellence in creative production while maintaining industry connections. Areas of expertise may include feature-length and short films, television series and specials, documentary filmmaking, emerging technologies such as augmented reality (AR) or virtual reality (VR), streaming series development and production, or hybrid formats that blend narrative with interactive elements. This role emphasizes a robust portfolio of creative work—such as films, documentaries, series, or interactive projects—alongside teaching and service contributions. The MPMT department recognizes the critical importance of creative tenure tracks where artistic output is valued as highly as traditional scholarly research. Tenure evaluation will prioritize impactful creative scholarship, evidenced by honors and awards at nationally recognized film festivals, publications in creative outlets, peer-reviewed exhibitions, grants from bodies like the National Endowment for the Arts, or collaborations with industry leaders. Responsibilities include teaching undergraduate courses in media production (for example, filmmaking techniques; camera and lens systems; documentary storytelling; VR/AR production; streaming content creation; mentoring students in hands-on projects; supervising capstone productions) and integrating emerging technologies into the curriculum. Candidates are expected to maintain an active agenda of original creative work, secure external funding, and disseminate outputs through festivals, broadcasts, online platforms, or academic conferences. Service duties include department and college committees, advising student media organizations, and contributing to college initiatives and other duties as assigned. About the Department of Media Production, Management, & Technology The Department of Media Production, Management, and Technology is one of the premier programs in offering courses related to mass media and emerging media. Our curriculum prepares students for the media industry through two specializations: Digital Film and Video Production, and Media Management and Technology. The department has about 500 student majors and 16 full-time faculty. Students from the department pursue careers in film production, TV program production, social media management, brand management, consumer analysis, UI/UX design, and digital marketing, among others. Media Production, Management, and Technology (MPMT) - UF College of Journalism and Communications About the College of Journalism and Communications We are recognized as a national leader in communication scholarship and professional skills development. Be part of an ambitious, progressive, and collaborative program at one of the top public research universities in the United States. Accredited by ACEJMC, the college has earned its reputation for excellence over the last half-century on the strength of its programs, faculty, students, and alumni; the stories they tell; and the difference they make around the world. With over 70 full-time faculty members and 175 part- time instructors serving in four academic departments— Advertising; Journalism; Media Production, Management, and Technology; and Public Relations —we enroll about 2,500 undergraduates both in-person and online. Additionally, more than 700 graduate students are earning master’s and doctoral degrees. The College of Journalism and Communications offers multiple research and teaching environments, including the Atlas Lab for social media listening; the Consortium on Trust in Media and Technology; the STEM Translational Communication Center; the Center for Public Interest Communications; the Media, Effects, and Technology Lab; the Institute for Public Relations; The Agency, a full- service student-run advertising and public relations agency; the Innovation News Center; Division of Media Properties; and more. The College is highly involved in UF’s AI Initiative. Homepage - UF College of Journalism and Communications About the University of Florida Our university ranked in the top 6 among public universities, in the top 28 overall, and as the number 1 in online undergraduate programs by the 2024 U.S. News and World Report. UF continues to expand its faculty ranks and the impact of its research and curriculum. UF faculty members annually generate more than $1 billion in research expenditures, with a comprehensive long-term AI initiative (https://ai.ufl.edu/about/) and access to state-of-the-art infrastructure, including one of the most powerful supercomputers in the U.S. The University of Florida is a member of the Association of American Universities and is categorized in the Carnegie Commission’s top tier of research universities. UF has a student body of more than 60,000 who come from all 50 states in the United States and more than 100 countries. The university and greater Gainesville communities enjoy a number of different cultural events, restaurants, year- round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. he University of Florida is situated in north-central Florida, a region renowned for its abundant natural beauty. Gainesville is within a two-hour drive of Tampa, Orlando, Jacksonville, the Atlantic Ocean, and the Gulf of Mexico. About Gainesville As home to Florida's largest and oldest university, Gainesville (http://cityofgainesville.org (http://cityofgainesville.org/)) is one of the state's centers of education, medicine, cultural events, and athletics. Gainesville and the surrounding area is home to about 250,000 people, a robust arts community, several museums, a world-class health care system anchored at UF, a downtown core known for its restaurants and brew pubs, excellent public and private schools, and abundant nature exemplified by clear, natural springs. Additionally, within 2 hours are attractions such as Disney World, Universal Studios, Busch Gardens, and historic St. Augustine. Explore Gainesville in 60 Seconds. (https://www.youtube.com/watch? v=e_7nUKAFanM) UF Benefits At the University of Florida, you will not only find competitive salaries, but you’ll enjoy elevated benefits and leave options, tuition assistance programs, and stability and support with opportunities for advancement and career growth that only UF can offer. To view the university’s Total Reward benefit package, please click here, https://benefits.hr.ufl.edu . Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience and education Required Qualifications: A terminal degree (MFA, PhD, or equivalent) in film, media production, or a related field by August 2027 A strong record of creative achievement, such as award-winning films, documentaries aired on network or streaming services, VR experiences showcased at SIGGRAPH, or new media installations. Evidence of teaching/mentoring effectiveness The candidate should demonstrate their potential in establishing a national and/or international reputation. Preferred: Expertise in software such as Adobe Premiere, After Effects, DaVinci Resolve, Unity for VR, or data-driven tools for audience engagement; experience in streaming series productions; screenings or selections at mid-major or major film festivals; familiarity with ethical considerations in AI-assisted production or sustainable filmmaking practices. Experience teaching at the university-level. Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications must be submitted online via the UF Careers portal at https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/ . Submit: cover letter (be creative in explaining how your unique talents, creative vision, and professional experiences will advance MPMT's mission of valuing creative output as scholarship, enhance our curriculum, and benefit our students) curriculum vitae; portfolio links or samples of creative work (e.g., reels, scripts, VR demos); teaching statement; evidence of teaching effectiveness; and contact information for three professional references. Review of applications begins November 1, 2026 and continues until the position is filled. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. For inquiries, contact Search Committee Chair Yu-Hao Lee leeyuhao@jou.ufl.edu If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-4621 or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). The University of Florida is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University is committed to non-discrimination with respect to race, creed, color, religion, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender identify and expression, marital status, national origin, political opinions or affiliations, genetic information and veteran status in also aspects of employment including recruitment, hiring, promotion, transfers, discipline, terminations, wage and salary administration, benefits and training. Florida’s Government-in-the-Sunshine Laws require that all documents relating to the search process, including letters of application, nomination, and reference, be available for public inspection. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http//www.naces.org/. The application packet will be submitted as one PDF file through the UF Jobs website at https://jobs.ufl.edu/. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening, which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. University of Florida is a member of the State University System of Florida and an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment based on merit. Florida’s Government-in-the-Sunshine Laws require that all documents relating to the search process, including letters of application, nomination and reference, be available for public inspection. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541510/data-management-analyst-iii-uf-department-of-emergency-medicine": {
      "title": "Data Management Analyst III – UF Department of Emergency Medicine",
      "description": "Data Management Analyst III – UF Department of Emergency Medicine Apply now Job no: 541510 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29290100 - MD-EMERGENCY MED-AED Classification Title: Data Management Analyst III Classification Minimum Requirements Master's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience. Job Description: The University of Florida, College of Medicine, Department of Emergency Medicine (DEM), actively seeks a motivated and experienced individual to join us as the Data Management Analyst III . Leveraging advanced statistical methods and data visualization tools, this role will analyze performance data to identify trends, uncover opportunities for improvement, and generate insightful reports for senior leadership. By collaborating with cross-functional stakeholders, they tailor data insights to support informed decision-making across the organization. This role ensures data quality and integrity by establishing governance procedures, monitoring data consistency, and setting data collection standards. Additionally, this role will develop interactive performance dashboards, providing stakeholders with real-time access to key metrics in a clear and actionable format. Through trend analysis and forecasting, they will help anticipate future challenges and opportunities, enabling proactive strategy development to drive continuous performance improvement. The UF Department of Emergency Medicine proudly maintains an affiliation with UF Health Shands Hospital, a distinguished 1,162-bed teaching facility renowned for its Level 1 trauma center and cutting-edge burn center. As the primary referral center for North Central Florida, our institution stands as a beacon of excellence in healthcare. This affiliation underscores our commitment to top-tier medical care and innovation. Key Responsibilities: Performance Measurement and Analysis Lead AAAEM Benchmark Survey submissions utilizing Business Objects. Collaborate with the health system to ensure data consistency and alignment with submissions like Vizient. Support administration of Earned Variable Compensation (EVC) with required wRVUs and other associated data to accurately administer multiple plans. Develop and implement KPIs aligned with departmental goals to assess provider performance, operational efficiency, and productivity. Utilize statistical analysis and data visualization tools to identify trends, patterns, and areas for improvement. Conduct trend analysis and forecasting to anticipate challenges and opportunities, supporting proactive decision-making. Data Management and Visualization Establish and maintain data governance procedures to ensure accuracy, consistency, and integrity in performance data. Create interactive dashboards and reports that translate complex data into actionable insights for key stakeholders. Manage the end-to-end lifecycle of departmental data requests, including intake form administration, prioritization of requests, coordination with data resources, tracking deliverables, and communication of results to key stakeholders. Monitor data quality and refine data collection processes to enhance reporting accuracy. Stakeholder Collaboration and Reporting Work closely with leadership, clinicians, and operational teams to understand their performance needs and provide tailored analytical support. Liaise with Shands Decision Support Services (DSS) team to build and update Emergency Medicine data reports. Generate comprehensive reports to inform strategic decisions and operational improvements. Effectively communicate data insights through visual storytelling and presentations to diverse audiences. The City of Gainesville: Gainesville, home to the University of Florida, is ranked #1 best city to move to in Florida by USA Today. Centrally located in North Florida, it offers a blend of small-town charm and big-city amenities, with close proximity to major airports, family entertainment, and some of the best beaches in the world. Known for its beautiful landscapes and urban forest, Gainesville is a hidden gem that provides year-round outdoor activities, award-winning college sports, and a rich cultural scene. The University of Florida and UF Health are the leading employers in the area, making Gainesville an excellent place to work and raise a family. Learn more about Florida’s premiere health system, UF Health, and the advantages of living in Gainesville at UF Health Destination . Expected Salary: The salary range is $85,000 - $90,000 and is commensurate with education and experience. At the University of Florida, we are committed to the well-being and professional growth of our employees. Our benefits package is designed to support your health, work-life balance, and financial security: Generous Paid Leave: Enjoy 22 days of paid vacation, 13 days of paid sick leave, 11 paid Holidays, and 8 weeks of paid parental leave. Individual and Family Health Insurance Benefits State of Florida Retirement Plan Options State of Florida Basic Life Insurance Public Service Loan Forgiveness Eligible Employer Professional Development Opportunities Wellness Programs available to employees For more details on our comprehensive benefits, visit UF Benefits . Required Qualifications: Master's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience. Preferred Strong expertise in performance measurement, data analysis, and statistical methods. Proficiency in data visualization tools such as Power BI, Tableau, or similar platforms. Experience in healthcare operations, clinical workflows, and data governance best practices. Excellent communication skills to translate complex data insights into actionable recommendations. Analytical problem-solving skills. Ability to work collaboratively with cross-functional teams and senior leadership. Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, candidates must submit the following documents: A cover letter detailing relevant experience and qualifications. A current resume/CV. For questions, please reach out to EM-Careers@ufl.edu Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541513/assistant-vice-president-development": {
      "title": "Assistant Vice President, Development",
      "description": "Assistant Vice President, Development Apply now Job no: 541513 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Business/Accounting/Finance, Advancement Department: 30010100 - JX-DEAN-ADMINISTRATION Classification Title: AST VP, Development Classification Minimum Requirements: Master’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and eight years of appropriate experience; or a bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and ten years of appropriate experience with demonstrated success soliciting gifts at $1 million and above in an institution of higher education. Job Description: The Assistant Vice President/Chief Advancement Officer, UF Health – Northeast will build and oversee a comprehensive philanthropy program for UF Health Northeast. The incumbent will report to the Sr. Vice President & Northeast Regional President, - UF Health (SVPNRP), the Dean of the College of Medicine – Jacksonville, and the Senior Associate Vice President/Chief Advancement Officer – UF Health. The AVP/CAO-NE will oversee development, grateful patient fundraising, annual giving, stewardship, capital projects, and CMN programs for UF Health’s Northeast units, including UF Health Jacksonville and, UF Health St. Johns and UF Health Durbin Park. The incumbent will serve as the primary point of contact for academic and hospital stakeholders in the Northeast for development-related activity and will collaborate with other members of the HSC Development leadership team to foster a culture of philanthropy. This position will manage a high-level portfolio of leadership and principal gift prospects and will lead the UFHNE Advancement teams and inspire a culture of high performance, collegiality, and professionalism throughout the organization. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS: Leadership/Management Create and oversee new campaign strategic plans for UF Health Northeast, and annual work plans for the Dean of the College of Medicine – Jacksonville and SVPNRP – Northeast, providing support and ensuring implementation of key elements, and aligning with the newly integrated health system strategic plans for the Northeast. Meet regularly with UF Health leadership and partner on philanthropy-related presentations and initiatives. Serve as a strategic member of UF Health – Northeast hospitals’ collaborative senior leadership team. Lead and manage high-performing teams, ensuring alignment with the goals of annual performance and business plans and assuming overall responsibility for implementation of strategic initiatives for UF Health – Jacksonville hospital, the College of Medicine – Jacksonville, UF Health – St. Johns, and UF Health Durbin Park Provide direction, strategic planning, leadership, counsel on matters relating to fundraising and operations. Ensure accountability and oversee staffing, including establishing goals in alignment with unit objectives, and develop and implement appropriate metrics. Evaluate staff, develop a strong sense of teamwork, and establish an environment of mentoring and support. Coach the team and bring cohesion to the department. Participate as part of the senior leadership team in setting direction and developing strategy for UF Health in partnership with the Chief Advancement Officer for UF Health. Special Initiatives & Campaign Development Lead in the creation and promotion of a culture of philanthropy at UF Health-NE. Effectively involve stakeholders, including center/institute directors, department chairs, physicians, administrators, volunteers and donors in fundraising efforts. Support and enhance a dynamic grateful patient philanthropy program. Implement best practices, oversee development of programs and materials, and determine allocation of resources to generate maximum return on investment. Provide coaching for leadership on strategic plans, business plans, and campaign planning and development activities, including cultivation/solicitation strategies. Collaborate with the UF Advancement to maximize philanthropic opportunities. Support and partner with the Sr. Associate Vice President/ Chief Advancement Officer – UF Health and UF Health leadership in Northeast on all major fundraising initiatives. Develop and manage campaign prospect pipeline for UF Health – Northeast and the College of Medicine - Jacksonville. Meet annual targets for the number of donor visits, proposals submitted, and gifts closed. Monitor all donor information; provide and present statistical analysis to leadership. Successfully oversee the plan for raising funds to support campaign priorities, including scholarships, endowed professorships/faculty support and select capital projects. Plan and monitor the budget, using resources for best results in fundraising and planning activities involving volunteers, prospects, faculty and administration. Portfolio Management Develop and maintain a personal fundraising agenda focused on leadership and principal gift prospects and donors. Strategically managing relationships through face-to-face visits and cultivation and stewardship activities to bring solicitations to closure. Develop and present major gift proposals and stewardship programs to acknowledge and recognize gifts in ways that are meaningful to donors and align to the vision, mission, and values of the unit, the University of Florida, and UF Advancement. Represent UF Health at donor and business and community events as assigned. Effectively articulate and communicate UF Health needs and priorities, particularly those among the Northeast units, within the context of the overall mission, goals and objectives. Expected Salary: Salary is competitive and commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: A valid Florida driver license. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: Eight to ten years of progressively responsible leadership experience in development with a demonstrated track record of successful fundraising in a large-scale university and medical services environment or one of similar complexity. Demonstrated ability and expertise in building fundraising and engagement programs. Track record of recruiting, developing, and retaining a high caliber staff, building a team, and creatively organizing and managing them to maximize the support of external constituencies. Excellent organizational and operational skills; confidence and independence in exercising leadership skills and in representing UF Health to external constituents. Ability to construct, articulate, and implement an annual strategic development plan for a department. Demonstrated ability to support UF Health’s mission, particularly in managing the responsibilities of the department, including strategic and operational planning at the department level as well as establishing metrics and processes to monitor and evaluate operational performance outcomes and implement performance improvement initiatives when required. Demonstrated ability to provide management oversight, leadership, and strategic and tactical direction. A strong understanding of budgeting practices and financial accountability and the ability to manage resources with prudence and efficiency. Ability to work both independently and as a team player who will productively engage with others at varying levels of seniority within and outside UF Health. Experience engaging, training, motivation, and staffing volunteers in the cultivation, solicitation, and closing of significant gifts; appreciation for the role of volunteers in building an effective development network. Ability to demonstrate the highest standards of ethics as accepted by the professional fundraising community. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, strong interpersonal abilities, and the capacity to engage effectively and collaboratively with a wide range of individuals are essential. A commitment to and support for workplace inclusion in all its forms; demonstrated success collaborating with stakeholders; strong project management skills; experience in a data-driven environment; excellent organizational abilities; and the capacity to lead, influence, and inspire others are essential. Proficient in the following areas: Windows operating systems, Microsoft Word, Excel, and Access, as well as internet browsers and email platforms. Familiarity with donor databases, contact management systems, and tracking systems is also highly preferred. The ability and willingness to travel. A valid driver license and good driving record are essential. Special Instructions to Applicants: The search committee is being assisted by the executive search firm ZRG Aspen Leadership Group. To view the position prospectus, please visit the ZRG Aspen Leadership Group website: Assistant Vice President/Chief Advancement Officer, Northeast Florida Region To nominate a candidate or inquire about the position, please contact Steven Wallace at stevenwallace@aspenleadershipgroup.com . In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541514/assistantassociate-professor-of-organic-chemistry": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Organic Chemistry",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Organic Chemistry Apply now Job no: 541514 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Grant or Research Administration Department: 16120100 - LS-CHEMISTRY-GENERAL Classification Title: Assistant/Associate Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in Chemistry or closely related field. Job Description: The University of Florida, Department of Chemistry, invites applications for a full-time, nine-month, tenure-accruing position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor in Organic Chemistry, broadly defined. The appointments will commence on August 16, 2027. Successful candidates are expected to conduct innovative research at the highest level and to establish an outstanding, independent, externally funded research program. We seek candidates with a record of excellence in research and scholarship, the ability to excel in teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels and who will contribute to the service mission of the Department of Chemistry and the University of Florida. For more information about the Department of Chemistry, please visit our website, www.chem.ufl.edu . The University of Florida is a land grant, sea grant, and space grant university and home to the most comprehensive university health sciences facilities in the southeast. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs including magnet schools and an international baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville. Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with rank, qualifications and experience. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Chemistry or closely related field. Preferred: Postdoctoral experience is desirable. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: Letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position. A complete curriculum vitae. Names and contact information for three professional references. A brief research summary A description of proposed research projects. A statement of teaching philosophy. Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026, and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 25 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540794/postdoctoral-associate": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate Apply now Job no: 540794 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Artificial Intelligence, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29240101 - MD-HOBI-GENERAL Classification Title: Postdoctoral Associate Classification Minimum Requirements: The Division of Biomedical Informatics and Data Science within the Department of Health Outcomes and Biomedical Informatics (HOBI) at the University of Florida (UF) is seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Researcher with a strong background in biomedical informatics, artificial intelligence, machine learning, or data science. The successful candidate will join a collaborative and interdisciplinary research team to contribute to cutting-edge research at the intersection of AI/ML, real-world data, and health outcomes. Ongoing projects leverage large-scale multimodal clinical data, including electronic health records (EHRs), medical imaging, genomics, wearable devices, and unstructured clinical text, to develop novel methods for disease prediction, precision medicine, and clinical decision support. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in biomedical informatics, computer science, data science, biomedical engineering, statistics, or a related STEM field. Experience with EHR data, machine learning or deep learning, natural language processing, medical imaging, or large language models (LLMs) is highly desirable. Familiarity with causal inference, real-world evidence, multimodal data integration, or cloud/high-performance computing is a plus. The Postdoctoral Researcher will collaborate closely with biomedical informaticians, clinicians, statisticians, and translational researchers across the University of Florida. Responsibilities include developing and evaluating AI/ML methods, analyzing large-scale clinical datasets, building computational tools and software, preparing manuscripts for publication in high-impact journals, presenting research at scientific conferences, and contributing to NIH and other federal grant proposals. The position offers excellent opportunities to lead research projects, publish extensively, develop new methodological expertise, and establish an independent research program within a highly collaborative and well-funded research environment. • Ph.D. in biomedical informatics, computer science, data science, biomedical engineering, statistics, biostatistics, or a related quantitative STEM field. • Strong programming skills in Python and/or R; experience with SQL and Linux/Unix is desirable. • Demonstrated research experience in biomedical informatics, artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning, or data science, including the analysis of large-scale biomedical or healthcare datasets (e.g., electronic health records, medical imaging, genomics, or other real-world data). • Excellent written and oral communication skills. • Ability to work effectively in a collaborative, multidisciplinary research environment. Job Description: Conduct independent and collaborative research in biomedical informatics, including the development and application of artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning (ML), and statistical methods to analyze large-scale, multimodal clinical data (e.g., electronic health records, medical imaging, genomics, wearable data, and unstructured clinical text). Design, implement, validate, and optimize computational methods and analytical pipelines to address clinically relevant research questions. Develop, implement, and maintain research software, data processing pipelines, and computational tools to support data integration, extraction, transformation, quality assurance, and analysis. Contribute to reproducible research practices, software documentation, and code sharing. Participate in the design, execution, analysis, and dissemination of research studies. Prepare scientific manuscripts, conference presentations, technical reports, and other scholarly products. Contribute to the interpretation of findings and collaborate with multidisciplinary teams on the publication of research results. Expected Salary: $61,008 - $65,000 Required Qualifications: • Ph.D. in biomedical informatics, computer science, data science, biomedical engineering, statistics, biostatistics, or a related quantitative STEM field. • Strong programming skills in Python and/or R; experience with SQL and Linux/Unix is desirable. • Demonstrated research experience in biomedical informatics, artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning, or data science, including the analysis of large-scale biomedical or healthcare datasets (e.g., electronic health records, medical imaging, genomics, or other real-world data). • Excellent written and oral communication skills. • Ability to work effectively in a collaborative, multidisciplinary research environment. Preferred: Experience working with electronic health records (EHRs), real-world data (RWD), clinical data warehouses, or other healthcare databases. Experience developing and applying machine learning, deep learning, natural language processing (NLP), computer vision, or large language models (LLMs) for biomedical or healthcare research. Experience integrating and analyzing multimodal data, including structured EHR data, clinical text, medical imaging, genomics, wearable devices, or other biomedical data sources. Familiarity with causal inference, statistical learning, clinical prediction modeling, or real-world evidence methodologies. Experience developing reproducible research software, computational pipelines, or open-source tools using modern software engineering practices (e.g., Git/GitHub, Docker, workflow management). Evidence of scholarly productivity through peer-reviewed publications and presentations at scientific conferences. Experience working on NIH-funded or other federally funded research projects and contributing to grant proposal development. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 06 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541386/assistant-professor-in-statistics": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Statistics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Statistics Apply now Job no: 541386 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16480000 - LS-STATISTICS Classification Title: Assistant Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in Statistics, Biostatistics, Computer Science, or a closely related field by the start date of the appointment. Job Description: The Department of Statistics in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences at the University of Florida invites applications for two full-time (1.0 FTE), nine-month, tenure-accruing Assistant Professor positions beginning August 16, 2027. We seek outstanding candidates with research interests in causal inference, Bayesian computation, statistics, machine learning, or related areas. Faculty responsibilities include developing and maintaining an active research program, teaching undergraduate and graduate courses, mentoring students, and contributing services to the department, university, and profession. The Department of Statistics has a long-standing reputation for excellence in research, education, and innovation, with a proud history of hosting distinguished scholars and producing impactful research. As one of the nation's leading public research universities, the University of Florida provides exceptional institutional support and fosters a highly collaborative academic environment. This search is part of the University of Florida's strategic initiative to expand the role of artificial intelligence in higher education and research. The university is home to HiPerGator , one of the most powerful high-performance computing systems at a U.S. public university, providing substantial computational resources to support cutting-edge research. Applicants are encouraged to learn more about the Department of Statistics and its programs by visiting https: Department of Statistics and about the University's AI initiative at https: Artificial Intelligence . UF is the state’s oldest, largest, and most comprehensive land grant university with an enrollment of over 50,000 students and was ranked 7th in the country among public universities (US News and World Report 2025 rankings), and 1st among public institutions in the Wall Street Journal 2023 survey. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf of Mexico, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs including magnet schools and an international baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville . Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Statistics, Biostatistics, Computer Science, or a closely related field by the start date of the appointment. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: Letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position A complete curriculum vitae including statistics teaching background and experience A statement on teaching and research goals. Three confidential letters of recommendation. Note, the names and email addresses of three references must be provided. An email will be sent to your references, requesting them to upload their confidential letter to the submission packet. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Review of applications will begin on November 3, 2026, and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541387/lecturer-statistics": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Statistics",
      "description": "Lecturer - Statistics Apply now Job no: 541387 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16480000 - LS-STATISTICS Classification Title: Lecturer Classification Minimum Requirements: MS in Statistics, Biostatistics, or closely related field. Job Description: The Department of Statistics in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences at the University of Florida invites applications for a full-time (1.0 FTE), 9-month, non-tenure-accruing Lecturer position beginning August 16, 2027. This is a renewable academic-year appointment. The successful candidate will primarily teach advanced undergraduate statistics courses for Statistics majors and advanced undergraduate courses for Data Science majors offered by the Department of Statistics. Experience or interest in online instruction is desirable. The department is home to thriving undergraduate programs in both Statistics and Data Science and is committed to excellence in teaching and student success. The University of Florida and the Department of Statistics highly value the contributions of teaching faculty. To support professional growth and long-term career development, lecturers are eligible for promotion through the ranks of Lecturer, Senior Lecturer, and Master Lecturer. This search is part of a university-wide initiative to expand the role of artificial intelligence in higher education and research. Applicants are encouraged to learn more about the University of Florida's AI efforts at Artificial Intelligence . The university is also home to HiPerGator , one of the most powerful high-performance computing systems at a U.S. public university. Additional information about the Department of Statistics, its academic programs, and faculty can be found at Department of Statistics . UF is the state’s oldest, largest, and most comprehensive land grant university with an enrollment of over 50,000 students and was ranked top 10 public universities. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf of Mexico, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs including magnet schools and an international baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville . Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Required Qualifications: MS in Statistics, Biostatistics, or closely related field. Preferred Qualifications: PhD. In Statistics, Biostatistics, or closely related field. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: Letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position A complete curriculum vitae including statistics teaching background and experience A link to a short (5-10 minute) instructional video prepared by the applicant on a topic of their choice Three confidential letters of recommendation. Note: the names and email addresses of three references must be provided. An email will be sent to your references, requesting them to upload their confidential letter to the submission packet. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026, and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541393/utilities-supervisor": {
      "title": "Utilities Supervisor",
      "description": "Utilities Supervisor Apply now Job no: 541393 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades, Environmental/Occupational Safety Department: 63700100 - FS-UTILITIES-ADMIN Classification Title: Utilities Supervisor Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma and five year of appropriate experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: Manages the Locates of all underground utility facilities for accuracy and within the state’s regulations. Responsible for field training and developing technical staff in highly technical work involving operating equipment and instruments in order to locate, mark and protect main and service lines used for electric, chilled water, steam, water, wastewater, fiber-optic, gas, and reclaimed water facilities. Coordinate resources to ensure the department meets required regulated response times and avoid reasonable contractors’ delays while protecting the university assets. Maintains equipment and coordinates supplies by following purchasing policies and monitoring inventory. Approves direct reports hourly and leave time. Develops, maintains, and enforces production, safety, and work standards. Maintains UF compliance with Utilisphere / Sunshine One Call regulations. Responsible for assisting with locating in the repair of all Utility systems during emergencies. Manages and coordinates with contractors dig permit applications. Responsible for assisting the applicant in the initiation the permit application by providing instructions regarding the process to the applicant, coordination of the process with other related University procedures, scheduling locale of University-owned underground utilities for the applicant. Reviews, approves and completes documentation related to work accomplished including UF Dig Permits. Ensures that all applicants follow the procedures according to the University of Florida Dig Permit, Sunshine One call and OSHA Excavation guide lines. Coordinates field appointments with contractors, engineers and other utilities to ensure locate and mark buried facilities in the vicinity of construction work are communicated effectively. The lead responsible for updating, maintaining, and coordinating Geographic Information System (GIS) utility records by coordinating field findings with mapping technicians. Responsible for reviewing and using utility mapping and record information through GIS, AutoCAD, and construction prints; identifying and communicating record discrepancies; and providing timely field information to support accurate utility locates, dig permits, maintenance, and construction activities. Assists the utilities staff and project managers on location of existing underground assets and assists in identifying underground assets as they are discovered. Ability to accurately distinguish and interpret colors as necessary to perform the essential functions of the position. Valid Florida Driver’s license required at time of appointment. Criminal Background Check and post offer physical assessment is required. NORMAL WORK SCHEDULE: This position is an Exempt position with schedule reflecting 5 days a week; Monday – Friday; total hours per week 40. Rotating On-Call schedule within the group to cover emergency utility failures and emergency locate requests. Expected Salary: $55,000.00 to $75,000.00 Annually Required Qualifications: High school diploma and five year of appropriate experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: Five years’ experience in multiple utility facility location for the University of Florida. Knowledge of approved principles, methods, materials used in electric, water, wastewater, gas, reclaimed water and telecommunications construction, maintenance and repair work. Knowledge of occupational hazards and safety precautions of construction work. Must be able to interpret utilities systems construction blueprints and drawings. Must be able to work effectively with co-workers and the general public. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541411/assistant-professor-in-criminal-justice-system": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Criminal Justice System",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Criminal Justice System Apply now Job no: 541411 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 16920000 - LS-SOCIOLOGY CRIMINOLOGY&LAW Classification Title: Assistant Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in Criminology or Criminal Justice, Sociology or a relevant social science field by date of hire. Job Description: The Department of Sociology and Criminology & Law at the University of Florida, College of Liberal Arts and Sciences invites applications for two full-time, nine-month, tenure-accruing positions beginning August 16, 2027 at the rank of Assistant Professor with research and teaching interests related to US criminal justice systems broadly defined. These positions support the Crime and Justice component of our program in Criminology & Law. We seek scholars who study the practice and implementation of criminal and/or juvenile justice systems, for example, local, state, and/or Federal law enforcement, courts, sentencing and/or corrections, capital punishment, juvenile programming and prevention. Applicant research programs can emphasize theory development, methodological applications (quantitative, qualitative and/or mixed), and/or policy analysis (including program evaluation). We seek applicants whose records show commitment to rigorous scholarship with the promise of external funding, as well as excellence in teaching. The ability to contribute significantly to graduate education and the mentoring of graduate students is highly desirable. Advanced assistant professors are welcome to apply. The successful candidate will be expected to teach in their topical areas of interest and one or more of our required courses, which include research methods, criminological theory, and/or advanced principles of criminal justice. At the graduate level, successful applicants may also be able to teach required courses in our quantitative methods sequence or a criminal justice process course in addition to their area of interest. The Department of Sociology and Criminology & Law is a large and dynamic department with nearly 700 undergraduate Criminology majors and roughly 20 Criminology, Law and Society graduate students. The Department has two undergraduate programs, both offered via in-person and online courses, and minors in Sociology of Social Justice & Policy and Environmental Justice & Policy. Faculty conduct research in diverse areas, including criminology and criminal justice; psychology and law; families and gender; health, aging, and the life course; environment and resources; and race and ethnicity. Many faculty are affiliated with other academic programs at UF, including the School of Natural Resources and the Environment, the Center for Latin American Studies, the Center for European Studies, the Department of Gender, Sexuality and Women’s Studies, and the Health Science Center. More information about the Department is available at https://soccrim.clas.ufl.edu/ , including biographical information about our faculty, students, and staff; and a description of the various projects and research interests in which our members are involved. Salary & Benefits: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience. The University of Florida, a top-ranked public research institution, offers a comprehensive and competitive benefits package to support employees’ health, financial security, and work-life balance. Comprehensive medical, dental, vision, and prescription coverage. Life insurance, long-term disability, accident, and illness insurance. Retirement savings and investment options with employer contributions. Generous paid time includes vacation, sick leave, annual holidays, personal holidays, and winter break. 8 weeks of paid parental and medical leave. Access to wellness programs, flexible spending accounts, and an employee assistance program. Discounts for on-campus and off-campus activities and services Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Criminology or Criminal Justice, Sociology or a relevant social science field by date of hire. Preferred: Evidence of rigorous scholarship and the potential to obtain external funding Evidence of excellence in teaching and the ability to mentor graduate students Ability to contribute to diverse theoretical viewpoints and open academic exchanges through scholarship, teaching, mentoring, and professional service Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online at jobs.ufl.edu. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: (1) a letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, experience, and suitability for the position. (2) a complete curriculum vitae . (3) a statement on teaching and research goals. (4) the names and email addresses of three references. An email will be sent to your references, requesting them to upload their confidential letter to the submission packet. Applications will be reviewed beginning October 20, 2026 , and the position will remain open until filled. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541449/postdoctoral-associate": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate Apply now Job no: 541449 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16360100 - LS-PHYSICS-GENERAL Classification Title: Postdoctoral Associate Theoretical and computational quantum information Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in Physics, Computer Science or closely related areas Job Description: A Postdoctoral Associate position in theoretical and computational quantum information science and condensed matter physics is available in the group led by Prof. Haining Pan in the Department of Physics at the University of Florida, starting Fall 2027. The initial appointment is for two years, renewed annually, and may be extended for a third year contingent upon strong performance, mutual satisfaction, and the availability of funds. The successful candidate will conduct numerical and theoretical research on quantum computing, AI-accelerated quantum simulation and materials discovery, quantum machine learning, topological quantum materials, and/or related areas. The successful candidate will have access to HiPerGator, the fastest university-owned supercomputer in the United States (TOP500), including 504 NVIDIA Blackwell B200 GPUs. These resources are provisioned directly through the group's institutional allocation. The University of Florida is the state's flagship land-grant, Carnegie R1, and AAU research university, ranked among the top 10 U.S. public universities (U.S. News & World Report, 2027). The Department of Physics hosts a large condensed matter and quantum information community of roughly twenty-five faculty across theory and experiment. The department operates the High B/T Facility of the National High Magnetic Field Laboratory through its Microkelvin Laboratory. Faculty also participate in the Center for Condensed Matter Sciences and the Quantum Theory Project, among the world's largest centers for the theory and computation of complex molecular and materials systems. Gainesville offers a substantially lower cost of living than most major research hubs and lies within about two hours of Orlando, Jacksonville, and Tampa; Florida levies no state income tax Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Physics, Computer Science or closely related areas Preferred: Demonstrated strength in one or more of the following. - Quantum many-body numerics: tensor-network methods, variational Monte Carlo (including neural quantum states), stabilizer and near-Clifford simulation, Gaussian/free-fermion methods, open-system dynamics (Lindblad master equations). - Machine learning for physical systems: deep learning, reinforcement learning, transformer architectures and large language models. - Analytical many-body and quantum information theory: replica field theory approaches to monitored and noisy quantum circuits, symmetry-protected topological phases, topological quantum computation, quantum error correction. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes (1) a cover letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position, (2) a complete curriculum vitae, (3) a statement on research goals, and (4) a list of at least three references. Application reviews will begin Dec 15, 2026. Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. Inquiries may be directed to Dr. Haining Pan at panhaining@ufl.edu . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Jun 2027 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541469/postdoctoral-associate-crop-transformation-center-ctc": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate - Crop Transformation Center (CTC)",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate - Crop Transformation Center (CTC) Apply now Job no: 541469 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60230000 - AG-HORTICULTURAL SCIENCES Classification Title: Postdoc Aso Classification Minimum Requirements: The appointee shall have received the doctorate in a field appropriate to the assigned duties and responsibilities. Job Description: The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . This is Postdoctoral Associate position available in the Baysal Lab within the Horticultural Sciences Department and the Crop Transformation Center (UF/IFAS CTC). The Baysal Lab seeks a motivated Postdoctoral Associate to advance plant transformation and genome engineering. The position focuses on developing virus-mediated delivery systems, with the goal of achieving tissue-culture-free, transgene-free, heritable edits in planta . The successful candidate will design and execute experiments in citrus and other crop species, using viral vectors. The position also includes coordinating day-to-day lab operations and contributing to experimental design, data analysis, and manuscript preparation. Background Information : The University of Florida is a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution, encompassing virtually all academic and professional disciplines, with an enrollment of more than 56,000 students. UF is a member of The Association of American Universities. The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences includes the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences , the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station , the Florida Cooperative Extension Service , the College of Veterinary Medicine , the Florida Sea Grant program, and encompasses 16 on-campus academic departments and schools, 12 Research and Educational Centers (REC) located throughout the state, 6 Research sites/demonstration units administered by RECs or academic departments, and Florida Cooperative Extension Service offices in all 67 counties (counties operate and maintain). The School of Natural Resources and Environment is an interdisciplinary unit housed in IFAS and managed by several colleges on campus. UF/IFAS employs nearly 4,500 people, which includes approximately 990 salaried faculty and 1,400 permanent support personnel located in Gainesville and throughout the state. IFAS, one of the nation’s largest agricultural and natural resources research and education organizations, is administered by a Vice President and four deans: the Dean of the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences, the Dean for Extension and Director of the Florida Cooperative Extension Service, the Dean for Research and Director of the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station, and the Dean for the College of Veterinary Medicine. UF/IFAS also engages in cooperative work with Florida A&M University in Tallahassee. The Horticultural Sciences Department is a unit within IFAS at the University of Florida and has diverse teaching, research, and extension education programs with 34 faculty in Gainesville and 26 faculty at various Research and Education Centers throughout the state. The Horticultural Sciences Department is currently ranked top 9 in the center for world university rankings. The Center for Crop Transformation , founded in 2023, addresses the need for rapid development and commercialization of genetically improved crops. The CTC is directed and led by Dr. Charlie Messina, a professor of predictive breeding specializing in symbiotic AI with over 16 years in the private sector. The center aims to accelerate genetic gain in specialty crops, ultimately improving human and environmental health. Their primary focus is creating citrus varieties that can tolerate or eventually resist citrus greening disease (HLB). The CTC harnesses core capabilities to activate an end-to-end approach to translate these novel ideas into products for Florida farmers and consumers. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Experience and Education Required Qualifications: A doctorate (foreign equivalent acceptable) in plant molecular biology, plant biotechnology, biochemistry or a closely related discipline is required. Candidates must be supportive of the mission of the Land-Grant system. Candidates must also have a commitment to UF core values. Preferred: Experience with molecular cloning and vector construction. Proficiency in standard molecular analyses: PCR, qPCR, DNA/RNA extraction, Sanger and amplicon sequencing, and analysis of editing outcomes. Track record of first-author peer-reviewed publications. Strong written and oral communication in English, and the ability to work independently and collaboratively. Experience with plant RNA or DNA virus vectors, VIGS, or virus-induced gene editing. Experience with CRISPR-Cas reagent design and delivery in plants. Familiarity with NGS data analysis and command-line bioinformatics (e.g., CRISPResso, Python or R). Experience mentoring students or technicians and coordinating lab operations. Special Instructions to Applicants: Employment Conditions This position is available November 2026 and will be filled as soon thereafter as an acceptable applicant is available. Compensation is commensurate with the education, experience, and qualifications of the selected applicant. Application Information Individuals wishing to apply should go online to Careers at UF by 11:55 PM of the closing date and submit: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for three individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Unofficial transcripts Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541470/postdoctoral-associate-nursery-science-production-and-agrophysics": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate, Nursery Science - Production and Agrophysics",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate, Nursery Science - Production and Agrophysics Apply now Job no: 541470 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60230000 - AG-HORTICULTURAL SCIENCES Classification Title: Postdoc Aso Classification Minimum Requirements: The appointee shall have received the doctorate in a field appropriate to the assigned duties and responsibilities. Job Description: The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . A postdoctoral Research Associate position is available in the Environmental Nursery Production lab at the University of Florida with an emphasis on establishing fundamental research that will unlock insight into the relationship between plants, substrate and environment in outdoor nursery crop production. This position will focus on developing and publishing high-caliber research that will expand our understanding of the substrate-plant-atmosphere continuum in commercial nurseries and lead to improved production practices for ornamental horticulture. The postdoctoral associate will spend approximately 50% of their time on original, fundamental research designed collaboratively with the PI to advance our understanding of the connectivity between nursery production, the environment, and the physical world. This includes developing concepts, initiating trials, collecting, analyzing and interpreting data, with the expectation of publishing in Q1 journals. The remaining 50% of the postdoctoral associate’s time will be dedicated to advancing ornamental production practices and moving applied nursery research forward. This includes analyzing data, generating reports, and writing manuscripts for publication with the PI, mentoring junior scientists and supporting additional lab activities. These endeavors involve exploration of innovative concepts and ideas to advance production techniques, understanding of plant stress response, substrate science, irrigation technologies, and fertilizer efficiency. Thus, this position will divide their time between fundamental nursery-based agrophysics research and supporting the lab’s motto of “Applied Solutions” all with a focus on container-grown ornamental plants in open-air and protected nursery cultivation systems. Overall, this position will participate in projects focusing on plant physiology, abiotic stress, water relations, biophysics, root zone temperature management, understanding the substrate-plant-water continuum, substrate engineering, and environmental modeling. This is an in-person position in Dr. Jeb S. Fields’ lab, which focuses on developing production applications for the ornamental horticulture industry. All facilities and space necessary for success will be provided. To accentuate the postdoctoral experience, we encourage and support participation in additional collaborative research, program development, grant writing, extension, and mentoring of junior scientists. There will be opportunities to present data and build industry networks. Moreover, the postdoctoral associate will have regular dedicated collaborative mentoring time with PI for support in research and career opportunities, with the aim of securing gainful employment after the program. The postdoctoral associate position is a 12-month full-time position, and renewable annually for up to three years depending on productivity. The pay is set at $58,656 / year and comes with associated benefits. An option for 0.75 FTE is also available. Background Information : The University of Florida is a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution, encompassing virtually all academic and professional disciplines, with an enrollment of more than 56,000 students. UF is a member of The Association of American Universities. The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences includes the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences , the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station , the Florida Cooperative Extension Service , the College of Veterinary Medicine , the Florida Sea Grant program, and encompasses 16 on-campus academic departments and schools, 12 Research and Educational Centers (REC) located throughout the state, 6 Research sites/demonstration units administered by RECs or academic departments, and Florida Cooperative Extension Service offices in all 67 counties (counties operate and maintain). The School of Natural Resources and Environment is an interdisciplinary unit housed in IFAS and managed by several colleges on campus. UF/IFAS employs nearly 4,500 people, which includes approximately 990 salaried faculty and 1,400 permanent support personnel located in Gainesville and throughout the state. IFAS, one of the nation’s largest agricultural and natural resources research and education organizations, is administered by a Vice President and four deans: the Dean of the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences, the Dean for Extension and Director of the Florida Cooperative Extension Service, the Dean for Research and Director of the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station, and the Dean for the College of Veterinary Medicine. UF/IFAS also engages in cooperative work with Florida A&M University in Tallahassee. The Horticultural Sciences Department is a unit within IFAS at the University of Florida and has diverse teaching, research, and extension education programs with 34 faculty in Gainesville and 26 faculty at various Research and Education Centers throughout the state. The Horticultural Sciences Department is currently ranked top 9 in the center for world university rankings. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Experience and Education Required Qualifications: A doctorate (foreign equivalent acceptable) in horticulture, soil science, environmental science, biophysics or a closely related discipline is required. Candidates must be supportive of the mission of the Land-Grant system. Candidates must also have a commitment to UF core values. Preferred: Experience with plant production Aptitude in scientific writing, data visualization and interpretation Advanced modeling/programming skills Proficiency with environmental remote sensing and interpretation Familiarity with HYDRUS computer models Self-sufficiency with statistical concepts and programs Self-motivated, well-organized, team player, and critical thinker Good written and verbal communication skills in English Driver’s license Ability to lift 30 lbs. Special Instructions to Applicants: Employment Conditions This position is available November 2026 and will be filled as soon thereafter as an acceptable applicant is available. Compensation is commensurate with the education, experience, and qualifications of the selected applicant. Application Information Individuals wishing to apply should go to Careers at UF by 11:55 PM of the closing date and submit: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for three individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Unofficial transcripts Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541471/ast-prof": {
      "title": "AST PROF",
      "description": "AST PROF Apply now Job no: 541471 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Construction/Design/Planning, Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 15060000 - DCP-URBAN REGIONAL PLANNING Classification Title: Assistant Professor in Urban and Regional Planning Classification Minimum Requirements: The candidate must have a PhD in urban and regional planning, urban studies, geography, public policy, finance and real estate, public administration, or related field. A successful candidate must have expertise in housing and a strong background in the planning field, either through education or experience, to enable them to teach specialized and core courses for the MURP and BUSP programs. The PhD must be completed prior to the start date of August 16, 2027. An ABD at the time of interviews will be considered with a letter from the candidate’s supervisor confirming the candidate’s date of graduation. Job Description: The Department of Urban and Regional Planning within the University of Florida’s College of Design, Construction and Planning invites applications for a nine-month, tenure-accruing faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor to begin in August 2027. The Department encourages applications from candidates with a background in the planning field and expertise in housing . The Department has particular interest in candidates whose work incorporates housing policy and finance, resiliency and disaster recovery in housing, socio-economic sustainability, and/or land use . Preferred candidates will have a proven track record of engaging with public interest concerns and interdisciplinary scholarship. Key Responsibilities The selected candidate will conduct research and publications commensurate with a tenure-track position and be entrepreneurial in obtaining research grants. The candidate should be open to collaborating with other faculty and researchers, including the Shimberg Center for Housing Studies, Florida Institute for Built Environment Resilience (FIBER), GeoPlan (for more, see below), and establishing relationships with related discipline programs on campus, such as real estate and political science. The candidate should also engage with housing innovation and practitioners working with Florida communities and beyond. The position requires teaching a total of four courses per year (two in fall and two in spring), which may be graduate and upper-level undergraduate courses delivered in-person and online in the candidate’s areas of expertise and in general planning topics. Anticipated courses include introduction to housing, housing finance, advanced housing topics, and at least one core general planning course. The successful candidate should have a well-developed pedagogy that includes methods to engage a multicultural student body. The candidate would also be expected to conduct service and community outreach consistent with the missions of the Department and the land-grant University. The Department of Urban and Regional Planning The Department of Urban and Regional Planning consists of a collegial faculty of fourteen core members and an additional eighteen members affiliated with research centers and other academic programs. Our faculty is the most productive in the College and consistently attracts the highest amount of research funding, with close to $4 million in new grants secured in the past year. We actively support mentorship of junior faculty, promote career development at every level, and foster a family-friendly environment. The Department, founded in 1975, offers an accredited Master of Urban and Regional Planning (MURP), a Master of Science in Urban Analytics (MSUA), a PhD URP concentration with STEM designation, a graduate minor in URP and certificate in UA, an undergraduate minor in URP, and a combined (4+1) bachelor-master degree. The Department’s fully online MURP was the country’s first and remains the only accredited online program. In 2023, the MURP program (campus and online) was ranked 22 nd in the nation, sixth in the South, and the 10 th largest by enrollment. The Department recently established a Bachelor of Urban Sciences and Planning (BUSP) that began in Fall 2026. The School and College As one of the two units in the School of Landscape Architecture and Planning , the Department of Urban and Regional Planning has a strong relationship with the Department of Landscape Architecture, as well as other units in the College, which include Architecture, Construction Management, Interior Design, Historic Preservation, and Sustainability and the Built Environment. Located in the Rinker School of Construction Management, the Shimberg Center for Housing Studies is the state’s primary public source of housing data for policymakers and local governments, and conducts wide-ranging research on housing topics such as affordability, preservation of assisted housing, and the intersections of housing with disaster recovery and climate resilience. In addition, the School and College are home to a range of research institutes and centers, including the GeoPlan Center, the Center for Health and the Built Environment, the Center for Landscape Conservation Planning, and FIBER. Other collaboration opportunities exist with the School of Architecture, which operates two off-campus CityLab programs in Orlando and Jacksonville to enhance access to highly urban contexts. The University of Florida The University of Florida is ranked #8 among the nation’s public universities according to the 2027 U.S. News & World Report “Best Colleges” list, and #4 among public universities in the inaugural TIME magazine “America’s Best Colleges of 2026-2027” ranking. As the flagship university of the state of Florida, and as a major land-grant research university in the Association of American Universities (AAU), the University offers many opportunities for faculty to develop a national and international reputation for research on issues that showcase Florida and the Southeastern United States as a living laboratory, and to shape the next generation of urban and regional planning professionals. Opportunities exist for cross-disciplinary appointments and collaboration, such as with the Warrington College of Business (including Finance, Insurance, and Real Estate), the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences (including the Political Science and Geography), the Levin College of Law, the School of Natural Resources and Environment, and UF’s six health-related colleges on campus, as well as university-wide institutes like the Water Institute and the Florida Climate Institute. In addition, UF’s HiPerGator, the nation's fastest university-owned supercomputer, is available to faculty for large-scale data analysis, spatial modeling, and AI research. The Campus and Gainesville The College is located on the University’s historic main campus, which is characterized by gothic brick buildings and expansive oak trees. The broader campus boasts world-class museums, a performing arts center, a childcare center, and a nationally ranked hospital complex. Situated within the city of Gainesville , the campus is surrounded by historic neighborhoods and a vibrant downtown, and the campus connects to the larger community and beyond by over one hundred miles of bike paths, a regional transit system, and a regional airport. The Gainesville metropolitan area within Alachua County is home to 280,000 residents and serves as the commercial, cultural, educational, and medical center of the north central Florida region. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of specialized programs. The County’s nature parks, waterways, and freshwater springs provide nature-lovers with abundant outdoor activities. The major cities of Orlando, Tampa, and Jacksonville, and the Gulf and Atlantic coasts, are all two hours or less away. Expected Salary: Salary is commensurate with qualifications and experience. Compensation includes a full benefits package with health insurance, retirement options, and paid leave. The University has a dual career academic hire program. Required Qualifications: The candidate must have a Ph.D in urban and regional planning, urban studies, geography, public policy, finance and real estate, public administration, or related field. A successful candidate must have expertise in housing and a strong background in the planning field, either through education or experience, to enable them to teach specialized and core courses for the MURP and BUSP programs. The Ph.D must be completed prior to the start date of August 16, 2027. An ABD at the time of interviews will be considered with a letter from the candidate’s supervisor confirming the candidate’s date of graduation. Preferred: Preferred candidates incorporate housing policy and finance, resiliency and disaster recovery in housing, socio-economic sustainability, and/or land use into their research, teaching, and service; connections with related discipline programs are also desired. Candidates should have a demonstrated track record or potential to publish in peer-reviewed journals and attract extramural research funding, particularly nationally competitive grants. Candidates should also have evidence of student-centered classroom instruction at graduate or undergraduate levels. An interest in incorporating online modalities and a willingness to serve the academy, the profession, and communities are also desired. Lastly, professional planning experience is desired, with AICP or other relevant credentials preferred. Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, submit a complete application with the following: Letter of interest describing your: Vision for the roles that housing and its intersection with planning will play in the future, and how you expect your work to contribute to this vision. Research agenda, including your goals and how you will build upon past work, including publications, projects, funding, and partnerships. Teaching philosophy and pedagogies; a list of courses of interest in teaching; a summary of courses taught including evidence of teaching effectiveness; and any instructional trainings and certifications received. Academic leadership, professional experience, and community service in relation to future academic goals. Specific interests in joining the Department of Urban and Regional Planning at the University of Florida. Writing sample or products demonstrating your research interests and strengths such as a portfolio of relevant research, design/creative, or professional work. Teaching evaluations (if received). Curriculum vitae. List of three references, including affiliations, phone numbers, and email addresses. To ensure full consideration, a complete application should be submitted by October 26, 2026 . Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the search committee and/or hiring authority. Review of complete applications will begin upon receipt and will continue until the position is filled. Three letters of reference will be required for shortlisted candidates. Submittal instructions will be provided upon notification. Inquiries can be sent to the search committee chair, Dr. Kristin Larsen, klarsen@ufl.edu . If accommodations due to a disability are needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. All candidates selected for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide official transcripts to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541472/lecturer": {
      "title": "LECTURER",
      "description": "LECTURER Apply now Job no: 541472 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 26040100 - HH-SPM ADMIN-INSTRUCTION Classification Title: LECTURER Classification Minimum Requirements: The position requires a doctorate in sport management or related discipline or a master’s degree and up to 10 years of sport industry experience. The successful candidate must have experience teaching sport management undergraduate and/or graduate students or extensive experience training sport industry professionals Job Description: The successful candidate will primarily be responsible for coordinating engaged learning and outreach activities in the online and residential programs, teaching undergraduate and/or graduate courses in the fall, spring, and summer, and participating in service at the department, college, university, and professional levels. The candidate will lead and collaborate in a variety of activities, including developing and nurturing new industry partnerships, maintaining alumni databases, mentoring and onboarding all incoming online master’s sport management students and guiding their career development, creating and supporting networking efforts for students, arranging professional industry trips, collecting post-trip data from experiential activities, facilitating and executing the SPM Voices From the Field Podcast, and overseeing final exams and/or capstone projects. The position will be in Gainesville, Florida, and performance effort is service (75%) and teaching (25%) responsibilities. The University of Florida is one of the top ranked Public Universities in the United States 1 and is Florida’s oldest and largest land grant university. UF is a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities, an organization comprising the top public and private institutions in North America. With an enrollment of over 61,000 students annually, UF is home to 16 colleges and more than 200 research, service and education centers, bureaus, and institutes. There is a vibrant culture of research, with over $1 billion in research expenditures last fiscal year. The UF campus is in Gainesville, a city of approximately 145,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 85 miles from the Georgia border, 50 miles from the gulf, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean. It has abundant wildlife, mature forests, prairies, lakes, and opportunities for many outdoor activities. Expected Salary: Salary is commensurate with qualifications and experience. Required Qualifications: The position requires a doctorate in sport management or related discipline or a master’s degree and up to 10 years of sport industry experience. The successful candidate must have experience teaching sport management undergraduate and/or graduate students or extensive experience training sport industry professionals. Preferred: Candidates will bring experience in sport industry engagement and networking, student mentoring, and teaching. Online teaching experience in sport management research methods, statistics, marketing, management and leadership, sociology, finance, or sport law courses is preferred Special Instructions to Applicants: Interested applicants must apply online at https://jobs.ufl.edu (job #541472) and include (a) an application letter summarizing qualifications; (b) a teaching philosophy; (c) a curriculum vitae or resume; and (d) names, addresses, email addresses, and telephone numbers of three references. The search committee will begin reviewing applications immediately. Review of complete applications begins October 20, 2026 , and the position will remain open until filled. Send inquiries and nominations to Dr. Cynthia Willming, Search Committee Chair, willming@hhp.ufl.edu. This is an on-campus, 12-month, full-time, non- tenure-accruing Lecturer position, beginning August 16, 2027. The University of Florida is an equal employment institution and is committed to non-discrimination with respect to race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy, gender identity, and sexual orientation), parental status, national origin, age, disability, genetic information (including family medical history), political affiliation, military service, or other non-merit-based factors. These protections extend to all management practices and decisions, including recruitment and hiring, appraisal systems, promotions, training, and career development programs. Selected candidates will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluations Services (NACES) which can be found at http://www.ncas.org. If accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-87. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541473/bioinformatics-research-scientist-ii-or-iii": {
      "title": "Bioinformatics Research Scientist (II or III)",
      "description": "Bioinformatics Research Scientist (II or III) Apply now Job no: 541473 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Computer Science, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 27320702 - HA-UFHCI BIOSTAT COMP BIOL SR Classification Title: Bioinformatics Analyst II/III Classification Minimum Requirements: Bioinformatics Analyst II: A bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and two years of experience. Bioinformatics Analyst III: A master's degree in appropriate area of specialization and two years of experience or a bachelor's degree and four years of experience. Job Description: About UFHCI The University of Florida Health Cancer Institute (UFHCI) is an NCI-designated cancer center comprising more than 350 basic, translational, and population-based scientists and clinicians who conduct research for the prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of cancer. A central collaborative research model encourages translation of basic laboratory findings into novel therapies, and the multidisciplinary membership of the Institute is its strength. Researchers are drawn from across UF, including six colleges within the Health Science Center — Medicine, Nursing, Dentistry, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine — as well as the Colleges of Liberal Arts and Sciences, Engineering, Health and Human Performance, Journalism and Communications, and Agricultural and Life Sciences. Research is organized around four programs: Cancer Control and Population Sciences; Cancer Targeting and Therapeutics; Immuno-Oncology and Microenvironment; and Mechanisms of Oncogenesis. About the Position UFHCI invites applications for both a Doctoral- and Masters-level bioinformatics research scientist in the Biostatistics and Computational Biology Shared Resource (BCB-SR). The position reports to the Associate Director for Cancer Quantitative Sciences and Director of the BCB-SR and works within the Bioinformatics Unit alongside biostatisticians and other computational scientists. The Bioinformatics Unit provides analytical support to UFHCI members. The successful candidate will work directly with cancer researchers from experimental design through analysis, interpretation, and publication. The position carries a broad and changing project portfolio, substantial investigator contacts, and regular co-authorship on peer-reviewed publications. Duties and Responsibilities: Analyze high-throughput genomics data in support of UFHCI investigators, including bulk and single-cell RNA-seq, ATAC-seq, DNA methylation assays (WGBS, EM-seq, RRBS), ChIP-seq, and whole-genome or whole-exome sequencing. Consult with investigators on experimental design, feasibility, and analysis plans before data are generated, and contribute analysis plans, preliminary data, and written text to grant applications. Develop, adapt, and maintain reproducible analysis pipelines and software on UF's HiPerGator high-performance computing cluster, using workflow managers, containers, and version control. Integrate multi-omic, clinical, and publicly available data sets; develop or adapt statistical and computational methods where existing tools are inadequate. Interpret results in collaboration with investigators and contribute figures, methods sections, and manuscript text to publications and presentations. Maintain organized project documentation, code repositories, and data management practices, and track service requests and effort for shared-resource reporting. Contribute to training and outreach for cancer center members and trainees, including workshops, tutorials, and individual instruction. Maintain current knowledge of the field through seminars, journal clubs, and scientific meetings, and evaluate emerging methods and tools for adoption by the unit. This is a professional position ideal for candidates with advanced training, education, and experience in bioinformatics, computational biology, or similar fields. Employees in these roles operate as part of our collaborative research enterprise and are fundamental to the progress of cancer research being conducted at the University of Florida. A complete job description will be provided to candidates selected for interview. Expected Salary: Salary will be commensurate with education and experience. This is a TEAMS position, and is eligible for a suite of excellent benefits, retirement options, and paid leave . About the City of Gainesville Home to the University of Florida, one of the nation's top public universities, Gainesville offers vibrant academic and healthcare communities alongside an affordable cost of living, no state income tax, and mild winters. Enjoy the outdoors with over 30 miles of bike trails, freshwater springs, and numerous water activities, plus easy access to beaches and world-class theme parks. Gainesville combines historic charm with modern amenities, including a great school system, a lively downtown, and farmers' markets and craft breweries. Discover professional growth and quality of life in Gainesville—where community, innovation, and work-life balance meet! For more information, check out: The Guide to Greater Gainesville and Explore Gainesville in 60 seconds . Required Qualifications: Bioinformatics Research Scientist: Masters' in bioinformatics, computational biology, genetics, molecular biology, computer science, or a closely related field. Demonstrated experience analyzing high-throughput sequencing data, evidenced by publications, preprints, dissertation work, or a public code portfolio. Proficiency in R and/or Python and working knowledge of the Linux command line and shell scripting. Strong written and verbal communication skills, and the ability to work effectively with a broad range of collaborators, including investigators, trainees, industry sponsors, partners at other institutions, and UFHCI staff. Sr. Bioinformatics Research Scientist: Doctorate in bioinformatics, computational biology, genetics, molecular biology, computer science, or a closely related field. Demonstrated experience analyzing high-throughput sequencing data, evidenced by publications, preprints, dissertation work, or a public code portfolio. Proficiency in R and/or Python and working knowledge of the Linux command line and shell scripting. Strong written and verbal communication skills, and the ability to work effectively with a broad range of collaborators, including investigators, trainees, industry sponsors, partners at other institutions, and UFHCI staff. Preferred: Experience in a shared resource, or other collaborative setting managing multiple concurrent projects and deadlines. Experience with high-performance computing environments and job schedulers such as SLURM. Experience with workflow systems (Nextflow, Snakemake, or WDL), containerization (Apptainer/Singularity, Docker), and version control (Git). Experience with single-cell and spatial transcriptomics analysis. Working knowledge of applied statistics relevant to genomics, including generalized linear models, mixed models, multiple testing correction, and multi-omic integration. Familiarity with public data resources and repositories such as TCGA, GTEx, ENCODE, GEO/SRA, and dbGaP. Experience with compiled languages (C++, Rust), SQL, or relational database design. Experience developing and releasing software packages (Bioconductor, CRAN, PyPI, or equivalent). Experience contributing to federal grant applications. Familiarity with handling protected health information and controlled-access data under HIPAA and dbGaP data use agreements. Special Instructions to Applicants: The University of Florida is a member of the State University System of Florida and an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment based on merit. Applicants must upload a copy of the following documents to be considered for this position: Cover Letter or Letter of Interest Curriculum Vitae or Resume List of Three Professional References This is not a remote work position, and is located on the University of Florida's Main Campus in Gainesville, FL. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541475/financial-analyst-ii": {
      "title": "Financial Analyst II",
      "description": "Financial Analyst II Apply now Job no: 541475 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Business/Accounting/Finance, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 21010000 - CFO-VP OFFICE Classification Title: Financial Analyst II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience. Job Description: This person will support the University’s internal loan program and various other duties in the Office of the Treasurer. Specific duties include: Responsible for preparing pro-forma financial statements to support the University’s internal loan program. Calculates and monitors the University’s financial profile by performing financial ratio analysis. Reviews financial models prepared by the Direct Support Organizations such as UF Health and UAA, analyzing multi-year accounting data and other information to make projections for use in financial planning. Projects future trends, prepares long-range operational forecasts, and conducts scenario analyses Responsible for the University’s daily cash process, allocation of interest earnings, and other financial and accounting functions in support of the Treasury Office. Create and maintain procedures on critical functions Create financial reports to inform financial decision making. Use financial systems and work collaboratively with other areas within the University (e.g. controller, budget, etc.), to create and maintain data sets to inform financial reporting Preparing Presentations - Prepare materials associated with bond financings, including documents and rating agency materials. Assists in preparing presentations for the Board of Trustees and Finance Committee including helping to develop new reporting models Use the University’s Treasury Management system (Kyriba) and ERP (Workday) to assist in the various processes discussed in this section. Assist as needed with special project financial analysis Expected Salary: $70,000-$80,000, commensurate with education and experience Preferred: Strongly preferred bachelor’s degree in finance Accounting experience Microsoft Excel, Treasury Management System, and/or Workday experience Excellent communication skills are required as well as the ability to work with individuals on multiple levels Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 15 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541476/postdoctoral-associate-astrophysics": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Associate Astrophysics",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Associate Astrophysics Apply now Job no: 541476 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16360100 - LS-PHYSICS-GENERAL Classification Title: Postdoctoral Associate Astrophysics Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in astronomy, physics, or a closely related field at the time of appointment Job Description: The Department of Physics at the University of Florida invites applications for a Postdoctoral Associate to conduct research on topics related to supermassive black hole astrophysics under the mentorship of Prof. Laura Blecha. Preference will be given to candidates with experience and/or interest in modeling gravitational-wave or multi-messenger source populations, the cosmological evolution of black holes and galaxies, or other topics that overlap with the research of Blecha’s group. The preferred start date is August 16, 2027. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in astronomy, physics, or a closely related field at the time of appointment. The position is renewable annually for two years and may be extended to a third year, subject to satisfactory performance and the availability of funds. A salary of $60,000 and travel funding will be provided, as well as a full benefits package. The successful candidate will have access to the state-of-the-art HiPerGator computing cluster: ( it.ufl.edu/rc/hipergator/ ). The Physics and Astronomy Departments at the University of Florida have active research groups in numerous areas of astrophysics. The University of Florida is an equal opportunity institution. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. If an accommodation due to disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Expected Salary: $60k Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in astronomy, physics, or a closely related field at the time of appointment. Preferred: Experience and/or interest in modeling gravitational-wave or multi-messenger source populations, the cosmological evolution of black holes and galaxies, or other topics that overlap with the research of Blecha’s group. Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications must include: (i) a cover letter, (ii) a statement of research interests and plans, and (iii) a CV that includes a list of publications. In addition, names and email addresses for three references must be provided. After initial review, letters of recommendation will be requested for selected applicants. To ensure full consideration, application materials should be received by December 4, 2026. Please contact Laura Blecha at lblecha@ufl.edu with any questions. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 28 Feb 2027 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541478/accounting-specialist": {
      "title": "Accounting Specialist",
      "description": "Accounting Specialist Apply now Job no: 541478 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Business/Accounting/Finance Department: 64701000 - FA-BMS-ADMIN Classification Title: Accounting Specialist Classification Minimum Requirements: A bachelor's degree in an appropriate area of specialization and one year of appropriate experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: This position supervises and manages the Deposit Control area in the Banking and Merchant Services department within the CFO’s Division. Deposit Control ensures that university deposits are timely and accurately recorded in the myUFL system and assists departments in completing and correcting deposits. This position performs monthly reviews of deposit activity and support and liaises with General Accounting to ensure deposits represent authorized revenue streams and are appropriately recorded. Duties also include researching incoming electronic payments to identify departmental beneficiaries and assisting entities in setting up electronic payments to the university. Deposit control also advises departments on policies and procedures related to cash handling. Expected Salary: $53,000 - $58,000 annually; commensurate with education and experience Preferred: Preference given to experience in higher education environment. Knowledge of banking procedures and/or practices for receiving, depositing, and disbursing cash. Ability to understand and apply applicable rules, regulations, and procedures. Excellent customer service skills. Excellent written and oral communication skills. Ability to work effectively as team member. Demonstrated ability to work effectively with diverse decentralized customers and constituents. Working knowledge of Microsoft Office including Teams, Word, and Excel. Demonstrated proficiency with spreadsheet, database, query and other analytical tools. Ability to draft policy documents and translate regulatory requirements into practical and actionable elements. Strong risk analysis and problem-solving skills in business, higher education and regulatory environments. Strong organizational skills Demonstrated attention to detail Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 24 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 15 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/538085/ops-diabetes-peer-coach": {
      "title": "OPS Diabetes Peer Coach",
      "description": "OPS Diabetes Peer Coach Apply now Job no: 538085 Work type: Temp Full-Time, Temp Part-Time Location: In the State of Florida, Alachua, Dade, Hardee, Hillsborough, Polk Categories: Health Care Administration/Support Department: 33080000 - HP-HEALTH SERVICES ADMIN Classification Title: OPS-Sponsored Projects Non-Clerical Classification Minimum Requirements: Associate's degree, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The University of Florida Diabetes Institute is seeking dedicated and caring Diabetes Peer Coaches to support medically underserved communities across Florida. These part-time, time-limited positions are funded through the State of Florida’s Extension for Community Health Outcomes (ECHO) Diabetes initiative and are available in Alachua, Polk/Hardee, Hillsborough, and Miami-Dade counties. As a Diabetes Peer Coach, you will work directly with adults living with Type 1 (T1D) and Type 2 Diabetes (T2D) in primary care and community settings. In this role, you will: Support patients in navigating their diabetes care. Connect patients with local diabetes-related resources, support groups, and outreach opportunities. Help link patients with peers in their community who are also living with T1D or T2D. Participate in weekly ECHO tele-education sessions, serving as a bridge between local clinics (“spokes\") and UF (“hub”). Peer coaches will work collaboratively with clinical and academic faculty to advance the goals of the ECHO Diabetes program and will work under the supervision of the project’s Principal Investigator, Dr. Ashby Walker. Multiple positions are available, and placement will be based on the needs in Alachua, Polk/Hardee, Hillsborough, and Miami-Dade counties. Expected Salary: $16-25 per hour; commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Associate's degree, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Lives in or near program site locations (i.e., Alachua, Polk/Hardee, Hillsborough, and Miami-Dade counties). Strong working knowledge of Type 1 Diabetes and Type 2 Diabetes. Strong communication skills. Excellent interpersonal skills. Ability to work in a team setting. Ability to work with people from varied backgrounds. Strong working experience in a clinical care setting. Experience in mentoring, teaching, and/or community health work. Fluent in Spanish and/or Haitian Creole. Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered for this position, please include a cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:59 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position has been reposted; previous applicants remain under consideration and need not re-apply. These positions offer both full-time and part-time opportunities, with part-time positions working between 10-20 hours per week. Hours may be flexible within the week, but are expected to occur between normal business hours, Monday through Friday, from 8 am to 5 pm. This recruitment is anticipated to hire up to 17 individuals. If an accommodation is needed to apply for this position, please call 352/392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800/955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the U.S. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 23 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541433/program-coordinator-collegiate-veterans-success-center": {
      "title": "Program Coordinator, Collegiate Veterans Success Center",
      "description": "Program Coordinator, Collegiate Veterans Success Center Apply now Job no: 541433 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services Department: 04011000 - SL-DISABILITY RESOURCE CENTER Classification Title: Program Coordinator, Collegiate Veterans Success Center Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience consistent with University requirements. Job Description: Military-Connected Student Outreach and Engagement • Coordinate outreach and engagement strategy that increases awareness of CVSC resources, programs, space, student organizations and opportunities for prospective, incoming, current, online, and graduating military-connected students. • Supervise peer mentoring, student advisory, ambassador, volunteer, and/or student employment initiatives, including recruitment, onboarding, training, scheduling, coaching, and recognition for student assistants supporting the CVSC area. • Conduct targeted outreach at key transition points, including admission, orientation, return from deployment or military leave, academic milestones, graduation, and post-graduate transition. • Serve as a point of contact for military-connected students and families seeking information about University resources, transition, engagement, academic success, wellbeing, career development, and community support. • Develop accessible and timely communications across digital platforms, presentations, tabling, campus events, and partner channels to promote CVSC resources and opportunities. • Plan, implement and assess programs, workshops, recognition activities, peer connections, and signature events that foster community, resilience, leadership, and engagement, synthesizing student feedback to identify needs, reduce barriers, and recommend program or service improvements. • Expand outreach through relationships with military installations, veteran-serving organizations, alumni, employers, families, and community partners. • Create opportunities for military-connected students and others interested in supporting this community to participate, lead, volunteer, and mentor through CVSC initiatives. • Refer education-benefit questions to the designated School Certifying Official or responsible office unless separately designated and trained to perform certification duties. Program Coordination, Experiential Learning, Career Design, and Campus Partnerships • Design and coordinate the development, logistics, delivery, and evaluation of CVSC programs, workshops, mentoring initiatives, leadership experiences, and collaborative events. • Incorporate experiential learning through defined learning outcomes, meaningful responsibilities, reflection, feedback, and opportunities for students to demonstrate and articulate their learning. • Develop and facilitate educational programs and resources that strengthen campus understanding of military-connected student experiences, needs, resources, and effective referral practices. • Collaborate with campus and community partners to connect students with career exploration, professional networks, internships, employment, graduate education, and other post-graduate pathways. • Maintain collaborative relationships with campus and community partners supporting admissions, financial aid, veteran education benefits, academic success, accessibility, wellbeing, career development, housing, legal services, alumni engagement, and community resources. • Represent the Student Engagement and CVSC at assigned at partner meetings, resource fairs, committees, various events, and professional development opportunities. • Maintain accurate and accessible resource guides, referral protocols, websites, contact lists, and partner communications. • Coordinate assigned collaborations with Veterans Affairs, state and federal contacts, veteran-serving organizations, and community partners under the direction of leadership. • Develop resources and activities that help students translate military service and campus involvement into transferable leadership and career-readiness competencies. • Manage program calendars, reservations, vendors, purchasing documentation, marketing, staffing, accessibility, risk-management requirements, and post-program evaluations. • Research best practices and recommend approaches that strengthen military-connected student engagement within the UF context. • Support evening, weekend, and campus-wide events, as assigned. Student Support, Resource Navigation, and Student Organization Advising • Advise assigned registered student organizations, student leaders, and organization advisors on operations, leadership transitions, membership engagement, event planning, budgeting, risk management, and University policies. • Assist in the management of the student organization program and database (GatorConnect) and work with software provider to make continued improvements to the database and technology. • Maintain accurate records of student contacts, outreach, referrals, and follow-up in accordance with privacy requirements and approved University practices. • Provide logistical and administrative support for departmental priorities, student organization needs, and major campus-wide events as assigned. • Interpret and communicate University policies and procedures related to students, student organizations, events, campus safety, risk, and liability. Assessment, Administration, and Operations • Collect, organize, and report participation, outreach, referral, student feedback, learning, engagement, and career-related data using approved tools and definitions. • Assist with reporting, dashboards, annual reports, needs assessments, program reviews, presentations, budget tracking, purchasing documentation, and other operational records. • Support daily Student Engagement, Community and Belonging, and CVSC operations, including desk coverage, event support, space readiness, service standards, student employee schedules, inventory, technology, records, communications, accessibility, safety, and continuity procedures. • Use assessment findings and student feedback to recommend and implement improvements to programs, outreach, communications, and resource coordination. • Support grants, partnerships, sponsorships, and gift-funded initiatives as assigned, including documentation and reporting consistent with University requirements. Departmental, Divisional, and University Contribution • Contributes to the Division and University through active participation on committees and division-wide initiatives (example opportunities): Emergency dean on call, event support team, conduct committee, screening committees, student welcome events, etc); and other duties as assigned • Position receives regular direction, coaching, and feedback from the Associate Director of the Collegiate Veterans Success Center. Work is guided by established goals, applicable policies and procedures, scheduled meetings, and performance review. The coordinator is expected to exercise initiative and sound judgment in coordinating assigned programs, outreach, communications, referrals, and daily responsibilities, and to consult the supervisor on significant policy, student welfare, fiscal, reputational, or operational matters. • The position may provide day-to-day direction, training, scheduling, and coaching to assigned student assistants, interns, ambassadors, peer mentors, and volunteers. The position does not directly supervise professional staff. • Normal work schedule is Monday through Friday, 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., 40 hours per week, including frequent evening and weekend hours as assigned. Expected Salary: $48,000 to $50,000 Annual Salary Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience consistent with University requirements. Preferred: Master’s degree in higher education/student affairs, counseling, social work, public administration, communications, education, leadership, or a related field with experience working in a higher education setting, particularly in Student Affairs. Knowledge of military culture, transition from military service to higher education and career, and campus and community resources for veterans and military-connected students. Veteran or military-connected lived experience is valued but not required. Experience advising student organizations, mentoring student leaders, coordinating peer programs, or supervising student employees or volunteers. Experience with events and event management, and compliance-based systems or protocols Experience with outreach strategy, digital communications, program marketing, presentations, event planning, and partnership development. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 23 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 31 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541451/astasofull-prof": {
      "title": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF",
      "description": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF Apply now Job no: 541451 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29050100 - MD-MEDICINE-ADMINISTRATION Classification Title: Ast/Aso/Full Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants should hold a PhD, MD or MD/PhD in the biomedical sciences or a related field of study. Job Description: The Department of Medicine at the University of Florida (UF) College of Medicine invites applications for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor. We are seeking an outstanding, creative investigator in any area of Immunology. Our faculty include some of the brightest and most innovative scholars spanning a broad variety of disciplines and disease models. The Department of Medicine has established the Research Hub to foster cross-disciplinary collaborations and continue a major expansion of the faculty as part of a 5-year strategic plan. We invite you to learn more about us by visiting https://medicine.ufl.edu/research-new/ . UF has more than 30 immunology research laboratories situated across multiple colleges and institutes that are supported by state-of-the-art resources and centers in advanced imaging, genomics, cytometry, virology, vector biology, metabolomics, lipidomics, and oncogenesis. UF is also developing a world-class artificial intelligence (AI) research infrastructure to support investigators. Research spans innate and adaptive immunity, with existing strengths in infectious disease, organ-specific immunology of lung and kidney, autoimmunity, cancer immune therapies, and antiviral immunity. We are particularly interested in recruiting candidates who will build a research program that complements these existing strengths. The successful candidate will be expected to develop or have created a vibrant, externally funded research program, participate in graduate-level teaching, and recruit and mentor outstanding trainees. Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience and education. Required Qualifications: Applicants should hold a PhD, MD or MD/PhD in the biomedical sciences or a related field of study. Preferred: Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications should include cover letter, CV, research statement, and contact information for three references. The position offers outstanding research space, a highly collaborative environment, and a substantial start-up package. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 23 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 23 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541456/sr-program-manager-fixel-centers-of-excellence": {
      "title": "Sr. Program Manager, Fixel Centers of Excellence",
      "description": "Sr. Program Manager, Fixel Centers of Excellence Apply now Job no: 541456 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Business/Accounting/Finance, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 27303200 - HA-COMMUNITY AND PATIENT PROGR Classification Title: Program Manager Classification Minimum Requirements: A bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and five years of appropriate experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: Centers of Excellence Portfolio Management • Serve as the Institute's primary operational liaison and steward of the Centers of Excellence portfolio. • Coordinate Center designation, certification, renewal, annual reporting, and related requirements. • Maintain an accurate portfolio inventory, including Center leadership, external affiliations, designation requirements, key milestones, reporting obligations, and Institute support. • Support the development and launch of new Centers of Excellence. • Monitor portfolio activities and identify emerging needs, opportunities, risks, and resource considerations for Institute leadership. • Promote collaboration, shared learning, and consistency across Centers while recognizing differences in disease-specific programs and external foundation requirements. Operational Infrastructure & Continuous Improvement • Develop and maintain shared operational infrastructure supporting the Centers of Excellence portfolio, including standard operating procedures (SOPs), playbooks, templates, dashboards, workflows, reporting tools, annual planning resources, and resource libraries. • Identify opportunities to reduce duplication, streamline administrative processes, and improve the experience of faculty and Center teams. • Evaluate and recommend systems, tools, and processes that strengthen portfolio management and reporting. • Document and continuously improve core Center processes to support continuity and organizational effectiveness. Faculty and Center Coordination • Build relationships with Centers Directors and faculty leaders, serving as a trusted operational partner. • Coordinate cross-center collaboration, orientation of new leaders, communication, and Institute support resources. • Help connect faculty and Center teams with appropriate Institute and university resources • Support faculty success by reducing unnecessary administrative burden while respecting existing departmental coordinators and disease-specific staff. Governance and Strategic Initiative Support • Coordinate governance processes supporting designation, accountability, transparency, operational consistency, and continuous improvement. • Support advisory committees, working groups, faculty task forces, and other governance activities assigned by Institute leadership. • Coordinate meeting materials, documentation, action items, and follow-up, maintaining appropriate records related to Center governance, designation, and performance Strategic Engagement and Partnership Coordination • Coordinate Institute-wide visibility for Centers of Excellence. • Partner with Communications, Advancement, Research Operations, Institute Convenings, and Community and Patient Initiatives, and other Institute teams on activities involving Centers of Excellence. • Coordinate operational aspects of educational programming, outreach, donor stewardship, foundation relationships, recognition, and other collaborative initiatives. • Help identify opportunities to strengthen visibility, collaboration, and engagement across the Centers of Excellence portfolio. • Serve as an Institute liaison to external Center-affiliated foundations and organizations, as appropriate, in partnership with faculty and other functional leaders. Selected Center & Strategic Initiative Support • Provide direct operational coordination for selected Centers of Excellence or related Institute initiatives as assigned. • Support newly established Centers, philanthropically or externally supported programs, pilot coordination models, or Centers requiring additional Institute support. • Help develop approaches that can be evaluated and, when appropriate, scaled across the broader portfolio Expected Salary: $70,000 - $92,000 Required Qualifications: A bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and five years of appropriate experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: • Master’s degree in healthcare administration, business administration, public administration, higher education, science, public health, or a related field. • Five or more years of progressively responsible experience in academic medicine, healthcare administration, higher education, faculty programs, strategic initiatives, or program management. • Experience supporting multidisciplinary faculty programs, Centers of Excellence, institutes, centers, or similar academic or healthcare programs. • Experience coordinating committees, governance activities, designation processes, or faculty leadership groups. • Experience developing operational infrastructure, dashboards, reporting tools, workflows, or program management systems. • Experience partnering with communications, advancement, community engagement, research, or other cross-functional teams. • Demonstrated experience building or improving programs, systems, or organizational infrastructure. • Project Management Professional (PMP) certification or equivalent project/program management training is preferred but not required. • Progressively responsible experience in program management, healthcare administration, academic administration, faculty programs, strategic initiatives, or a related operational field. • Experience coordinating complex programs involving multiple stakeholders. • Strong organizational, analytical, written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. • Ability to manage multiple priorities and work independently with sound judgment. • Demonstrated ability to build effective relationships and coordinate work across organizational boundaries Special Instructions to Applicants: Please upload the following - CV or Resume, Cover Letter and List of Professional References. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 23 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541459/software-licensing-coordinator": {
      "title": "Software Licensing Coordinator",
      "description": "Software Licensing Coordinator Apply now Job no: 541459 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Computer Science, Information Technology, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 27010701 - HA-INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY Classification Title: End User Computing Spec III Classification Minimum Requirements: Associate’s degree and four years of appropriate experience; or a high school diploma or equivalent and 6 years of relevant experience. Appropriate college course work or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate of required experience. Job Description: Software Fulfillment Fulfills new software licensing requests submitted through the service management system. Actively manages annual licensing periods for existing applications and coordinates with departmental administration as required. Coordinates with IT asset management as required to maintain accurate licensing asset records. Departmental Software Liaison Serves as the departmental liaison for software requests in coordination with UF Software Licensing services. Utilizes the UF Liaison Dashboard to obtain and update licensing information as required. Software Installation Coordination Coordinate with customers and support teams regarding the installation and activation of software applications. Process optimization, risk mitigation, strategic initiatives Works closely with organizational leadership to optimize software licensing and deployment processes, identify and mitigate risks, and facilitate strategic initiatives. Expected Salary: $61,000 Required Qualifications: Associate’s degree and four years of appropriate experience; or a high school diploma or equivalent and 6 years of relevant experience. Appropriate college course work or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate of required experience. Preferred: Demonstrated experience with the licensing, deployment and support of common administrative, educational and research (statistical analysis, reference management, etc.) applications. Knowledge of service management, asset management and software management / deployment systems. Experience with supporting enterprise environments (10,000+ devices) and/or supporting academic health centers / clinical care. The successful candidate will have a strong customer-centric service orientation and excellent troubleshooting, communication, organizational and collaboration skills. Discretion, sound judgment, confidentiality, initiative, and interpersonal skills are critical to the success of this position. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please upload the following - CV or Resume, Cover Letter and List of Professional References. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 23 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540493/director-of-development-college-of-veterinary-medicine": {
      "title": "Director of Development, College of Veterinary Medicine",
      "description": "Director of Development, College of Veterinary Medicine Apply now Job no: 540493 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Advancement, Executive/Director/Management, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 50010000 - AD-VP ADVANCEMENT Classification Title: DIR, Development Classification Minimum Requirements: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and three years of appropriate experience in Development or a related field; or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and five years of experience in Development or a related field. Job Description: Portfolio Management Responsible for the identification, qualification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship of major and principal gift prospects as well as grateful clients. Build a portfolio of fundraising prospects at the major gift levels ($100,000+) and strategically manage relationships through face-to-face visits to bring gift solicitations to closure. Focus on individual prospects rated at a capacity of $100,000 or above. Assess and refine the portfolio by identifying new high-net-worth individuals and families with the capacity and affinity to support college priorities. Develop and implement tailored fundraising strategies for grateful client donors, aligning philanthropic interests with the College's priorities in research, education, patient care, and capital initiatives. Collaborate with the Office of Estate & Gift Planning team to develop and implement a gifting strategy for qualified donor portfolios at the planned giving level. Development Strategy & Donor Engagement Must develop comprehensive understanding of the college mission, strategy, and goals as they relate to fundraising. Partner with college leadership and unit/program directors to create and execute tailored, multi-year engagement and solicitation strategies for each assigned prospect, leveraging a moves management framework. Each plan should be rooted in the donor's philanthropic interests and aligned with university strategic priorities. Develop and present concept papers and gift proposals to donors. Organize and facilitate high-level engagement experiences, including campus visits, personalized briefings and engagement opportunities to deepen prospect relationships. Attend and support activities and events involving volunteers and prospects. This may include pre-and post- event publicity, as well as event set-up, break-down, and serving as a host. Deliver thoughtful, personalized donor stewardship by recognizing gifts in meaningful ways aligned with the mission and values of CVM and UF Advancement, while providing customized reporting, facilitating naming opportunities, managing long-term relationships, and ensuring donors are appropriately engaged and recognized through events and communications. Communications Develop and implement a comprehensive communications plan to inform constituents and identify markets about the mission, vision, and values of the unit, its funding priorities, and gift opportunities. Build and maintain effective relationships with college administration and faculty through regular, consistent, and relevant communication. Work with marketing and communications to design and produce collateral or promotional materials that relate to events, including programs, pledge forms, signage, etc. Administrative Ensure best practices in office systems, files and record keeping. Provide research, market, and prospect information for UFA’s integrated database, and handle confidential prospect information in a sensitive, ethical manner. Contribute to the planning and monitoring of the budget, using resources for best results in fundraising and planning activities involving volunteers, prospects, faculty and administration. Expected Salary: $85,000-95,000 annually, commensurate with education and experience. Exceptional Benefits We Offer Affordable State Health Plans: Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Comprehensive Coverage: Life and Disability Insurance Secure Retirement Options: Generous plans to safeguard your future Paid Time Off: Including 11 paid holidays, as well as family, sick, and vacation leave Professional and Personal Growth: Opportunities through UF Training & Organization Development, leadership development, LinkedIn learning, and more Tuition Assistance: Benefit from the UF Employee Education Program PSLF Eligibility: We are a Public Service Loan Forgiveness Eligible Employer The University of Florida offers a competitive benefits package. Click here to learn more. Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and three years of appropriate experience in Development or a related field; or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and five years of experience in Development or a related field. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: At least 4-6 years of major gift fundraising experience with documented success in cultivating and soliciting major gifts of $100,000 and above from individual prospects with campaign experience. Prior experience in a college or university setting, enthusiasm for fundraising in a comprehensive research university, and the desire to work collegially within a goal-oriented fundraising organization. Knowledge of fundraising principles, methods, and standards. Expertise in developing solicitation strategies. The ability to work strategically, develop and implement constituency programs and activities, along with a talent for motivating volunteers. Ability to work collaboratively and a team player Competency in problem solving. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, strong interpersonal abilities, and the capacity to engage effectively and collaboratively with a wide range of individuals are essential. Support the University’s commitment to creating a respectful and collaborative work environment that values integrity, professionalism, and mutual respect in all interactions; demonstrated success collaborating with stakeholders; strong project management skills; experience in a data-driven environment; excellent organizational abilities; and the capacity to lead, influence, and inspire others are essential. Proficient in the following areas: Windows operating systems, Microsoft Word, Excel, and Access, as well as internet browsers and email platforms. Familiarity with donor databases, contact management systems, and tracking systems is also highly preferred. The ability and willingness to travel. A valid driver license and good driving record are essential.* *Business expenses are reimbursed through a standard accounting system. The ability to pay for expenses using your own personal credit card or funds and have them reimbursed is essential. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume and three references. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Work sponsorship is not available for this position. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 22 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541430/astaso-prof": {
      "title": "AST/ASO PROF",
      "description": "AST/ASO PROF Apply now Job no: 541430 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Social/Behavioral Science Department: 26040100 - HH-SPM ADMIN-INSTRUCTION Classification Title: AST/ASO PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: An earned doctorate in sport management or related discipline is required by the start date. Job Description: The Department of Sport Management at the University of Florida (UF) invites applications and nominations for one, full-time, tenure-track Assistant/Associate Professor position. This is a 9-month, tenure-track, on-campus appointment scheduled to begin August 16, 2027. Qualifications We are seeking candidates who will pursue an innovative, rigorous research program; teach undergraduate and graduate courses; and provide department, college, and professional service. An earned doctorate in sport management or related discipline is required by the start date. Candidates should have the ability or experience in conducting high-impact research, publishing in top sport management journals, and seeking external funding to support their scholarship. They should be passionate and enthusiastic toward student learning and demonstrate a commitment to teaching excellence. We are seeking applications from candidates with teaching and research expertise related to sport law, risk management or other related research area (e.g., policy, organizational behavior). Preference will be given to applicants who have demonstrated ability to publish in high-impact journals and pursue external funding to support their scholarship. The Department of Sport Management The Department of Sport Management (SPM) is committed to excellence and impact through (a) innovative, socially impactful research; (b) the preparation and training of future industry and academic professionals; (c) engaging with the community, and (d) being stewards of the future. The department includes 9 instructional and 8 tenured or tenure-track faculty who are supported by award winning advisory and administrative staff. Across residential and online programs, SPM reaches more than 900 students (over 600 undergraduate majors, 350 master’s students, and 14 PhD students). The Sport Management online program was one of the first in the country and is a thriving part of the department. Our website ( https://hhp.ufl.edu/about/departments/spm/ ) expands upon our research and teaching activities. The University of Florida The University of Florida is one of the top ranked Public Universities in the United States 1 and is Florida’s oldest and largest land grant university. UF is a member of the prestigious Association of American Universities, an organization comprising the top public and private institutions in North America. With an enrollment of over 61,000 students annually, UF is home to 16 colleges and more than 200 research, service and education centers, bureaus, and institutes. There is a vibrant culture of research, with over $1 billion in research expenditures last fiscal year. The UF campus is in Gainesville, a city of approximately 145,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 85 miles from the Georgia border, 50 miles from the gulf, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean. It has abundant wildlife, mature forests, prairies, lakes, and opportunities for many outdoor activities. Alachua County Public Schools is an excellent school system, with many public schools ranked highly in the state and nation. Expected Salary: Salary is commensurate with qualifications and experience. Required Qualifications: An earned doctorate in sport management or related discipline is required by the start date. Preferred: We are seeking candidates who will pursue an innovative, rigorous research program; teach undergraduate and graduate courses; and provide department, college, and professional service. An earned doctorate in sport management or related discipline is required by the start date. Candidates should have the ability or experience in conducting high-impact research, publishing in top sport management journals, and seeking external funding to support their scholarship. They should be passionate and enthusiastic toward student learning and demonstrate a commitment to teaching excellence. We are seeking applications from candidates with teaching and research expertise related to sport law, risk management or other related research area (e.g., policy, organizational behavior). Preference will be given to applicants who have demonstrated ability to publish in high-impact journals and pursue external funding to support their scholarship. Special Instructions to Applicants: Application Instructions Interested applicants must apply online at https://jobs.ufl.edu (reference job #541430) and include (a) an application letter summarizing qualifications; (b) a research statement and evidence of research proficiency; (c) a teaching statement and evidence of teaching proficiency; (d) a curriculum vitae; and (e) names, addresses, email addresses, and telephone numbers of three references. The search committee will begin reviewing applications immediately. To receive full consideration, complete applications must be received by October 30, 2026, however, the position will remain open until filled. Send inquiries and nominations to Dr. Dan Connaughton, Search Committee Chair, at: danc@hhp.ufl.edu. The University of Florida is an equal employment institution. Selected candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluations Services (NACES) which can be found at http://www.ncas.org. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD). Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 22 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541431/astasofull-prof-division-of-surgical-oncology-bmse": {
      "title": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF: Division of Surgical Oncology - BMSE",
      "description": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF: Division of Surgical Oncology - BMSE Apply now Job no: 541431 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Allied Health, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29141201 - MD-SURGERY-SURGONC-BMSE Classification Title: AST/ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: This position requires an MD degree or equivalent; board certification or eligibility in general surgery. Job Description: The Department of Surgery at the University of Florida is recruiting a tenure or non-tenure track surgeon to join the Division of Surgical Oncology – Breast, Melanoma, Sarcoma and Endocrine (BMSE). A devotion to endocrine surgery is required. The academic rank will be based upon the career stage and development of the candidate. This new physician will join the division’s five full-time and one part-time surgeons with a reputation of excellence in patient care. The division has a long-standing commitment to medical student, resident and fellow education and the new surgeon will be involved in this effort. Special consideration will be given to applicants with a commitment to research. Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: This position requires an MD degree or equivalent; board certification or eligibility in general surgery. Preferred: Fellowship training in Endocrine Surgery Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 22 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541434/assistant-director-for-programs-community-and-belonging-student-life": {
      "title": "Assistant Director for Programs, Community and Belonging, Student Life",
      "description": "Assistant Director for Programs, Community and Belonging, Student Life Apply now Job no: 541434 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Executive/Director/Management, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 04080602 - SL-SE-COMMUNITY & BELONGING Classification Title: Assistant Director for Programs, Community and Belonging, Student Life Classification Minimum Requirements: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and two years of appropriate experience or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and four years of experience. Job Description: Provide leadership, management, and oversight for registered student organization support services, advisor resources, and student organization operations. Manage the coordination, education, and processing of student organization registration, event permitting, risk management, on-site event management, officer eligibility, space allocations, and related administrative processes. Develop and implement training, resources, consultations, and educational opportunities for student organization leaders and advisors related to leadership development, event planning, budgeting, policy interpretation, risk management, and program assessment. Serve as a primary resource to student leaders, advisors, and campus partners regarding student organization policies, procedures, systems, and best practices. Support the coordination and implementation of student organization management systems, including GatorConnect, and maintain accurate records, resources, and operational processes. Develop and maintain collaborative relationships with campus partners, including Event Services, University Police Department, Student Conduct and Conflict Resolution, venue managers, and other university departments that support student organization activity. Interpret university policies for students and student organizations relating to the management of programs, contracts, safety, liability and risk management, and fiscal management. Lead, coordinate, and elevate Community and Belonging programs that promote community development, student engagement, involvement, leadership development, and student connection. Provide leadership for the planning, implementation, supervision, and assessment of programs within the Community and Belonging portfolio, including but not limited to Speed Friending, individual and group involvement consultations, Break Buddies, MLK campus celebration, Gator Wellness Day, and other emerging initiatives. Develop and refine programs that foster meaningful student connection, reduce isolation, support leadership development, and increase student engagement across campus. Coordinate program timelines, logistics, budgets, staffing plans, marketing needs, risk considerations, and assessment strategies to ensure high-quality and sustainable program delivery. Identify opportunities to create, elevate, and implement new programs that respond to student needs and advance the goals of the Office of Community and Belonging. Collaborate with campus departments, student leaders, and community partners to strengthen programmatic efforts and expand student participation. Serve as a resource for event planning and management for students, staff, and faculty as needed. Assist with budget planning, monitoring, and reporting for events. Assist in developing and maintaining collaborative relationships in programming efforts with other UF departments, including academic departments. Assist with administrative leadership, supervision, assessment, and strategic operations for the Office of Community and Belonging. Provide supervision, training, and support for professional staff, graduate assistants, and student employees assigned to student organization support and community programming functions. Assist with strategic planning, goal setting, budget planning, financial monitoring, reporting, and resource allocation for assigned programs and services. Develop, conduct, and analyze assessment efforts related to student organization services, program effectiveness, student satisfaction, learning outcomes, and emerging student needs. Contribute to comprehensive marketing, communication, and outreach strategies that promote student involvement opportunities, programs, services, and resources. Stay informed of trends and issues related to student engagement, student organizations, community development, and leadership development; make recommendations for improvements to programs, services, and operational practices. Contributes to the Division and University through active participation on committees and division-wide initiatives (example opportunities: emergency dean on call, event support team, conduct committee, student welcome events, etc; and other duties as assigned. Expected Salary: $58,000 to $60,000 Annual Salary Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in appropriate area of specialization and two years of appropriate experience or a bachelor’s degree in appropriate areas of specialization and four years of experience. Valid driver's license. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 22 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/537055/assistantassociateprofessor-nephrologist": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Professor - Nephrologist",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate/Professor - Nephrologist Apply now Job no: 537055 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty, Non-Tenure-Track Faculty, Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29051300 - MD-NEPHROLOGY Classification Title: Assistant/Associate/Full Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Board certified/board eligible and eligible for a Florida Medical License. Job Description: Faculty Position – Division of Nephrology, Hypertension, and Renal Transplantation University of Florida, Department of Medicine The University of Florida (UF) Department of Medicine invites applications for a faculty position in the Division of Nephrology, Hypertension, and Renal Transplantation. We seek a board-certified or board-eligible nephrologist eager to contribute to a thriving quaternary academic medical center, at the rank of tenure /non-tenure Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor. We are looking for passionate physician-leaders who demonstrate teamwork, service, and a commitment to training the next generation of physicians. Why UF Nephrology? Our Division is nationally recognized for excellence in research, education, and clinical care. Faculty members have the opportunity to: Practice world-class nephrology across varied settings, including inpatient and subspecialty outpatient clinics. Contribute to programs in onconephrology, glomerulonephritis, stone disease, lupus nephritis, CRRT, home dialysis, and transplant services. Collaborate on innovative basic, clinical, and AI research with strong institutional support. Advance their careers in an environment that values innovation, scholarship, and mentorship. What We Offer: Competitive compensation with a strong base salary, productivity incentives, and excellent benefits. Flexible career pathways, including VA affiliation if desired. A culture that promotes work-life balance in sunny Florida. Rank and tenure status commensurate with experience. About UF Health: UF Health is one of the Southeast’s premier academic health systems and home to UF Health Shands Hospital, a 1,000-bed quaternary referral center ranked among the Top 50 hospitals in multiple specialties by U.S. News & World Report. With six health colleges and nine institutes on a single campus, UF fosters unparalleled collaboration and discovery. As a Top 5 public research university, UF provides abundant resources to support teaching, research, and clinical excellence. Life in Gainesville: Gainesville offers the energy of a university town with the warmth of a welcoming community, including: Affordable living and nationally ranked schools. Year-round outdoor activities such as hiking, biking, kayaking, snorkeling, and cave diving. Easy access to pristine beaches, state parks, and world-famous theme parks. Convenient travel via Gainesville Regional Airport with direct flights to major hubs. Join us at UF to transform lives, advance nephrology, train future nephrologists, and thrive in a supportive environment for both your career and well-being. Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Board certified/board eligible and eligible for a Florida Medical License. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . This requisition has been reposted and will be used to fill multiple positions. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes. Advertised: 21 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540942/academic-assistant-iii-school-of-physician-assistant-studies": {
      "title": "Academic Assistant III - School of Physician Assistant Studies",
      "description": "Academic Assistant III - School of Physician Assistant Studies Apply now Job no: 540942 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services Department: 29710100 - MD-SCHOOL OF PA STUDIES - GEN Classification Title: Academic Assistant III Job Description: The Academic Assistant III serves as the Registrar & Clinical Year Assistant for the School of Physician Assistant Studies (SPAS), responsible for student enrollment, course development, and academic verification. Additionally, the position will provide course support for five clinical year courses and one clinical year didactic course, and is responsible for Academic Activities reporting for didactic and clinical courses. About This Role Student Registration and Academic Verification Prepare course registrations and processes student enrollment accurately, including resolving any discrepancies in student records. Review student transcripts and academic records, monitor progress toward graduation requirements, and provide guidance to clinical students regarding graduation processes, requirements, and deadlines. Support assigned courses by maintaining course information, processing grade updates, and ensuring academic records are updated in accordance with university procedures and deadlines. Process and fulfill requests for postgraduate education verification, providing essential documentation to outside institutions and licensing boards, and perform quality checks to ensure the validity of educational credentials and records. Prepare and update working manuals. Clinical Year Course Support Develop and maintain assigned Canvas courses by posting and updating syllabi, schedules, instructional materials, assignments, announcements, and other course content based on guidance from course director(s). Collaborate with other staff to ensure that all lectures and learning activities are added to the calendar/curriculum management system. Provide administrative support to course directors, including preparing and maintaining course-related communications, documentation, and student informational materials. Work with the staff of the Center for Experiential Learning and Simulation to schedule learning activities and performance-based assessments for assigned courses. Manage student assignments and grades for all assigned courses. Provide back-up coverage to SPAS Didactic and Clinical Coordinators. Academic Administration Compile, maintain, and distribute Academic Activities Reporting (AAR) documentation; review reported information for completeness and address discrepancies for resolution. Evaluate faculty effort and resolve discrepancies. Serve as administrative support for the Professional Standards and Promotions Committee, take minutes, and maintain tracking documents. Set up and maintains evaluation system for assigned courses. About the College of Medicine The University of Florida's College of Medicine is committed to advancing health through education, research, and patient care. With a focus on innovation and excellence, the college prepares future healthcare leaders through a rigorous curriculum that combines basic sciences with hands-on clinical experience. The College of Medicine emphasizes interdisciplinary collaboration and community engagement, fostering and environment where students, faculty, and staff work together to improve healthcare outcomes. The College is home to cutting-edge research initiatives and state-of-the-art facilities, providing an exceptional training ground for aspiring medical professionals. Dedicated to enhancing health care, the College of Medicine plays an impactful role in shaping the future of medicine. For more information about the College of Medicine and its programs, visit College of Medicine . We Offer Exceptional Benefits Low-cost State Health Plans : Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Life and Disability Insurance Generous Retirement Options to secure your future Comprehensive Paid Time Off Packages with 10+ paid holidays, paid family, sick and vacation leave Exceptional Personal and Professional Development Opportunities : Access to UF Training & Organizational Development programs, leadership development, LinkedIn Learning, and more Tuition Assistance through the UF Employee Education Program Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) Eligible Employer Learn more about what we have to offer here ! Expected Salary: $23.00 - $25.50 per hour Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree; or an equivalent combination of education and experience Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: Proficiency in Microsoft Office (Excel, Word, PowerPoint, Outlook), Google/GSuite, and Windows Platform. Experience with the UF PeopleSoft system. Be willing to learn new programs and systems including web-based services. Strong organizational skills and detail-oriented abilities to assess for accuracy and quality of work. Ability to manage multiple projects and deadlines with minimal supervision. Exhibits intellectual curiosity and critical thinking skills. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume, and contact information for three professional references. This position is eligible for veterans' preference. If you are claiming veterans' preference, please indicate your claim on the application and upload a copy of your DD-214 or equivalent certification from the Department of Veterans Affairs with your application for consideration. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 21 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541383/forensic-pediatrics-clinical-assistantassociate-professor": {
      "title": "Forensic Pediatrics- CLINICAL ASSISTANT/ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR",
      "description": "Forensic Pediatrics- CLINICAL ASSISTANT/ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR Apply now Job no: 541383 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30090000 - JX-PEDIATRICS-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: CLIN AST/ASO/FULL PROF Minimum Requirements: Applicants must possess a MD/DO degree and possess or be eligible for a Florida Medical License. Job Description: The Department of Pediatrics at the University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville has an opening for a full-time position in the Division of Forensic Pediatrics and Child Protection. The position will be at the non-tenure accruing level of Assistant/Associate Professor and will assume the role of Medical Director of the University of Florida’s Child Protection Team (CPT) and fellowship program. Major responsibilities will include directing the day-to-day clinical operation of the Child Protection Team, clinical care to victims of child abuse, and teaching of fellows, residents and medical students. Conducting community trainings on child abuse is also required. The Director supervises the largest CPT in Florida, in what is an advanced statewide Child Protection Team system. This is a large busy clinical and academic team that includes 10 child abuse examiners of different disciplines, 23 case coordinators/interviewers, a supervising Team Coordinator to meaningfully manage contracts, reports, hiring and scheduling, a sexual abuse trauma therapy program, and focused senior CPT assistants working on death reviews and Death Review Teams for multiple counties. Services are provided at CPT Clinics as well as to area hospitals. Including Wolfson Children’s Hospital (Level I Pediatric Trauma Center) and the University of Florida Health – Jacksonville (Level II Trauma Center). This position will report to the Chief of the Division of Forensics and Chairman of the Department of Pediatrics at the University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville. Following is a general description of duties and responsibilities: Provide overall direction and supervision of the CPT team for program service delivery and administration. Provide leadership in establishing an effective and coordinated response to Child Abuse Hotline reports with the designated area Department of Children and Families representatives. Participate and represent the team in the designated community service area as appropriate. Provide advanced medical consultations and supervision, interfacing with 8 different county child protection agencies (Department of Children and Families), the medical examiners, and officers of the State Attorney. Provide medical evaluations of children, and supervise and review the work of the provider agency’s team medical practitioners for the provision of team medical evaluations of children suspected to be abused or neglected. Arrange for and provide in-service training and other professional development for team staff and medical personnel. Assist the statewide CPT Medical Director in the planning and provision of training for regional and statewide training sessions. Participate as a committee member (or appoint a medical designee) in the local Child Abuse Death Review (CADR) Committee. Annually, plan and conduct a minimum of two training sessions in the detection of child abuse and neglect for area physicians and other medical staff, particularly those working in hospital emergency rooms. Provide guidance and oversee the team’s screening of child abuse reports made to the Florida Abuse Hotline, and provide selected direct screening as required by law, or when otherwise determined appropriate. Be available 24 hours a day, seven days a week for consultation to team members and team physicians including telemedicine consultation, when available and appropriate, for medical assessment needs. Arrange coverage by another CMS approved CPT Medical Director when unavailable. Assure medical representation for team staffing. Participate in peer reviews with other team physicians and other medical providers. If designated and approved, participate in monitoring of child protection teams as a member of the statewide-designated monitoring team. Attend statewide child protection team meetings and other meetings, and training sessions as required. Provide medical consultation in an area of expertise to other teams when requested. Provide depositions and expert court testimony when appropriate. Provide teaching of fellows, residents and students. About UF Health: UF Health is the Southeast’s premier academic health center and an integral part of the University of Florida. Our mission is to promote health through outstanding and high-quality patient care, innovative and rigorous education in the health professions and biomedical sciences, and research across the spectrum of basic, translational and clinical investigation. UF Health encompasses the Gainesville-based University of Florida colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine; the UF Health Shands family of hospitals in Gainesville; UF Health Jacksonville medical center; UF Health North; an academic campus in Jacksonville that is home to the UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville and includes degree programs offered by the colleges of Nursing and Pharmacy; UF Health St. Johns in St. Augustine; UF Health Neighborhood Hospital in Ocala; and our campus in Central Florida, which includes UF Health Spanish Plaines Hospital and UF Health Leesburg Hospital. It also includes primary care and specialty practices throughout Central, North Central and northern Florida and Southeast Georgia. The UF Health network of hospitals and physician practices manages more than 3 million inpatient and outpatient visits each year, and serves patients from all 67 Florida counties, from across the nation and from dozens of countries around the world. About College of Medicine – Jacksonville (COM-J): The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three colleges at the Health Science Center Jacksonville. The college's 16 clinical science departments house more than 500 faculty members, 1,560 staff and 450 residents and fellows, as well as medical students from UF and around the country. We offer an incredible breadth of clinical training programs and proud to train many of best primary care providers and specialists throughout the region, the state and the country. Research, discovery and innovation are critical aspects of our clinical campus and we have some of the country’s leading researchers at our locations searching for and finding new treatments and clinical options. The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville faculty, administrators, residents, fellows, students and staff work as a team in pursuit of our common mission — to heal, to comfort, to educate and to discover through quality health care, elimination of health disparities, medical education, innovation and research. To learn more about our college, leadership, mission, faculty resources, and the city of Jacksonville visit https://med.jax.ufl.edu/administrative-affairs/faculty-orientation-guide/ . Expected Salary: Negotiable base on experience and academic appointment. Preferred: BE/BC Pediatrician Special Instructions to Applicants: The Search Committee will begin to review applications as soon as an applicant pool is identified. Please provide letter of intent and Curriculum Vitae. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 21 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541417/lecturerinstructional-assistant-professor": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Instructional Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Lecturer/Instructional Assistant Professor Apply now Job no: 541417 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60150000 - AG-FOOD SCIENCE - HUMAN NUTR Classification Title: Lecturer Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates shall have received the academic degree appropriate to the assignment of duties. Job Description: The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences (IFAS) is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . This is a 12-month, non-tenure-accruing faculty position that will be 100% teaching, available in the Food Science and Human Nutrition Department within the College of Agriculture and Life Sciences and the Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences at the University of Florida. The salary is competitive and comes with a full benefits package. Duties will include teaching undergraduate and graduate food science courses in both in-person and online formats. Teaching responsibilities may include courses such as Food Microbiology, Food Product Development, and other core food science courses, as well as the development and delivery of upper-level undergraduate courses aligned with the successful candidate’s area of expertise and interests. The faculty member is expected to contribute to excellence in teaching through innovative instructional approaches and curriculum development. The successful candidate will also mentor and advise undergraduate students and support student professional development through participation in experiential learning and competitive activities. This includes coaching and mentoring students involved in organizations and competitions such as the Food Science Club, FFA-related activities, IFT Student Quiz Bowl, Product Development Competitions, and other regional and national student competition events. The successful candidate is encouraged to engage in scholarly activities related to instruction, participating in curriculum revision and enhancement, actively seeking funding for the teaching program, publishing teaching-related scholarship, producing learning tools, and engaging in professional development activities related to teaching and advising. Faculty are encouraged to support and participate in the CALS Honors Program, distance education, and international education. A successful candidate with a Ph.D. degree would be eligible to use the ‘Instructional Assistant Professor’ working title. Because of the IFAS land-grant mission, all faculty are expected to be supportive of and engaged in all three mission areas—Research, Teaching and Extension—regardless of the assignment split specified in the position description. Background Information : The Food Science and Human Nutrition Department (FSHN), a large interdisciplinary unit within IFAS, comprises three distinct undergraduate programs: Food Science, Nutritional Sciences, and Dietetics. The Food Science program is an approved curriculum with the Institute of Food Technologists (IFT). The department has approximately 30 full-time faculty members, including a significant group of federally funded researchers with a rich history of national research awards. The undergraduate student enrollment in Food Science and Human Nutrition is approximately 550, including 72 Food Science students. The graduate program enrolls over 60 M.S. and Ph.D. students in Nutritional Sciences, Dietetics, and Food Science. Within walking distance are superb opportunities to participate in basic and translational research with the UF Diabetes Institute, the Clinical and Translational Science Institute, and collaborate with faculty from the Colleges of Medicine, Health and Human Performance, Public Health, Pharmacy, Liberal Arts and Sciences, Engineering, and Agricultural and Life Sciences. The University of Florida is a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution, encompassing virtually all academic and professional disciplines, with an enrollment of more than 56,000 students. UF is a member of The Association of American Universities. The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences includes the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences , the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station , the Florida Cooperative Extension Service , the College of Veterinary Medicine , the Florida Sea Grant program, and encompasses 16 on-campus academic departments and schools, 12 Research and Educational Centers (REC) located throughout the state, 6 Research sites/demonstration units administered by RECs or academic departments, and Florida Cooperative Extension Service offices in all 67 counties (counties operate and maintain). The School of Natural Resources and Environment is an interdisciplinary unit housed in IFAS and managed by several colleges on campus. UF/IFAS employs nearly 4,500 people, which includes approximately 990 salaried faculty and 1,400 permanent support personnel located in Gainesville and throughout the state. IFAS, one of the nation’s largest agricultural and natural resources research and education organizations, is administered by a Vice President and four deans: the Dean of the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences, the Dean for Extension and Director of the Florida Cooperative Extension Service, the Dean for Research and Director of the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station, and the Dean for the College of Veterinary Medicine. UF/IFAS also engages in cooperative work with Florida A&M University in Tallahassee. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Experience and Education Required Qualifications: A master’s degree (foreign equivalent acceptable) in food science or a closely related discipline is required. Candidates should have demonstrated skills in teaching, verbal and written communication, and interpersonal relationships. The successful candidate must be able to work effectively with students, faculty, and staff and demonstrate a commitment to student success. Candidates must be supportive of the mission of the Land-Grant system and committed to the University of Florida’s core values. Preferred: A Ph.D. degree (foreign equivalent acceptable) in Food Science or a closely related discipline is preferred, but not required (only required for use of the ‘Instructional Assistant Professor’ working title). Preference will be given to candidates with experience teaching food science courses in both in-person and online learning environments, including course development and curriculum enhancement. Familiarity with the Institute of Food Technologists (IFT) Higher Education Review Board (HERB) approval process assessment, or securing extramural funding to support educational programs is also desirable. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, candidates should apply and submit materials by November 1, 2026. The position will remain open until a viable applicant pool is determined. Employment Conditions This position is available after January 1st, 2027 and will be filled as soon thereafter as an acceptable applicant is available. Compensation is commensurate with the education, experience, and qualifications of the selected applicant. Nominations Nominations are welcome. Nominations need to include the complete name and address of the nominee. This information should be sent to: Please refer to Requisition # 541417 Soohyoun Ahn, Ph.D. Chair, Search and Screen Committee University of Florida Food Science and Human Nutrition P.O. Box 110370 Gainesville, FL 32611 Telephone: 352-294-3710 Electronic Mail: sahn82@ufl.edu Application Information Individuals wishing to apply should go online to Careers at UF and submit the following documents: Cover letter that states the applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above (2-page limit) Statement of teaching and mentoring philosophy and interests relative to the duties and responsibilities above (2-page limit) Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for three individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Unofficial transcripts Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 21 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541424/procurement-agent-iii": {
      "title": "Procurement Agent III",
      "description": "Procurement Agent III Apply now Job no: 541424 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Business/Accounting/Finance, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 21030000 - CFO-PURCHASING SERVICES Classification Title: Procurement Agent III Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and five years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: Analyzes requisitions/contracts, checking for compliance with BOG, UF and Foundation regulation, directives, policies and Florida Statutes, proper information and approvals to include insurance. Reviews and edits vendor contracts. Works with vendors to negotiate terms. When possible, consolidate requisitions for like items to obtain economies of scale and the benefits of maximum competition. Approves change requests as necessary. Assists in identification of, and works on, purchases exceeding University bid/solicitation limit. Determines appropriate acquisition method – solicitation, sole source or other methods. Facilitates and assists with specification development for public solicitations: Invitation to Bid (ITB), Request for Proposal (RFP) and Invitation to Negotiate (ITN). Solicits and evaluates vendor responses to solicitations. Engages stakeholder participation. Facilitates negotiation process. Works on contract implementation including internal UF business processes and communication to campus (web pages, listserv emails). Participates in vendor business reviews and vendor requests for contractual changes. Maintains a high level of customer service to UF departments regarding current and future requirements, specifications, results of price quotations and bids, etc. through open lines of communication, providing and developing training and periodic department visits. Promptly answers inquiries from vendors and UF employees concerning public purchasing process and UF purchasing process. Works to resolve vendor disputes. Contributes to development of external training including review of resources educating campus about contracts related to areas of commodity/service concentration. Assists in documentation of internal processes. Maintain expertise in assigned commodity/service areas, product cost trends, market conditions and purchasing techniques by attending professional purchasing association meetings, courses, workshops, reading trade publications and journals, participating in webinars, listservs, reading web sources, visiting vendors’ facilities to continuously improve and broaden knowledge of products and services. Develop a network for assigned commodity areas, consisting of technical and manufacturer’s representatives, salespersons and customers/stakeholders, to keep current on new products, mergers, ideas, etc. Stay current with existing and new policy matters of the State and University regarding the procurement of needed goods and services. Expected Salary: $75,000-$85,000 Preferred: Certified Purchasing Supply Management (C.P.S.M.), Certified Public Purchasing Officer (CPP) or equivalent certification preferred. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 21 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541427/program-manager-internal-awards-and-research-administration": {
      "title": "Program Manager, Internal Awards and Research Administration",
      "description": "Program Manager, Internal Awards and Research Administration Apply now Job no: 541427 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Grant or Research Administration Department: 27302400 - HA-RESEARCH FUNDING INTERNAL Classification Title: Research Administrator III Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree and three years of experience in grant and contract administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: Institute Awards Administration • Coordinate the full lifecycle of Institute-supported funding opportunities, including planning, announcement, application or nomination, review, selection, award notification, setup, monitoring, reporting, closeout, and maintenance of historical records. • Administer internal RFAs, pilot and seed awards, fellowship awards, faculty development awards, strategic initiatives, donor-supported or donor-directed awards, discretionary allocations, and other Institute funding mechanisms. • Serve as a primary administrative resource for applicants, award recipients, faculty leaders, departments, and Institute leadership regarding award processes and requirements. • Maintain award documentation, timelines, terms, reporting requirements, and recipient records. • Coordinate timely closeout of awards and disposition of remaining balances in accordance with Institute and university requirements. Research Administration & Financial Coordination • Coordinate administrative activities associated with Institute-supported research and other funded initiatives. • Support Research FSEAs and other Institute research or investment accounts. • Partner with investigators, department administrators, Institute Finance, Sponsored Programs, and other university offices on award setup, financial monitoring, reporting, transfers, and closeout. • Help ensure funding is administered in accordance with Institute requirements, donor or funding restrictions, and applicable university policies. • Assist faculty and departments in navigating administrative requirements associated with Institute-supported research activities. Scientific & Programmatic Review Coordination • Coordinate operational aspects of ad hoc Scientific Review Panels and other faculty-led review processes. • Coordinate reviewer identification and recruitment, conflict-of-interest documentation, review materials, scheduling, scoring, meeting logistics, and maintenance of appropriate records. • Provide administrative support that enables faculty reviewers and Institute leadership to conduct fair, consistent, and well-documented review processes. • Support development and continuous improvement of review procedures, tools, templates, and reviewer guidance. Program Operations & Systems Development • Develop, implement, and continuously improve systems, workflows, templates, databases, dashboards, and other tools supporting Institute awards and research administration. • Support implementation and optimization of award-management platforms or similar systems. • Develop standardized processes and documentation that improve consistency, transparency, efficiency, and the applicant and recipient experience. • Identify operational gaps and recommend practical improvements to Institute leadership. Communications & Program Coordination • Partner with Marketing & Communications, Advancement, Strategic Engagement & Partnerships, and other Institute teams to coordinate award announcements, recipient recognition, donor stewardship activities, presentations, and other communications related to Institute-supported initiatives. • Maintain clear and timely communications with applicants, recipients, reviewers, faculty leaders, departments, and administrative partners throughout the award lifecycle. • Coordinate information needed by functional partners while allowing the appropriate teams to lead communications, fundraising, donor engagement, or other specialized activities. Reporting & Portfolio Analytics • Maintain accurate operational reporting for the Institute’s funding portfolio, including award metrics, funding summaries, progress reports, outcomes, historical records, and portfolio dashboards. • Track key deadlines, commitments, balances, and reporting requirements. • Prepare information and analyses that support Institute leadership in stewardship, planning, resource allocation, and decision-making Expected Salary: $61,000 - $83,000 Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree and three years of experience in grant and contract administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: • Master’s degree in business, healthcare administration, public administration, science, research administration, higher education, or a related field. • Five or more years of progressively responsible experience in research administration, academic medicine, higher education, or an academic health center. • Experience administering internal funding programs, fellowships, research awards, pilot programs, or similar grant programs. • Experience coordinating scientific peer review panels, faculty committees, advisory groups, or similar review processes. • Experience with proposal or award management systems such as Proposal Central, InfoReady, SmartSimple, Workday, or similar platforms. • Experience developing dashboards, metrics, portfolio reports, or executive-level reporting. • Demonstrated experience building or improving programs, systems, workflows, or operational infrastructure. • Project Management Professional (PMP) certification or equivalent project/program management training is preferred but not required. The successful candidate should demonstrate: • Progressively responsible experience in research administration, grants or award administration, higher education administration, healthcare administration, or a related operational field. • Experience coordinating complex projects or programs involving multiple stakeholders. • Strong organizational, analytical, written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills. • Ability to manage multiple priorities, meet deadlines, and work independently with sound judgment. • Demonstrated ability to develop or improve administrative processes and systems Special Instructions to Applicants: Please upload the following - CV or Resume, Cover Letter and List of Professional References. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 21 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/539938/astasofull-prof": {
      "title": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF",
      "description": "AST/ASO/FULL PROF Apply now Job no: 539938 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty, Non-Tenure-Track Faculty, Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29350000 - MD-DERMATOLOGY Classification Title: Ast/Aso/Full Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants must have an M.D. degree and be board-certified or board-eligible in Dermatology. Applicant must be eligible for a Florida Medical License. Job Description: The Department of Dermatology invites applications for a Medical Dermatologist to pursue a career with the Department of Dermatology in the College of Medicine at the University of Florida. This is a part-time .60 FTE clinical, non-tenure-track position. Primary duties include providing care for dermatology patients at the UF Health Springhill in Gainesville, FL. It is expected that the candidate participates in teaching dermatology residents/fellows, medicine residents, and medical students. Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: Applicants must have an M.D. degree and be board-certified or board-eligible in Dermatology. Applicant must be eligible for a Florida Medical License. Preferred: Special Instructions to Applicants: Please upload a CV, cover letter, and three letters of recommendation to be considered for this position. Application review will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"issued to student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This is a time-limited position. This requisition has been extended. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541251/research-coordinator-ii": {
      "title": "Research Coordinator II",
      "description": "Research Coordinator II Apply now Job no: 541251 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Health Care Administration/Support, Grant or Research Administration Department: 29240105 - MD-HOBI-BIOMED INFORMATICS Classification Title: Research Coordinator II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Biomedical Informatics and Data Science Program (BIDS), hosted within the Department of Health Outcomes and Biomedical Informatics and closely affiliated with the Clinical and Translational Science Institute (CTSI), engages in diverse informatics research and service across the University of Florida. Service initiatives include, but are not limited to, supporting the informatics infrastructure for the CTSI, the OneFlorida+ Clinical Research Network, and the University of Florida Health Cancer Institute (UFHCI). The Biomedical Informatics team is seeking a qualified and enthusiastic individual to serve as a Research Coordinator. The Coordinator will play an important role in the management and execution of cutting-edge research studies at the intersection of health care delivery and information technology to enhance the quality of care and population health. The Coordinator will work under the supervision of the Associate Director of Project and Program Management of the Biomedical Informatics (BMI) Program, and will support projects led by BMI faculty. The job responsibilities include a full range of research-related and organizational support efforts across multiple initiatives. The Coordinator will facilitate team communication, coordinate research study execution, and support grant proposals and research dissemination. The Coordinator will collaborate with internal and external research partners, clinical providers, and other stakeholders. Within these activities, opportunities will be available for the Coordinator to foster their professional interests and develop new skills. Successful candidates must be committed to working with various professional and community populations. Duties: Biomedical Informatics Research Study Coordination: Track objectives, milestones, deliverables, status reports, study results. Translate study design decisions into concrete tasks, and assist in setting implementation priorities. Manage communication among staff, study sponsors, healthcare professionals, and investigators, to ensure on-time reports and other deliverables. Work with investigators and administrative staff to maintain budgets, monitor project spending, and develop and maintain IRB protocols, prepare and submit progress reports, and other research-related documentation. Communication: Organize and lead project and team meetings. Track and communicate project updates to team members, leadership, and other internal and external stakeholders. Grant Proposal Development: Coordinate grant proposal development with investigators and grants administrators. Collaboratively manage submission milestones and deadlines. Identify and review extramural funding opportunities and share opportunities with investigators and other partners. Research Protocol Implementation: Work collaboratively with investigators and project teams to conduct all phases of quantitative and qualitative research including study recruitment, informed consent, retention, and data collection and analysis. Communicate with study participants to address participant questions/issues and implement study protocols Dissemination: Work with Investigators to produce publications in peer-reviewed journals and presentations to academic, organizational, and community audiences. Expected Salary: $60,000 - $65,000 Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Master’s Degree with at least two years of experience in conducting health services, informatics, or other health-related research. Excellent attention to detail, particularly with regard to interpreting and implementing protocols and in terms of collecting data to ensure accuracy and integrity Excellent written (in English) and oral communication skills. Ability to work independently and to collaborate with faculty, staff, and healthcare professionals at multiple levels Demonstrate strong skills and proficiency in Excel, Word, Powerpoint, Adobe, and Project Management software (Asana and Atlassian products preferred) Working knowledge of informatics and/or health-related research Ability to handle protected health information and human subject issues Ability to interpret basic statistical results Ability to work with investigators to draft project proposals and plans, manuscripts for publication in peer-review journals, and project reports Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This is a time-limited position. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 16 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541285/digital-communications-student-assistant": {
      "title": "Digital Communications Student Assistant",
      "description": "Digital Communications Student Assistant Apply now Job no: 541285 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Communications/Public Relations/Marketing, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services Department: 50010000 - AD-VP ADVANCEMENT Classification Title: STU AST - CLERICAL & ADMIN Classification Minimum Requirements: Skills needed for this position include: • A basic working knowledge of Microsoft Office Suite • Basic understanding of HTML and CSS preferred. • A basic understanding of Adobe products is helpful. • Must be able to work independently • Have a strong work ethic Job Description: The University of Florida Advancement Digital Communications team has an opening for a Student Assistant starting Fall 2026. This position provides support to the Digital Marketing Team in the following areas: Producing digital communications to our Alumni and Donors Data reporting and analysis to our collaborators Project Coordination Website and graphic design support Assistance with event marking as needed. Salary starts at $15.00/hour with 10 hours a week required. Work hours are between 8 am – 5 pm (candidate picks schedule based on class schedule). Expected Salary: $15.00 per hour Required Qualifications: Skills needed for this position include: A basic working knowledge of Microsoft Office Suite Basic understanding of HTML and CSS preferred. A basic understanding of Adobe products is helpful. Must be able to work independently Have a strong work ethic Preferred: Skills needed for this position include: A basic working knowledge of Microsoft Office Suite Basic understanding of HTML and CSS preferred. A basic understanding of Adobe products is helpful. Must be able to work independently Have a strong work ethic Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume, and application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 15 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541379/ops-learning-technologies-specialist": {
      "title": "OPS Learning Technologies Specialist",
      "description": "OPS Learning Technologies Specialist Apply now Job no: 541379 Work type: Temp Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 18300000 - ED-LASTINGER CENTER Classification Title: OPS - Learning Technologies Specialist Job Description: LMS and Course Design & Development Design, develop, and maintain online courses and learning experiences within D2L Brightspace or Articulate Rise. Apply instructional design, accessibility, and visual design principles when developing and updating courses and learning experiences. Utilize Brightspace/Articulate Rise features, HTML, CSS, and other approved technologies to create and enhance online learning experiences. Create, organize, and maintain course content, learning materials, resources, and digital assets. Collaborate with instructional designers, subject matter experts, and other project stakeholders to develop and improve courses and learning experiences. Conduct quality assurance and accessibility checks to ensure courses meet established standards for content, functionality, navigation, and accessibility. Ensure integrated tools/applications and SCORMs perform as intended. Course Project Management and LMS Support Take ownership of assigned course development and revision projects, working with project stakeholders to complete deliverables and meet established timelines. Manage and maintain assigned courses and learning experiences, including routine updates, revisions, and improvements. Communicate with instructional designers, content reviewers, subject matter experts, and program staff regarding course development and updates. Maintain course documentation, version history, and other records needed to support course continuity and ongoing maintenance. Gather and incorporate feedback from stakeholders to support improvements to existing courses and learning experiences. Assist the Instructional Design team with the development of learning materials, course activities, and other project needs as assigned. Troubleshooting and Stakeholder Collaboration Troubleshoot LMS, course, and learning experience issues and escalate more complex concerns to appropriate team members when needed. Respond to internal stakeholder questions and requests related to LMS functionality and assigned courses. Document reported issues, resolutions, and course changes according to established team processes. Participate in user acceptance testing and quality assurance activities for course launches, updates, and LMS enhancements. Collaborate with team members to identify and resolve issues affecting course quality or functionality. Other tasks as assigned Expected Salary: $31.00/hour Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Bachelor’s degree in Instructional Design, Educational Technology, Education, or a related field. Experience with instructional design principles, adult learning practices, Universal Design for Learning (UDL), and accessibility standards for online learning. Experience designing and developing online courses or learning experiences using D2L Brightspace, Articulate Rise, or similar learning technologies. Working knowledge of HTML and CSS, as well as eLearning standards and technologies such as SCORM. Strong organizational, project coordination, communication, and interpersonal skills, with the ability to manage multiple projects and meet established deadlines. Experience working in an academic or educational setting and collaborating with subject matter experts, instructional designers, program staff, and other stakeholders. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is soft-funded and time-limited. In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is a full-time position, working Monday - Friday 8am - 5pm Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541403/ops-secretary": {
      "title": "OPS Secretary",
      "description": "OPS Secretary Apply now Job no: 541403 Work type: Temp Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Dentistry Department: 34170100 - DN-RESTORATIVE DENT GEN DEPT Classification Title: OPS Secretary Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and two years of relevant experience Job Description: Serve as primary administrative support for the department faculty. Responsibilities include receptionist duties (phones, front desk, building issues), scheduling, producing reports and correspondence, and rendering consistent office support as needed. Assist in the coordination and be primary contact in scheduling department meetings/lunches and events (reserving conference rooms, place catering orders, event set up/clean, etc.) Handle routine requests for information for faculty, staff, and outside inquiries via phone, mail, and email. Daily pick-up and delivery of mail. Assist guests and delivery personnel. Place information technology and maintenance tickets. Lab support/chemical inventory/training/maintenance. Maintain office and break room supplies/cleanliness. Manage department conference room reservations. Assist with various onboarding/offboarding activities. Other duties as assigned. Expected Salary: $15.50 - $16.50/hr Benefits: The University of Florida offers low-cost State Health plans, a number of Dental plans to fit you and your family's needs, and Vision. Build your retirement with the FICA Alternative Plan and a host of voluntary add-ons, such as 457 deferred compensation and 403(b) plans. Optional insurance plans such as life, disability, legal, and accident insurance are also available. UF Employees are also eligible for the GatorPerks discount program , which provides big savings at various businesses and retailers! Explore UF's benefit options here: OPS Benefits Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and two years of relevant experience. Preferred: The successful candidate will have experience with Microsoft Office AS or AA degree Experience with Adobe Acrobat Experience working in a healthcare or educational setting Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. This is a time-limited position. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 26 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541413/resfellowship-prog-assistant-infectious-diseases-global-medicine": {
      "title": "Res/Fellowship Prog Assistant - Infectious Diseases & Global Medicine",
      "description": "Res/Fellowship Prog Assistant - Infectious Diseases & Global Medicine Apply now Job no: 541413 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support, Education/Training/Instructional Design, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 29050800 - MD-INFECTIOUS DISEASES Classification Title: Res/Fellowship Program Assistant Classification Minimum Requirements: Two year college degree and one year of relevant experience, or combination of education and relevant experience. Job Description: This position is located in the Department of Medicine, Division of Infectious Diseases and Global Medicine. Under the direction of the Division Administrator, the Program Assistant is responsible for providing administrative support and coordination to the Division Fellows, as well as the Fellowship Director and Assistant Director. The position must provide a high level of customer service both in person and on the telephone to all patients, faculty, referring physicians, fellows, staff, and other visitors. The Program Assistant performs responsibilities by anticipating needs and proactively providing support with minimal direction. The position is responsible for maintaining sensitive and confidential materials that are received and/or developed during the course of the position’s job duties. Position reports to the Division Administrator. The Program Assistant will serve as the fellowship program’s fiscal manager, responsible for assisting in the fiscal management and maintenance of the program. Responsibilities include understanding basic financial and operational concepts and trends, identifying financial results that do not meet expectations and working with the Chief/Administrator to determine the reason for the variance, and translating analyses into actionable information for decision making. Requires ability to effectively use New Innovations, interpret and apply scheduling principles to work hours reports; to work accurately with numerical detail; and to analyze, consolidate, and interpret accounting data. This position requires on campus presence. General Fellowship Program Duties Schedules all academic conferences and fellowship meetings, including maintaining conference room calendar, individual fellow/faculty calendar invites, sending weekly reminders and taking notes Maintains an electronic progress log and files on all applicants (and other files as requires) as Fellowship Program moves into the match Develop and maintain a system to assist the faculty with review of applicant packets and creation of the final match list Schedule interviews (virtually or in person depending on the programs’ preference) and create itinerary for fellowship applicant interviews with appropriate faculty, Chief, etc. Confirms date, time, and location of all appointments in the week prior to the interview. Ensures the applicants’ interview day flows properly and is presented in a professional manner Create annual fellow schedule and ensure divisional scheduling system and EPIC On-Call schedules are up-to-date and accurate. Including VA schedule Responsible for typing and mailing all correspondence related to the Fellowship Program in a timely manner with attention to spelling, grammar, punctuation, and formatting Organizes new fellow orientation annually, including but not limited to assisting Program Director with orientation handbook, scheduling onboarding sessions with UF Housestaff Office and VA Hospital, obtaining network access, ACLS training, electronic medical record training, security and confidentiality training and research orientation Annual Fellowship Graduation, including securing a venue, catering, event set-up and teardown, running the event, and adhering to a modest budget Create budget tracking spreadsheets for fellows, monitor spending and ensure that expenses are tracked and all purchases are within budget. Expected to understand basic financial and operational concepts and trends, identify financial results that do not meet expectations and work with the Chief/Administrator to determine the reason for the variance, and translate their analyses into actionable information for decision making Fellowship Accreditation Activities Serve as the primary point of contact for all inquiries of the Infectious Diseases Fellowship Program Creates, maintains, and updates all fellowship documents required by the ACGME guidelines. Ensures that the Training Program adheres to ACGME standards and brings any concerns to the attention of the Program Director Upkeep of New Innovations evaluation system, including entering schedules, running reports, and monitoring the compliance of fellows, faculty and staff to evaluation requests Ensure fellows schedules are accurately reflected in necessary systems and rotation assignments align with fellow funding lines and program requirements Maintains filling system for all fellowship documents and correspondence Compiles necessary information for the ACGME site visits in an accurate and timely manner Attends DOM and COM meetings regarding fellowship activities Responsible for monitoring updates and changes in University and ACGME requirements for fellowships through email, list serves, websites, and official correspondence Other Responsibilities Division Admin office coverage, including consult distribution Clinical learner rotation management Prioritize assignments and messages based on urgency or deadlines and work cooperatively with other Division, Department, and University staff to ensure that all assignments are completed in a timely manner and meet or exceed the standards for quality and performance, as established by the Division Serve as a liaison to multiple internal and external entities, including but not limited to: Departments in the College of Medicine or Department of Medicine and its Divisions, Fellowship Office, Division Clinics and VA Medical Center. Consistently strive to represent the Division, Department, and University in a positive and professional manner Work closely with administrative support staff in the Division to ensure that all faculty are supported in their clinical and academic needs Provide direct back-up support when absences occur Adhere to all office policies, procedures, and protocols established by the Division Expected Salary: $22.11 - $27 per hour; commensurate with education and experience Minimum Qualifications: Two year college degree and one year of relevant experience, or combination of education and relevant experience. Preferred Qualifications: Proficiency in Word, Excel, PowerPoint, E-mail and Internet Demonstrated outstanding ability to work under pressure in a busy office with frequent interruptions, changing priorities, and short deadlines Ability to plan, organize, and coordinate work assignments Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with others Ability to function under stressful circumstances always maintaining respectful and supportive interactions Ability to communicate effectively both verbally and in writing Ability to work independently Strong interpersonal and telephone skills Knowledge of ACGME rules and guidelines Special Instructions to Applicants: Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been extended and reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541418/senior-compensation-analyst": {
      "title": "Senior Compensation Analyst",
      "description": "Senior Compensation Analyst Apply now Job no: 541418 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Executive/Director/Management, Human Resources Department: 62010900 - HR-CLASSIFICATION Classification Title: Senior Compensation Analyst Classification Minimum Requirements Masters degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience; or a bachelors degree in an appropriate area and six years of relevant experience. Job Description: The University of Florida Human Resources is seeking a strategic thinker who pairs deep compensation expertise with exceptional analytical, consulting, and executive communication skills. You should be comfortable navigating complex structures, working with sensitive information, challenging conventional approaches, and translating sophisticated compensation data into practical recommendations leaders can act on. Reporting under the general direction of the Vice President for Human Resources, you will work with significant independence and collaborate with senior leaders, HR and Finance partners, colleges and administrative units, external consultants, and other key stakeholders. You will influence some of UF’s most important compensation decisions while leading high-visibility initiatives that affect employees across the university. KEY RESPONSIBILITIES: Shape enterprise compensation strategy : Develop and evaluate compensation philosophies, programs, salary structures, and practices that strengthen UF’s ability to compete for and retain exceptional talent. Advise on executive compensation: Provide sophisticated market analysis and strategic recommendations related to executive and senior leadership compensation, including recruitment, retention, market positioning, peer groups, and compensation practices. Turn data into strategy: Lead complex market benchmarking, financial modeling, compensation analytics, and workforce analyses, transforming data into clear recommendations for senior decision-makers. Lead university-wide salary initiatives: Guide the planning, design, implementation, and evaluation of annual merit and salary increase programs in partnership with university leadership, Finance, HR, colleges, and administrative units. Help transform the future of compensation at UF: Provide strategic compensation leadership as the university transitions to Workday and identify opportunities to strengthen compensation programs through technology, analytics, automation, standardization, and improved governance. Lead through influence: Manage complex cross-functional initiatives and partner with external compensation consultants, survey organizations, and market data providers while providing strategic direction and expertise across organizational boundaries. If you’re ready to bring your compensation expertise to a complex, high-impact environment where your insights can shape consequential workforce decisions, we invite you to explore this opportunity with a University dedicated to its mission “to be a premier university that the state, nation, and world look to for leadership.” For more information, visit www.ufl.edu . WHY GAINESVILLE As Senior Compensation Analyst working from the University's main campus in Gainesville, FL, you'll enjoy a vibrant hub of education, healthcare, culture, and sports in the state. The Greater Gainesville area serves as the cultural, educational, and commercial heart of the north central Florida region and offers a full range of municipal services, including cultural and administrative support. Renowned for its preservation of historic landmarks and natural beauty, the city boasts numerous parks, museums, and lakes that attract thousands of visitors. With its lush urban forest and Tree City USA® designation, Gainesville stands out as one of Florida's most picturesque cities. Its central location provides convenient access to both the Gulf of Mexico and the Atlantic Ocean, offering easy exploration of Florida's famed beaches, springs, and nature reserves. Explore Gainesville in 60 Seconds. OUR COMMITTMENT The University of Florida is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer. Hiring is contingent on eligibility to work in the U.S. The University of Florida is a public institution and is subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . Salary $100,000.00 with consideration to market, education and experience. Required Qualifications: Masters degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience; or a bachelors degree in an appropriate area and six years of relevant experience. Preferred Highly competitive candidates may also bring experience in several of the following areas: Enterprise-wide compensation strategy and program design Executive compensation and market benchmarking Large-scale merit or salary planning programs Advanced compensation analytics and financial modeling Executive-level consultation and presentations Complex, cross-functional project leadership Compensation surveys, market data, and external consulting partnerships Higher education or another large, complex, decentralized organization CCP, SHRM-SCP, SPHR, or a comparable professional certification Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Inquiries can be sent to stg@hr.ufl.edu. Candidates must apply online at Careers at UF. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 18 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 16 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/535112/post-doctoral-associate-in-multiomics": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Associate in Multi-Omics",
      "description": "Post Doctoral Associate in Multi-Omics Apply now Job no: 535112 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Allied Health, Grant or Research Administration, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 36020000 - PHHP-COM EPIDEMIOLOGY Classification Title: Post Doctoral Associate Job Description: The Department of Epidemiology ( http://epidemiology.phhp.ufl.edu/) is strategically situated within the College of Public Health and Health Professions (PHHP) and the College of Medicine. Faculty members are jointly appointed to create synergies and collective strength, resulting in a competitive research profile and highly trained students who receive exceptional exposure to a variety of expertise. Our faculty and students have received numerous national awards in research and mentoring and are committed to excellence in training. The department offers an epidemiology PhD, Master of Science, and concentration in the MPH program. It is also home to two National Institutes of Health Training grants (T32). Faculty members are strongly supported by our college-level instructional design team for course development and delivery and by our research core for grant development and submission. Dr. Jinling Liu’s laboratory ( https://phhp.ufl.edu/profile/liu-jinling/ ) at the University of Florida's Department of Epidemiology and The Cener for Genetic Epidemiology and Bioinformatics (GeneBio; https://genebio.phhp.ufl.edu/ ) is accepting applications for 1-2 fully-funded postdoctoral associate positions, starting immediately. Dr. Liu has extensive experience in big data analytics, systems biology, probabilistic graphical models, causal inference and machine learning. Dr. Liu's research interests lie in modeling the rapidly-accumulating big data (e.g., muti-omics) in biology and medicine for precision medicine via a variety of statistical and machine learning techniques – one of her projects along these lines has been recently funded by a prestigious NIH Career Development Award for young investigators. She is committed to advancing precision medicine through her work. She is also collaborating with researchers in other domains for data analytics and decision making. Liu lab has access to rich high-dimensional multi-omics data, including WGS, WES, DNA methylation, RNA-seq, proteomics, lipidomics and metabolomics in large-scale human populations. The lab is interested in integrating and mining the different types of data to understand the genomic causes and other biomarkers as well as the underlying signaling network for a variety of diseases including aging, cardiovascular diseases, brain diseases, cancer, et al., through novel statistical or deep learning methods development and application. The postdoc associates will be exposed to rich multi-omics data, a variety of diseases, advanced statistical and machine learning methods and wide collaborations with experts from different fields. Key responsibilities for the postdoctoral associates include multi-omics data analysis, novel method development and application, manuscript writing, research presentation at conferences, and assistance with grant applications and student mentoring. Additional Information: The University of Florida ( www.ufl.edu ) is a comprehensive research-extensive university that includes a full range of academic departments and programs. The College of Public Health and Health Professions ( www.phhp.ufl.edu ) is part of the University’s Health Science Center. The UF Health Science Center ( https://ufhealth.org/about-us ) is comprised of six health-related colleges located on a single, contiguous campus. They include the colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine. The Health Science Center also partners with the immediately adjacent UF Health Shands Hospital, the Malcom Randall VA Medical Center, and the UF Health Science Center Regional campus located in Jacksonville, FL. Additional major institutes and centers located at the University of Florida include the Emerging Pathogens Institute, the Institute of Child Health Policy, the UF Health Cancer Center, the UF Genetics Institute, the Clinical and Transactional Science Institute and the McKnight Brain Institute, all of which provide state-of-the-art environments and access to large data sets for faculty collaboration. Expected Salary: $63,480 annually; commensurate with experience level and in accordance with current NIH postdoctoral salary guidelines. Postdoctoral associates are eligible for comprehensive health and leave benefits and have access to libraries and other research resources available for faculty and postdoctoral associates. Additionally, postdoctoral trainees may receive funds each training year to support professional development (e.g., travel to workshops or national conferences, purchase of education materials). Minimum Requirements: PhD degree in computer science, statistics, biostatistics, biomedical informatics or related fields OR a PhD degree in biology or related fields. Preferred Qualifications: A strong background in programming (e.g., Python or R or C++) is essential. Research experience utilizing skills of programming, statistics, and AI are essential. The ideal candidate should demonstrate independent and critical thinking abilities as well as good communication skills. Prior research experience in analyzing biomedical data is preferred. Special Instructions to Applicants: Interested applicants should submit an application, including a CV, statement of interest, and contact information for three references. Substantive queries may be addressed to Dr. Jinling Liu at jinling.liu@ufl.edu (Assistant Professor and Principal Investigator). Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position has been reposted; previous applicants remain under consideration and need not re-apply. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . University of Florida is a member of the State University System of Florida and an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment based on merit. The University of Florida is an Equal Employment Opportunity institution. If an accommodation is needed to apply for this position, please call 352/392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800/955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the U.S. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 17 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/536501/assistantassociatefull-professor-classical-hematology": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor -  Classical Hematology",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Classical Hematology Apply now Job no: 536501 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty, Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29050604 - MD-HEMATOLOGY-ONCOLOGY-OTHER Classification Title: Assistant/Associate/Full Professor Job Description: Hematologist at the University of Florida. The University of Florida Department of Medicine is seeking applications for a full or part-time Classical Hematologist for a clinician-educator or clinician-researcher track position in the Division of Hematology and Oncology at the Assistant Professor rank or higher. This position seeks a board-certified/board-eligible Hematologist. Applicants must be eligible for a Florida Medical License. This individual will have a primarily clinical assignment but will also be expected to develop, participate in, and lead clinical/educational/translational research projects; embrace team-oriented research; engage in teaching students, housestaff, and fellows in the clinic and inpatient settings; and provide direct patient care at our Gainesville campus. A track record of prior clinical research and scholarly activity is desirable, but not required. Academic interests could include translational research, early-phase clinical trials, symptom management, observational studies, and/or health disparities. The UF Division of Hematology and Oncology faculty are closely engaged and partners with the University of Florida Health Cancer Institute (UFHCI), the only NCI-Designated Cancer Institute based at a public university in Florida. The UFHCI blends comprehensive patient care and innovative research in a collaborative, multidisciplinary environment, with members from 11 of the 16 colleges at the University of Florida (UF). UF, ranked #7 among public universities by U.S. News & World Report and #4 by Forbes in 2025, stands as a premier research institution, driving innovation and discovery while delivering exceptional value and academic excellence. Resources available for professional development at the University of Florida Health Sciences Center include leadership, education, and research training through support from the UF Health Cancer Institute, Clinical Translational Science Institute (CTSI), formal mentorship programs, and supported opportunities for teaching and research. Foreign national candidates whose employment conditions meet federal and University requirements under an immigrant classification are eligible to apply. Tenure accruing and non-tenure accruing tracks are available. Rank and tenure status will be commensurate with qualifications. About Gainesville, Florida Gainesville ( http://cityofgainesville.org ) is home to Florida's largest and oldest university and is one of the state's centers of education, medicine, cultural events, and athletics. Gainesville is also the largest city in Alachua County and is the county seat, with approximately 269,000 residents county-wide. The city provides a full range of municipal services, including cultural and nature services and necessary administrative services to support these activities. Additionally, Gainesville is home to a regional transit system, a regional airport, and a 72-par championship golf course. Gainesville is known for the preservation of historic buildings and the beauty of its natural surroundings, and the city's numerous parks, museums, and lakes provide entertainment to thousands of visitors. Because of its beautiful landscape and urban \"forest,\" Gainesville is one of the most attractive cities in Florida. It is a hidden gem in North Central Florida, within a couple of hours of major cities such as Orlando, Jacksonville, and Tampa. We are in a central location providing easy access within a couple of hours' drive to beaches, nature preserves, and water-based activities. We have warm weather much of the year, allowing for year-round outdoor activities. Due to the vibrant atmosphere generated by The University of Florida and UF Health Shands, the leading employers in Gainesville, the city offers a range of amenities for residents. Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience. Minimum Requirements: Applicants must be board-certified or board eligible in Hematology and be eligible for a Florida Medical License. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 26 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540414/end-user-computing-spec-2": {
      "title": "END USER COMPUTING SPEC 2",
      "description": "END USER COMPUTING SPEC 2 Apply now Job no: 540414 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Information Technology, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 27010701 - HA-INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY Classification Title: End User Computing II Classification Minimum Requirements: Associate’s degree and two years of appropriate experience; or a high school diploma and four years of appropriate experience. Appropriate college coursework may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience Job Description: Provides Tier-2 hardware and operating system support for end user computing equipment including workstations, printers, scanners, and mobile devices. May also include specialty computing devices which interface with clinical or research equipment. Provides Tier-1 support for telephony, network, and audio-visual devices or services. Support includes standard configuration and/or troubleshooting steps as documented in process documentation and the knowledgebase. Escalates unresolved incidents to the appropriate Tier-2 support team. Provides Tier-2 support for enterprise and unit level applications which support clinical, educational, research, and administrative functions. Support includes client installation and configuration, basic functionality troubleshooting, and assistance with application features or interfaces Documents all work and processes in service management systems, performs initial hardware and software setup and reimages production systems; provides customers guidance and instruction on the use of hardware and software Contributes to the improvement of workflow processes, knowledge support systems, and IT provided services through active participation in team meetings. Participates in technical and customer service training initiatives and inter-team technical projects as directed Expected Salary: $53,000 Required Qualifications: Associate’s degree and two years of appropriate experience; or a high school diploma and four years of appropriate experience. Appropriate college coursework may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Class E Driver's License is required. Preferred: Demonstrated knowledge of current Windows and/or Mac OS X operating systems, network troubleshooting (LAN/wireless), and performing hardware diagnostics and repair. Experience with supporting enterprise environments (10,000+ devices) and/or supporting academic health centers / clinical care; Working knowledge of common administrative, educational and research applications and services and familiarity with service management industry best practices is strongly preferred. The successful candidate will have a strong customer-centric service orientation and excellent troubleshooting, communication, organizational and collaboration skills. Discretion, sound judgment, confidentiality, initiative, and interpersonal skills are critical to the success of this position Special Instructions to Applicants: Please upload the following - CV or Resume, Cover Letter and List of Professional References. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541001/clinical-research-coordinator-ii-epilepsy-division": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Coordinator II - Epilepsy Division",
      "description": "Clinical Research Coordinator II - Epilepsy Division Apply now Job no: 541001 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration Department: 29180200 - MD-NEUROLOGY-EPILEPSY Classification Title: Clinical Research Coordinator II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Clinical Research Coordinator II will support clinical research activities within the iBRAIN Laboratory in the Department of Neurology at the University of Florida. This position is responsible for coordinating human subjects research focused on neurological disorders, including epilepsy and dementia. The incumbent will work closely with the Principal Investigator and research team to ensure efficient, compliant, and timely implementation of study protocols. More About This Role Participant Recruitment and Study Coordination Recruit, screen, consent, enroll, and retain participants Schedule and coordinate study visits, assessments, imaging, electrophysiology, biospecimen collection, and follow-up activities Maintain screening/enrollment logs and communicate with participants, caregivers, clinical teams, and investigators Data Collection and Management Collect, enter, verify, and track clinical, behavioral, imaging, electrophysiology, and biospecimen data Maintain accurate source documents, case report forms, and coded records Complete REDCap/Epic data entry, quality checks, query resolution, secure data transfer, and archiving Regulatory Compliance, Quality Assurance, and Study Operations Prepare and maintain IRB submissions, consent documents, regulatory files, deviations, adverse-event records, and required reports Track study milestones, support monitoring and audits, coordinate start-up and closeout activities, and ensure compliance with protocols, Good Clinical Practice, and UF/UF Health requirements Research Team and General Support Participate in research team meetings and complete required training and relevant professional development activities Provide general research and administrative support and perform other related duties as assigned by the Principal Investigator Managing Clinical Trial funding Coordinate and process participant payments for clinical research study visits in accordance with study protocols, sponsor requirements, institutional policies, and regulatory guidelines Verify visit completion and participant eligibility for reimbursement, maintain accurate payment documentation, reconcile payment records, resolve payment-related inquiries, and ensure timely and compliant disbursement of participant compensation while maintaining confidentiality and data integrity About the UF Division of Epilepsy in the Department of Neurology The University of Florida’s Epilepsy Division is dedicated to improving the lives of individuals affected by epilepsy and seizure disorders, conditions that impact millions of people across the United States. Epilepsy is a complex neurological condition that can range from well-controlled seizures with medication to cases that require advanced diagnostic evaluation and specialized treatments, including surgical and device-based therapies. UF Epilepsy Neurology is committed to delivering patient-centered care by integrating innovative research with comprehensive clinical treatment. Through multidisciplinary collaboration and access to advanced neurodiagnostic technologies, the division works to develop new approaches to seizure management and improve outcomes for patients living with epilepsy. For more information about UF Epilepsy Neurology, visit UF College of Medicine’s Epilepsy Neurology page here . About the City of Gainesville Home to the University of Florida, one of the nation's top public universities, Gainesville offers vibrant academic and healthcare communities alongside an affordable cost of living, no state income tax, and mild winters. Enjoy the outdoors with over 30 miles of bike trails, freshwater springs, and numerous water activities, plus easy access to beaches and world-class theme parks. Gainesville combines historic charm with modern amenities, including a great school system, a lively downtown, and thriving farmers' markets and craft breweries. Discover professional growth and quality of life in Gainesville—where community, innovation, and work-life balance meet! For more information, check out: The Guide to Greater Gainesville and Explore Gainesville in 60 seconds Expected Salary: $53,000 - $60,000 annually Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: The ideal candidate will possess: Three (3) or more years of human-subjects or clinical research experience Proficiency with REDCap, Epic, OnCore, or similar systems Familiarity with Good Clinical Practice and research requirements Experience with clinical, behavioral, neuroimaging, electrophysiology, or biospecimen data Strong organizational and communication skills Ability to manage multiple studies with minimal supervision Clinical research certification, such as CCRC Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, please upload the following documents with your application: Cover Letter Resume Contact information for three professional references This is a time-limited position. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 17 Dec 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541363/lecturerinstructional-assistant-professor": {
      "title": "LECTURER/Instructional Assistant Professor",
      "description": "LECTURER/Instructional Assistant Professor Apply now Job no: 541363 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 15060000 - DCP-URBAN REGIONAL PLANNING Classification Title: Instructional Assistant Professor/Lecturer Classification Minimum Requirements: The candidate must have an advanced degree in urban and regional planning, landscape architecture, urban design, or closely related field. Job Description: School of Landscape Architecture and Planning Department of Urban and Regional Planning The Department of Urban and Regional Planning within the University of Florida’s College of Design, Construction and Planning invites applications for a 12-month, nontenure-accruing faculty position at the rank of Instructional Assistant Professor to begin in January 2027. The Department encourages applications from candidates with background and expertise in the urban and regional planning field. The Department has particular interest in candidates whose teaching contribute to future-oriented, innovative urban and regional transformation and the empowerment of communities and residents. We seek a high-caliber candidate with the planning-based skills to develop interdisciplinary lecture and studio-based courses related to sustainability and resilience delivered on-campus and online. The successful candidate is anticipated to have a well-developed pedagogy that includes methods to engage a multicultural, interdisciplinary student body and a capacity to teach in the professional, accredited MURP degree program. The candidate would be expected to engage in service consistent with the missions of the Department, the School and the land-grant University. Key Responsibilities The successful candidate is anticipated to lead community-engaged, interdisciplinary teaching initiatives for the PAB-accredited Master of Urban and Regional Planning program and the new Bachelor of Urban Sciences and Planning program. These will center around a course that involves students in planning project-based work of a professional nature for paying and/or pro bono clients. Teaching duties will include a total of nine credit hours per semester (Fall, Spring, Summer), which may be graduate and upper-level undergraduate courses delivered in-person and online, in the candidate’s areas of expertise and in general planning topics. Anticipated courses include at least one core general planning course to include at least one urban planning project course. The additional courses may include topics such as Neighborhood Planning, Sustainability and Resilience Planning, Planning Information Technologies, or other courses that fill specific departmental needs. The candidate will establish collaborative relationships within the college and with related discipline programs on campus towards developing interdisciplinary and experiential learning opportunities for undergraduate and graduate planning students. The Department of Urban and Regional Planning The Department of Urban and Regional Planning consists of a collegial faculty of thirteen core members and an additional eighteen members affiliated with research centers and other academic programs. Our faculty is the most productive in the College and consistently attracts the highest amount of research funding, with close to $4 million in new grants secured in the past year. We foster a family friendly environment and promote career development at every level, including mentorship of junior faculty. The Department, founded in 1975 and first accredited in 1978, offers a Master of Urban and Regional Planning (MURP) with in-person and online deliveries, a Master of Science in Urban Analytics (MSUA), a PhD URP concentration with STEM designation, a graduate minor in URP and certificate in UA, an undergraduate minor in URP, and a combined (4+1) bachelor-master degree. The Department’s fully-online MURP is the country’s first and remains the only accredited such program. In 2023, the MURP program (campus and online) was ranked 22nd in the nation, sixth in the South, and the 10th largest by enrollment. The Department recently established a Bachelor of Urban Sciences and Planning (BUSP) and accepted its first cohort in Fall 2026. The School and College As one of the two units in the School of Landscape Architecture and Planning, the Department of Urban and Regional Planning has a strong relationship with the Department of Landscape Architecture, as well as other units in the College, which include Architecture, Construction Management, Interior Design, Historic Preservation, and Sustainability and the Built Environment. The School of Architecture has established two off-campus CityLab programs in Orlando and Jacksonville to enhance access to highly urban contexts. The School and College are home to a range of research institutes and centers, including the Shimberg Center for Housing Studies, the GeoPlan Center, the Center for Health and the Built Environment, the International Center for Adaptation Planning and Design (iAdapt), the Center for Landscape Conservation Planning, and the Florida Institute for Built Environment Resilience (FIBER). The University of Florida The University of Florida is ranked #7 among the nation’s public universities according to the U.S. News & World Report Best Colleges 2025 list. As the flagship university of the state of Florida, and as a major land-grant research university in the Association of American Universities (AAU), the University offers many opportunities for faculty to develop a national and international reputation for research on issues that showcase Florida and the Southeastern United States as a living laboratory, and to shape the next generation of urban and regional planning professionals. Opportunities exist for cross-disciplinary appointments and collaboration, such as with the Warrington College of Business, the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences, the Levin College of Law, UF’s six health-related colleges on campus, the School of Natural Resources and Environment, the Water Institute, and the Florida Climate Institute. In addition, the University’s AI initiative includes access to Nvidia’s HiPerGator supercomputer. The Campus and Gainesville The College is located on the University’s historic main campus, which is characterized by gothic brick buildings and expansive oak trees. The broader campus boasts world-class museums, a performing arts center, a childcare center, and a nationally ranked hospital complex. Situated within the city of Gainesville, the campus is surrounded by historic neighborhoods and a vibrant downtown, and the campus connects to the larger community and beyond by over one hundred miles of bike paths, a regional transit system, and a regional airport. The Gainesville metropolitan area within Alachua County is home to 280,000 residents and serves as the commercial, cultural, educational, and medical center of the north central Florida region. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of specialized programs. The County’s nature parks, waterways, and freshwater springs provide nature-lovers with abundant outdoor activities. The major cities of Orlando, Tampa, and Jacksonville, and the Gulf and Atlantic coasts, are all two hours or less away. Expected Salary: Salary is commensurate with qualifications and experience. Compensation includes a full benefits package with health insurance, retirement options, and paid leave. Required Qualifications: The candidate must have an advanced degree in urban and regional planning, landscape architecture, urban design, or closely related field. We will consider a wide range of backgrounds in filling this position; however, candidates should have a record of excellence in education, research, creative works, or professional practice in urban and regional planning. Preferred: Professional planning experience is desired, with AICP or other relevant credentials preferred. Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, submit a complete application with the following: Letter of interest describing your: Vision for the roles that planning will play in the future, and how you expect your work to contribute to this vision. Teaching philosophy and pedagogies, a list of courses of interest in teaching, a summary of courses taught including evidence of teaching effectiveness, and instructional trainings and certifications received. Academic leadership, professional experience, and community service in relation to future academic goals. Specific interests in joining the Department of Urban and Regional Planning at the University of Florida. Writing sample demonstrating research interests and strengths, and/or a portfolio of relevant research, design/creative, or professional work. Teaching evaluations (if received). Curriculum vitae. List of three references, including affiliations, phone numbers, and email addresses. Interested applicants must apply online at https://jobs.ufl.edu using the requisition number TBD. To ensure full consideration, a complete application should be submitted by October 15, 2026. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the search committee and/or hiring authority. Review of complete applications will begin upon receipt and will continue until the position is filled. Three letters of reference will be required for shortlisted candidates. Submittal instructions will be provided upon notification. Inquiries can be sent to Search Committee Chairs, Dr. Emre Tepe, emretepe@ufl.edu , and Prof. Kyle Dost, kyledost@ufl.edu . If accommodations due to a disability are needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. All candidates selected for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. Employment is contingent upon eligibility to work in the United States. The selected candidate will be required to provide official transcripts to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541371/assistant-professor": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Assistant Professor Apply now Job no: 541371 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Alachua Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16320000 - LS-MATHEMATICS Classification Title: Assistant Professor – Mathematics Department Classification Minimum Requirements Ph.D. degree in Mathematics or a closely related field is required. Job Description: The Department of Mathematics in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences at the University of Florida invites applications for tenure-track Assistant Professor position, beginning August 16, 2027. This is a nine-month, full-time (1.0 FTE), position. The search is open to all areas of mathematics, with candidates in partial differential equations (PDE) and algebra especially encouraged to apply. Candidates should demonstrate a strong and active research program, a compelling vision for future scholarship, the ability to mentor graduate and undergraduate researchers, and excellence in teaching. The successful candidate will join a vibrant department of approximately 60 faculty and 85 graduate students conducting research across a broad spectrum of pure and applied mathematics. For more information, visit Department of Mathematics . The University of Florida offers exceptional support for research and scholarships through significant investments in faculty, facilities, and technology. Resources include HiPerGator, one of the leading university supercomputers in the United States, as well as modern classroom and laboratory facilities that enhance both teaching and research endeavors. More information is available at HiPerGator . The University of Florida is consistently ranked among the nation’s leading public universities and offers an exceptional academic environment. Gainesville provides a high quality of life, with a rich array of cultural opportunities and year-round outdoor recreation. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. degree in Mathematics or a closely related field is required. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applicants must submit their application materials via MathJobs and separately through the University of Florida online application system. Click the link above to apply. A complete application must include: Cover letter Curriculum vitae, including a complete list of publications Research statement describing the applicant’s qualifications, prior research accomplishments, and future research plans Teaching statement outlining teaching experience, interests, and philosophy Contact information for at least three references, including names and email addresses. References will be contacted directly and asked to upload confidential letters of recommendation through the University of Florida application system. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Review of applications will begin on November 15, 2026, and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. Questions may be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Professor Lei Zhang ( leizhang@ufl.edu ). All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541375/compliance-analyst-ii": {
      "title": "Compliance Analyst II",
      "description": "Compliance Analyst II Apply now Job no: 541375 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Legal/Audit Services/Compliance Department: 11150000 - RE-RESEARCH INTEGRITY Classification Title: Compliance Analyst II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of experience in appropriate area; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: Perform investigator duties for the Research Misconduct (RM) program, working independently. Including the following: identify documents for review, catalog evidence, and maintain chain of custody. Conduct review and analysis of pertinent materials, in accordance with UF and federal regulations. Conduct analysis of source data, other evidence and interview transcripts. Draft assessments and reports for RIO approval and maintain compliant templates. Maintain internal logs and records related to the RM program. Perform data entry, assist with scheduling management plan meetings, and maintain files, including record retention and destruction requirements. Maintain timelines for cases, including federal deadlines and communications. Manage faculty committees, including appointments, reviewing for conflicts of interest, facilitating meetings, scheduling witness interviews, recording interviews, reviewing and correcting transcriptions and providing committees catalogued and organized evidence. Maintain an understanding of research regulations, ethics, and guidelines to ensure requirements are met for each case depending on its disposition under the relevant UF regulation and sponsor-specific requirement. Maintain a familiarity with UF Research contracting, award negotiation and financial management, including an understanding of 2 CFR 200. Expected Salary: Salary Commensurate with Education and Experience Required Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of experience in appropriate area; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Experience understanding complex regulations and applying them in an academic setting. Experience working with university faculty and staff. Research administration or compliance experience in an academic or federal regulatory setting. Experience writing reports and creating original analysis based on multiple sources. Special Instructions to Applicants: • In order to be considered, applicants must provide a cover letter, resume, and list of professional references. • Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. • This is not a remote position and is located on the University of Florida main campus in Gainesville, FL. • A criminal background check is required. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541380/biological-scientist-ii": {
      "title": "Biological Scientist II",
      "description": "Biological Scientist II Apply now Job no: 541380 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science Department: 29020100 - MD-NEUROSCIENCE-GENERAL Classification Title: Biological Scientist II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in biology or a related field and three years of experience or Master's degree in biology or a related field with one year of relevant experience. Job Description: The Department of Neuroscience and the McKnight Brain Institute at the University of Florida is seeking applications for a Biological Scientist II in the laboratory of Dr. Ziyuan Guo. The Guo Laboratory focuses on human stem cell biology, 3D brain and barrier organoid/assembloid modeling, microfluidic platforms, and molecular/epitranscriptomic mechanisms of neurodegenerative and neurodevelopmental diseases. The incumbent will work closely with the PI and laboratory personnel to conduct research, assist with experimental design and multi-omic data analytics, oversee day-to-day laboratory operations, and mentor student research assistants. Duties include: Conducting Laboratory Experiments: Perform mammalian cell culture, maintenance, and directed differentiations of human induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) into neural and vascular lineages. Generate and maintain 3D brain organoids, blood-brain barrier (BBB) / blood-spinal cord barrier (BSCB) assembloids, and organ-on-a-chip platforms. Execute molecular protocols including qPCR, DNA/RNA extractions, molecular cloning, CRISPR/Cas9 manipulations, Western blotting, tissue sectioning (cryostat/vibratome), immunofluorescence staining, and confocal microscopy. Experimental Design & Data Analytics: Collaborate with the PI to design, optimize, and execute experimental protocols. Perform quantitative data analysis, image quantification, and statistical analysis. Organize, curate, and maintain large data repositories (including single-cell transcriptomic, genomic, and imaging datasets) required for manuscript preparation. Laboratory Management & Maintenance: Manage day-to-day laboratory ordering of supplies and reagents, record keeping, and inventory tracking via UF purchasing portals. Maintain stem cell culture suites, biosafety cabinets, incubators, and common reagent stocks. Maintain active safety logs, equipment certifications, and compliance documentation under EH&S, IBC, and IRB/SCRO guidelines. Scientific Reporting & Trainee Mentoring: Assist with literature searches, figure preparation, and scientific progress summaries for grant applications and manuscripts. Assist with onboarding new lab personnel and teaching fundamental stem cell and molecular biology skills to undergraduate research assistants. Expected Salary: $53,000-$56,000 Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in biology or a related field and three years of experience or Master's degree in biology or a related field with one year of relevant experience. Preferred: Direct hands-on experience with mammalian cell culture (hiPSC/hESC culture experience is highly preferred). Experience with 3D tissue culture, organoids, microfluidic/organ-on-a-chip platforms, or tissue sectioning and immunofluorescence staining. Technical proficiency in molecular biology workflows (qPCR, molecular cloning, Western blot, CRISPR editing). Familiarity with computational data analysis tools in R, Python, or MATLAB. Demonstrated desire to learn new neuroscience and stem cell engineering techniques. Excellent organizational, record-keeping, and interpersonal communication skills. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume and Contact Information for Three (3) References: Names, email addresses, and phone numbers of professional references. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This requisition has been extended. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not apply. This is a time-limited position. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 16 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541388/sponsored-programs-officer-iiiiii": {
      "title": "Sponsored Programs Officer I/II/III",
      "description": "Sponsored Programs Officer I/II/III Apply now Job no: 541388 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Grant or Research Administration Department: 11060000 - RE-SPONSORED PROGRAMS Classification Title: Sponsored Programs Officer I/II/III Classification Minimum Requirements: Level I : Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area; or an equivalent combination of education and experience in research administration. Level II : Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of experience in sponsored program administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Level III : Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and three years of experience in sponsored program administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The position is located within UF's Division of Sponsored Programs responsible for learning and facilitating a wide range of sponsored programs administration activities, primarily proposal submission on behalf of the University of Florida. Coordinate and support faculty and staff in the preparation of proposals being submitted to federal, state, corporate, and other funding agencies. Review proposals for accuracy and compliance with federal, state, university and sponsor regulations. Ensure that university policies, such as the F&A Policy, Cost Sharing Policy, PI Status Policy, Effort Policy and others, are followed. Review and analyze proposed budgets for compliance with guidelines; check budget details for accurate reflection of line items and proper indirect cost rates. Learn and maintain knowledge with university and sponsor electronic research administration systems in order to ensure timely and compliant submission of proposals. Act as a liaison between Principal Investigator (PI), campus administrators, agency officials, as well as federal, state, and local offices to resolve problems or issues that develop during course of proposal review; interacts closely with PI and their associates to provide technical assistance during the development process of proposals; and provides guidance to departmental staff, investigators, and research administrators on agency policy and requirements. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Education and Experience Required Qualifications: Level I : Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area; or an equivalent combination of education and experience in research administration. Level II : Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and two years of experience in sponsored program administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Level III : Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area and three years of experience in sponsored program administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Level I : Facility to learn electronic research administration software preferred. In addition, skills required include: the ability to provide excellent customer service; excellent verbal and written communication skills; ability to work independently and with others ability to search out federal, sponsor and university policy and guidance and apply critical thinking skills to make effective decisions; negotiation skills; ability to maintain positive and effective working relationships; effectiveness in use of electronic business applications with other systems; experience in the Microsoft Office suite; experience in PeopleSoft grants; experience at the University of Florida; and being a strong problem solver, seeking win-win answers. Level II : At least three years of sponsored programs administration experience that demonstrates knowledge of federal policies governing the administration of grants, contracts, and cooperative agreements at educational institutions, including but not limited to the Federal Uniform Guidance, Federal Acquisition Regulations, Code of Federal Regulations, NIH Grants Policy Statement, NSF Grants Policy Manual, knowledge of contracting and regulations applicable to the University of Florida. Ability to provide excellent customer service. Ability to be an effective communicator verbally and in writing. Ability to critically think and use good judgment. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships. Ability to interface with electronic business applications with other systems. Ability to use with personal computers, Windows, Word, Access Database, Excel, UFIRST and myUFL Grants. Level III : At least three years of sponsored research administration experience that demonstrates knowledge of federal policies governing the administration of grants, contracts, and cooperative agreements at educational institutions, including 2 CFR 200, Federal Acquisition Regulations, Code of Federal Regulations, NIH Grants Policy Statement, NSF Grants Policy Manual, and knowledge of State regulations applicable to the University of Florida. Ability to provide excellent customer service. Ability to be an effective communicator verbally and in writing. Ability to critically think and use good judgment. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships. Ability to interface with electronic business applications with other systems. Ability to use enterprise applications and various personal computing tools. Special Instructions to Applicants: This is not a remote work position, and it is located on the University of Florida's Main Campus in Gainesville, Florida. In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume, and a list of three professional references. This position is open until filled. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until a suitable pool of candidates is established. This requisition will be used to fill multiple positions. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 17 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/539209/treatment-plant-operator-iiiiii": {
      "title": "Treatment Plant Operator I/II/III",
      "description": "Treatment Plant Operator I/II/III Apply now Job no: 539209 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 63700700 - FS-UTILITIES-WASTE WATER Classification Title: Treatment Plant Operator I/II/III Classification Minimum Requirements: Treatment Plant Operator I : High school diploma or equivalent and one year of appropriate experience. Appropriate vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Possession of a current class C wastewater operator’s certificate in accordance with Florida Statutes 403.101 and 468.540 552 and Regulation 61E1241, F.A.C. Treatment Plant Operator II : High school diploma or equivalent and three years of appropriate experience. Appropriate vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Possession of a current class B wastewater operator's certificate in accordance with Florida Statutes 403.101 and 468.540 552 and Regulation 61E1241, F.A.C. Treatment Plant Operator III : High school diploma or equivalent and five years of appropriate experience. Appropriate vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Possession of a current class A wastewater operator’s certificate in accordance with Florida Statutes 403.101 and 468.540 552 and Regulation 61E1241, F.A.C. Job Description: Operate the Wastewater Treatment Plant at times independently in a safe and effective manner while maintaining facility discharge compliance. Record all required instrumentation readings, review data and take corrective action based on analysis. Performs required laboratory sample collection and analysis, take corrective operational action based on analysis. Monitor the status of facility systems, change processes from automatic operation to manual operation as necessary. Performs required system checkout procedures and calibration adjust equipment as required. Record keeping - data entry, logbooks, checklists, lab and calibration sheets etc. Perform routine cleaning and painting of all facility systems: buildings, tanks, equipment, flumes etc. Operate and maintain sludge de-watering equipment. Maintain plant operation and security status weekends, holidays and after normal working hours. Periodically drive UF vehicle, provide tours to the public, students and faculty Performs preventive or repair maintenance on assigned equipment necessary for plant operations with and/or without the assistance of and in cooperation with maintenance and instrumentation personnel. Train operations staff when required Proof of a State of Florida Class E operator’s license required. Valid Florida Class \"A\", “B” or \"C\" Wastewater Operators Certification required. THIS POSITION REQUIRES A POST OFFER HEALTH ASSESSMENT AND CRIMINAL BACKGROUND CHECK. NORMAL WORK SCHEDULE: 4 Days/Week – 10 Hours/Day either Sun-Wed or Wed-Sat (Midnight 0000-1000, Day 0600-1600, Evening 1400-0000) Can rotate or work other shifts and/or 12 hours as needed. May work a four-day shift rotation and/or a five-day shift schedule, on call as required and emergencies. Benefits & Perks: Vacation Leave - 22 days per year Sick Leave - 13 days per year Paid Holidays - 11 days per year Personal Leave Days - 4 day during annual closure period 12/26 - 12/31 Paid Family leave - up to 8 weeks Paid Parental Leave - up to 8 weeks Competitive salary, plus weekend and overnight shift differential pay Annual raise eligibility (staff performance dependent) Discounts for many restaurants, retail, theme parks, cell phone carriers, and more with (gatorperks) Employee recognition programs State of Florida Retirement Plans Offering various types of Insurance options: Health, Life, Dental, Vision, Long Term Disability, Short Term Disability, Accident, Cancer, etc. Employee Education Program - Six credit hours per semester Higher Education Opportunities for children - Covers up to 132 credit hours toward undergraduate degree Expected Salary: Treatment Plant Operator I - $25.00 Hourly based on experience Treatment Plant Operator II- $27.25 Hourly based on experience Treatment Plant Operator III- $31.25 Hourly based on experience Required Qualifications: Requirements and position level will be based on skill and experience level applicant selected. Treatment Plant Operator I : High school diploma or equivalent and one year of appropriate experience. Appropriate vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Possession of a current class C wastewater operator’s certificate in accordance with Florida Statutes 403.101 and 468.540 552 and Regulation 61E1241, F.A.C. Treatment Plant Operator II : High school diploma or equivalent and three years of appropriate experience. Appropriate vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Possession of a current class B wastewater operator's certificate in accordance with Florida Statutes 403.101 and 468.540 552 and Regulation 61E1241, F.A.C. Treatment Plant Operator III : High school diploma or equivalent and five years of appropriate experience. Appropriate vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Possession of a current class A wastewater operator’s certificate in accordance with Florida Statutes 403.101 and 468.540 552 and Regulation 61E1241, F.A.C. Preferred: KNOWLEDGE OF: Wastewater process control principles, use of hand tools, computer use and safety rules. SKILL IN: Chemistry, biology, mechanical working, process control and computer programs i.e. MS Suite, data management and SCADA systems etc. ABILITY TO: Operate plant with limited supervision, interpret results of process control tests and adjust plant accordingly, maintain proper log sheet, and communicate with preceding and following shift workers. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Position has been reposted. Previous applicants still under consideration. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 16 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 14 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541315/ops-physician-prn-pulmonary-critical-care-uf-health-st-johns": {
      "title": "OPS Physician - PRN Pulmonary & Critical Care - UF Health St. Johns",
      "description": "OPS Physician - PRN Pulmonary & Critical Care - UF Health St. Johns Apply now Job no: 541315 Work type: Temp Part-Time Location: St. Johns Categories: Allied Health, Health Care Administration/Support, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30010100 - JX-DEAN-ADMINISTRATION Classification Title: PRN Pulmonary & Critical Care Classification Minimum Requirements: Licensed to practice medicine in accordance with Chapter 458, Florida Statutes plus four years of appropriate experience. Completion of a residency program may substitute for the required experience Federal DEA Certification Job Description: UF Health St. Johns is seeking PRN Critical Care Physicians to join our growing critical care and pulmonology team in Northeast Florida. This opportunity offers the flexibility to maintain your existing practice or professional commitments while providing clinical coverage within the UF Health system. Physicians will have the opportunity to provide coverage across hospital-based critical care settings and, depending on training and departmental needs, outpatient pulmonology. Clinical Responsibilities Provide clinical coverage in one or more of the following settings based on departmental needs, physician qualifications, and availability: UF Health Flagler Hospital – Intensive Care Unit -12-hour clinical shifts UF Health Durbin Park Hospital – Intensive Care Unit - 10-hour clinical shifts UF Health St. Augustine – Pulmonology Outpatient Clinic - 8-hour clinical shifts Shift Requirements This position requires a minimum of one clinical shift per month, with scheduling coordinated based on departmental staffing needs and physician availability. About St. Johns County St. Johns County is one of the fastest-growing and most desirable counties in Florida, consistently ranked for its exceptional quality of life, strong public schools (#1 in the state of Florida), family-friendly communities, and easy access to the Jacksonville metro area. About UF Health Flagler: UF Health St. Johns is a total health care enterprise aimed at advancing the physical, social and economic health of Northeast Florida communities. From serving as the lead agency for St. Johns County’s Continuum of Care with an aim to end homelessness, to bringing a new concept in health villages throughout the region, UF Health St. Johns is your caring partner throughout the journey of life. UF Health St. Johns is an extension of UF Health Flagler Hospital, which has a 130-year legacy of caring for the community. The 335-bed hospital has been named among America’s 50 Best hospitals out of nearly 4,500 nationwide. About UF Health Durbin: UF Health Durbin is a 42.5 acre health and wellness campus that will advance health care services as part of UF’s regional expansion plan for Jacksonville and St. Johns County. Located in northeast St. Johns County, UF Health Durbin Park will include a full -service 99-bed hospital with a focus on acute, intensive, emergency care, orthopaedics, neurosurgery, general surgery, cardiovascular medicine, and women’s services as well as a multidisciplinary medical office building. Future potential site development include the ability to expand hospital beds as well as an ambulatory surgical center, outpatient imagining and additional medial office building. Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications Licensed to practice medicine in accordance with Chapter 458, Florida Statutes plus four years of appropriate experience. Completion of a residency program may substitute for the required experience. Federal DEA Certification Preferred: MD or DO degree Board certification or board eligibility in Critical Care Medicine Pulmonary Medicine training/certification preferred for physicians interested in outpatient pulmonology coverage Active or eligible for an unrestricted Florida medical license Active or eligible for federal DEA registration, as applicable Ability to obtain appropriate UF Health medical staff privileges and credentialing Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, please submit an application at Careers at UF with the following documents attached: Resume/Curriculum Vitae Applications will be reviewed as they are received, and consideration will continue until the positions are filled. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at https://naces.org/members/ The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: Yes (Patient Contact and Bloodborne Pathogens) Advertised: 16 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541373/assistant-director-of-provider-workforce-strategy-and-administration-uf-emergency-medicine": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Provider Workforce Strategy and Administration – UF Emergency Medicine",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Provider Workforce Strategy and Administration – UF Emergency Medicine Apply now Job no: 541373 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Executive/Director/Management, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 29290100 - MD-EMERGENCY MED-AED Classification Title: AST DIR, Health Care Admin Classification Minimum Requirements Master's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience. Job Description: The University of Florida, College of Medicine, Department of Emergency Medicine (DEM), actively seeks a motivated and experienced individual to join us as the Assistant Director of Provider Workforce Strategy and Administration . The Assistant Director of Healthcare and Provider Workforce Administration leads workforce strategy and operations for the Department of Emergency Medicine, ensuring 24/7/365 coverage across all practice locations. Reporting to the Vice Chair of Administrative Affairs, this role manages scheduling approximately 60 daily shifts for physicians, fellows, clinical faculty, and APPs with a focus on optimizing utilization, productivity, documentation compliance, and performance outcomes. The UF Department of Emergency Medicine proudly maintains an affiliation with UF Health Shands Hospital, a distinguished 1,162-bed teaching facility renowned for its Level 1 trauma center and cutting-edge burn center. As the primary referral center for North Central Florida, our institution stands as a beacon of excellence in healthcare. This affiliation underscores our commitment to top-tier medical care and innovation. Key Responsibilities: Key responsibilities include managing the PRN physician group, OPS, and locum tenens operations (onboarding, integration, and utilization); leading workforce analytics and cross-functional projects; overseeing APP recruitment and the scribe program; and enforcing timely note completion to support billing and compliance. The Assistant Director also supports strategic planning, workforce projections, and emergency preparedness, and advises on workforce policy to ensure alignment with institutional and regulatory standards. Provider Workforce Strategy and Leadership Lead strategic workforce planning for physicians, faculty, fellows, and APPs Align workforce utilization with departmental, organizational, and patient care goals Oversee workforce analytics and scheduling performance metrics to guide provider deployment Lead the PRN physician group (~25% of workforce), ensuring effective deployment and integration Drive long-term workforce planning through forecasting and staffing projections Partner with departmental and health system leadership to align staffing with clinical priorities Oversee provider documentation compliance, monitoring timely note completion with clinical leadership Direct cross-functional projects to improve workforce efficiency, documentation compliance, and operational workflows Participate in emergency preparedness and disaster response planning for provider deployment Co-lead the Provider Workforce Clinical Scheduling and Utilization Committee with the Vice Chair of Clinical Affairs Clinical Scheduling and Operations Management Oversee scheduling operations ensuring 24/7/365 coverage across five clinical sites Manage approximately 60 daily shifts across emergency, observation, and inpatient care Oversee real-time staffing adjustments based on patient volume, acuity, and operational needs Review and approve leave requests per guidelines while maintaining site coverage Collaborate with the Administrator on Call and site/clinical leadership to resolve urgent staffing issues Oversee OPS and locum tenens scheduling, onboarding, and utilization tracking Ensure accurate implementation of faculty effort allocations and assignment changes in schedules Coordinate scheduling workflows that reinforce timely, complete provider documentation Program Oversight and Operational Support Lead operational reporting on staffing utilization, coverage trends, and workforce analytics Direct recruitment, onboarding, and education processes for APPs Supervise the departmental scribe program and associated staff Lead special projects within provider workforce administration Manage day-to-day PRN and locum administration, including compensation support and serving as primary liaison with staffing vendors Drive continuous improvement in workforce operations through data analysis and process optimization Monitor note completion trends and address barriers with clinical leadership Schedule: 8:00 am - 5:00 pm Monday through Friday, with additional hours depending upon specific deadlines. The City of Gainesville: Gainesville, home to the University of Florida, is ranked #1 best city to move to in Florida by USA Today. Centrally located in North Florida, it offers a blend of small-town charm and big-city amenities, with close proximity to major airports, family entertainment, and some of the best beaches in the world. Known for its beautiful landscapes and urban forest, Gainesville is a hidden gem that provides year-round outdoor activities, award-winning college sports, and a rich cultural scene. The University of Florida and UF Health are the leading employers in the area, making Gainesville an excellent place to work and raise a family. Learn more about Florida’s premiere health system, UF Health, and the advantages of living in Gainesville at UF Health Destination . Expected Salary: The annual salary ranges between $105,000 - $110,000 and is commensurate with education and experience. At the University of Florida, we are committed to the well-being and professional growth of our employees. Our benefits package is designed to support your health, work-life balance, and financial security: Generous Paid Leave: Enjoy 22 days of paid vacation, 13 days of paid sick leave, 11 paid Holidays, and 8 weeks of paid parental leave. Individual and Family Health Insurance Benefits State of Florida Retirement Plan Options State of Florida Basic Life Insurance Public Service Loan Forgiveness Eligible Employer Professional Development Opportunities Wellness Programs available to employees For more details on our comprehensive benefits, visit UF Benefits . Required Qualifications: Master's degree in an appropriate area and two years of relevant experience; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area and four years of relevant experience. Preferred Master’s in Healthcare Administration (MHA), Business Administration (MBA), Public Health (MPH), or related field Progressive leadership experience in healthcare operations, physician workforce management, or academic medicine Expertise in complex clinical scheduling and provider workforce planning in a multi-site, 24/7 environment Experience managing physician, APP, PRN, and/or locum tenens workforce operations Strong analytical skills with experience in workforce data, productivity metrics, and operational reporting Proven ability to lead staff, manage teams, and oversee operational programs Experience collaborating with physician leadership and senior executives Knowledge of emergency medicine, acute care operations, or similar high-acuity clinical environments Familiarity with workforce management systems, EMRs, and reporting/analytics tools Demonstrated ability to lead process improvement, change initiatives, and operational optimization efforts Special Instructions to Applicants: To be considered, candidates must submit the following documents: A cover letter detailing relevant experience and qualifications. A current resume/CV. For questions, please reach out to EM-Careers@ufl.edu Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 16 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541233/assistant-professor-in-american-politics": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in American Politics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in American Politics Apply now Job no: 541233 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 16380000 - LS-POLITICAL SCIENCE Classification Title: Assistant Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: A Ph.D. in Political Science or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Evidence of high-quality scholarship or strong potential for publication in leading peer-reviewed journals and other appropriate scholarly outlets. The ability to teach effectively at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Job Description: The Department of Political Science at the University of Florida invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in American Politics, with a focus on American institutions , beginning in August 2027. We seek a scholar whose research advances the study of American national political institutions and their development, operation, and consequences. Special preference will go to candidates whose research focuses on the executive and legislative branches. We welcome applications from scholars working across the breadth of American national institutions and especially encourage candidates whose research engages Historical Political Economy (HPE), American Political Economy (APE), and/or American Political Development (APD). This position is part of a broader effort to strengthen the department’s capacity in historical and political economy approaches. In addition to this position in American Politics, the department anticipates making at least one additional tenure-track appointment in Historical Political Economy. The field and subfield of subsequent positions will be open. We therefore encourage scholars working in HPE in Comparative Politics, International Relations, or other areas of Political Science to apply. The successful candidate will maintain an active and innovative research program that makes important theoretical and empirical contributions to the study of American politics. We are open to a wide range of methodological approaches. Candidates whose research would benefit from the University of Florida’s substantial investments in high-performance computing, artificial intelligence, and data-intensive research are especially encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will contribute to the department’s undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentoring missions, including its doctoral methods curriculum. Teaching opportunities include courses on American political institutions, American political development, political economy, historical political economy, and research methods, as well as courses in the candidate’s areas of specialization. The Department of Political Science offers a vibrant environment for research spanning American politics, comparative politics, international relations, political theory, and methodology. The University of Florida provides extensive opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration and research support, including substantial intramural seed-funding programs and access to HiPerGator, the largest university-owned supercomputer in the United States, as well as a growing range of university-wide initiatives in artificial intelligence and computational research. Expected Salary: The starting salary range is competitive, commensurate with qualifications and experience. The position includes a comprehensive University of Florida benefits package. Required Qualifications: A Ph.D. in Political Science or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Evidence of high-quality scholarship or strong potential for publication in leading peer-reviewed journals and other appropriate scholarly outlets. The ability to teach effectively at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Preferred: We are especially interested in candidates whose research examines American institutions through the lenses of Historical Political Economy, American Political Economy, and/or American Political Development. Preference may be given to candidates who: Demonstrate a strong and promising research agenda on American political institutions, their development, operation, and consequences. Show evidence of or potential for excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching and mentoring, including contributions to the department’s graduate curriculum. Employ rigorous empirical methods and have the potential to contribute to the department’s doctoral methods curriculum. Have research interests that complement and extend existing departmental strengths and create opportunities for collaboration across fields and with relevant interdisciplinary initiatives at the University of Florida Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applicants must submit the following materials: A cover letter describing the applicant’s interest in and qualifications for the position, including teaching and research. A current curriculum vitae. One representative writing sample. Three letters of recommendation submitted directly by the referees. Only complete applications will be considered. Applications must be received by October 14, 2026. Review of applications will begin October 15, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. To apply for this position, visit jobs.ufl.edu. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Questions about the position may be directed to Michael Bernhard, Chair of the Search Committee, at bernhard@ufl.edu . All candidates for employment are subject to pre-employment screening, which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered official if it bears a designation such as “Issued to Student.” A qualifying degree earned from an institution outside the United States requires an evaluation by a credential-evaluation service approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES). Additional information is available at www.naces.org . The selection process will be conducted in accordance with Florida’s Government in the Sunshine and Public Records laws. Applications, résumés, letters of recommendation, and other documents related to the search may be available for public inspection. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 15 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541321/assistant-instructional-professor": {
      "title": "Assistant Instructional Professor",
      "description": "Assistant Instructional Professor Apply now Job no: 541321 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16120100 - LS-CHEMISTRY-GENERAL Classification Title: Lecturer Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in Chemistry or a closely related discipline, and demonstrable expertise in teaching at the university level. Job Description: The Department of Chemistry at the University of Florida, one of the nation's leading public research universities, invites applications for a full-time (1.0 FTE), 12-month, non-tenure-track faculty position in Organic Chemistry at the rank of Lecturer (Assistant Instructional Professor). The successful candidate will join a dynamic academic community with an anticipated start date as early as January 1, 2027. The successful candidate will teach Organic Chemistry lecture and laboratory courses, maintain instructional laboratory instrumentation, and contribute to the development and implementation of innovative pedagogical materials and undergraduate curriculum. Responsibilities also include managing electronic course content and online learning resources, supervising and training graduate teaching assistants, fostering a strong culture of laboratory safety and ethical conduct, mentoring undergraduate students, and collaborating with faculty within and beyond the department. The Department encourages the submission of proposals focused on chemistry education and supports faculty engagement in the scholarship of teaching and learning. Teaching faculty are fully integrated members of the department and have opportunities to participate in departmental, university, and professional service activities. Applicants must demonstrate a sustained record of teaching and a strong commitment to undergraduate chemistry education. The successful candidate will have evidence of scholarly and professional achievement, a passion for student success, and a demonstrated ability to develop and implement innovative, evidence-based instructional approaches. The ideal candidate will also exhibit a commitment to pedagogical innovation and a commitment to service, including active involvement in professional organizations and the broader chemistry education community. Expected Salary: $80,000 - $85,000 Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Chemistry or a closely related discipline, and demonstrable expertise in teaching at the university level. Preferred: We seek candidates who have demonstrated creativity in chemistry education and a strong commitment to pursuing a career dedicated to the education and mentorship of exceptional undergraduate students within one of the largest chemistry departments in the country. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Complete applications include the following files in PDF format: A cover letter Curriculum vitae A statement of teaching practices and philosophy Names and contact information for three professional references (the search committee will request confidential letters of recommendation only from candidates selected for its long list) Additional documents should be uploaded as one PDF to the “other documents” selection in the application. This position is open until filled. Review of applications will begin on October 8, 2026, and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541328/assistant-associate-professor-of-inorganic-chemistry": {
      "title": "Assistant / Associate Professor of Inorganic Chemistry",
      "description": "Assistant / Associate Professor of Inorganic Chemistry Apply now Job no: 541328 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16120100 - LS-CHEMISTRY-GENERAL Classification Title: Assistant/Associate Professor Job Description: The University of Florida, Department of Chemistry, invites applications for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor in Inorganic Chemistry, broadly defined. The appointments will commence on August 16, 2027. Successful candidates are expected to conduct innovative research at the highest level and to establish an outstanding, independent, externally funded research program. We seek candidates with a record of excellence in research and scholarship, the ability to excel in teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels and who will contribute to the service mission of the Department of Chemistry and the University of Florida. For more information about the Department of Chemistry, please visit our website, www.chem.ufl.edu. The University of Florida is a land grant, sea grant, and space grant university and home to the most comprehensive university health sciences facilities in the southeast. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs including magnet schools and an international baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville. Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Chemistry or closely related field. Preferred: Postdoctoral experience is desirable. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: Letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position. A complete curriculum vitae. Names and contact information for three professional references. A description of proposed research projects (maximum 5 pages). A statement of teaching philosophy. Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026 and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541331/postdoctoral-associate-in-artificial-intelligence-and-quantum-chemistry": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Associate in Artificial Intelligence and Quantum Chemistry",
      "description": "Post-Doctoral Associate in Artificial Intelligence and Quantum Chemistry Apply now Job no: 541331 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Artificial Intelligence, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16120100 - LS-CHEMISTRY-GENERAL Classification Title: Post-Doctoral Associate in Artificial Intelligence and Quantum Chemistry Classification Minimum Requirements: A Ph.D. in physical/theoretical chemistry or artificial intelligence/machine learning (or related fields). Job Description: A Post-doctoral Associate in Artificial Intelligence and Quantum Chemistry position is available to work in a project exploring the interface of AI for QC led by Dr. Thorpe in the Department of Chemistry. The research will focus on the development and implementation of AI/ML techniques to extend and accelerate quantum chemical simulations of complex molecules and reactions. Must have PhD in Physical/Theoretical Chemistry or Artificial Intelligence/Machine Learning or related fields. This position will be initially awarded for one year, and, contingent upon strong performance and conduct and availability of funds, may be renewed for up to three years. Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. To see more, visit, benefits.hr.ufl.edu. Required Qualifications: Experience in the implementation and application of machine learning to quantum chemistry and a programming background in Python and C++. Preferred: Familiarity with wavefunction-based quantum chemistry. Experience and interest in fundamental studies of chemical reactions, kinetics, thermochemistry, and spectroscopy. Interest in mentoring graduate students. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes (1) a letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position, (2) a complete curriculum vitae , (3) a statement on teaching and research goals, and (4) a list of at least three references. After initial review, letters of recommendation will be requested from the references for selected applicants. Applications will be reviewed beginning 10/1/2026, and the position will remain open until filled. Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 15 Dec 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541334/assistantassociate-full-professor-neuroimmunology-neurologist": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate /Full Professor - Neuroimmunology Neurologist",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate /Full Professor - Neuroimmunology Neurologist Apply now Job no: 541334 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Allied Health, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29181100 - MD-NEURO-MULTIPLE SCLEROSIS Classification Title: AST/ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: MD, DO, or equivalent medical degree Completion of an ACGME-accredited Neurology residency program Completion of fellowship training in Neuroimmunology, Multiple Sclerosis, or a related subspecialty Board certified or board eligible in Neurology Eligibility for unrestricted medical licensure in Florida Demonstrated commitment to patient-centered care, education, and academic excellence Job Description: The Department of Neurology invites applications for a full-time Neuroimmunology Specialist at the rank of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor , commensurate with qualifications and experience. We seek an outstanding clinician-scholar committed to excellence in patient care, education, research, and service. The successful candidate will join a multidisciplinary neuroscience team dedicated to the diagnosis, treatment, and research of neuroimmunologic disorders. Responsibilities include providing comprehensive outpatient and inpatient neuroimmunology services, caring for patients with multiple sclerosis and related autoimmune neurologic diseases, contributing to educational programs, and engaging in scholarly activities that advance the field of neuroimmunology. Essential Responsibilities Clinical Duties Provide expert care for patients with multiple sclerosis (MS), neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder (NMOSD), myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein antibody-associated disease (MOGAD), autoimmune encephalitis, and other neuroimmunologic disorders Deliver comprehensive outpatient and inpatient neuroimmunology consultation services Develop and implement individualized treatment plans utilizing current evidence-based therapies and disease-modifying treatments Collaborate with specialists in neuroradiology, neuro-ophthalmology, rehabilitation medicine, rheumatology, immunology, and related disciplines to provide multidisciplinary care Participate in departmental call schedules and clinical coverage responsibilities Contribute to quality improvement initiatives and the growth and development of the Neuroimmunology and Multiple Sclerosis Program Academic Responsibilities Teach and mentor medical students, residents, fellows, and other healthcare trainees Develop and participate in educational conferences, lectures, and curriculum activities Engage in scholarly activities, including clinical, translational, immunologic, outcomes, or health services research Pursue and maintain an active academic portfolio appropriate to faculty rank Participate in departmental, institutional, and professional service activities Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: MD, DO, or equivalent medical degree Completion of an ACGME-accredited Neurology residency program Completion of fellowship training in Neuroimmunology, Multiple Sclerosis, or a related subspecialty Board certified or board eligible in Neurology Eligibility for unrestricted medical licensure in Florida Demonstrated commitment to patient-centered care, education, and academic excellence Special Instructions to Applicants: Candidates must apply online at https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/ and should include a letter of interest, current curriculum vitae, and three letters of recommendation. Letters of recommendation may be attached to the application or sent directly to Polly Glattli, glattpa@ufl.edu. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/. The Search Committee will accept applications until the position is filled. Applications will be reviewed on an ongoing basis by the committee. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541337/assistantassociate-full-professor-vascular-neurologist": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate /Full Professor - Vascular Neurologist",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate /Full Professor - Vascular Neurologist Apply now Job no: 541337 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Allied Health, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29180700 - MD - VASCULAR NEUROLOGY Classification Title: AST/ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: MD, DO, or equivalent medical degree Completion of an ACGME-accredited Neurology residency program Completion of fellowship training in Vascular Neurology Board certified or board eligible in Neurology Board certified or eligible in Vascular Neurology Eligibility for unrestricted medical licensure in the state of Florida Demonstrated commitment to patient-centered care, education, and academic excellence Job Description: The Department of Neurology invites applications for a full-time Vascular Neurologist at the rank of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor , commensurate with qualifications and experience. We seek an outstanding clinician-scholar committed to excellence in patient care, education, research, and service. The successful candidate will join a multidisciplinary neuroscience team dedicated to the diagnosis, treatment, and prevention of cerebrovascular diseases. Responsibilities include providing comprehensive inpatient and outpatient vascular neurology services, participating in acute stroke management, contributing to educational programs, and engaging in scholarly activities that advance the field of vascular neurology. Essential Responsibilities Clinical Duties Provide expert care for patients with stroke, transient ischemic attack (TIA), cerebrovascular disorders, and related neurological conditions Participate in acute stroke evaluation and management, including telestroke and inpatient consultation services as applicable Collaborate with neurosurgery, neurointerventional radiology, emergency medicine, rehabilitation medicine, and other specialties to deliver comprehensive stroke care Participate in departmental call schedules and clinical coverage responsibilities Contribute to quality improvement initiatives and stroke program development Academic Responsibilities Teach and mentor medical students, residents, fellows, and other healthcare trainees Develop and participate in educational conferences, lectures, and curriculum activities Engage in scholarly activities, including clinical, translational, health services, or outcomes research Pursue and maintain an active academic portfolio appropriate to faculty rank Participate in departmental, institutional, and professional service activities Expected Salary: Salary is negotiable, commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: MD, DO, or equivalent medical degree Completion of an ACGME-accredited Neurology residency program Completion of fellowship training in Vascular Neurology Board certified or board eligible in Neurology Board certified or eligible in Vascular Neurology Eligibility for unrestricted medical licensure in the applicable state Demonstrated commitment to patient-centered care, education, and academic excellence Special Instructions to Applicants: Candidates must apply online at https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/ and should include a letter of interest, current curriculum vitae, and three letters of recommendation. Letters of recommendation may be attached to the application or sent directly to Polly Glattli, glattpa@ufl.edu. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/. The Search Committee will accept applications until the position is filled. Applications will be reviewed on an ongoing basis by the committee. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541340/associate-director-of-development-fredric-g-levin-college-of-law": {
      "title": "Associate Director of Development, Fredric G. Levin College of Law",
      "description": "Associate Director of Development, Fredric G. Levin College of Law Apply now Job no: 541340 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Advancement, Executive/Director/Management, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 24030100 - LW-DEVELOPMENT - ALUMNI AFFRS Classification Title: ASO DIR, Development Classification Minimum Requirements: Master’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and one year relevant experience; or a bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and three years of relevant experience. Job Description: The future of legal education is built by those who invest in the next generation of leaders, advocates, and changemakers. The Fredric G. Levin College of Law is growing its Advancement team and seeking an Associate Director of Development to help strengthen donor relationships and expand philanthropic support. Working closely with alumni, donors, college leadership, and advancement colleagues, this role cultivates meaningful partnerships, advances major gift opportunities, and helps bring the college's strategic priorities to life. This position focuses on securing and stewarding gifts of $100,000 and above while fostering lifelong connections with the law school community. If you enjoy building authentic relationships and inspiring generosity, this role offers the chance to make a lasting impact on the next generation of legal leaders. RESPONSIBILITIES Prospect Cultivation & Portfolio Management Build a portfolio of fundraising prospects at the major gift levels ($100,000+) and strategically manage relationships through face-to-face visits to bring gift solicitations to closure. Build and manage a robust relationship portfolio targeting small and mid-level gift donations that support the strategic goals of the College of Law. Maximize travel plans to strategic geographic regions for major gift solicitation/donor engagement. Research, identify, and cultivate individual alumni and friends as prospective donors to the College of Law. Collaborate with the Office of Estate & Gift Planning team to develop and implement a giving strategy for qualified donors at the planned giving level. Qualify prospects by making contact with them, both virtually and in person, to evaluate their readiness to engage with the law school and their interest in making a gift. Development Strategy Set and implement strategies for identification, qualification, relationship-building, and solicitation to close and steward major gifts. Collaborate with team members to create and implement annual fundraising initiatives that support the strategic goals of the law school including end-of-fiscal-year and end-of-calendar-year campaigns and other special fundraising activities created by the College of Law and the UF Foundation. Collaborate with college partners to understand and formulate priorities and opportunities related to individual gifts at the appropriate level. Create gift proposals, concept papers, and other documents used for soliciting restricted and unrestricted contributions to the College of Law. Develop comprehensive understanding of the UF Law mission, strategy, and goals as they relate to fundraising. Acknowledge and recognize gifts in ways that are meaningful to donors and appropriate to the mission, visions, and values of UF Law and UF Advancement. Stewardship Communications, Events, and Administration Serve as the liaison to the Law Alumni Council Membership Committee, working directly with alumni committee members as a representative of UF Law. Attend and support alumni events and initiatives, partnering with colleagues to involve alumni when appropriate. Plan and execute select regional alumni engagement events. Support College of Law and UF functions to help steward donors and move prospects through the gift continuum toward closure. Events include, but are not limited to, class reunions, alumni receptions, alumni board meetings, local and state bar conferences and bar exams, and other special occasions. Develop and implement communication and engagement plans to keep prospects connected to the law school and informed about the vision, goals, and values of the College of Law, including its funding priorities and current giving opportunities. Ensure best practices in office systems, files, and record keeping. Ensure effective use of strategic tools and resources, such as contact reports, prospect management, research analysis, and UFF database. Contribute to the planning and monitoring of event and travel budget. Steward gifts by completing thank you cards and other gift recognition with donors and prospects. Other Duties Perform related duties as required or deemed appropriate to the accomplishment of the responsibilities and functions related to this position. Expected Salary: $65,000-75,000 annually, commensurate with education and experience. Exceptional Benefits We Offer Affordable State Health Plans: Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance Comprehensive Coverage: Life and Disability Insurance Secure Retirement Options: Generous plans to safeguard your future Paid Time Off: Including 11 paid holidays, as well as family, sick, and vacation leave Professional and Personal Growth: Opportunities through UF Training & Organization Development, leadership development, LinkedIn learning, and more Tuition Assistance: Benefit from the UF Employee Education Program PSLF Eligibility: We are a Public Service Loan Forgiveness Eligible Employer The University of Florida offers a competitive benefits package. Click here to learn more. Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and one year relevant experience; or a bachelor’s degree in an appropriate area of specialization and three years of relevant experience. A valid Florida driver’s license is required. Preferred: The successful candidate will possess: One to three years of fundraising, development, sales, or related experience. Prior experience in a university or nonprofit environment. General knowledge and understanding of business/economic environment. Knowledge of fundraising principles, methods, and standards. Demonstrated successful experience in prospect identification, relationship building, solicitation, and stewardship. The ability to work strategically, develop and implement constituency programs and activities, along with a talent for motivating volunteers. Ability to work collaboratively and be a team player. Competency in problem-solving. Exceptional written and verbal communication skills, strong interpersonal abilities, and the capacity to engage effectively and collaboratively with a wide range of individuals are essential. Support the University’s commitment to creating a respectful and collaborative work environment that values integrity, professionalism, and mutual respect in all interactions; demonstrated success collaborating with stakeholders; strong project management skills; experience in a data-driven environment; excellent organizational abilities; and the capacity to lead, influence, and inspire others are essential. Proficient in the following areas: Windows operating systems, Microsoft Word, Excel, and Access, as well as internet browsers and email platforms. Familiarity with donor databases, contact management systems, and tracking systems is also highly preferred. The ability and willingness to travel. A valid driver's license and good driving record are essential.* *Business expenses are reimbursed through a standard accounting system. The ability to pay for expenses using your own personal credit card or funds and have them reimbursed is essential. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume and three references. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Work sponsorship is not available for this position. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541341/assistant-professor": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR",
      "description": "ASSISTANT PROFESSOR Apply now Job no: 541341 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Social/Behavioral Science Department: 16400100 - LS-PSYCHOLOGY-GENERAL Classification Title: Assistant Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants will have a doctorate degree in Psychology, Neuroscience, or a closely related field by the start date of the position, August 16, 2027. Job Description: The Department of PSYCHOLOGY at the UNIVERSITY OF FLORIDA, College of Liberal Arts and Sciences, invites applications for a full-time, nine-month, tenure-accruing Assistant Professor in the field of Developmental Science. The specific area of research is open but should focus on developmental research in both brain and behavior that complements and expands existing strengths in the department and the university at large, including the development of cognition, perception, attention, memory, language, decision making, emotion, and/or social functioning. Priority will be given to candidates with research expertise in children from early childhood through middle childhood. Research and training in our developmental psychology program encompasses the lifespan; hence, we will also consider candidates whose expertise spans multiple life stages, including childhood. Applicants should have an outstanding record of research achievement and a commitment to undergraduate and graduate instruction in developmental psychology and neuroscience. The successful candidate will be expected to (1) maintain a program of research with high potential for external funding, (2) teach graduate and undergraduate courses in developmental psychology, (3) mentor students, and (4) provide service to the institution and academy. The Psychology Department offers doctoral programs in behavioral and cognitive neuroscience, behavior analysis, counseling psychology, developmental psychology, and social psychology. We currently have 44 full-time faculty, ~ 100 PhD students, and > 2,000 undergraduate majors. About the University of Florida A member of the prestigious Association of American Universities (AAU), the University of Florida is the state’s oldest and most comprehensive land-, sea-, and space-grant university, with an enrollment of over 50,000 students. For 2026, the University of Florida was ranked the seventh-best public university in the United States by U.S. News & World Report, with exceptional research, teaching, and a university-wide commitment to open intellectual discourse. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf of Mexico, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs, including magnet schools and an International Baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville . The Department of Psychology is committed to promoting an environment that welcomes all abilities, classes, ethnicities/races, religions, gender identities and expressions, etc. We particularly welcome applicants who can contribute to such an environment through their scholarship, teaching, mentoring, and professional service. Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. Required Qualifications: Applicants will have a doctorate degree in Psychology, Neuroscience, or a closely related field by the start date of the position, August 16, 2027. Preferred: Candidates with post-doctoral experience will be prioritized. Candidates with evidence of methodological expertise in human neuroscience or modeling/AI will be prioritized. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online at [insert link]. A complete application includes: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Research Statement Statement of teaching experience, interests, and philosophy Three letters of recommendation. Please provide the names and email addresses of three references and the application system will automatically request letters at the time of application submission. Review of applications will begin on October 15th, 2026 , and will continue until the position is filled. Questions about the position may be directed to search committee co-chairs Lisa Scott ( lscott@ufl.edu ) and Natalie Ebner ( natalie.ebner@ufl.edu ). Only complete applications will be reviewed at this time. Applications received after this date may be considered at the discretion of the committee and/or hiring authority. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening, which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No. Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541342/assistant-professor-in-history-of-modern-philosophy": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in History of Modern Philosophy",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in History of Modern Philosophy Apply now Job no: 541342 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 16340000 - LS-PHILOSOPHY Classification Title: Assistant Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants will have a Ph.D. in Philosophy or complete the Ph.D. by date of hire, will have an active research agenda in Early Modern Philosophy, and will provide evidence of effective teaching. Job Description: The Department of Philosophy at the University of Florida, College of Liberal Arts and Sciences, invites applications for a nine-month, full-time, tenure-accruing position at the rank of Assistant Professor beginning August 16, 2027, in the History of Modern Philosophy. The successful candidate will pursue an active research agenda in Early Modern Philosophy and provide service to the department, the university, and the profession. Although candidates must have Early Modern Philosophy as their core area of research, additional expertise in either Medieval Philosophy or 19 th -Century Philosophy would be useful as the department has teaching needs in all these areas. Tenure-line faculty are typically assigned four courses per academic year at the undergraduate and graduate levels. In addition to regularly teaching required courses for the undergraduate Philosophy major and minor, the ability to contribute significantly to graduate education and the mentoring of graduate students will also be expected. The department has recently grown with multiple hires in the last several years and currently has a significant number of faculty working primarily in the history of philosophy. It is committed to ensuring a robust coverage of all the core areas of philosophy, though it has developed special strength in the ethics of technology and the philosophy of artificial intelligence. The department also has a good working relationship with the newly established Hamilton School for Classical and Civic Education, which has hired several faculty members with interests in the history of philosophy during the past few years. To learn more about the Department of Philosophy, applicants are encouraged to visit the departmental website ( Department of Philosophy ). Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Required Qualifications: Applicants will have a Ph.D. in Philosophy or complete the Ph.D. by date of hire, will have an active research agenda in Early Modern Philosophy, and will provide evidence of effective teaching. Preferred: Additional expertise in either Medieval Philosophy or 19 th -Century Philosophy would be useful as the department has teaching needs in all these areas. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, applications must be submitted online. Click on Apply Now at the top of this posting. A complete application includes: Letter of application summarizing the applicant's qualifications, interests, and suitability for the position A complete curriculum vitae A statement regarding the applicant’s research agenda A statement and supporting materials regarding teaching effectiveness A sample of written work of moderate length Names and contact information for three professional references Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026, and will continue until a successful applicant is selected. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541347/assistantassociate-professor-of-maternal-and-child-nutrition": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Maternal and Child Nutrition",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Maternal and Child Nutrition Apply now Job no: 541347 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60150000 - AG-FOOD SCIENCE - HUMAN NUTR Classification Title: Ast/Aso Prof Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates for this rank shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Job Description: The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences (IFAS) is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF. IFAS and the Department of Food Science and Human Nutrition invites applications from qualified individuals for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant or Associate Professor level. The successful candidate will develop a collaborative, externally funded, and internationally engaged research program focused on maternal and child nutrition, with particular emphasis on improving health and developmental outcomes for women, infants, and young children in global and underserved settings. This is a 9-month, tenure-accruing position that will be 70% research ( Florida Agricultural Experiment Station ) and 30% teaching ( College of Agricultural and Life Sciences ) available in the Department of Food Science and Human Nutrition, Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences, at the University of Florida, based in Gainesville, FL, expected to start in Spring 2027. The position is supported by a competitive salary with benefits including health, life, dental and vision insurance; flexible spending accounts; retirement options; paid holidays and wellness benefits. This assignment may be revised in accordance with the unit's needs. Tenure will accrue in the Department of Food Science and Human Nutrition. The faculty member will seek contract and grant funding actively to support their program. Expected duties We seek candidates who conduct research and integrative studies in global nutrition and health, public health nutrition, food and nutrition security, and maternal and child health. Relevant areas may include the role of nutrient-dense foods and dietary patterns—including animal-source foods, fruits, vegetables, legumes, nuts, fortified foods, and complementary foods—in supporting maternal health, infant growth, early childhood development, and long-term health outcomes. Successful candidates will use interdisciplinary, systems-based, and population-level approaches and may integrate primary data collection, epidemiology, intervention research, implementation science, dietary assessment, nutritional biomarkers, data science, and predictive modeling. The position will include responsibilities in research and teaching, with opportunities to mentor undergraduate and graduate students and contribute to courses in nutrition, public health, global health, and food systems. The successful candidate will engage in scholarly activities related to instruction. Examples of these scholarly activities include teaching undergraduate and/or graduate courses, advising and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students, participating in curriculum revision and enhancement, seeking funding for the teaching program, supervising undergraduate and graduate research and creative work, publishing teaching-related scholarship, producing learning tools, and engaging in professional development activities related to teaching and advising. Faculty are encouraged to support and participate in the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences Honors Program, distance and international education. Because of the IFAS land-grant mission, all faculty are expected to be supportive of and engaged in all three mission areas—Research, Teaching and Extension—regardless of the assignment split specified in the position description. Background information This position is one of two new faculty positions which will seed a nexus in global health nutrition and food security between Global Food Systems Institute (GFSI) within IFAS and the One Health Center of Excellence (OHCE) within the College of Public Health and Health Professions ’ (PHHP) Department of Environmental and Global Health (EGH). These synergistic positions, a food safety epidemiologist in EGH and a public health nutritionist in Food Science and Human Nutrition, will complement the existing expertise in interdisciplinary research on global food security and nutrition across the lifespan. The position will be housed in the Department of Food Science and Human Nutrition, strengthening existing expertise in maternal and child nutrition, community and public health nutrition, nutritional sciences, food systems, food safety, food processing, and global nutrition. The successful candidate will have opportunities to collaborate with faculty across the department and throughout UF/IFAS, PHHP, the Emerging Pathogens Institute , and other university programs engaged in global health, One Health, agriculture, nutrition, and international development. The Department of Food Science and Human Nutrition is a unit in CALS. The department has approximately 27 full-time faculty members including a significant group of federally funded researchers with a rich history of national research awards. The graduate program enrolls over 50 M.S. and Ph.D. students in Nutritional Sciences, Dietetics, and Food Science. The doctoral program in Nutritional Sciences is a part of the Center for Nutritional Sciences , which is interdisciplinary and includes a number of faculty from different departments across campus, along with a vibrant seminar series. The Department enrolls more than 450 undergraduate students across its three majors: Nutritional Sciences, Food Science, and Dietetics. Nutritional Sciences faculty conduct research in multiple areas that span micronutrients, molecular nutrition, human metabolism, physiology, clinical, and community nutrition. Our faculty collaborate with colleagues from the Colleges of Medicine, Health and Human Performance, Public Health, Pharmacy, Liberal Arts and Sciences, Engineering, and Agricultural and Life Sciences. Within walking distance are superb opportunities to participate in basic and translational research with the UF Diabetes Institute, the Clinical and Translational Science Institute. The University of Florida is a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution, encompassing virtually all academic and professional disciplines, with an enrollment of more than 56,000 students. UF is a member of The Association of American Universities. The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences includes the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences , the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station , the Florida Cooperative Extension Service , the College of Veterinary Medicine , the Florida Sea Grant program, and encompasses 16 on-campus academic departments and schools, 12 Research and Educational Centers (REC) located throughout the state, 6 Research sites/demonstration units administered by RECs or academic departments, and Florida Cooperative Extension Service offices in all 67 counties (counties operate and maintain). The School of Natural Resources and Environment is an interdisciplinary unit housed in IFAS and managed by several colleges on campus. UF/IFAS employs nearly 4,500 people, which includes approximately 990 salaried faculty and 1,400 permanent support personnel located in Gainesville and throughout the state. IFAS, one of the nation’s largest agricultural and natural resources research and education organizations, is administered by a Vice President and four deans: the Dean of the College of Agricultural and Life Sciences, the Dean for Extension and Director of the Florida Cooperative Extension Service, the Dean for Research and Director of the Florida Agricultural Experiment Station, and the Dean for the College of Veterinary Medicine. UF/IFAS also engages in cooperative work with Florida A&M University in Tallahassee. Expected Salary: Commensurate with Experience and Education Required Qualifications: Applicants must hold a doctoral degree, or a foreign equivalent, in nutrition, public health nutrition, nutritional epidemiology, community nutrition, global health, epidemiology, public health, or a closely related field by the position’s start date. Candidates must demonstrate the ability to develop a research program involving human populations and to work effectively in multidisciplinary and cross-cultural collaborations. Relevant research may include maternal and child nutrition, community or population nutrition, global nutrition, food and nutrition security, dietary interventions, or the evaluation of nutrition-related health outcomes. Candidates should demonstrate a commitment to excellence in scholarship, teaching, and student mentoring. Applicants at the Assistant Professor level should show strong potential to secure external research funding and develop an independent research program. Applicants at the Associate Professor level should demonstrate an established record of scholarly productivity, external funding, student mentorship, and teaching appropriate to rank. Candidates must be supportive of the mission of the Land-Grant system. Candidates must also have a commitment to UF core values. Preferred: Preferred candidates will have experience conducting nutrition or public health research in low- and middle-income countries or other resource-constrained settings. Additional preferred qualifications may include experience in one or more of the following areas: Designing and implementing human nutrition interventions, community-based trials, randomized controlled trials, or longitudinal studies involving women, infants, children, families, or communities; Conducting community-engaged, implementation, translational, or population-based nutrition research; Working with international universities, governments, nongovernmental organizations, community organizations, or other local partners; Using dietary assessment methods, nutritional biomarkers, anthropometric measurements, developmental or health outcomes, or food-security indicators; Applying quantitative, qualitative, or mixed-methods approaches, including nutritional epidemiology, meta-analysis, population-level modeling, data integration, or implementation evaluation; Managing research activities across international sites, including ethical review, participant recruitment, research staff training, data quality assurance, and culturally appropriate community engagement; Developing externally funded, interdisciplinary research programs addressing maternal and child nutrition, global health, or food and nutrition security. Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, candidates should apply and submit materials by October 15 th , 2026. The position will remain open until a viable applicant pool is determined. This position is available January 1, 2027, and will be filled as soon thereafter as an acceptable applicant is available. Compensation is commensurate with the education, experience, and qualifications of the selected applicant. Nominations are welcome. Nominations need to include the complete name and address of the nominee. This information should be sent to: Please refer to Requisition # 541347 Dr. Juan E. Andrade Laborde Chair, Search and Screen Committee University of Florida, IFAS Department of Food Science and Human Nutrition 572 Newell Dr. Gainesville, FL 32611-0620 Email: jandrade2@ufl.edu Applicants should go to Careers at UF and submit: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position, background, and qualifications relative to duties, responsibilities and qualifications listed above (2-page limit) Statement of teaching and mentoring philosophy and interests relative to the duties and responsibilities above (2-page limit) Statement of research activities and interests relative to the duties and responsibilities above (2-page limit) Current curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for three individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Unofficial transcripts Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 15 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541316/assistant-professor-astrophysics": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Astrophysics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor Astrophysics Apply now Job no: 541316 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 16360100 - LS-PHYSICS-GENERAL Classification Title: Assistant Professor in Physics Job Description: The Department of Physics in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences at the University of Florida has an opening for a full-time (1.0 FTE), nine-month, tenure-accuring Assistant Professor in experimental particle astrophysics to begin August 16, 2027. We seek highly qualified candidates from areas related to current and novel experimental dark matter direct detection searches. This position offers the opportunity to interact with and build upon the Department’s extensive research efforts in related and complementary directions, including dark matter detection (ADMX, ALPS, SuperCDMS), experimental particle physics (Belle II, COHERENT, CMS, DUNE, SBND), gravitational wave detection (LIGO, LISA, NANOGrav), theoretical astrophysics, and condensed matter physics. It also provides access to the world-class HiPerGator research computing center. Expected Salary: The salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience, and the compensation includes a full benefits package. UF offers a strong benefits package for eligible faculty appointments, including health insurance, paid leave (as applicable), and retirement options. Learn more here . Minimum Requirements: The successful candidate must hold a Ph.D. degree. Potential for establishing a well-recognized research program, excellence in teaching, and mentoring undergraduate and graduate student body is essential. Preferred: Relevant postdoctoral research experience is preferred About University of Florida UF is the state’s oldest, largest, and most comprehensive land grant university with an enrollment of over 50,000 students and was ranked 7th in the country among public universities (US News and World Report 2025 rankings), and 1st among public institutions in the Wall Street Journal 2023 survey. UF is located in Gainesville, a city of approximately 150,000 residents in North-Central Florida, 50 miles from the Gulf of Mexico, and 67 miles from the Atlantic Ocean, and within a 2-hour drive to large metropolitan areas (Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville). The beautiful climate and extensive nearby parks and recreational areas afford year-round outdoor activities, including hiking, biking, and nature photography. UF’s large college sports programs, museums, and performing arts center support a range of activities and cultural events for residents to enjoy. Alachua County schools are highly rated and offer a variety of programs including magnet schools and an international baccalaureate program. Learn more about what Gainesville has to offer at Visit Gainesville . Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications must be submitted online at jobs.ufl.edu and should include: (1) a cover letter, (2) a curriculum vitae with publication list, (3) a research statement, (4) a teaching statement (in which you are welcome to discuss outreach activities and other contributions you’ve undertaken to enhance student experience), (5) a statement of broader impacts (as described by the National Science Foundation ) of past or planned future activities, (6) at least three confidential letters of reference. Reference providers will only be notified and provided an upload link once the application is submitted in full, so early completion of an application is highly recommended. To ensure full consideration, applications and letters of reference must be submitted by November 8, 2026. For more information, please contact Prof. Tarek Saab ( tsaab@ufl.edu ). Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States require evaluation by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 14 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 08 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541320/postdoctoral-fellow-hamilton-school": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Hamilton School",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Fellow - Hamilton School Apply now Job no: 541320 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Humanities Department: 57301000 - HS-HAMILTON SCHOOL ADMIN Classification Title: Postdoctoral Fellow Classification Minimum Requirements: Applicants must have completed all requirements for the Ph.D. or equivalent terminal degree by June 2027. Candidates who received the doctorate after June 2023 are eligible to apply. Job Description: Position Description The Hamilton School for Classical and Civic Education at the University of Florida invites applications for two Postdoctoral Fellowships in English literature, intellectual history, political theory, theology and religion, philosophy, political science, and strategic studies. Fellows will pursue ambitious research and participate in an interdisciplinary community devoted to serious inquiry into enduring questions of human nature, political order, religion, literature, philosophy, history, war, statecraft, and the ideas and institutions that have shaped Western civilization and the American experience. The Fellowship is a two-year, residential appointment beginning August 2027. It is designed to provide an optimal context for pursuing research toward publications. Consequently, while Fellows have the option of teaching a course in their areas of expertise, teaching does not constitute a requirement for the Fellowship. Applications from scholars whose work transcends traditional disciplinary boundaries are particularly welcome. Fellows can expect structured mentoring and job-market advising. Fellows are expected to participate in regular Fellows and Faculty Seminars as well as visiting speakers sponsored by the Hamilton School. About the Hamilton School The Hamilton School is a multi-disciplinary academic unit at the University of Florida, devoted to excellence teaching and research in the humanities and social sciences. Its work engages the ideas and institutions, traditions and texts that have shaped Western civilization and the American experience, while fostering open inquiry, civil discourse, and robust engagement across disciplinary boundaries. Fellows will join a rapidly growing community of scholars working in history, literature, philosophy, political thought, religion, political science, international affairs, and war, statecraft, and strategy. Expected Salary: Salaries begin at approximately $80,000, and include benefits, and $5,000 for research support. Fellows are eligible for UnitedHealthcare Student Health Insurance Plan. Required Qualifications: Applicants must have completed all requirements for the Ph.D. or equivalent terminal degree by June 2027. Candidates who received the doctorate after June 2023 are eligible to apply. Preferred: Special Instructions to Applicants: A complete application must include: A cover letter A curriculum vitae A research statement of approximately 3-5 single-spaced pages describing the project to be pursued during the fellowship A writing sample of no more than 40 single-spaced pages Three names and contact information for referees who can be reached for reference letters Successful applicants will undergo two-stages of zoom interviews. The appointment will begin in August 2027. Appointments will be made on the basis of exceptional scholarly achievement and promise; the significance, originality, and feasibility of the proposed research; evidence of excellent research; intellectual range; and the potential to contribute to a demanding and collegial interdisciplinary community. This position is open until filled. Review of Applications will begin immediately and continue until the position has been filled. Questions may be directed to: Paul C.H. Lim Professor of Humanities Associate Director for Faculty Affairs Hamilton School for Classical and Civic Education University of Florida paul.lim@ufl.edu Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 14 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/539497/clinical-programs-coordinator-i-peds-id": {
      "title": "Clinical Programs Coordinator I- Peds ID",
      "description": "Clinical Programs Coordinator I- Peds ID Apply now Job no: 539497 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Allied Health, Health Care Administration/Support Department: 30090000 - JX-PEDIATRICS-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: Clinical Programs Coordinator I Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelor's degree in in an appropriate; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The Clinical Programs Coordinator I under the supervision of the Quality Officer will provide support to the UF CARES CQI Plan. This position will coordinate requirements and logistics for Patient Centered Model Home-PCMH annual recognition. Coordinate the patient services and administrative activities of clinical data and monitor ongoing clinical activities for NCQA regulations. Develops and coordinate patient services to review NCQA standards Assists and support the Quality Officer to review and monitor COI strategic plan Assists providers by monitoring patient care plan, and treatment problems Works collaboratively with clinical team members of the patient’s treatment team. Maintains all records and reports related to NCQA/PCMH. and keeps track of data in an Excel spreadsheet. Coordinates PCMH committee meetings, serves on health-related task forces and advisory committees, including the Clinical Effectiveness Group (CEG). Attending NCQA workshops and webinars 5% Provides administrative services for assistance with any additional needs. Provides health education to patients, staff, and practitioners within area of specialty as it relates to NCQA compliance Expected Salary: $43,888 to $55,000 annually depending on education and experience Required qualifications Bachelor's degree in in an appropriate; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: 1-year experience with HIV population Adult/ Pediatric outpatient clinic, National Continuing Quality Assurance (NCQA) PCMH and Quality Improvement Projects Special Instructions to Applicants: This is a time-limited position. Time limited positions are based on circumstances, such as funding sources, which control the length of time for which the position is available. Level II Background required. To be considered you must upload your Cover Letter, Resume/CV, and 3 References. University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville: Visit this link to watch the video. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 11 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 26 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541305/director-tropical-conservation-and-development-program": {
      "title": "Director, Tropical Conservation and Development Program",
      "description": "Director, Tropical Conservation and Development Program Apply now Job no: 541305 Work type: Faculty Admin Title Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Executive/Director/Management Department: 57140100 - LT-CTR-LT AMER STUDIES-ADMIN Classification Title: PRG DIR & ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: An earned doctorate or equivalent terminal degree in a relevant field Job Description: The Center for Latin American Studies of the University of Florida (UF) welcomes applications for the position of Director for its Tropical Conservation and Development (TCD) Program . We seek a dynamic and innovative leader in the field of conservation and development to build on the strengths of this internationally recognized program of training, research, and practice. The mission of the TCD Program is to bridge theory and practice to advance biodiversity conservation, sustainable resource use, and human well-being. The program: offers a cross-campus graduate certificate which complements the knowledge and skills that students develop in their home units, adding significant value to their graduate education; promotes cross-national, interdisciplinary, integrative, and comparative problem-centered research at UF; and embraces collaborative learning and action with partners involved in day-to-day conservation and development realities around the world. The TCD Director will: lead and advocate for this cutting-edge, internationally recognized interdisciplinary research and training program with over 120 faculty affiliates and over 80 enrolled graduate students; direct budget allocations of investment returns on ~$6 million in endowments (most of which directly supports students); secure extramural funding to support research and training activities; oversee the innovative TCD graduate certificate program, maintaining academic rigor and relevance to conservation and development practice; support the vibrant TCD community of students, faculty, alumni, and partners through multiple and varied events; fortify existing and build new relationships with units and constituencies across the UF campus; strengthen and expand collaborations with organizations and partners across the globe, particularly with the more than 550 TCD alumni; spearhead shared program governance and a long-range TCD vision for the future; and commensurate with candidate background and interest, maintain a teaching/mentoring and/or research portfolio. The incoming TCD Director will have an appointment and be housed in the UF Center for Latin American Studies. We anticipate an appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor. The Director may also be appointed jointly with another campus unit, depending on candidate profile, preferences, and university considerations. About the Center: The UF Center for Latin American Studies (LAS) is the oldest and one of the largest programs of research and education about the region in the United States, established as the Institute of Interamerican Affairs in the 1930s and renamed the Center for Latin American Studies in 1963. Today, the Center is one of the top-ranked centers in the world. In addition to the TCD certificate, the Center offers a Masters in Latin American Studies (MALAS) degree, an undergraduate minor, and graduate and undergraduate certificates. Jointly with the UF Center for African Studies, LAS offers the Master of Sustainable Development Practice (MDP) degree. The Center for African Studies and other campus units also collaborate in the TCD program. About the University: Founded in 1853, the University of Florida (UF) is the state’s flagship public research university and a Land-Grant, Sea-Grant, and Space-Grant institution. One of the nation’s most academically diverse universities, UF offers virtually every academic and professional discipline and is a member of the Association of American Universities. With more than 56,000 students across 16 colleges, a major academic health center, and championship-winning athletics, UF provides an exceptional environment for learning, discovery, and innovation. Consistently ranked among the nation’s top public universities, UF was ranked the nation’s No. 1 public university by The Wall Street Journal in 2024 and among the top public universities by U.S. News & World Report , Forbes , and TIME . UF faculty conducted a record $1.33 billion in research in fiscal year 2025, including a record $817 million in federal research funding, underscoring the University’s growing national impact and commitment to advancing knowledge and addressing complex challenges. About the Area: As home to Florida's largest and oldest university, Gainesville is one of the state's centers of education, medicine, cultural events, and athletics. Gainesville and the surrounding area are home to about 250,000 people, a robust arts community, several museums, and a world-class health care system anchored at UF. The area offers a low cost of living, strong public schools, and year-round outdoor recreation with easy access to Florida's coasts and major metropolitan areas, such as Jacksonville, Orlando, and Tampa, making it an attractive location for candidates and their families. https://www.visitgainesville.com/ Expected Salary: Commensurate with the qualifications and experience of the candidate. In addition to a competitive salary, the university offers a comprehensive benefits package. Explore more here https://benefits.hr.ufl.edu/ . Required Qualifications: An earned doctorate or equivalent terminal degree in a relevant field; Demonstrated record of relevant research and professional experience in tropical conservation and development, including significant field experience in the Global South; Experience with program management, administration, and fundraising; Experience with leading facilitative decision-making processes and shared governance; and The ability to build collaborative relationships across disciplines and communicate effectively with diverse constituencies. Preferred: Fluency in Spanish and/or Portuguese or another language of the tropics. Special Instructions to Applicants: Interested applicants must apply online at jobs.ufl.edu . For full consideration applicants must include: Complete curriculum vitae; A letter of application describing experience, qualifications, and vision related to this position; and The names and contact information for three professional references. Review of applications will begin on November 2, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. For inquiries concerning this position, contact search committee chair, Dr. Karen Kainer at kkainer@ufl.edu . All candidates for employment are subject to a pre-employment screening which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. The successful candidate will be required to provide official transcript(s) upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://naces.org/ . The University of Florida is an equal-opportunity institution. If an accommodation, due to disability, is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 11 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541306/student-assistant-innovation-academy-prototyping-studio-technical-assistant": {
      "title": "Student Assistant - Innovation Academy Prototyping Studio Technical Assistant",
      "description": "Student Assistant - Innovation Academy Prototyping Studio Technical Assistant Apply now Job no: 541306 Work type: Student Ast Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support, Student Services Department: 02070400 - PV-INNOVATION ACADEMY Classification Title: STU AST NON-CLERICAL & ADMIN Classification Minimum Requirements: • This position is open to students who are eligible to work on campus, be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS), and maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. • Registered students are employed on a part-time basis • Demonstrated experience with prototyping technologies. Job Description: Innovation Academy Technical Assistants support the day-to-day operation of the IA Prototyping Studio and help IA students safely and effectively use Studio equipment and prototyping technologies. Technical Assistants staff scheduled Studio hours, assist students with technical and fabrication questions, verify required training, oversee approved equipment workflows, maintain Studio records, and enforce Studio safety and access requirements. Current activities include 3D printing and related prototyping workflows, but equipment, software, and supported technologies may change as the Studio develops. Technical Assistants may also provide technical assistance for Innovation Academy courses, programs, workshops, and projects. This position does not include grading student coursework. Expected Salary: $16.00 per hour Required Qualifications: • This position is open to students who are eligible to work on campus, be employed through student Other Personnel Services (OPS), and maintain a minimum 2.0 grade point average. • Registered students are employed on a part-time basis • Demonstrated experience with prototyping technologies. Preferred: Strong troubleshooting and problem-solving skills and a willingness and ability to learn new technologies. Ability to follow and consistently enforce established safety procedures and Studio rules. Confidence assisting students in one-on-one and group settings. Excellent communication, customer service, and teamwork skills. Reliability, punctuality, and ability to commit to an established work schedule. Previous teaching, tutoring, TA, makerspace, or technical-support experience. Additional experience with 3D printing, CAD, electronics, programming, or fabrication. Successful completion of IDS 1940 Creativity and Design Thinking for Innovation. Enrollment in or completion of the Innovation Minor. Special Instructions to Applicants: Required Portfolio Submission Contents: Submit a small portfolio containing 2–3 examples of technical or prototyping work completed primarily or substantially by you. Examples may include: A 3D modeling/3D printing project, including an image and model file. An Arduino or electronics project, including an image or video and code. A laser-cutting or Adobe Illustrator project, including an image and original design file. Another project demonstrating relevant technical, fabrication, or problem-solving skills. Team projects are acceptable if your individual contribution is clearly identified. In order to considered, applicants must include a cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 11 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 25 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541310/ops-research-assistantscientist": {
      "title": "OPS Research Assistant/Scientist",
      "description": "OPS Research Assistant/Scientist Apply now Job no: 541310 Work type: Temp Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Grant or Research Administration, Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 28100000 - VM-INFECT DISEASE & IMMUNOLOGY Classification Title: OPS Research Assistant/Scientist Classification Minimum Requirements: MSc plus relevant research experience or PhD in immunology, reproductive biology, or a related life science field Flow cytometry experience Mouse handling experience Basic molecular technique experience Job Description: Design and execute experiments across mouse and human systems, including primary cell and organoid work Perform and support rodent surgeries, injections, and colony management Run and troubleshoot flow cytometry, PCR, Western blot, and electroporation protocols Perform animal work, in accordance with IACUC compliance Contribute to data analysis, and manuscript drafting Mentor junior lab members as needed Expected Salary: $25.00 - $30.00 / hourly Required Qualifications: ● MSc plus relevant research experience or PhD in immunology, reproductive biology, or a related life science field ● Flow cytometry experience ● Mouse handling experience ● Basic molecular technique experience Preferred: General reproductive biology experience CRISPR/Cas9 experience Electroporation experience Reproductive immunology background, particularly ovarian or uterine tissue Primary cell culture experience Histology experience Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 11 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 31 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541311/assistant-professor-in-ai-and-advertising": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in AI and Advertising",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in AI and Advertising Apply now Job no: 541311 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Communications/Public Relations/Marketing, Education/Training/Instructional Design Department: 23020100 - CJC-ADVERSTISING-GENERAL Classification Title: AST Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates must have completed a Ph.D. in communication, mass communication, advertising, marketing, or a related field by the time of appointment. Job Description: The Department of Advertising in the College of Journalism and Communications (CJC) at the University of Florida invites applications for a nine-month tenure-track Assistant Professor in AI and Advertising position to begin in Fall 2027. Candidates must have completed a Ph.D. in communication, mass communication, advertising, marketing, or a related field by the time of appointment. We seek a scholar whose research makes a strong contribution to the field of advertising, with a focus on artificial intelligence and its implications for the industry, professional practice, and pedagogy. Relevant areas for research specialization include but are not limited to AI-mediated advertising and persuasion, human-AI interaction, generative AI, algorithmic and automated communication, AI-driven social media and consumer insights, and the broader implications of emerging technologies for consumers, brands, media, and society. This faculty member will work in partnership with UF’s Consortium on Trust in Media and Technology, which is based at CJC. The Consortium connects scholars and thought leaders across disciplines to develop applications supporting media and technology that is credible and equitable. The Consortium’s work is part of UF’s commitment to artificial intelligence as the centerpiece of a major, long-term initiative combining world-class research infrastructure, cutting-edge research, and a transformational approach to curriculum. The successful candidate also will have opportunities to collaborate across the College and University on research related to artificial intelligence, emerging media technologies, consumer behavior, and strategic communication. The successful candidate will be expected to teach undergraduate courses in the advertising curriculum and established graduate courses approved by CJC’s Graduate Division. They may also be given the opportunity to teach specialized course offerings such as AI and Advertising and AI-Driven Social Media Insight, as well as develop new courses in their area of expertise. The successful candidate will also mentor doctoral and master’s students and participate in service to the department, college, university, and discipline. Candidates will be evaluated on the quality of their current research output and momentum, capacity for scholarly development and advancement, potential to cultivate a nationally and internationally acclaimed research agenda, and demonstrated contributions to teaching, mentorship, and interdisciplinary collaboration. Candidates must have completed a Ph.D. in communication, mass communication, advertising, marketing, or a related field by the time of appointment; demonstrate a record of, or strong potential for, original scholarly research relevant to advertising and strategic communication; demonstrate a research program that substantially engages artificial intelligence or related emerging technologies; demonstrate potential to secure external research funding; and demonstrate the ability to contribute effectively to undergraduate and/or graduate teaching. About UF’s College of Journalism and Communications The University of Florida College of Journalism and Communications, accredited by ACEJMC, is a nationally recognized leader in journalism and communications education and research. The College serves nearly 3,500 undergraduate and graduate students across four academic departments and is committed to attracting and retaining top talent through a culture that values diverse perspectives, skills, and experiences. Learn more at the College of Journalism and Communications and Department of Advertising. https://www.jou.ufl.edu ) and the Department of Advertising ( https://www.jou.ufl.edu/advertising/) for more information. About the University of Florida The University of Florida is a member of the Association of American Universities and a nationally recognized public research university. UF faculty conducted a record $1.33 billion in research in fiscal year 2025, including $817 million in federal research funding. UF offers a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits package. Learn more about UF Total Rewards . About Gainesville, Florida Gainesville is a vibrant college community of approximately 250,000 residents and home to the University of Florida. Known for its arts and culture, excellent schools, healthcare, restaurants, athletics, and abundant natural beauty, Gainesville offers an exceptional quality of life. The city is also within a two-hour drive of Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville, and St. Augustine. Learn more about Gainesville and Explore Gainesville in 60 Seconds Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience and education Required Qualifications: Candidates must have completed a Ph.D. in communication, mass communication, advertising, marketing, or a related field by the time of appointment; ● Demonstrate a record of, or strong potential for, original scholarly research relevant to advertising and strategic communication; ● Demonstrate a research program that substantially engages artificial intelligence or related emerging technologies; ● Demonstrate potential to secure external research funding; and ●Demonstrate the ability to contribute effectively to undergraduate and/or graduate teaching. Preferred: Preference will be given to candidates who provide evidence of: A strong trajectory of scholarly research Excellence or demonstrated potential in undergraduate and/or graduate teaching; effective mentorship of students Willingness to collaborate on research within and beyond the University of Florida Relevant professional experience in advertising, marketing, artificial intelligence, or related fields. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please submit your application online via Careers at UF at https://jobs.ufl.edu . Applications should include: A cover letter describing the candidate’s research program, including its connection to artificial intelligence and advertising or strategic communication, as well as teaching and mentoring experience and interests A complete curriculum vitae A teaching philosophy statement (evidence of teaching effectiveness, such as course evaluations, is recommended where available) A research statement Names, addresses, email addresses, and telephone numbers of three references. The Search Committee may request additional materials at a later time. Review of applications begins October 15, 2026, and continues until the position is filled. Questions about the position may be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Won-Ki Moon, at wonkimoon@ufl.edu . If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-4621 or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Degrees earned from educational institutions outside of the United States must be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES). All candidates for employment are subject to pre-employment screening, which includes a review of criminal records, reference checks, and verification of education. This position is open until filled. Review of application materials will begin immediately, and continue until a suitable pool of applicants has been established. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 11 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541319/research-assistant-scientist": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Scientist",
      "description": "Research Assistant Scientist Apply now Job no: 541319 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Grant or Research Administration, Engineering, Physical/Mathematical Sciences Department: 19050000 - EG-ELECTRICAL - COMPUTER ENG Classification Title: Research Assistant Scientist Classification Minimum Requirements: PhD in Electrical Engineering or a closely related field. Job Description: The Department of Electrical & Computer Engineering in the Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering (HWCOE) at the University of Florida is soliciting applications for a full-time, nine-month, time-limited, non-tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Research Assistant Scientist. The Research Assistant Scientist position will provide strategic leadership in establishing and expanding a nationally recognized, externally funded research program at the intersection of artificial intelligence (AI) and microelectronics failure analysis (FA), inspection, metrology, reliability, and advanced manufacturing. A central expectation is to identify emerging research opportunities and lead the development and submission of competitive proposals to federal agencies, industry sponsors, consortia, and other funding organizations. The successful candidate will define new research thrusts and long-term technical roadmaps; build multidisciplinary proposal teams; and develop sustained collaborations with academic institutions, government agencies, national laboratories, semiconductor manufacturers, equipment vendors, and other industry partners. Research directions may include machine learning (ML), deep learning (DL), large language models (LLMs), vision-language models (VLMs), large multimodal models (LMMs), agentic AI, federated learning (FL), physics-informed AI, and related emerging technologies applied to advanced semiconductor packaging, integrated circuits, printed circuit boards, and broader microelectronics manufacturing and reliability challenges. The position will also provide intellectual and technical leadership for interdisciplinary research programs in AI-enabled microelectronics inspection and failure analysis, with responsibility for guiding projects from concept development and experimental design through model development, validation, deployment, publication, and technology transition. Research may address multimodal defect detection and classification, computational imaging and reconstruction, anomaly detection, automated root-cause analysis, predictive reliability, multimodal data fusion, digital twins, trustworthy and explainable AI, autonomous and agentic inspection systems, and privacy-preserving collaborative learning. Relevant modalities may include X-ray imaging and computed tomography, scanning acoustic microscopy, optical microscopy, infrared and terahertz imaging, SEM/FIB, electrical measurements, and manufacturing or process data. Additional responsibilities include building and mentoring research teams; supervising graduate and undergraduate researchers and research staff; contributing to instruction, curriculum development, seminars, workshops, and workforce-training activities; disseminating research through high-impact publications and technical presentations; advancing intellectual property and technology transfer; and supporting the continued growth, visibility, and research infrastructure of the laboratory and associated academic–government–industry programs. Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: PhD in Electrical Engineering or a closely related field. Preferred: Demonstrated expertise in AI/ML, including deep learning, computer vision, LLMs, VLMs, multimodal AI, agentic AI, physics-informed AI, or related emerging technologies. Research experience applying AI to microelectronics, semiconductor manufacturing, advanced packaging, failure analysis, inspection, metrology, reliability, or related engineering challenges. Experience with multimodal imaging or characterization techniques such as X-ray/CT, scanning acoustic microscopy, optical microscopy, infrared/terahertz imaging, SEM/FIB, or electrical measurements. Strong record of peer-reviewed publications and demonstrated potential to develop an externally funded research program. Experience developing proposals and multidisciplinary collaborations with federal agencies, industry, national laboratories, or academic partners. Experience mentoring graduate and undergraduate researchers and working effectively in interdisciplinary teams. Familiarity with large-scale multimodal datasets, computational imaging, digital twins, autonomous inspection, or AI-enabled experimental systems is highly desirable. Demonstrated potential for technology translation, intellectual property development, industry engagement, and workforce training. Special Instructions to Applicants: The search committee will begin reviewing applications immediately, with the first full committee screening occurring on September 20th, and will continue to review applications until the position is filled. You must apply by submitting an application through UFCareers via https://jobs.ufl.edu/ . ( Job 541319 ) The application consists of attached PDF files of the following required documents: 1. Cover letter (summary, introduction related to hiring emphasis areas) 2. Curriculum vitae (including a 1-page CV highlights) 3. Names, addresses, phone numbers, and email addresses of no less than three and up to five references. Additional documents should be uploaded as one PDF to the “other documents” selection in the application. 4. A personal research program vision statement detailing short-and long-term goals. 5. A vision statement for area/unit detailing short-and long-term goals (identify any synergies with personal research program). 6. Encouraged, but not required , A personal teaching statement describing the applicant’s teaching experience and interests. The anticipated start date for this position is Fall 2026. For further questions, please contact Navid Asadi nasadi@ece.ufl.edu. The University of Florida (UF) is the flagship campus of the State of Florida university system and is consistently ranked among the nation’s top public universities. UF is home to HiPerGator, the fastest AI supercomputer in higher education, and the Herbert Wertheim College of Engineering is undergoing a major expansion in faculty, research, and facilities with a strong focus on artificial intelligence. In Fall 2023, UF opened Malachowsky Hall for Data Science & Information Technology, a 260,000-square-foot facility that serves as the hub for AI. For more information about college initiatives, visit eng.ufl.edu Additional Requirements The selected candidate must provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. Transcripts marked “Issued to Student” will not be considered official. Degrees earned outside the United States must be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Additional Information The University and College count among its greatest strengths a community of faculty, students and staff who support a culture of respect and dignity where learning and research thrive. If you require a reasonable accommodation to apply, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) . Employment is contingent upon eligibility to work in the United States. All searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law . The University of Florida is an equal opportunity employer. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 11 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 26 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541284/business-admin-specialist-iii-jupiter-fl": {
      "title": "Business Admin Specialist III - Jupiter, FL",
      "description": "Business Admin Specialist III - Jupiter, FL Apply now Job no: 541284 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Palm Beach Categories: Business/Accounting/Finance, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 37010100 - SR-HA-ADMINISTRATION Classification Title: Business Admin Specialist III Classification Minimum Requirements: High school diploma or equivalent and five years of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: This position works in The Wertheim UF Scripps Institute shared services center (SSC) to provide oversight and coordination of the administrative and fiscal operations by gathering critical information necessary to complete tasks in a variety of administrative areas, such as fiscal, human resources, commitment accounting, or grants processes. This includes providing guidance to end users and acting as a subject matter expert for the relevant system functionality. Performs administrative and clerical support for the acquisition of goods and services. This position also facilitates advancing service delivery and efficiency by reviewing current processes for efficiency and effectiveness and exploring innovative solutions. Responsible for building relationships with the units served, supervising the daily transactions of the SSC, and supporting a culture centered on delivering excellent customer service. Responsibilities and Duties: Fiscal Operations & Financial Support Analyzes financial operational problems and implements appropriate resolutions. Assists with monitoring budgetary performance of various operational and scientific laboratories, such as tracking/reconciling certain accounts and initiating follow-up discussions with specific functional areas regarding budget variances. Oversees and ensures the full procure-to-pay lifecycle is completed. Interprets, explains, and applies complex procure-to-pay policies and procedures while monitoring compliance with UF, State, and Federal regulations. Serves as a resource and effective liaison to internal and external constituents and customers. Maintains transactional databases and records. Finance Transaction Processing Responsible for analyzing, preparing, and entering both routine and complex transactional activities, including fiscal, human resources, commitment accounting, and grants processing. Examples include processing personnel reimbursements and labor account distribution work by completing PDRR forms. Reconciles and approves purchasing transactions. Review and research transactions to provide information as requested. Researches, analyzes, and resolves complex accounts transaction problems. Will work frequently with the accounting team, laboratory administrators, principal investigators, the Office of Sponsored Programs, and the Controller’s Office to provide administrative and clerical support for the successful acquisition of goods and services. Administrative Operations & Supplier Management Responsible for managing the purchasing of commodities by processing purchase orders and auditing invoices. Confers with labs and department personnel to determine their needs and budgetary requirements to purchase materials, supplies, and equipment, and makes recommendations in terms of quality, quantity, and type of product or order. Serves as a resource and customer liaison for supplier add requests and information gathering. Assists in the management of onsite contracted suppliers to ensure compliance and effectiveness, including meeting with key personnel on a regular basis. Responsible for managing the campus asset program, including disposals and transfers. Performs other duties as assigned. Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience. Required Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent and five years of relevant experience. Appropriate college coursework or vocational/technical training may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: Analytical and problem-solving skills, including researching and analyzing complex data. Understand and practice excellent customer service. Possess a professional attitude which is conveyed in all interactions, communications and appearance. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with faculty, administrative staff, and other campus departments. Work well in a team environment. Ability to take ownership of the resolution of problems, be flexible and accountable, manage conflicting responsibilities, prioritize tasks, and consistently meet deadlines. Self-motivated and capable of working independently. Strong people and team management skills. Knowledge of and experience with university and state regulations regarding human resources, and fiscal and budget matters. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. Please provide names and email addresses of three to five references as part of the application process. When ready, the hiring department will contact the listed references via email requesting their reference letters to be uploaded directly to the application website. This is a time-limited position. The University of Florida is a member of the State University System of Florida and an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment based on merit. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . This position is open until filled. Application review will begin September 17, 2026. This requisition will be used to fill multiple positions. Health Assessment Required: No. Advertised: 10 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541282/clinical-assistant-or-associate-professor-of-veterinary-clinical-pathology": {
      "title": "Clinical Assistant or Associate Professor of Veterinary Clinical Pathology",
      "description": "Clinical Assistant or Associate Professor of Veterinary Clinical Pathology Apply now Job no: 541282 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Veterinary Medicine/Animal Care Department: 28060000 - VM-COMP, DIAG & POP MEDICINE Classification Title: CLIN AST/ASO PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Assistant Professor Candidates for this rank shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess equivalent qualifications based on professional experience. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Associate Professor Candidates for this rank shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess professional qualifications above those equivalent to the highest degree relevant to their field. They are expected to have produced creative work, professional writing, or research and successful teaching or professional experience. A candidate for this rank must have a distinguished record of achievement beyond the assistant professor level. Job Description: The Bronson Animal Disease Diagnostic Laboratory (BADDL) and the Department of Comparative, Diagnostic, and Population Medicine (CDPM) at the University of Florida-College of Veterinary Medicine (UF-CVM) invite applications for a full time, Clinical Asst/Assoc/Professor of Clinical Pathology. This position is a multi-mission non-tenure track position in the CDPM that is promotable following UF-CVM guidelines. The position will be headquartered in Kissimmee, Florida and will perform clinical (80%) and extension (20%) duties at the BADDL. The candidate will function primarily as a clinical pathologist at the BADDL. The diagnostic caseload includes a variety of companion animals, livestock, avian and wildlife species. Interaction with veterinarians, owners, and livestock producers is an integral part of this position. Creative use of diagnostic case material including publication of results in appropriate journals is encouraged. The successful candidate will have daily interaction with the current pathologists and the resident at the BADDL. Additionally, the candidate may have limited opportunities to teach DVM and/or graduate students or engage in research at the UF-CVM depending on the candidate’s qualifications and interests. University and public service through departmental, college and university committee work, participation in professional organizations, continuing education and other appropriate activities is expected. The faculty hire must have experience in diagnostic cytopathology, hematology, clinical chemistry, and urinalysis, and have the ability to independently sign out cases, in support of the clinical pathology service. The BADDL clinical pathology service is supported by a high quality, high volume, and diverse caseload with submissions from outside practices. The clinical pathology laboratory is well-equipped and includes dedicated sections for cytopathology, hematology and clinical chemistry, endocrinology (Siemens Immulite® 2000xpi), urinalysis, and other specialty analyses. Current tests offerings are listed on the BADDL website. The incumbent will provide diagnostic and consultative services to veterinary practitioners, animal owners and producers, and regulatory officials. The incumbent is expected to develop a working knowledge of routine laboratory assays and obtain proficiency for testing in the assigned area, maintaining a working knowledge of the Laboratory Information Management System (LIMS) and utilizing this tool for case coordination and reporting. The incumbent is also expected to provide mentorship, coaching, training, and guidance to residents, and will train employees who are assigned to clinical pathology section, as needed. Consultation with in-house pathologists, professional laboratory staff, the National Veterinary Services Laboratory (NVSL), and university pathologists, is anticipated. Fully accredited by the American Association of Veterinary Laboratory Diagnosticians (AAVLD), the BADDL provides quality diagnostic services for all animal species, including necropsy, bacteriology, serology, histopathology, virology, parasitology, and molecular diagnostics. As a national leader and the premier animal disease diagnostic laboratory in the state of Florida, it serves veterinarians, animal owners, livestock and poultry industry, educators and researchers to protect animal and human health. BADDL processes about 34,000 cases each year and conducts more than 80,000 tests annually. The incumbent will be responsible and accountable for managing budget/dollars/resources (staff, vehicles, computers, cell phones, etc.) of any assigned programs in an efficient and accountable manner. They will perform other duties as assigned. The UF-CVM is the sixth-ranked College of Veterinary Medicine in the US and is the only one that is part of a top 10 public research university The CVM is located on campus in close proximity to the College of Medicine and numerous other Health Sciences units that provide ample opportunities for translational research and collaboration. Expected Salary: Rank and salary will be based upon the successful candidate’s professional qualifications and experience. Required Qualifications: Required qualifications for this position are a DVM or equivalent degree from an approved institution and completion of a veterinary clinical pathology residency training program or equivalent training. ACVP or ECVP board eligibility or certification is required. Experience in clinical pathology cases, and the ability to independently sign out cases, is required. Preferred: None Special Instructions to Applicants: For full consideration, all applicants must apply online through Careers at UF. Applicants must submit the following items as a PDF file for full consideration: (1) a letter outlining clinical/diagnostic service, teaching and research experiences as well as professional goals, (2) a current curriculum vitae (CV), and (3) names and contact information for three professional references able to speak to all aspects of the candidate’s professional and personal abilities as they relate to this position’s responsibilities. E-mail inquiries to Dr. Justin Stilwell ( stilwellj@ufl.edu ), Search Committee Chair, are welcome. Review of applications for job number 541282 will begin immediately and will continue until a candidate is recommended for appointment. Candidates may be invited to interview prior to the application review date, though a hiring decision will not be made until after that date. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is An Equal Employment Opportunity Institution. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352/392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800/955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 12 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541290/postdoctoral-research-associate": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Associate Apply now Job no: 541290 Work type: Post Doc Associate Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science Department: 29020100 - MD-NEUROSCIENCE-GENERAL Classification Title: Postdoctoral Research Associate Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in Neurobiology, Stem Cell Biology, Biomedical Engineering, Cell Biology, Molecular Biology, or a closely related ﬁeld. Job Description: The Guo Laboratory at the University of Florida is seeking a highly motivated, talented, and creative Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join our dynamic multi-disciplinary team. Our research sits at the cutting edge of regenerative medicine and neurobiology, utilizing state-of-the-art human induced pluripotent stem cell (hiPSC) models, 3D tissue engineering, and advanced multi-omic / bioinformatics integrations to model and dissect human diseases. Our lab focuses heavily on inventing and reﬁning Next Approach Methodologies (NAMs)—speciﬁcally, brain organoids, Blood-Brain Barrier (BBB) / Blood-Spinal Cord Barrier (BSCB) assembloids, and spinal cord/motor neuron models—to investigate the molecular and epitranscriptomic mechanisms driving neurodegenerative conditions like Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) and Alzheimer’s Disease (AD), as well as neurodevelopmental conditions including autism and Fragile X Syndrome (FXS). The Guo Lab is exceptionally well-funded through major federal agencies and foundation awards—including NINDS, NIMH, NIDA, NIA, and the NSF—and regularly publishes in high-impact journals such as Nature, Cell Stem Cell, Nature Neuroscience, Nature Communications, Science Advances, Molecular Psychiatry, Advanced Science, The Journal of Clinical Investigation, Cell Discovery, and Protein & Cell. This position oﬀers an unparalleled opportunity to spearhead high-proﬁle projects, leverage robust resources, and work within the deeply collaborative, state-of-the-art academic ecosystem at UF Health and the McKnight Brain Institute. Key Responsibilities: • Advanced Cellular Engineering: Lead the directed diﬀerentiation of hiPSCs into highly enriched neural and vascular lineages (e.g., cortical neurons, motor neurons, astrocytes, microglia, brain endothelial cells, pericytes) to construct complex 3D assembloids and organ-on-a-chip platforms. • Mechanistic Dissection: Deploy CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing, shRNA/ASO tools, and pharmacological screens to interrogate pathological cellular axes. • Multimodal Analytics: Perform and integrate high-resolution functional readouts, including whole-cell patch-clamp electrophysiology, quantitative confocal/super-resolution microscopy, single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq), and spatial transcriptomics. • Scientiﬁc Leadership: Independently manage project milestones, draft high-impact manuscripts, present ﬁndings at premier international conferences, and mentor graduate and undergraduate students within a supportive lab ecosystem. Expected Salary: $63,480-$65,000 commensurate with experience Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Neurobiology, Stem Cell Biology, Biomedical Engineering, Cell Biology, Molecular Biology, or a closely related ﬁeld. Preferred: • Strong technical background in mammalian cell culture (experience with hiPSC/hESC maintenance and directed diﬀerentiation is highly preferred, but not required for candidates with exceptional molecular biology or electrophysiology expertise). • Hands-on experience with 3D organoid, assembloid, or microﬂuidic/organ-on-a-chip modeling. • Experience with whole-cell patch-clamp electrophysiology or multi-electrode arrays. • Familiarity with computational biology pipelines (e.g., handling transcriptomic or scRNA-seq datasets using R or Python). Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter and resume. What We Oﬀer: • Comprehensive University of Florida beneﬁts package. • State-of-the-Art Facilities: Access to a brand-new, modern laboratory space within the Department of Neuroscience and McKnight Brain Institute, equipped with dedicated stem cell suites, high-resolution imaging cores, and advanced electrophysiology rigs. This requisition will be used to fill multiple positions. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 09 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/523259/federal-work-study-landscaper-kanapaha-botanical-gardens": {
      "title": "Federal Work Study - Landscaper (Kanapaha Botanical Gardens)",
      "description": "Federal Work Study - Landscaper (Kanapaha Botanical Gardens) Apply now Job no: 523259 Work type: Student Ast Location: Alachua Categories: Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades, Agriculture Operations Department: 06030000 - EM-FWS-COMMUNITY SERVICE Classification Title: STU AST - FEDERAL COMMUNITY SERVICE Job Description: Federal Work Study Position is with the Kanapaha Botanical Gardens. Looking for 3-9hrs per week. Landscaping including weeding, mulching, planting, trimming, blowing sidewalks and rental set up. Any day Monday, Tuesday and Friday mornings, with a 3-4 hour shift on days coming in (8:30am or 9am-12 or 12:30pm). Must be able to work on Saturday October 17th to help with the Fall Plant Sale. Please include your current available days and times you are available to work. Expected Salary: $15/hour Minimum Requirements: Federal Work Study required, no exceptions. Must have a good work ethic. Preferred Qualifications: Must have a good work ethic. Must have Federal Work Study award. * Students seeking FWS jobs should print/screenshot a copy of their Financial Aid Award Page, which contains the Federal Work Study award as proof of FWS eligibility. To locate your Financial Aid Award Page, go to https://one.uf.edu/financialaid/aidyear Special Instructions to Applicants: DO NOT APPLY TO THIS POSTING, APPLY DIRECTLY TO EMAIL. Applicants should send resume, cover letter, and references to Alexis at kbotanical@gmail.com to apply. Applicants should list their available schedule to work. PLEASE INCLUDE UFID IN APPLICATION Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 08 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541175/academic-advisor-i": {
      "title": "Academic Advisor I",
      "description": "Academic Advisor I Apply now Job no: 541175 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Academic Advising/Support Department: 19400100 - EG-ENGINEERING EDUCATION ADMIN Classification Title: Academic Advisor I Classification Minimum Requirements: Master's degree in education, counseling, or academic field related to the college OR Bachelor's degree in similar fields with one year of relevant experience. Job Description: Undergraduate Advising of Computer Engineering Majors The incumbent will be responsible for advisement/counseling for undergraduate students pursuing a computer engineering major. First and foremost, the incumbent will advise students regarding their progress in the major and necessary resources to ensure her/his retention in the major. The incumbent may work directly with UF Registrar, Student Financial Aid, Counseling and Wellness Center, Career Resources Center, and other units within the Dean of Students Office as necessary. The incumbent will assist students with course registration and processing of academic status paperwork. Additionally, the incumbent will manage preparation and processing of student graduation documentation in coordination with Undergraduate Student Affairs personnel, and will direct students to necessary resources in the College and on campus for internship and job placement. Recruiting and Admitting New Undergraduate Students In consultation with the program administration and staff, the incumbent will oversee the transfer student admissions process for computer engineering majors. The incumbent will work with the Undergraduate Student Affairs office that coordinates all transfer student admissions and work within the deadlines established by the College of Engineering. The advisor will also participate in UF’s orientation process (“Preview”). Additionally, the incumbent will actively engage community colleges, and particularly students enrolled in Gator Engineering @ SFC, ensuring they have accurate information concerning preparation for transfer to UF in the computer engineering major, which will include answering inquiries from prospective students. The incumbent will also work actively with student groups and other university entities to organize outreach and recruitment events, specifically targeting under-represented minority (URM) populations. The incumbent will collaborate to develop, support, and administer programs to improve communication with students and improve retention. Preparing and Summarizing Information Regarding Courses Offered by the Unit The incumbent will review, compile, collate, and summarize information regarding courses taught in Computer Engineering. The incumbent will also assist with companies interested in creating mentor/early identification programs with students of the program and help coordinate and implement such programs. Policy Coordination The incumbent will be responsible for interpreting educational and administrative rules and polices and recommending new and improved approaches to efficient advisement and academic function. The incumbent will also act as liaison for advising with the university, college, and department to keep abreast of regulation, procedures, and opportunities regarding undergraduate recruitment. Keep Abreast of Advising Field Professionally The incumbent will pursue opportunities for professional development, including taking advantage of appropriate UF Human Resources classes, and will stay current in the profession through attendance at workshops and memberships in professional associations such as the National Academic Advising Association (NACADA). Other Duties Other duties as assigned. Expected Salary: Up to $50,000 annually; commensurate with education and experience Required Qualifications: Master's degree in education, counseling, or academic field related to the college OR Bachelor's degree in similar fields with one year of relevant experience. Preferred: • Master of Science degree. • Experience in advising and counseling students at the undergraduate level. • Experience in developing and maintaining electronic database. • Working knowledge of University of Florida regulations and procedures to facilitate recruitment and student counseling. • Ability to effectively interface with faculty, students, and staff in a responsive, courteous, and professional manner. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered for this position, you must upload a cover letter and resume with application. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Posting has been extended, current applicants do not need to re-apply. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 15 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541272/research-administrator-ii": {
      "title": "Research Administrator II",
      "description": "Research Administrator II Apply now Job no: 541272 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Construction/Design/Planning, Office/Administrative/Fiscal Support Department: 15040000 - DCP-LANDSCAPE ARCHITECTURE Classification Title: Research Administrator II Classification Minimum Requirements: Bachelors degree and two years of experience in grant and contract administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Job Description: The School of Landscape Architecture and Planning (SLA&P) studies and designs ways to improve natural and built environments to create healthy, equitable, and resilient communities. Our research involves environmental, urban and social challenges at the intersection of art, sciences and the built environment. We work at a range of scales - site, city, region and context - both domestic and international. Our research and community outreach themes and focus capabilities are: 1) design thinking, 2) socio-cultural, economic and human needs, 3) adaptive policy, planning and design, 4) land use water, infrastructure and resource interactions, and 5) geospatial analysis and modeling. This position will play pivotal role is getting this research processed and approved so we can provide the community and the world with positive changes in our ever changing environment. Research Project Administration Supports all aspects of a grant or contract award lifecycle including proposal submission, award set-up, award management, and assistance with closeout activities by providing analysis and comprehensive administration, while abiding by UF, sponsor, and various levels of jurisdiction on policies and procedures. Works as primary contact and liaison for departmental faculty and staff, the College, UF Research and other stakeholders for all research related activities. Pre-Award Administration : The administrator starts with pre-award support which includes but is not limited to: budget development; policy review; UFIRST entry, submission, and routing; preparation and submission on sponsor websites. Work closely with Faculty to prepare budgets that include the calculation of personnel salaries, project expenses, and administrative & facilities costs. Ensure all documents line up equally such as the budget and budget justification, formatting as needed, entering proposals in UFIRST, uploading documentation, coordinating and reviewing all aspects of the proposal submission to ensure thorough and timely submission to the College and DSP for review and submission to the funding agency in a timely manner. Work with various electronic proposal systems (such as UFIRST, Grants.gov, Research.gov, etc.) as needed to enter additional sponsor application requirements. Award Management : Manage the timeline of the award and process adjustments as necessary, which may include but is not limited to: entering modifications in UFIRST for project changes; create sub-awards as needed, enter center designations, Cost Accounting Standards (CAS) exemptions, requests for no cost extensions, carryover, re-budgeting requests, changes in scope, and other changes to the award. Meet with Faculty on a regular basis for grant monthly report reconciliation and overall lifecycle management. Make sure funds are being spent appropriately and distributions have been entered on time to avoid retros. Post Award Administration : Complete the lifecycle of the research activity such as: release the awards in UFIRST including subawards and subprojects, effort and commitments. Assist in the transfer of grant funds from other institutions for new incoming Faculty and grant transfers for Faculty who are leaving UF. Work with UF Division of Contracts & Grants (C&G) to close-out projects and confirm PI fund distribution accounts. Fiscal, Travel, & HR: Review and approve travel requests, expense reports, reimbursements and other travel expenses on research funds. Approve invoices, pcard purchases, purchase orders (Pos), and any other purchasing expenses occurring on research funds. Work with HR to review and approve employee hires, assisting with entering payroll distributions and tuition waivers that are on research funds. Other duties as assigned UF offers a variety of benefits to our employees that can be found here: https://hr.ufl.edu/benefits/ Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience. $62,000-68,000 Required Qualifications: Bachelors degree and two years of experience in grant and contract administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Preferred: Extensive knowledge and ability to interpret University of Florida, State, Federal and sponsor rules, regulations, policies and procedures as it pertains to conducting research at UF. Comprehensive knowledge of the grant proposal process at UF and experience with using multiple research programs such as UF’s UFIRST system. research.gov and/or grants.gov preferred. Must be able to work independently with demonstrated problem-solving skills and attention to details. Good verbal and written communication skills and ability to communicate with all stakeholders. Skilled in the use of operating systems such as Microsoft Excel and Word, Adobe and have the ability to learn new operating systems. UF will be changing to workday in 2027 from peoplesoft. Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your cover letter, resume and list of at least 3 references. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 13 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541274/assistant-curator": {
      "title": "ASSISTANT CURATOR",
      "description": "ASSISTANT CURATOR Apply now Job no: 541274 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Libraries/Museums, Humanities Department: 56020500 - NH-ENVIROMENTAL ARCHAEOLOGY Classification Title: Assistant Curator Classification Minimum Requirements: Ph.D. in Anthropology or a related field by the date of appointment; evidence of collections-based research using archaeological collections; and evidence of excellence or strong potential for excellence in research through publication record, grant record, or potential to conduct grant-funded research. Job Description: Assistant Curator of Environmental Archaeology The Florida Museum of Natural History at the University of Florida (UF) seeks qualified applicants for a full-time,12-month, tenure-accruing, Assistant Curator of Environmental Archaeology. This position will be filled at the Assistant faculty level in the Florida Museum’s Department of Natural History. For over 40 years, the faculty and staff of the Environmental Archaeology Program (FM-EAP) at the Florida Museum of Natural History have played a foundational role in the development of zooarchaeology, with secondary emphases in archaeobotany and archaeopedology. We curate archaeological animal, plant, and soil assemblages from across the Americas, including the southeastern United States, insular Caribbean, Mesoamerica, and South America. Several of the collections from sites are co-curated and managed across other archaeological museum collections (e.g., Florida Archaeology, Historical Archaeology, Caribbean Archaeology). The collections represent more than 1.7 million specimens from over 800 sites, including the largest zooarchaeological comparative collection in the southeastern USA. Through museum research, student mentorship, and collaboration, the FM-EAP is an international leader in zooarchaeological methods of identification and analysis. The zooarchaeological collections are complemented by macrobotanical resources, including comparative wood and seed collections from the southeastern United States. The FM-EAP faculty, staff, and collections support research on a wide range of questions related to human-environment interactions, including subsistence, domestication, coastal adaptations, migration, trade and exchange, resource management, landscape use, and cultural and environmental change. The program also supports interdisciplinary research and training that incorporates geochemical and biochemical analyses as complementary approaches to these questions. The FM-EAP provides an exceptional opportunity for a curator to build on a strong tradition of collections-based research, especially in the circum-Caribbean region, while developing innovative approaches to environmental archaeology that will continue to advance our anthropological understanding of human-environment relationships through time and across the globe. About the Florida Museum The Florida Museum of Natural History is the official state museum of Florida. It is located on the campus of the University of Florida, which is ranked no. 7 among the nation’s public research universities by U.S. News and World Report, and is the flagship of the state university system. The Florida Museum’s mission includes stewardship of 40 million specimens and artifacts, award-winning exhibitions, public programs, and emerging virtual and digital engagement. The research and collections programs of the Department of Natural History are world class and attract about $10 million annually in government and philanthropic support. The Florida Museum is a national and international leader in AI and biodiversity informatics, enhanced by its formative role in iDigBio, the national hub for digitization of natural history specimens. The Department of Natural History, a college-level unit within UF, is a vibrant community of about 300 employees, including 30 full-time faculty, ~80–100 graduate students, ~20 postdoctoral research associates, hundreds of undergraduates engaged in research, curation, and education, as well as administrative, collections and research support staff. The department has dedicated labs for digital imaging, molecular genetics and genomics, and preparing skeletal specimens. Natural History enjoys cross-campus collaborations with many of the 16 UF colleges, including those of potential relevance to this new faculty position: Liberal Arts and Sciences, Agricultural and Life Sciences (Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences), Education, Engineering, Libraries, and Veterinary Medicine. Other campus resources relevant to this position include the UF Museum Studies Program, FM Randell Research Center, UF Genetics Institute, UF Biodiversity Institute. Expected Salary: The starting salary range for this position is $90,000 to $105,000, commensurate with qualifications and experience. The University of Florida (UF) offers an exceptional benefits package, including health, dental and vision insurance; state retirement plans; a generous leave program; and tuition assistance. UF is a Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF) eligible employer. For more information on benefits, please visit UF HR Benefits and Rewards at https://benefits.hr.ufl.edu/. Required Qualifications: We seek to hire an innovative environmental archaeologist whose research and teaching will foster an interdisciplinary approach to natural history scholarship, including collections-based research design and student mentorship. We invite candidates who address questions in the broad field of environmental archaeology and who will contribute to, and make use of, the Florida Museum’s extensive and world-renowned environmental archaeology collection. We anticipate that the successful candidate will have the opportunity to both bolster and expand the FM-EAP’s collections through sustained research, formal academic advising, teaching and service. An ideal candidate will contribute to the museum’s goals of understanding, preserving, and interpreting biological and cultural diversity, and conduct research with opportunities for integrative collaborations with other faculty and divisions within the Florida Museum and across the 16 colleges of the University of Florida. Candidates using multidisciplinary approaches are highly encouraged to apply. Successful candidates must have a Ph.D. in Anthropology or a related field, conduct collections-based research on archaeological collections, and demonstrate excellence or strong potential for excellence in research through publication record, grant record, or potential to conduct grant-funded research. Special Instructions to Applicants: Interested applicants must apply online at: careers.ufl.edu. For full consideration, applications must include: • Cover letter • Full curriculum vitae, including lists of peer-reviewed publications and grants received • Research statement describing the applicant’s current and planned research • Statement describing the applicant’s experience with museum/research collections and vision for curating, managing, and using FM-EAP collections • Statement describing the applicant’s experience or potential in teaching, mentorship, and outreach • List of the names and email addresses of three professional references. Letters of recommendation are not required at this stage. Review of applications will begin on October 16, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered official if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an educational institution outside of the United States must be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES). For inquiries concerning this position, contact Dr. Michal Kowalewski, Search Chair, at kowalewski@ufl.edu. The University of Florida is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer. If accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD). Employment is contingent upon eligibility to work in the United States. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida’s Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541275/emergency-medicinecommunity-faculty-physician-clinical-assistantassociatefull-professor": {
      "title": "Emergency Medicine/Community Faculty Physician - Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor",
      "description": "Emergency Medicine/Community Faculty Physician - Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor Apply now Job no: 541275 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Duval Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Health Care Administration/Support, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30290000 - JX-EMERGENCY MEDICINE-JAX Classification Title: CLIN AST/ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: MD/DO - be able to obtain a Florida State Medical License Job Description: The University of Florida Department of Emergency Medicine - Jacksonville is recruiting a motivated and energetic board certified or board-eligible emergency medicine physician to join our Faculty in a Community Faculty position. This will be a non-tenured position up to a 1.00 FTE. Clinical activity occurs at a variety of sites and works alongside colleagues in other specialty services. Successful candidates will receive an appointment as either a Clinical Assistant, Clinical Associate or Clinical Professor. Qualified candidates must be ABEM/ABOEM board eligible or certified. About UF Health: UF Health is the Southeast’s premier academic health center and an integral part of the University of Florida. Our mission is to promote health through outstanding and high-quality patient care, innovative and rigorous education in the health professions and biomedical sciences, and research across the spectrum of basic, translational and clinical investigation. UF Health encompasses the Gainesville-based University of Florida colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine; the UF Health Shands family of hospitals in Gainesville; UF Health Jacksonville medical center; UF Health North; an academic campus in Jacksonville that is home to the UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville and includes degree programs offered by the colleges of Nursing and Pharmacy; UF Health St. Johns in St. Augustine; UF Health Neighborhood Hospital in Ocala; and our campus in Central Florida, which includes UF Health Spanish Plaines Hospital and UF Health Leesburg Hospital. It also includes primary care and specialty practices throughout Central, North Central and northern Florida and Southeast Georgia. The UF Health network of hospitals and physician practices manages more than 3 million inpatient and outpatient visits each year, and serves patients from all 67 Florida counties, from across the nation and from dozens of countries around the world. About College of Medicine – Jacksonville (COM-J): The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three colleges at the Health Science Center Jacksonville. The college's 16 clinical science departments house more than 500 faculty members, 1,560 staff and 450 residents and fellows, as well as medical students from UF and around the country. We offer an incredible breadth of clinical training programs and proud to train many of best primary care providers and specialists throughout the region, the state and the country. Research, discovery and innovation are critical aspects of our clinical campus and we have some of the country’s leading researchers at our locations searching for and finding new treatments and clinical options. The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville faculty, administrators, residents, fellows, students and staff work as a team in pursuit of our common mission — to heal, to comfort, to educate and to discover through quality health care, elimination of health disparities, medical education, innovation and research. To learn more about our college, leadership, mission, faculty resources, and the city of Jacksonville visit https://med.jax.ufl.edu/administrative-affairs/faculty-orientation-guide/ . Expected Salary: Salary will be commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: MD/DO - be able to obtain a Florida State Medical License Preferred: ABEM/ABOEM board eligible or certified. Special Instructions to Applicants: The Search Committee will begin to review applications on September 14, 2026 . Please provide letter of intent and Curriculum Vitae. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 08 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/537337/assistant-associate-full-professor-sleep-medicine": {
      "title": "Assistant/ Associate/ Full Professor - Sleep Medicine",
      "description": "Assistant/ Associate/ Full Professor - Sleep Medicine Apply now Job no: 537337 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty, Non-Tenure-Track Faculty, Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Medicine/Physicians Department: 29050900 - MD-PULMONARY MEDICINE Classification Title: AST/ASO/FULL PROF Job Description: The University of Florida Division of Pulmonary, Critical Care & Sleep Medicine is seeking a board-eligible or board certified Sleep Medicine physician to become an active and integral member of the team. This is a full-time appointment in the Department of Medicine at the rank of Assistant/Associate/Full Professor level for a tenure/non-tenure track position. Rank and tenure status will be determined based on education and experience. The successful candidate must have a strong interest in developing an academic career. General Duties and Responsibilities: The primary duties will include seeing sleep medicine clinic patients and interpreting sleep studies, with opportunities for clinical research and teaching. Requisite attributes include a commitment to service, a strong sense of teamwork, and a desire to train tomorrow's doctors. About us: UF Health is one of the Southeast’s most comprehensive academic health centers and includes UF Health Shands, an 1,100-bed quaternary referral hospital, which consistently ranks among the top 50-hospitals by U.S. News & World Report in multiple specialties. In addition, the University of Florida is a top 5 public research university and includes substantial resources to support teaching, research, and collaboration, including one of only two Clinical Translational Science Institute (CTSI) hubs in the state of Florida. The CTSI features a Training and Research Academy for Clinical and Translational Science for early career faculty seeking to develop research skills and pursue careers as clinical investigators. Benefit highlights - Faculty compensation plan that includes quarterly incentives and annual salary increase opportunities University employer-paid benefits include individual and family health insurance, life insurance and long-term disability insurance Retirement benefits include participation in 403(b) and 457 plans as well as an employer match Professional liability protection and sovereign immunity administered by the Florida Board of Governors self-insurance program Relocation costs assistance About the area: The Gainesville, Florida region has an inexpensive cost of living, nationally ranked public middle and high schools, and superb weather with multiple year-round recreational opportunities including biking, hiking, kayaking, snorkeling, and cave diving. Pristine wilderness areas, coastal communities, famous theme parks, and other attractions are within a short driving distance. MSA Population: 330,000 Average High Temp in January: 66 F No State Income Tax Strong public and private schools 1 hour to Gulf of Mexico and Atlantic Ocean 2 hours or less to Jacksonville, Orlando and Tampa Expected Salary: Salary and academic appointment are commensurate with training and level of experience. Minimum Requirements: MD or DO with Board Certification/Board Eligibility in Sleep Medicine. Candidates must be eligible for licensure in the State of Florida. Special Instructions to Applicants: Please attach curriculum vitae and three letters of recommendation. Final candidate will be required to provide official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants remain under consideration and do not need to reapply. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes. Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 03 Nov 2026 Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/540775/assistant-associate-professor": {
      "title": "Assistant / Associate Professor",
      "description": "Assistant / Associate Professor Apply now Job no: 540775 Work type: Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Biology/Life Science, Medicine/Physicians Department: 29030000 - MD-BIOCHEM - MOLECULAR BIOL Classification Title: Assistant/Associate Professor Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates for this rank shall hold the highest degree appropriate to their field or possess equivalent qualifications based on professional experience. A candidate shall be expected to demonstrate successful experience pertinent to the position they are being recommended and have demonstrated qualities relevant to the goals of the academic unit in which they will be employed. Job Description: The Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology in the University of Florida (UF) College of Medicine invites applications for a tenure-track appointment at the Assistant Professor level. The successful candidate must have a Ph.D. or equivalent degree in Biochemistry, Molecular Biology, or a broadly related field. The successful candidate will conduct cutting-edge, exceptional research within the broadly constituted disciplines of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology that would synergize with current UF research strengths. Demonstrated ability to obtain external funding will enhance the application. Exceptional candidates at the Associate Professor level will be considered. Biochemistry and Molecular Biology offers a growing and vibrant research atmosphere with a newly appointed chairperson and 15 research-intense tenure-track faculty members, including 4 recent hires. Current strengths of the department that we seek to complement or expand include: metabolism (spatial-biology, metabolomics, metabolic flux analysis, and artificial intelligence), structural biochemistry (macromolecular structure using cryo-EM, X-ray crystallography, and NMR), and molecular biology (RNA structure/function, epigenetics, gene regulation, DNA repair, and replication). UF offers world-class facilities for NMR/MRI (AMRIS center), structural biology (CSB), mass spectrometry (CASBR), epigenetics (CEG), cancer and neuroscience (McKnight Brain Institute), and computing that are readily accessible to researchers. In partnership with Nvidia, UF built HiPerGator, one of the largest supercomputing facilities sited at a university in the world with a particular emphasis on the use of AI in biomedical research. Notable achievements include the National Cancer Institute designation for the UF Health Cancer Institute, UF surpassing $1.33 billion in research spending, and UF ranks number 4 among public universities in TIME magazine’s America’s Best Colleges 2027. Biochemistry and Molecular Biology is part of a larger Biomedical Sciences program within the College of Medicine that includes eight fundamental science departments. Educational activities of research faculty include mentoring graduate students and research fellows and team-teaching at graduate/professional levels. Our Commitment Being part of the UF College of Medicine means joining a community that fosters the personal and professional goals and achievements of its employees. UF and COM offer multiple tools, resources, and training to help you learn and grow. When you rise, we do too – and we welcome applications from individuals who can contribute to the university’s vision and mission, including the strategic plan of the College of Medicine. To learn more about the College of Medicine’s strategic plan “Engage, Innovate, Excel 2026”, please visit: https://med.ufl.edu/about/strategic-plan/. Expected Salary: Salary is commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Biochemistry or Molecular Biology, or a related field. Preferred: Demonstrated proficiency in obtaining grant funding from the NIH, NSF, or DOD. Special Instructions to Applicants: Applications will be reviewed beginning on October 14, 2026, and the search will continue until the position is filled. Applications and nominations will be kept confidential. Inquiries and nominations can be sent to Professor Linda Bloom, lbloom@ufl.edu . Please plan to submit the following in PDF format to the sections of the online application system listed below: (The job portal does not offer the flexibility to alter the fields for the application components. Therefore, please upload the components below in the sections that are indicated.) A curriculum vitae, including contact information for three references. References will only be contacted for those in final list of top candidates. (Upload your CV to the “Curriculum Vitae or Resume” Section) Statement of research plans (2-3 pages) (Make a combined document that contains your research plans and your teaching philosophy. Upload this combined PDF document to the “Other Document” section.) Citations and description of significance for up to three publications (total of 200 words or less each) (Upload this PDF document to the “Work Sample” section.) Include PDF copies of the above-mentioned three publications. (Upload these PDFs into the “Letter of Recommendation” section. Add one publication to each Letter of Recommendation section.*) Statement of teaching/mentoring philosophy (1 page) (Make a combined document that contains your research plans and your teaching philosophy. Upload this combined PDF document to the “Other Document” section.) *Note: Our application system has a maximum attachment size limit of 5mb. If any of your application materials exceed this limit, please email your materials to our HR Departmental Associate, Alex Franklin, at alexander.franklin@medicine.ufl.edu . Final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an institution outside the U.S. must be evaluated by a credentialing service approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES): http://naces.org/. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541189/cardiothoracic-radiologist-clinical-assistantassociatefull-professor": {
      "title": "Cardiothoracic Radiologist - Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor",
      "description": "Cardiothoracic Radiologist - Clinical Assistant/Associate/Full Professor Apply now Job no: 541189 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Health Care Administration/Support, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30130000 - JX-RADIOLOGY-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: CLIN AST/ASO /FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidate must have a MD/DO in Diagnostic Radiology Job Description: The University of Florida College of Medicine - Jacksonville (UF COM-J) Department of Radiology is seeking a fulltime Cardiothoracic Radiologist at the non-tenure accruing level of Assistant/Associate/Full Professor Job responsibilities include: Cardiothoracic Imaging Radiologist with academic focus at UF Jacksonville Health System at the Assistant, Associate Professor/Full Professor level with 100% specialization. Full-time academic position with UF Radiology and Imaging, UFJPI, Non-Tenure Accruing. Provide high quality service in a friendly academic setting. Serve as a mentor to train a great group of Residents & Fellows. Work with colleagues in surgery, oncology, radiation oncology & pathology, via daily interactions/multidisciplinary meetings. Have the drive and leadership skills necessary to be an integral part of the Cardiovascular & Thoracic Imaging Division. About UF Health: UF Health is the Southeast’s premier academic health center and an integral part of the University of Florida. Our mission is to promote health through outstanding and high-quality patient care, innovative and rigorous education in the health professions and biomedical sciences, and research across the spectrum of basic, translational and clinical investigation. UF Health encompasses the Gainesville-based University of Florida colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine; the UF Health Shands family of hospitals in Gainesville; UF Health Jacksonville medical center; UF Health North; an academic campus in Jacksonville that is home to the UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville and includes degree programs offered by the colleges of Nursing and Pharmacy; UF Health St. Johns in St. Augustine; UF Health Neighborhood Hospital in Ocala; and our campus in Central Florida, which includes UF Health Spanish Plaines Hospital and UF Health Leesburg Hospital. It also includes primary care and specialty practices throughout Central, North Central and northern Florida and Southeast Georgia. The UF Health network of hospitals and physician practices manages more than 3 million inpatient and outpatient visits each year, and serves patients from all 67 Florida counties, from across the nation and from dozens of countries around the world. About College of Medicine – Jacksonville (COM-J): The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three colleges at the Health Science Center Jacksonville. The college's 16 clinical science departments house more than 500 faculty members, 1,560 staff and 450 residents and fellows, as well as medical students from UF and around the country. We offer an incredible breadth of clinical training programs and proud to train many of best primary care providers and specialists throughout the region, the state and the country. Research, discovery and innovation are critical aspects of our clinical campus and we have some of the country’s leading researchers at our locations searching for and finding new treatments and clinical options. The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville faculty, administrators, residents, fellows, students and staff work as a team in pursuit of our common mission — to heal, to comfort, to educate and to discover through quality health care, elimination of health disparities, medical education, innovation and research. To learn more about our college, leadership, mission, faculty resources, and the city of Jacksonville visit https://med.jax.ufl.edu/administrative-affairs/faculty-orientation-guide/ . Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience. Required Qualifications: Candidate must have a MD/DO Preferred: Board Certified / Board Eligible in Diagnostic Radiology. The successful candidate will be fellowship trained in Cardiothoracic Imaging and experienced in all aspects of Cardiothoracic. The candidate should be a team player and expect to join a pleasant group of colleagues Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is open until filled. The Search Committee will begin to review applications as soon as an applicant pool is identified. Please provide letter of intent and Curriculum Vitae. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541246/reitz-union-building-manager": {
      "title": "Reitz Union Building Manager",
      "description": "Reitz Union Building Manager Apply now Job no: 541246 Work type: Staff Full-Time Location: Main Campus (Gainesville, FL) Categories: Facilities Operations/Skilled Trades, Recreation/Event Services, Veteran's Preference Eligible Department: 05260000 - RU-NIGHT WEEKEND MANAGEMENT Classification Title: Univ Union Program Specialist Classification Minimum Requirements: A high school diploma and four years of appropriate experience. Appropriate college coursework may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Job Description: Building Management and Oversight Provide support and oversight to all staff on duty during assigned shift, including but not limited to housekeeping, maintenance, productions, and event coordinators; provide guidance and resolve operational issues and concerns; assist with special tasks as needed Maintain a safe and secure environment by monitoring facility activity, addressing safety concerns, and coordinating with University Police and other emergency personnel as needed Open and close the facility according to established procedures Conduct regular facility inspections to identify operational, maintenance, safety, and security concerns Periodically monitor adjacent grounds and parking areas to deter theft, vandalism, and unauthorized use Respond to facility emergencies, critical incidents, and facility disruptions; direct staff response efforts, coordinate resources, and ensure timely documentation and communication with supervisors Uphold and enforce all university, departmental and facility policies, procedures, and standards Ensure compliance with safety regulations, ADA requirements, and other applicable guidelines Serve as a role model for professionalism, accountability, customer service, and operational excellence Event & Facility Support Serve as the facility management representative for meetings, conferences, and special events held in the facility Collaborate with event staff, facility users, and clients to ensure successful event execution and exceptional customer experiences Review event diagrams and set up requirements to ensure rooms, audiovisual equipment, and support services are prepared accurately and efficiently Assist with room setups, event transitions, strikes, and equipment staging as needed Monitor event operations and staff performance, providing real-time coaching and guidance to maintain service standards Troubleshoot facility and audiovisual issues and coordinate solutions with appropriate departments Ensure timely access to event spaces and provide responsive customer service to guests, clients, and campus partners Assist hotel staff with addressing facility concerns presented during guest stays Reporting & Documentation Complete comprehensive shift reports detailing operational activities, staffing concerns, facility inspections, events, incidents, and maintenance issues Prepare and submit incident reports, work orders, and required documentation in a timely and accurate manner Report urgent situations, security concerns, and facility issues to appropriate leadership personnel Manage Lost & Found operations, including documentation, retention, and disposal processes Training & Development Assist with onboarding and training of event operations staff Assist in development, maintenance, and implementation of event operations training programs, manuals, reference materials, and operational procedures Observe staff on shift and provide coaching, constructive feedback, and retraining as needed Other Duties Performs special projects, tasks, and other duties, as assigned, to support departmental needs Expected Salary: $18.93/HR; A shift differential is added to the 2nd shift Required Qualifications: A high school diploma and four years of appropriate experience. Appropriate college coursework may substitute at an equivalent rate for the required experience. Preferred: Knowledge of building operations, facility management, and/or event management General knowledge of audio-visual systems and event technology Experience reviewing event diagrams and coordinating set-ups to meet client specifications Proficiency with computers, scheduling software, and common business applications Strong verbal and written communication skills Excellent customer service skills with a commitment to creating a positive experience for guests, clients, and campus partners Effective interpersonal skills and the ability to build positive working relationships Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively and contribute to a team-oriented environment Strong problem-solving skills, sound judgement, and the ability to make decisions in a fast-paced environment Function effectively in a fast-paced environment with frequent interruptions Handle multiple projects or tasks simultaneously Understand, interpret, and apply rules, regulations, policies, and procedures Reliable, punctual, self-motivated, and capable of working independently with minimal supervision Ability to manage emergencies, critical incidents, and challenging situations calmly, professionally, and effectively Special Instructions to Applicants: In order to be considered, you must upload your resume and a cover letter. Criminal background check is required. Normal Work Schedule: This is an evening shift ; 4:00pm - 12:00am Days off rotate to meet department needs Working every other weekend is required Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. This position is eligible for Veteran’s preference. If you are claiming Veteran’s preference, please upload a copy of your DD 214 Member Copy 4 with your application for consideration. See our Veteran's Preference Page for more specific information. This requisition has been reposted. Previous applicants are still under consideration and need not reapply. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 15 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541247/chair-associateprofessor-obstetrics-and-gynecology": {
      "title": "Chair & Associate/Professor - Obstetrics and Gynecology",
      "description": "Chair & Associate/Professor - Obstetrics and Gynecology Apply now Job no: 541247 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Grant or Research Administration, Health Care Administration/Support, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30070000 - JX-OB-GYN-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: Obstetrics - Gynecology - CHAIR & ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: • An M.D., D.O., or equivalent degree from an accredited institution. • Board certification in Obstetrics and Gynecology. • Eligibility for medical licensure in the State of Florida. • Qualifications for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor in either the clinical or tenure-accruing track. • A distinguished record of clinical excellence, academic achievement, scholarly activity, and educational leadership. • Significant leadership experience, such as serving as a division chief, vice chair, associate chair, department chair, or in a comparable leadership role, with demonstrated responsibility for strategic planning, faculty affairs, financial management, and operational oversight. Job Description: The University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville (UF COM-J) invites applications and nominations for the position of Chair, Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. The Department The Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is a dynamic and growing academic department committed to excellence in patient care, education, research, and community engagement. The department is staffed by University of Florida physicians and encompasses specialty divisions in Maternal-Fetal Medicine, Female Pelvic Medicine and Reconstructive Surgery, Gynecologic Oncology, and General Obstetrics and Gynecology. The department provides comprehensive women’s health services across Northeast Florida through a growing network of clinical locations, including UF Health Jacksonville, the region’s premier academic health center and tertiary referral hospital; UF Health North, a full-service medical campus in North Jacksonville offering inpatient and outpatient women’s health services; and multiple ambulatory practices throughout the region. These include the Emerson Women’s Health Center, the Elizabeth G. Means Center, Generation-to-Generation Women’s Care in Orange Park, and the expanding UF Health St. Johns (Durbin Park) campus, serving one of the fastest-growing communities in Northeast Florida. The department has a robust research enterprise supporting faculty scholarship, clinical trials, and translational research. It also sponsors an Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME)-accredited Obstetrics and Gynecology Residency Program and is committed to preparing the next generation of physicians, educators, and physician leaders. With expertise spanning the full spectrum of women’s health, advanced clinical capabilities, and a highly accomplished faculty, the Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is well positioned for continued growth and innovation across its clinical, academic, and research missions. The Opportunity The Chair will join a strong and collaborative leadership team committed to advancing the academic, clinical, and research missions of UF Health Jacksonville and the University of Florida. The continued growth of Northeast Florida, combined with the statewide reputation, resources, and academic strength of UF Health, creates significant opportunities for the Chair to expand clinical programs, develop innovative models of care, strengthen research and scholarship, enhance graduate medical education, and advance the department’s regional and national reputation. The Chair will provide strategic and operational leadership for the department and will be responsible for advancing its clinical, academic, research, and educational missions. The Chair will work closely with faculty, department leadership, hospital and health system leaders, and University partners to identify opportunities for growth, strengthen collaboration, and ensure alignment with the broader priorities of UF COM-J and UF Health. A particular opportunity for the next Chair will be to lead the continued development and strategic expansion of women’s health services throughout Northeast Florida, including growth within the rapidly expanding UF Health St. Johns market. University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three University of Florida health colleges located on the approximately 110-acre UF Health Jacksonville campus. The College’s 16 clinical science departments include more than 500 faculty members and 450 residents and fellows. The College sponsors 39 accredited graduate medical education programs and serves as a major regional campus for the University of Florida College of Medicine, providing third- and fourth-year medical student clerkships and electives. The College is an integral part of UF Health Jacksonville, an academic health center providing tertiary and quaternary care to patients throughout Northeast Florida and the surrounding region. The College’s faculty are engaged across the full continuum of clinical care, medical education, research, and community service. Responsibilities Reporting directly to the Dean of the College of Medicine – Jacksonville, the Chair will have overall responsibility for the leadership, strategic direction, administration, and performance of the Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. Key responsibilities include: Developing and advancing a compelling strategic vision for the department that aligns with the priorities of UF COM-J and UF Health. Leading the continued growth and integration of clinical services across Northeast Florida. Advancing the department’s academic and research missions, including scholarly activity, clinical research, and translational research. Fostering excellence in undergraduate and graduate medical education and strengthening the department’s educational programs. Recruiting, retaining, mentoring, and developing talented faculty. Promoting a culture of collaboration, innovation, accountability, and continuous improvement. Strengthening partnerships across departments, specialties, the College, UF Health, and the broader Jacksonville medical community. Providing effective fiscal and administrative oversight of the department and ensuring responsible stewardship of resources. Supporting quality, safety, patient experience, and operational excellence across the department’s clinical enterprise. Representing the department effectively within the University, health system, professional organizations, and broader community. Identifying opportunities to enhance the department’s regional, national, and academic reputation. Expected Salary: Negotiable Required Qualifications: • An M.D., D.O., or equivalent degree from an accredited institution. • Board certification in Obstetrics and Gynecology. • Eligibility for medical licensure in the State of Florida. • Qualifications for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor in either the clinical or tenure-accruing track. • A distinguished record of clinical excellence, academic achievement, scholarly activity, and educational leadership. • Significant leadership experience, such as serving as a division chief, vice chair, associate chair, department chair, or in a comparable leadership role, with demonstrated responsibility for strategic planning, faculty affairs, financial management, and operational oversight. Preferred: The ideal candidate will be an accomplished physician leader with a clear vision for the future of academic obstetrics and gynecology and a demonstrated ability to translate that vision into meaningful clinical, academic, and organizational results. Demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in patient care, medical education, research, and faculty development. Have a track record of recruiting, mentoring, developing, and retaining high-performing faculty. Bring experience leading growth and transformation within a complex academic or integrated health system environment. Demonstrate strong financial, operational, and organizational leadership skills. Foster a culture of collaboration, transparency, accountability, and innovation. Champion interdisciplinary and cross-organizational partnerships. Demonstrate a commitment to quality improvement, patient safety, and health equity. Have experience advancing graduate medical education and developing future physician leaders. Be well positioned to expand the department’s clinical, academic, and research footprint throughout Northeast Florida. Possess national recognition and active engagement in professional societies and academic organizations, which are highly desirable. The next Chair will have an exceptional opportunity to build upon the Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology’s strong foundation while shaping its future growth and impact across Northeast Florida and beyond. Special Instructions to Applicants: The search committee is assisted by the executive search firm Spencer Stuart. Applications, nominations and comments may be sent to SBryant@SpencerStuart.com . The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Application must be submitted by 11:55 p.m. (ET) of the posting end date. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541257/chair-associatefull-professor-department-of-surgery": {
      "title": "Chair & Associate/Full Professor - Department of Surgery",
      "description": "Chair & Associate/Full Professor - Department of Surgery Apply now Job no: 541257 Work type: Multi-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Grant or Research Administration, Health Care Administration/Support, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30140000 - JX-SURGERY-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: CHAIR & ASO/FULL PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates must possess: • An M.D., D.O., or equivalent degree from an accredited institution. • Board certification in a surgical specialty appropriate to the department. • Eligibility for medical licensure in the State of Florida. • Qualifications for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor in either the clinical or tenure-accruing track. • A distinguished record of clinical excellence, academic achievement, scholarly activity, and educational leadership. • Significant leadership experience, such as serving as a division chief, vice chair, associate chair, department chair, or in a comparable leadership role, with demonstrated responsibility for strategic planning, faculty affairs, financial management, and operational oversight. Job Description: The University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville (UF COM-J) invites applications and nominations for the position of Chair, Department of Surgery. The Department The Department of Surgery is a dynamic and nationally recognized academic department committed to excellence in patient care, education, research, and service to the community. The department provides a broad range of surgical services and serves as a major referral resource for patients throughout Northeast Florida and Southeast Georgia. The department encompasses seven major divisions: Acute Care Surgery, Cardiothoracic Surgery, General and Gastrointestinal Surgery, Surgical Oncology, Otorhinolaryngology, Plastic and Reconstructive Surgery, and Vascular Surgery. With expertise spanning general and specialty surgery, trauma, surgical oncology, and complex surgical care, the department is well positioned for continued growth and innovation across its clinical, academic, and research missions. The department provides inpatient and outpatient surgical services at multiple locations and is closely integrated with UF Health Jacksonville, the region’s academic health center and tertiary referral hospital. A defining strength of the department is its leadership of UF Health TraumaOne, which is the only adult and pediatric Level I trauma program in Northeast Florida and Southeast Georgia and provides comprehensive care for patients with complex and life-threatening injuries. The Department of Surgery has a robust educational mission. It sponsors a five-year, ACGME-accredited General Surgery Residency Program that provides broad operative and clinical experiences, including trauma, emergency general surgery, surgical critical care, vascular surgery, surgical oncology, colorectal surgery, breast surgery, minimally invasive surgery, pediatric surgery, and plastic surgery. The department also offers fellowship training in complex gastrointestinal surgery, surgical critical care, advanced rhinology and endoscopic sinus and skull base surgery, laryngology, and medical otorhinolaryngology. The department is also committed to advancing surgical knowledge through clinical research, faculty scholarship, translational research, and collaboration across the University of Florida. Its research enterprise includes clinical research and opportunities for faculty and trainees to engage in innovative investigations that improve surgical care and patient outcomes. With a broad clinical footprint, nationally significant trauma and surgical programs, a strong graduate medical education enterprise, and a highly accomplished faculty, the Department of Surgery is well positioned for continued growth and innovation across its clinical, academic, educational, and research missions. The Opportunity The Chair will join a strong and collaborative leadership team committed to advancing the academic, clinical, educational, and research missions of UF Health Jacksonville and the University of Florida. The continued growth of Northeast Florida and Southeast Georgia, combined with the statewide reputation, resources, and academic strength of UF Health, creates significant opportunities for the Chair to expand surgical services, strengthen centers of excellence, develop innovative models of care, advance surgical research, enhance graduate medical education, and elevate the department’s regional and national reputation. The Chair will provide strategic and operational leadership for the department and will be responsible for advancing its clinical, academic, research, and educational missions. The Chair will work closely with faculty, division chiefs, department leadership, hospital and health system leaders, and University partners to identify opportunities for growth, strengthen collaboration, and ensure alignment with the broader priorities of UF COM-J and UF Health. A particular opportunity for the next Chair will be to lead the continued development and strategic growth of the department’s surgical enterprise throughout Northeast Florida and Southeast Georgia. This includes strengthening existing surgical programs, expanding access to advanced and specialized surgical care, optimizing the integration of inpatient and outpatient services, and identifying opportunities for growth across the department’s diverse clinical divisions. The next Chair will also have the opportunity to build upon the department’s significant trauma and acute care surgery infrastructure, including UF Health TraumaOne, while strengthening multidisciplinary partnerships and developing innovative approaches to complex surgical care. The Chair will be expected to foster an environment that supports clinical excellence while advancing the department’s academic and research profile, strengthening surgical education, and recruiting and developing the next generation of surgical leaders. University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three University of Florida health colleges located on the approximately 110-acre UF Health Jacksonville campus. The College’s 16 clinical science departments include more than 500 faculty members and 450 residents and fellows. The College sponsors 39 accredited graduate medical education programs and serves as a major regional campus for the University of Florida College of Medicine, providing third- and fourth-year medical student clerkships and electives. The College is an integral part of UF Health Jacksonville, an academic health center providing tertiary and quaternary care to patients throughout Northeast Florida and the surrounding region. The College’s faculty are engaged across the full continuum of clinical care, medical education, research, and community service. Responsibilities Reporting directly to the Dean of the College of Medicine – Jacksonville, the Chair will have overall responsibility for the leadership, strategic direction, administration, and performance of the Department of Surgery. Key responsibilities include: Developing and advancing a compelling strategic vision for the department that aligns with the priorities of UF COM-J and UF Health. Leading the continued growth, integration, and strategic development of surgical services across Northeast Florida and Southeast Georgia. Strengthening the department’s clinical programs and centers of excellence while identifying opportunities for new programs and services. Advancing the department’s academic and research missions, including clinical research, translational research, scholarly activity, and external funding. Supporting the continued development and excellence of UF Health TraumaOne and the department’s acute care, trauma, and surgical critical care programs. Fostering excellence in graduate medical education and strengthening the department’s residency and fellowship programs. Recruiting, retaining, mentoring, and developing talented faculty and future surgical leaders. Promoting a culture of collaboration, innovation, accountability, inclusion, and continuous improvement. Strengthening partnerships across surgical divisions, departments, specialties, the College, UF Health, and the broader Jacksonville medical community. Providing effective fiscal and administrative oversight of the department and ensuring responsible stewardship of resources. Supporting quality, safety, patient experience, access, and operational excellence across the department’s clinical enterprise. Developing strategies to enhance faculty productivity, engagement, professional development, and academic advancement. Representing the department effectively within the University, health system, professional organizations, and broader community. Identifying opportunities to enhance the department’s regional, national, and academic reputation. The next Chair will have an exceptional opportunity to build upon the Department of Surgery’s strong clinical, academic, and educational foundation while shaping its future growth and impact across Northeast Florida, Southeast Georgia, and beyond. The Chair will lead a highly specialized surgical enterprise with the opportunity to further strengthen its reputation for clinical excellence, surgical education, research, innovation, and service to the region. Expected Salary: Salary commensurate with experience and training. Required Qualifications: Candidates must possess: An M.D., D.O., or equivalent degree from an accredited institution. Board certification in a surgical specialty appropriate to the department. Eligibility for medical licensure in the State of Florida. Qualifications for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor in either the clinical or tenure-accruing track. A distinguished record of clinical excellence, academic achievement, scholarly activity, and educational leadership. Significant leadership experience, such as serving as a division chief, vice chair, associate chair, department chair, or in a comparable leadership role, with demonstrated responsibility for strategic planning, faculty affairs, financial management, and operational oversight. Preferred: Desired Attributes The ideal candidate will be an accomplished physician leader with a clear vision for the future of academic surgery and a demonstrated ability to translate that vision into meaningful clinical, academic, and organizational results. The successful candidate will: Demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in patient care, medical education, research, and faculty development. Have a track record of recruiting, mentoring, developing, and retaining high-performing faculty. Bring experience leading growth and transformation within a complex academic or integrated health system environment. Demonstrate strong financial, operational, and organizational leadership skills. Foster a culture of collaboration, transparency, accountability, and innovation. Champion interdisciplinary and cross-organizational partnerships to improve patient care and advance the academic mission. Demonstrate a commitment to quality improvement, patient safety, and health equity. Have experience advancing graduate medical education and developing future physician and surgical leaders. Demonstrate an understanding of the unique clinical, educational, and operational requirements of a comprehensive academic surgical department. Have experience developing or expanding clinical programs and centers of excellence within a competitive healthcare environment. Be well positioned to expand the department’s clinical, academic, research, and educational footprint throughout Northeast Florida and Southeast Georgia. Possess national recognition and active engagement in professional societies and academic organizations, which are highly desirable. Special Instructions to Applicants: The executive search firm Spencer Stuart has been retained to assist in this recruitment. Application, nominations and inquiries may be sent to TSorota@SpencerStuart.com . Review of application will begin immediately and will continue until position is filled. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541261/extension-agent-iiiii-environmental-horticulture": {
      "title": "Extension Agent II-III – Environmental Horticulture",
      "description": "Extension Agent II-III – Environmental Horticulture Apply now Job no: 541261 Work type: Permanent Status Faculty Location: Broward Categories: Biology/Life Science, Agricultural Sciences Department: 60355006 - AG-BROWARD Classification Title: EXT AGENT II-III Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates shall hold a master’s degree or 50% of hours completed towards a master’s degree along with qualifying experience or accomplishments. Job Description: You Belong at a University that’s Leading the Way The Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences (UF/IFAS) is creating an environment that affirms community across all dimensions. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call 352-392-2477 or the Florida Relay System at 800-955-8771 (TDD) or visit Accessibility at UF . The Opportunity This 12-month, permanent-status accruing Extension Agent II-III position provides leadership for Environmental Horticulture programming in Broward County and reports directly to the County Extension Director. The successful candidate will develop, implement, deliver, and evaluate a UF/IFAS Extension Pathway Initiative 4 aligned environmental education program to serve landscape professionals, arborists, nursery operators, municipal employees, property managers, pest management professionals, youth audiences (5% programmatic appointment), and other green industry stakeholders. The agent will identify community and industry needs then deliver impactful educational programs and services that address emerging issues affecting Broward County's green industry sector while promoting environmental stewardship, urban forest sustainability, and responsible resource management. Environmental Horticulture Program Development and Delivery Develop and implement a comprehensive environmental horticulture Extension program based on stakeholder needs, county priorities, and statewide Extension Pathway statewide action plans. Deliver educational workshops, demonstrations, field days, webinars, certification training, and technical assistance programs. Prepare fact sheets, newsletters, digital resources, social media content, and other educational materials. Evaluate program effectiveness and document outcomes and impacts in accordance with Extension reporting requirements. Utilize innovative technologies and educational methods to expand program reach and effectiveness. Green-Industry Best Management Practices Provide leadership in Green Industries Best Management Practices education, outreach and testing for certification. Conduct training programs focused on nutrient management, irrigation efficiency, integrated pest management, and environmental compliance. Promote practices that improve landscape performance while protecting water quality and natural resources. Collaborate with industry professionals and regulatory agencies to encourage adoption of science-based management practices. Urban Forestry and Tree Management Develop and deliver educational programs related to tree selection, planting, pruning, maintenance, risk reduction, and storm resilience. Promote proper tree trimming standards and arboricultural best practices. Assist municipalities, landscape professionals, and arborists in addressing urban forestry issues. Support initiatives that enhance Broward County’s urban forest health, sustainability, and resilience. Invasive Species Management Develop educational programs addressing invasive plant identification, prevention, monitoring, and management. Promote integrated management strategies that protect native ecosystems and urban landscapes. Collaborate with local, regional, and state partners to address invasive species concerns and emerging threats. Partnerships, Leadership, and Administration Establish and maintain effective working relationships with industry organizations, governmental agencies, educational institutions, and community groups. Facilitate advisory group participation to determine educational needs and service gaps. Participate in county, district, and statewide Extension Initiative Teams Seek external support, sponsorships, and collaborative funding opportunities that enhance program capacity. Incorporate youth education and career awareness opportunities into programming activities, with youth engagement representing a minimum of five percent of the appointment. Prepare annual reporting, impact summaries, and other administrative documents as required. Supervision Directly supervise at least one Program Assistant. Provide leadership, performance coaching, work planning, and professional development opportunities to Program Assistant. Coordinate support activities that ensure effective program delivery and customer service. Working Conditions The position requires a combination of office, classroom, and field-based responsibilities. A significant portion of the assignment involves conducting educational programs, demonstrations, and site visits in outdoor settings throughout Broward County. Candidates should be comfortable working in South Florida environmental conditions, including heat, humidity, direct sunlight, rain, and exposure to insects and other natural elements. The position requires the ability to walk on uneven terrain, stand for extended periods, and transport educational materials and equipment necessary for program delivery. Travel within the county and occasional travel elsewhere in Florida will be required. About UF The University of Florida is one of the nation’s leading public land-grant universities, recognized for excellence in teaching, research, and service. As a member of the Association of American Universities, the University of Florida is committed to addressing critical societal challenges through innovation, scholarship, and community engagement. The university provides students, faculty, and staff with opportunities to contribute to meaningful work that improves lives locally, nationally, and globally. About UF/IFAS Extension UF/IFAS Extension is the statewide outreach arm of the Institute of Food and Agricultural Sciences. Through county Extension offices, research and education centers, and statewide program teams, UF/IFAS Extension delivers research-based information and educational programs that help individuals, businesses, communities, and governments make informed decisions. Extension professionals serve as trusted resources, translating university research into practical applications that address agricultural, environmental, economic, and social issues affecting Florida’s residents. UF/IFAS Extension reaches communities throughout Florida by providing educational leadership in agriculture, horticulture, natural resources, youth development, family and consumer sciences, sustainability, water resources, and community development. Extension programs are developed through stakeholder input and are designed to create measurable impacts that improve quality of life, strengthen industries, and support environmental stewardship. Extension professionals work collaboratively with local governments, industry organizations, schools, nonprofit partners, and residents to identify challenges and develop innovative solutions. Educational efforts include workshops, demonstrations, consultations, field days, online training, publications, applied research, and technical assistance programs. This position provides a unique opportunity to serve one of Florida’s largest and most dynamic urban green industry markets. The successful candidate will play a critical role in supporting green industry professionals, municipalities, arborists, landscape contractors, and other stakeholders through science-based education focused on sustainable landscape management and natural resource protection. The University of Florida offers a competitive compensation package and excellent benefits. Information regarding employee benefits is available at UF Benefits . About Broward County Broward County is located in southeastern Florida between Palm Beach and Miami-Dade counties and encompasses approximately 1,323 square miles. With a population exceeding two million residents, Broward is one of Florida’s most populous counties and includes 31 municipalities ranging from highly urbanized coastal communities to western areas adjacent to the Everglades. The county supports a substantial urban horticulture industry that includes landscape maintenance companies, arboriculture firms, municipal parks departments, golf courses, nurseries, property management organizations, and environmental consulting businesses. Year-round growing conditions create significant opportunities for the green industry while also presenting challenges associated with tropical pests, invasive species, water conservation, fertilizer management, urban forestry, and hurricane preparedness. UF/IFAS Extension Broward County maintains strong partnerships with local governments, state agencies, industry associations, schools, environmental organizations, and community groups. The county’s urban landscape, extensive tree canopy, and environmental resources provide exceptional opportunities for innovative Extension programming that supports both economic vitality and environmental sustainability. Expected Salary: Commensurate with education and experience. Required Qualifications: A master’s degree in environmental Horticulture, Horticultural Sciences, Plant Sciences, Urban Forestry, Arboriculture, Natural Resources, Agriculture, or a closely related field. Applicants with at least 50 percent completion toward a master’s degree may be considered for appointment at the Extension Agent II level in accordance with university requirements. Demonstrated knowledge of horticulture, nursery management, landscape management, arboriculture, or related green industry disciplines. Experience planning, implementing, and evaluating educational, outreach, training, or technical assistance programs. Knowledge of landscape best management practices, nursery production, irrigation principles, nutrient management, and environmental stewardship. Knowledge of tree care, pruning standards, urban forestry, and arboriculture concepts. Knowledge of invasive species identification and management principles. Strong written, verbal, interpersonal, facilitation, and public speaking skills. Ability to establish and maintain productive relationships with stakeholder groups and partner organizations. Experience using technology and digital communication tools for educational programming, reporting and evaluation. Ability to collect, analyze, and communicate program outcomes and impacts. Ability to supervise support personnel and manage multiple assignments simultaneously. Ability to obtain a valid driver’s license and ability to travel throughout the county and state as required. Because youth engagement will comprise at least five percent of the appointment, successful completion and maintenance of applicable UF youth protection requirements, including Level 2 DCF screening, is required. Candidates must also have a commitment to the University of Florida's core values. The successful candidate must obtain and maintain the appropriate Pesticide Applicator license and Green Industry/Best Management Practices certification . Preferred: Doctoral degree in Horticulture, Environmental Horticulture, Urban Forestry, Arboriculture, Plant Sciences, Natural Resources, or a closely related discipline for applicants seeking appointment at the Extension Agent III level. Experience working with Florida’s landscape, nursery, arboriculture, or green industry sectors. Experience delivering Green Industries Best Management Practices training programs. Experience conducting arboriculture, urban forestry, tree risk management, or storm preparedness education. Experience implementing invasive species management or environmental stewardship initiatives. Experience supervising staff, managing budgets, or coordinating volunteers. Demonstrated success securing grants, sponsorships, or external funding. Experience developing partnerships with municipalities, homeowner associations, industry groups, schools, and youth-serving organizations. Ability to communicate effectively in English and Spanish. Experience using evaluation methods to measure program effectiveness and impact. Professional certifications such as International Society of Arboriculture Certified Arborist and Commercial Pesticide Applicator certification are highly desirable and will strengthen the candidate’s ability to serve Broward County’s green industry clientele. Special Instructions to Applicants: Apply at Careers at UF by 11:55 p.m. (Eastern) of the posting end date, and attach: Cover letter that states applicant’s interest in the position and qualifications relative to the credentials listed above Curriculum vitae Contact information (including email addresses) for 3 individuals willing to write letters of recommendation Selected candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered “official” if a designation of “Issued to Student” is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) . Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under Florida Sunshine and Public Record laws. The University and greater Gainesville community enjoy a variety of cultural events, restaurants, year-round outdoor recreational activities, and social opportunities. Health Assessment Required: No Advertised: 04 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Oct 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541154/womens-imaging-radiologist-clinical-assistant-professor": {
      "title": "Women's Imaging Radiologist - Clinical Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Women's Imaging Radiologist - Clinical Assistant Professor Apply now Job no: 541154 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30130000 - JX-RADIOLOGY-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: CLIN AST PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Candidates should have an MD/DO and specialty in Diagnostic Radiology Job Description: The University of Florida College of Medicine - Jacksonville, Department of Radiology is seeking a full-time Women’s Imaging Radiologist at the non-tenure clinical accruing level of Assistant Full Professor. Job Responsibilities Include: Women's Imaging Radiologist with academic focus at UF Jacksonville Health System at the Assistant Professor level with 100% specialization. Full-time academic position with UF Radiology and Imaging, UFJP Provide high quality service in a friendly academic setting. Serve as a mentor to train a great group of Residents & Fellows. Work with colleagues in surgery, oncology, radiation oncology & pathology, via daily interactions/multidisciplinary meetings. Have the drive and leadership skills necessary to be an integral part of the Women's Imaging Division. About UF Health: UF Health is the Southeast’s premier academic health center and an integral part of the University of Florida. Our mission is to promote health through outstanding and high-quality patient care, innovative and rigorous education in the health professions and biomedical sciences, and research across the spectrum of basic, translational and clinical investigation. UF Health encompasses the Gainesville-based University of Florida colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine; the UF Health Shands family of hospitals in Gainesville; UF Health Jacksonville medical center; UF Health North; an academic campus in Jacksonville that is home to the UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville and includes degree programs offered by the colleges of Nursing and Pharmacy; UF Health St. Johns in St. Augustine; UF Health Neighborhood Hospital in Ocala; and our campus in Central Florida, which includes UF Health Spanish Plaines Hospital and UF Health Leesburg Hospital. It also includes primary care and specialty practices throughout Central, North Central and northern Florida and Southeast Georgia. The UF Health network of hospitals and physician practices manages more than 3 million inpatient and outpatient visits each year, and serves patients from all 67 Florida counties, from across the nation and from dozens of countries around the world. About College of Medicine – Jacksonville (COM-J): The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three colleges at the Health Science Center Jacksonville. The college's 16 clinical science departments house more than 500 faculty members, 1,560 staff and 450 residents and fellows, as well as medical students from UF and around the country. We offer an incredible breadth of clinical training programs and proud to train many of best primary care providers and specialists throughout the region, the state and the country. Research, discovery and innovation are critical aspects of our clinical campus and we have some of the country’s leading researchers at our locations searching for and finding new treatments and clinical options. The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville faculty, administrators, residents, fellows, students and staff work as a team in pursuit of our common mission — to heal, to comfort, to educate and to discover through quality health care, elimination of health disparities, medical education, innovation and research. To learn more about our college, leadership, mission, faculty resources, and the city of Jacksonville visit https://med.jax.ufl.edu/administrative-affairs/faculty-orientation-guide/ . Expected Salary: Commensurate with experience. Required Qualifications: Candidates should have an MD/DO and specialty in Diagnostic Radiology Preferred: The candidate be Board Certified/Board Eligible in Diagnostic Radiology. The successful candidate will be fellowship trained in Women’s Imaging and experienced in all aspects of Woman's Imaging. The candidate should be a team player and expect to join a pleasant group of colleagues. Special Instructions to Applicants: The Search Committee will begin to review applications as soon as an applicant pool is identified. Please provide letter of intent and Curriculum Vitae. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/ . The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: \"Yes\" Advertised: 03 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explore.jobs.ufl.edu/en-us/job/541155/psychologysurgical-psychology-clinical-assistant-or-associate-professor": {
      "title": "Psychology/Surgical Psychology - Clinical Assistant or Associate Professor",
      "description": "Psychology/Surgical Psychology - Clinical Assistant or Associate Professor Apply now Job no: 541155 Work type: Non-Tenure-Track Faculty Location: Jacksonville Campus Categories: Education/Training/Instructional Design, Grant or Research Administration, Health Care Administration/Support, Medicine/Physicians Department: 30140000 - JX-SURGERY-JACKSONVILLE Classification Title: CLIN AST/ASO PROF Classification Minimum Requirements: Doctoral Level Licensed Psychologist (or license-eligible), Doctorate in Clinical and/or Counseling Psychology, with clinical experience working within an acute care hospital setting. Job Description: The University of Florida College of Medicine – Jacksonville, Department of Surgery, Division of Acute Care Surgery, seeks a full-time faculty position for a Surgical Psychologist at the non-tenure accruing level of Clinical Assistant or Associate Professor. The position will include overall responsibility for the on-going, day-to-day operations of the psychological services/family management component of the trauma system, inclusive of crisis interventions, grief counseling, individual, family and group therapies, stress debriefings, psychological assessments and diagnoses, in-patient consultation/liaison services, and out-patient follow-up/referral as needed. Also provide on-going stress management consultation and debriefings as needed for surgical residents, medical students, nursing staff, and other allied health professionals. Must have experience in program development and initiative and must be familiar working with a culturally diverse patient population that will include adults and children. Teaching responsibilities include co-direct supervision of the psychology post-doctoral fellow within the Division of Acute Care Surgery, and clinical teaching to medical residents, students, allied professionals and nursing staff on the psychological aspects of physical injury and other related topics specific to a collaborative care model, i.e. effective communication, capacity issues, stress management. Expected administrative responsibilities include membership on various committees at the hospital, local and University level. Also responsible for consistently obtaining and maintaining extra-mural funding for research activities, development and administration of research studies, scholarly presentations at the local, regional and national level, and publications in peer-reviewed journals. About UF Health: UF Health is the Southeast’s premier academic health center and an integral part of the University of Florida. Our mission is to promote health through outstanding and high-quality patient care, innovative and rigorous education in the health professions and biomedical sciences, and research across the spectrum of basic, translational and clinical investigation. UF Health encompasses the Gainesville-based University of Florida colleges of Dentistry, Medicine, Nursing, Pharmacy, Public Health and Health Professions, and Veterinary Medicine; the UF Health Shands family of hospitals in Gainesville; UF Health Jacksonville medical center; UF Health North; an academic campus in Jacksonville that is home to the UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville and includes degree programs offered by the colleges of Nursing and Pharmacy; UF Health St. Johns in St. Augustine; UF Health Neighborhood Hospital in Ocala; and our campus in Central Florida, which includes UF Health Spanish Plaines Hospital and UF Health Leesburg Hospital. It also includes primary care and specialty practices throughout Central, North Central and northern Florida and Southeast Georgia. The UF Health network of hospitals and physician practices manages more than 3 million inpatient and outpatient visits each year, and serves patients from all 67 Florida counties, from across the nation and from dozens of countries around the world. About College of Medicine – Jacksonville (COM-J): The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville is the largest of the three colleges at the Health Science Center Jacksonville. The college's 16 clinical science departments house more than 500 faculty members, 1,560 staff and 450 residents and fellows, as well as medical students from UF and around the country. We offer an incredible breadth of clinical training programs and proud to train many of best primary care providers and specialists throughout the region, the state and the country. Research, discovery and innovation are critical aspects of our clinical campus and we have some of the country’s leading researchers at our locations searching for and finding new treatments and clinical options. The UF College of Medicine – Jacksonville faculty, administrators, residents, fellows, students and staff work as a team in pursuit of our common mission — to heal, to comfort, to educate and to discover through quality health care, elimination of health disparities, medical education, innovation and research. To learn more about our college, leadership, mission, faculty resources, and the city of Jacksonville visit https://med.jax.ufl.edu/administrative-affairs/faculty-orientation-guide/. Expected Salary: Salary and academic appointments are commensurate with experience. Required Qualifications: Doctoral Level Licensed Psychologist (or license-eligible), Doctorate in Clinical and/or Counseling Psychology, with clinical experience working within an acute care hospital setting. Preferred: Clinical experience working within an acute care, hospital setting, as part of an interdisciplinary treatment team; experience in teaching within an academic medical setting; research experience; versed in clinical interventions with patients across the lifespan and also within a diverse patient population. Special Instructions to Applicants: The Search Committee will begin to review applications as soon as an applicant pool is identified. Please provide letter of intent and Curriculum Vitae. The final candidate will be required to provide an official transcript to the hiring department upon hire. A transcript will not be considered \"official\" if a designation of \"Issued to Student\" is visible. Degrees earned from an education institution outside of the United States are required to be evaluated by a professional credentialing service provider approved by the National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES), which can be found at http://www.naces.org/. The University of Florida is a public institution and subject to all requirements under the Florida Sunshine and Public Records laws. If an accommodation due to a disability is needed to apply for this position, please call (352) 392-2HRS or the Florida Relay System at (800) 955-8771 (TDD). Hiring is contingent upon eligibility to work in the US. Searches are conducted in accordance with Florida's Sunshine Law. Health Assessment Required: Yes Advertised: 03 Sep 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Open until filled Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
